{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","toolName":"read","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: vLLM PR #54129 (`VLLM_PLE_MMAP`, branch `ple-mmap-upstream`) — AMD/ROCm reachability\n\nInvestigator session: 2026-08-28. Analysis only; no code written or patched.\n\n**Checkouts read this session**\n- `#54129`: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-tom-mecattaf-notes-qwen-next/c4262a6d-5cde-494b-9143-685dd896a84f/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129`\n  HEAD `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream`, `2026-08-28 11:52:45 +0700`, author *Christopher Deodat* [M: `git log -1 --format='%ci %an %s'`]\n- `#53896`: `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr53896`\n  HEAD `89d0bb7 update CI`, `2026-08-28 16:54:24 +0800`, author *huanghaoyan.hhy* [M: same command]\n- Raw diff: `.../scratchpad/pr54129.diff`, 26483 lines [M: `wc -l`]\n\nGrades: **[S]** read from source · **[M]** measured (command output) · **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n---\n\n## Q0 — Headline answer\n\n**Yes.** The mmap/engram path is wired exclusively into `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/`. On a ROCm build the `amd/` tree is imported instead, `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is never imported, the custom op is never registered, and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` is silently a **no-op** — no error, no warning, the stock GPU-resident `VocabParallelEmbedding` is built as if the flag were unset.\n\n**Measured reachability** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n 'ple_mmap\\|PLE_MMAP\\|mmap\\|Mmap\\|MMAP' \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output — zero hits in all four files)\n```\nversus 11 hits in `nvidia/ple_layer.py` and 5 in `nvidia/model.py`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'nvidia' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output)\n```\nThe `amd/` tree never reaches into `nvidia/`. [M]\n\n**Correction to the campaign record**: the port is **not ~15 lines**. See §\"Exact port specification\" — it is ~12 lines in `amd/model.py` plus ~45 code lines (~60 with the load-bearing comments) in `amd/ple_layer.py`, **and** it depends on an FP8 dequant capability that `amd/ple_layer.py` does not currently have at all (§Blocker A). That last item is the real finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Platform dispatch — how a ROCm build lands in `amd/`\n\nSelection is a lazy module `__getattr__` on the model package, not a registry-level split.\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/__init__.py:22-49` [S]:\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Any:\n    if name in {\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\", \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\", \"Qwen4ExpMTP\"}:\n        from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n        if current_platform.is_xpu() or current_platform.is_tpu():\n            raise NotImplementedError(\"Qwen4Exp currently supports CUDA and ROCm only\")\n        if current_platform.is_rocm():\n            from .amd.model import (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM, Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration)\n            from .amd.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n        else:\n            from .nvidia.model import (...)\n            from .nvidia.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n```\n\nChain of evidence:\n- Registry points at the package, not a platform subtree: `vllm/model_executor/models/registry.py:114-117` `\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\": (\"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\", \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\")`; `:580-583` for `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration`; `:669` for `Qwen4ExpMTP`. [S]\n- `is_rocm()` is `self._enum == PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/interface.py:192-193`. [S]\n- `RocmPlatform._enum = PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:498-499`. [S]\n- Platform selection: `vllm/platforms/__init__.py:110-128` — `rocm_platform_plugin()` returns `\"vllm.platforms.rocm.RocmPlatform\"` iff `amdsmi` imports and `amdsmi_get_processor_handles()` is non-empty. [S]\n\n**Is there any chance a ROCm build imports the nvidia tree?** No, on the model path. [S]\n- The `else:` branch at `__init__.py:38-43` is the only importer of `nvidia/model.py` / `nvidia/mtp.py`, and it is unreachable when `is_rocm()` is true.\n- `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is imported from exactly two places, both inside the nvidia tree: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:52` and `nvidia/model.py:77`. [S/M]\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/__init__.py` is a 3-line SPDX-only stub (no re-exports) [S] — so a hypothetical `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` from `amd/` would import *only* `ple_mmap.py`, dragging in no CUDA-specific nvidia module. That is what makes option (a) in §Port viable.\n\nConsequence: the module-scope `direct_register_custom_op(...)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:981-986` never runs on ROCm, so `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` does not exist there — even though the op *name* is unconditionally present in the default splitting-ops list (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`). [S]\n\n---\n\n## 2. TP semantics of the mmap path\n\n### Vocab-parallel path: a per-lookup all-reduce\n`vllm/model_executor/layers/vocab_parallel_embedding.py:490-518` [S]:\n```python\ndef forward(self, input_):\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        masked_input, input_mask = get_masked_input_and_mask(...)   # :493-500\n    ...\n    output_parallel = self.quant_method.embedding(self, masked_input.long())   # :504\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        if output_parallel.dtype in (torch.float8_e4m3fn, torch.float8_e5m2):\n            comm_output = output_parallel.view(torch.int8)                     # :512\n            comm_output.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :513\n            output = tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(comm_output)             # :514\n            return output.view(output_parallel.dtype)\n        output_parallel.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :516\n        return tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(output_parallel)               # :517\n    return output_parallel\n```\nThe FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n\nSo: **yes, one `tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce` per PLE lookup, per PLE layer, per forward** whenever `tp_size > 1`.\n\n### Mmap path: plain `nn.Module`, whole table per rank, no collective\n`MmapNgramEmbedding` is declared `class MmapNgramEmbedding(nn.Module)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:506` [S] and constructed with the **full padded vocab**: `ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:288-290` [S] — the identical `padded_vocab_size` handed to the vocab-parallel constructor in the `else:` branch at `:292-293`.\n\nIts `forward` (`ple_mmap.py:545-573`) contains **no collective, no mask, no rank arithmetic**:\n```\n$ grep -n 'rank\\|tp_\\|world_size\\|parallel\\|all_reduce\\|distributed' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n(no output; exit 1)\n```\n[M] — zero hits across the whole 1010-line file.\n\n`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n```python\nfor idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n    self.mm[idx] = np.memmap(path, dtype=np.uint8, mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))\n    self.rows_total += rows\n```\nand `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n\n**Verdict**: switching to the mmap path removes the per-token all-reduce/all-gather at the PLE lookup site entirely. The trade is that every rank independently gathers the full-width rows for every token — TP-redundant I/O and TP-redundant host→device copies, in exchange for zero NCCL/RCCL traffic at that site. (The redundancy hits page cache, shared per host, so the *disk* cost is not multiplied — that last clause is my inference from `mode=\"r\"` shared file mappings, not read from source; treat as reasoning, not evidence.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. `load_weights` under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n\nThe interception lives in `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:450-547`. Two mmap-specific branches:\n\n**(a) weight_scale kept** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:477-490` [S]:\n```python\nif (isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding)\n        and name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"):\n    ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight,\n                              cast(torch.Tensor, self.layer_multipliers).device)\n    loaded.add(name); continue\n```\n`set_weight_scale` registers a non-persistent buffer, `ple_mmap.py:587-590`. [S]\n\n**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n```python\nif isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding):\n    # Served from disk via mmap; the loader still streams\n    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n    embedding.weights_streamed = True\n    loaded.add(\"ngram_embedding.weight\")\n    continue\n```\n`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n\nThe stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n\n**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n- Header-only reads; the docstring at `:186-191` says \"never the multi-GiB tensor data\". [S/CL]\n- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n\n**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n\nNuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n\nContract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory/staging behavior relevant to UMA (line numbers at THIS head)\n\nAll in `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`:\n\n| Property | Line(s) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `np.memmap(..., mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))` | **331-333** | [S] read-only shared mapping, `dtype=np.uint8` |\n| Dedup via `np.unique(..., return_inverse=True)` | **360** | [S] `uniq, inverse = np.unique(ids, return_inverse=True)` |\n| Re-expansion to input order | **405** | [S] `gathered = out[inverse]` |\n| `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self.workers)` | **335** | [S] |\n| Chunked tasks, `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` rows each | **377-378** | [S] `for c in range(s, e, self.chunk): tasks.append((si, c, min(c + self.chunk, e)))` |\n| Fan-out over the pool | **391-395** | [S] single task runs inline; else `self.pool.map(run, tasks)` |\n| Fancy-index gather off the memmap (page faults do the I/O; GIL released) | **387** + comment **385-386** | [S/CL] `out[a:b] = mm[local[a:b]]` |\n| **Staging buffer is pageable, not pinned** | **369** | [S] `out = np.empty((uniq.size, self.row_bytes), dtype=np.uint8)` — plain NumPy heap alloc; `torch.from_numpy(rows)` at **567** wraps it |\n| Explicitly stated non-pinned | **568-571** | [CL — comment] \"`rows` … is pageable host memory, not pinned, so this H2D copy is effectively synchronous. Pinned staging (Phase-4 lever 5) is a separate, not-yet-pulled lever\" |\n| H2D copy | **572** | [S] `out = out.to(ids.device, non_blocking=True)` — no-op non-blocking per the comment above |\n| **Device→host sync of the ids, every call** | **558** | [S] `ids_np = ids.detach().to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=False).numpy().reshape(-1)` — an explicit blocking D2H per PLE layer per forward |\n| Prewarm streams via `open(..., buffering=0)` in 64 MiB blocks | **442**, **456-465** | [S] `block = 64 << 20` |\n| Prewarm bound = `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable - 8 GiB))` | **91**, **493-500** | [S] `_PREWARM_HEADROOM_BYTES = 8 << 30` |\n| `MemAvailable` read from `/proc/meminfo` | **484-490** | [S] |\n| Pool teardown idempotent | **468-481** | [S] |\n| p99 latency logging, at most once per 60 s | **92**, **411-429** | [S] `_LOG_INTERVAL_S = 60.0` |\n\n**All four campaign claims about staging are confirmed at this head**, with no line drift beyond what is tabulated above.\n\n### CUDA-specific calls that would break under ROCm/HIP — the grep\n```\n$ grep -n 'torch\\.cuda\\|pin_memory\\|cudaHost\\|hip\\|\\.cuda()' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n604:        of splitting_ops membership.\n```\n[M] — the single hit is a false positive: `hip` inside the word \"mem­ber**ship**\" on a comment line. There are **no** `torch.cuda.*` calls, no `pin_memory`, no `cudaHostRegister`, no explicit `torch.cuda.Stream`/`current_stream`, and no `.cuda()` in the file.\n\nThe only platform touchpoints in `ple_mmap.py`, and their ROCm status:\n\n1. `from vllm.platforms import current_platform` (`:67`) and `if current_platform.dispatch_key != \"CPU\": vllm_lib.impl(OP_NAME, ..., dispatch_key=\"CPU\")` (`:993-994`). [S]\n   **Safe on ROCm**: `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key: str = \"CUDA\"` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:502` [S] (same value as `vllm/platforms/cuda.py:212` [S]). PyTorch/HIP reuses the CUDA dispatch key, so `direct_register_custom_op` at `:981-986` registers correctly and the CPU-key double-registration guard behaves identically.\n2. `check_cudagraph_safety` (`:597-635`) reads only `CompilationConfig` fields. `has_full_cudagraphs()` is defined in `vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84` [S] — platform-agnostic. ROCm already drives `cudagraph_mode` through the same enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`, incl. `compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE`) and returns `\"vllm.compilation.cuda_graph.CUDAGraphWrapper\"` at `rocm.py:1002`. [S]\n3. `_mem_available_bytes` reads `/proc/meminfo` (`:484-490`) — Linux-generic, not vendor-specific. [S]\n4. **The one real ROCm risk: hardcoded `torch.float8_e4m3fn`.** `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` at `:78-84`, and `MmapNgramEmbedding.__init__` defaults `self.torch_dtype = torch.float8_e4m3fn` at `:535`. [S] On gfx94x (MI300X/MI325X) `RocmPlatform.fp8_dtype()` returns `torch.float8_e4m3fnuz` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:975-984` [S]. `is_fp8()` accepts both (`vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py:42-45` [S]), so the dequant *gate* still fires; but the tensor handed downstream is `e4m3fn`, not the platform's fnuz. Whether a ROCm PyTorch build on gfx942 supports `float8_e4m3fn` storage + the `.to(bf16)` cast used by the dequant is **UNDETERMINED** from source — settling it needs `torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\").to(torch.bfloat16)` executed on the target ROCm build. Note the bytes on disk are OCP e4m3 either way, so re-labelling them fnuz would be numerically *wrong*; if fnuz-only kernels are required downstream, that is a conversion problem, not a relabel.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The two PRs: superset or drift?\n\n**Neither is a strict superset.** `#54129` is the mmap branch built on a **newer upstream-main merge**; `#53896` has a **later-timestamped head commit** (`2026-08-28 16:54 +0800` = 08:54 UTC vs `11:52 +0700` = 04:52 UTC) carrying CI work that `#54129` does not have. [M — `git log -1 --format='%ci'` on both]\n\n`diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm` → **20 differing paths** [M]:\n```\nvllm/config/compilation.py, vllm/envs.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/{fp8,mxfp4}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/quark/schemes/quark_ocp_mx.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/{config_utils,flashinfer_utils}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/models/{qwen2_moe,qwen3_next}.py,\nvllm/models/kimi_k3/nvidia/kda_metadata.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nOnly in vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia: ple_mmap.py,\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py\n```\n\nModel-tree drift, `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp` [M]: 6 differing files + `ple_mmap.py` only in 54129. Per-file:\n\n| File | 54129 vs 53896 | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `amd/ple_layer.py` | +1 line | **Only** `# TODO: need double-check` inserted above the `NULL_BLOCK_ID` remap (53896 line 519 region → 54129 `amd/ple_layer.py:522`). [S] Zero functional change; zero mmap wiring. |\n| `amd/model.py` | −8/+0 mmap-unrelated | Drops `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` import, the `self.is_fused_shared_expert_enabled` computation, and the `enabled=` kwarg to `maybe_fuse_shared_experts`. Upstream-main API drift. [S] |\n| `amd/mtp.py` | same drift | identical `is_fused_shared_expert` removals. [S] |\n| `nvidia/mtp.py` | same drift | identical. [S] |\n| `nvidia/model.py` | +14/−12 [M] | 12 of those are upstream drift (as above); the mmap wiring is `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config` (`:12`), `from . import ple_mmap` (`:77`), and two 5-line `build_tables` blocks at `:812-817` and `:1011-1016`. [S] |\n| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | +89/−62 [M] | The whole mmap wiring, plus **a removal that changes the env-off path** — see below. |\n\n### Newer: `#54129`, by base. Later: `#53896`, by head timestamp.\n`#54129`'s head is literally a merge of `upstream-main` into `ple-mmap-upstream`, and its non-PLE churn (fused-MoE oracle, quark OCP MX, flashinfer utils, kimi_k3) is that merge. `#53896` never had `ple_mmap.py` (`grep -c PLE_MMAP vllm-pr53896/vllm/envs.py` → `0`; `find vllm-pr53896 -name 'ple_mmap*' -o -name 'test_ple_mmap*' | wc -l` → `0`) [M]. So the mmap feature is strictly additive on top of #53896's model code, but the two branches have **already diverged on shared non-mmap files** and a merge will conflict on `amd/{model,mtp}.py`, `nvidia/{model,mtp}.py`, `vllm/config/compilation.py`, and `vllm/envs.py`.\n\n### Surprise: #54129 changed the **env-off** nvidia path\n`#53896` routed the ID hashing through its own split-out custom op:\n```python\n# 53896 nvidia/ple_layer.py (removed by 54129)\n# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n# which dispatch only on the padded token count.\ntorch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, ngram_ids, self.layer_name)\n```\n`#54129` **deletes** `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids`, its fake impl, and its `direct_register_custom_op` registration (−35 lines), renames `compute_ngram_ids` → `_hash_ngram_ids`, and in the env-off branch calls it **inline, traced**: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448` `ngram_ids = self._hash_ngram_ids(...); return self.ngram_embedding(ngram_ids).flatten(-2)`. [S] The op name also disappears from `_attention_ops`: `#53896` `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` had `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids\"`; `#54129` `:771` has `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` in its place. [M — `grep -n qwen4_exp` on both]\nIt is gone tree-wide: `grep -rn 'compute_ple_ngram_ids\\|compute_ngram_ids' vllm/ tests/` → no output. [M]\n\nAlso changed in the shared hashing body: `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs, :num_tokens]` (53896) → `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs]` (54129, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:379`). [S]\n\nThis contradicts `ple_mmap.py:32-35`'s claim [CL] that \"With `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` unset, nothing in this module is ever invoked and the stock classes are untouched.\" Nothing in *ple_mmap.py* is invoked — true — but the stock nvidia PLE forward and its cudagraph-splitting behavior **did** change. Whether that regresses PIECEWISE dispatch (the removed comment's stated concern) is **UNDETERMINED** from source; settling it needs a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and a check of graph-split boundaries, or the PR author's rationale.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The piecewise-cudagraph guard, and its AMD analogue\n\n`nvidia/ple_mmap.py:597-635`, `check_cudagraph_safety(compilation_config)` — three independent refusals, any one of which alone would miss a route into a capture [S]:\n\n1. `:614-621` — `if compilation_config.cudagraph_mode.has_full_cudagraphs(): raise RuntimeError(...)`. Message: *\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 requires piecewise-only CUDA graphs … Pass `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\"*\n2. `:622-628` — `if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — *\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.\"*\n3. `:629-635` — `if QUALIFIED_OP_NAME not in (compilation_config.splitting_ops or []): raise` — guards against an operator-supplied `-cc.splitting_ops` or an attn-fusion reset silently dropping the op.\n\nCall site: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:284`, inside `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__`, i.e. at **construction time, before the ~78 GiB backbone streams** (`ple_mmap.py:703-704` comment [CL]). Tests pin all three: `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:661`, `:680`, `:691`, `:705`, plus construction-level mirrors at `:758`, `:786`, `:814`. [S]\n\n**What is the AMD analogue supposed to be? The same function, unchanged.** [S] The guard reads nothing platform-specific: `has_full_cudagraphs()` is `self.max_cudagraph_mode() == CUDAGraphMode.FULL` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84`), `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` and `splitting_ops` are plain config. ROCm drives the identical `CUDAGraphMode` enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`) and the identical `CUDAGraphWrapper` (`rocm.py:1002`). No HIP-graph-specific analogue is needed or exists.\n\nThe third check is **already satisfied on ROCm by default**: `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` sits unconditionally in the class-level `_attention_ops` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:764-782`, entry at `:771`), and `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` copies that list wholesale when `splitting_ops is None` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:1152-1161`, `self.splitting_ops = list(self._attention_ops)`). [S] The op *name* is in the default split set on every platform; only the op *implementation* is nvidia-gated.\n\n**AMD-specific tension worth flagging**: the AMD PLE short-conv path is written for FULL cudagraphs — `amd/ple_layer.py:522-525` [S]:\n```\n# TODO: need double-check\n# FULL cudagraph padded decode rows use NULL_BLOCK_ID. Remap them to\n# slot 0 for a safe gather, then zero output and skip write-back.\nvalid_state = state_indices != NULL_BLOCK_ID\n```\n(also `:642`, `:748`). Enabling `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` on AMD would force `cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` via check #1, taking the AMD tree off the mode its short-conv code was tuned for. That is a **performance/coverage decision the port must make explicitly**, not an incidental detail.\n\nAlso note: AMD's *existing* gather op, `vllm::qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (registered at `amd/ple_layer.py:1236-1241`, defined `:1217-1232`), is **not** in `_attention_ops` — `grep -n qwen4_exp vllm/config/compilation.py` returns only lines 770/771/772, none of which is the AMD op. [M] So today's AMD gather is opaque to Inductor but still *inside* the capture region. The port must not reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Every env var the PR introduces\n\nFour, all in `vllm/envs.py`. Type stubs at `:325-328`, lambdas at `:2168-2177`. [S]\n\n| Var | Default | Parse | Effect | Pins |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Master gate. `ple_mmap.enabled()` (`ple_mmap.py:116-118`) returns it; drives the constructor swap (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`), the three guards, the `load_weights` interception, and `build_tables` (`nvidia/model.py:813`, `:1012`). | `envs.py:325`, `:2170` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS` | `32` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)` for the gather pool; clamped `max(1, int(workers))`. Page faults overlap across threads. | `envs.py:326`, `:2172`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:906`; used `:327/:335` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` | `2048` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | Rows per gather task; clamped `max(1, int(chunk))`. | `envs.py:327`, `:2174`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:907`; used `:325/:377` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Streams the table once at load to warm page cache, bounded by `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable − 8 GiB))`. | `envs.py:328`, `:2177`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:913-921`; bound `:493-500` |\n\n**Compile-hash semantics** — `vllm/envs.py:2342-2350` adds the three *tuning* knobs to the ignored-factors set, deliberately leaving `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` itself a compile factor [S]:\n```\n# PLE mmap gather-pool tuning: affects only the CPU body of a\n# split-out op, never the compiled graph. VLLM_PLE_MMAP itself stays\n# a factor since it changes which op the graph splits around.\n\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM\",\n```\nConfirmed the PR adds no other env var: `grep -n '^+.*os\\.getenv\\|^+.*envs\\.VLLM_' pr54129.diff | grep -v PLE_MMAP` returns only pre-existing `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` reads and one `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` false positive. [M]\n\n---\n\n## Exact port specification (analysis only — NOT written)\n\n### Blocker A (must be resolved first): `amd/ple_layer.py` has no FP8 dequant at all\nThe mmap path is **FP8-only by construction**: `_FP8_DTYPES` admits only `F8_E4M3` (`ple_mmap.py:78-84`), and `_validate_layer_shards` raises for anything else (`ple_mmap.py:687-693`). Its output is a raw `float8_e4m3fn` tensor (`ple_mmap.py:567-573`), which the nvidia layer dequantizes at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:1194`:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nembeddings = self._dequantize_embeddings(embeddings, hidden_states.dtype)   # :1194\n```\n`_dequantize_embeddings` is `nvidia/ple_layer.py:634-648`; `_get_embedding_weight_scale` is `:632-634`.\n\n**The AMD file has none of this** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n '_dequantize_embeddings\\|is_fp8\\|weight_scale\\|quant_config\\|params_dtype' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py\n317:            dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype\n407:        quant_config = vllm_config.quant_config\n438:            quant_config=quant_config,     # key_proj only\n445:            quant_config=quant_config,     # value_proj only\n```\nand its forward goes straight into the projection with no dequant: `amd/ple_layer.py:1018-1020` [S]:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nkey, _ = self.key_proj(embeddings)\nvalue, _ = self.value_proj(embeddings)\n```\nThis gap **pre-dates #54129**: `grep -c '_dequantize_embeddings\\|Fp8EmbeddingMethod' vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` → `0`, while `#53896`'s nvidia file already had `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` at `:140` and `_dequantize_embeddings` at `:581`. [M] AMD's `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding` is constructed with no `quant_config`/`params_dtype` at all (`amd/ple_layer.py:425-433`, 7 positional args vs nvidia's 7 + 2 kwargs at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:580-589`), and its `__init__` signature (`amd/ple_layer.py:127-136`) lacks both parameters.\n\nTherefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\n\n### The mmap wiring, line by line (what to mirror)\n\n**`nvidia/ple_layer.py` — the wiring is exactly five sites:**\n\n| # | Lines | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | `:7` | `from typing import cast` |\n| W2 | `:52` | `from . import ple_mmap` |\n| W3 | **`:281-291`** | the constructor branch — annotation `self.ngram_embedding: VocabParallelEmbedding \\| ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding`; `if ple_mmap.enabled():`; `vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()`; `ple_mmap.check_cudagraph_safety(vllm_config.compilation_config)`; `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(vllm_config.model_config, layer_name, self.head_dim)`; `self.ngram_embedding = ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)`; `else:` + reindent of `:292-301` |\n| W4 | **`:428-446`** | the forward branch — `isinstance(...)` gate, symbolic `num_tokens = input_ids.reshape(-1).shape[0]`, `output = input_ids.new_empty((num_tokens, self.embedding_dim), dtype=self.ngram_embedding.torch_dtype)`, `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, output, self.layer_name)`, `return output` (10 of the 19 lines are the load-bearing ConstraintViolationError comment) |\n| W5a | **`:477-490`** | `load_weights` weight_scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(...)` |\n| W5b | **`:517-527`** (+ `:533`, `:535`, `:538-539`) | `load_weights` shard-drop → `embedding.weights_streamed = True; loaded.add(...); continue`, plus `stock_embedding = cast(VocabParallelEmbedding, embedding)` and the three renamed reads; also the hoist of `embedding = self.ngram_embedding` to `:461` |\n\nThe campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\n\n**`nvidia/model.py` — three sites:**\n- `:12` `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`\n- `:77` `from . import ple_mmap`\n- `:812-817` and `:1011-1016` — after `loader.load_weights(...)`: `if ple_mmap.enabled(): ple_mmap.build_tables(self.model_config, get_current_vllm_config().compilation_config)`; `return loaded`\n\nMeasured: `diff -u vllm-pr53896/.../nvidia/model.py vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia/model.py` = **+14/−12**, of which 12 removed + 2 added are upstream `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` drift; the mmap portion is **+12 net**. [M] `amd/model.py` differs from `nvidia/model.py` by *exactly and only these four hunks* — verified: `diff -u amd/model.py nvidia/model.py` produces 4 hunks, all mmap. [M]\n\n### Imports / env checks / guards the port must mirror\n1. `from . import ple_mmap` — but `ple_mmap.py` lives in `nvidia/`. Two options: **(a)** `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` in both `amd/ple_layer.py` and `amd/model.py` — safe, because `nvidia/__init__.py` is an SPDX-only stub (3 lines, no re-exports) [S], so this pulls in *only* `ple_mmap.py` and none of the CUDA-heavy nvidia modules; **(b)** relocate to `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py` alongside `common/ple.py` and update both trees + all `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` imports (the test file imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap` at `:27` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.model` at `:26`). (b) is the cleaner upstream shape; (a) is the smaller diff.\n2. `get_current_vllm_config` — **already imported** in `amd/ple_layer.py:12` (`from vllm.config import CacheConfig, ModelConfig, VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`) [S]. Must be *added* to `amd/model.py:12` (currently `from vllm.config import VllmConfig`) [S].\n3. `from typing import cast` — must be added to `amd/ple_layer.py` (absent; nvidia has it at `:7`) [S].\n4. Env checks: exactly one, `ple_mmap.enabled()` — three call sites, mirroring `nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`, `nvidia/model.py:813`, `nvidia/model.py:1012`. The three tuning vars are read inside `ple_mmap.py` (`:906`, `:907`, `:913`) and need no per-tree wiring.\n5. Guards: `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for`, both at construction (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:284-287`), unchanged — no AMD variant.\n6. **Op registration**: none needed. `direct_register_custom_op` fires at `ple_mmap.py:981-986` on first import of the module, under `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == \"CUDA\"` (`rocm.py:502`). The CPU double-registration guard at `:993-994` also behaves correctly on ROCm.\n7. **`splitting_ops`**: already correct — `vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` is in `_attention_ops` unconditionally (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`), copied into `splitting_ops` by `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` (`:1152-1161`).\n8. The AMD-only op `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:1217-1241`) stays for the env-off path; it must **not** be reused for the mmap path (not in `_attention_ops`, so not a split boundary, so it would run inside the capture).\n\n### Size, honestly measured\n- `amd/model.py`: **+12** lines.\n- `amd/ple_layer.py`: W1–W5 ≈ **+58 lines** including the comments the nvidia file carries (≈ **+35** code-only), plus the `else:` reindent of `:292-301`.\n- Plus Blocker A: **+10** (minimal dequant) to **+80** (full FP8 stack parity).\n- Plus, if option (b), a file move and a test-import sweep across 2048 lines of `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which is nvidia-only today: `grep -cni 'amd\\|rocm' tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` → `0`. [M]\n\n**Realistic total: 80–150 lines, not 15.**\n\n---\n\n## Open / UNDETERMINED\n\n1. **Does `float8_e4m3fn` storage + `.to(bf16)` work on the target gfx arch?** Looked at: `ple_mmap.py:78-84`, `:535`, `:567`; `rocm.py:975-984`; `fp8_utils.py:42-45`. Settled by running the cast on the target ROCm build.\n2. **Did removing `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids` regress env-off PIECEWISE dispatch on nvidia?** Looked at: `#53896` `nvidia/ple_layer.py` op + its comment, `#54129` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448`, `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` (53896) vs `:771` (54129), and the tree-wide grep showing no remaining reference. Settled by a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` inspecting graph-split boundaries, or by the author.\n3. **Is the `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522` a known AMD FULL-cudagraph correctness doubt?** It is the sole delta between the two branches' AMD PLE files. Settled by the PR thread.\n4. **Whether AMD is expected to run FULL cudagraphs for this model in practice** — `amd/ple_layer.py:522/642/748` handle `NULL_BLOCK_ID` padded decode rows, implying yes; guard #1 would forbid it under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`. Settled by the AMD serving config actually in use.\n"}]},"isError":false}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: vLLM PR #54129 (`VLLM_PLE_MMAP`, branch `ple-mmap-upstream`) — AMD/ROCm reachability\n\nInvestigator session: 2026-08-28. Analysis only; no code written or patched.\n\n**Checkouts read this session**\n- `#54129`: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-tom-mecattaf-notes-qwen-next/c4262a6d-5cde-494b-9143-685dd896a84f/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129`\n  HEAD `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream`, `2026-08-28 11:52:45 +0700`, author *Christopher Deodat* [M: `git log -1 --format='%ci %an %s'`]\n- `#53896`: `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr53896`\n  HEAD `89d0bb7 update CI`, `2026-08-28 16:54:24 +0800`, author *huanghaoyan.hhy* [M: same command]\n- Raw diff: `.../scratchpad/pr54129.diff`, 26483 lines [M: `wc -l`]\n\nGrades: **[S]** read from source · **[M]** measured (command output) · **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n---\n\n## Q0 — Headline answer\n\n**Yes.** The mmap/engram path is wired exclusively into `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/`. On a ROCm build the `amd/` tree is imported instead, `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is never imported, the custom op is never registered, and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` is silently a **no-op** — no error, no warning, the stock GPU-resident `VocabParallelEmbedding` is built as if the flag were unset.\n\n**Measured reachability** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n 'ple_mmap\\|PLE_MMAP\\|mmap\\|Mmap\\|MMAP' \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output — zero hits in all four files)\n```\nversus 11 hits in `nvidia/ple_layer.py` and 5 in `nvidia/model.py`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'nvidia' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output)\n```\nThe `amd/` tree never reaches into `nvidia/`. [M]\n\n**Correction to the campaign record**: the port is **not ~15 lines**. See §\"Exact port specification\" — it is ~12 lines in `amd/model.py` plus ~45 code lines (~60 with the load-bearing comments) in `amd/ple_layer.py`, **and** it depends on an FP8 dequant capability that `amd/ple_layer.py` does not currently have at all (§Blocker A). That last item is the real finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Platform dispatch — how a ROCm build lands in `amd/`\n\nSelection is a lazy module `__getattr__` on the model package, not a registry-level split.\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/__init__.py:22-49` [S]:\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Any:\n    if name in {\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\", \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\", \"Qwen4ExpMTP\"}:\n        from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n        if current_platform.is_xpu() or current_platform.is_tpu():\n            raise NotImplementedError(\"Qwen4Exp currently supports CUDA and ROCm only\")\n        if current_platform.is_rocm():\n            from .amd.model import (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM, Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration)\n            from .amd.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n        else:\n            from .nvidia.model import (...)\n            from .nvidia.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n```\n\nChain of evidence:\n- Registry points at the package, not a platform subtree: `vllm/model_executor/models/registry.py:114-117` `\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\": (\"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\", \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\")`; `:580-583` for `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration`; `:669` for `Qwen4ExpMTP`. [S]\n- `is_rocm()` is `self._enum == PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/interface.py:192-193`. [S]\n- `RocmPlatform._enum = PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:498-499`. [S]\n- Platform selection: `vllm/platforms/__init__.py:110-128` — `rocm_platform_plugin()` returns `\"vllm.platforms.rocm.RocmPlatform\"` iff `amdsmi` imports and `amdsmi_get_processor_handles()` is non-empty. [S]\n\n**Is there any chance a ROCm build imports the nvidia tree?** No, on the model path. [S]\n- The `else:` branch at `__init__.py:38-43` is the only importer of `nvidia/model.py` / `nvidia/mtp.py`, and it is unreachable when `is_rocm()` is true.\n- `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is imported from exactly two places, both inside the nvidia tree: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:52` and `nvidia/model.py:77`. [S/M]\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/__init__.py` is a 3-line SPDX-only stub (no re-exports) [S] — so a hypothetical `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` from `amd/` would import *only* `ple_mmap.py`, dragging in no CUDA-specific nvidia module. That is what makes option (a) in §Port viable.\n\nConsequence: the module-scope `direct_register_custom_op(...)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:981-986` never runs on ROCm, so `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` does not exist there — even though the op *name* is unconditionally present in the default splitting-ops list (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`). [S]\n\n---\n\n## 2. TP semantics of the mmap path\n\n### Vocab-parallel path: a per-lookup all-reduce\n`vllm/model_executor/layers/vocab_parallel_embedding.py:490-518` [S]:\n```python\ndef forward(self, input_):\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        masked_input, input_mask = get_masked_input_and_mask(...)   # :493-500\n    ...\n    output_parallel = self.quant_method.embedding(self, masked_input.long())   # :504\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        if output_parallel.dtype in (torch.float8_e4m3fn, torch.float8_e5m2):\n            comm_output = output_parallel.view(torch.int8)                     # :512\n            comm_output.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :513\n            output = tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(comm_output)             # :514\n            return output.view(output_parallel.dtype)\n        output_parallel.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :516\n        return tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(output_parallel)               # :517\n    return output_parallel\n```\nThe FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n\nSo: **yes, one `tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce` per PLE lookup, per PLE layer, per forward** whenever `tp_size > 1`.\n\n### Mmap path: plain `nn.Module`, whole table per rank, no collective\n`MmapNgramEmbedding` is declared `class MmapNgramEmbedding(nn.Module)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:506` [S] and constructed with the **full padded vocab**: `ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:288-290` [S] — the identical `padded_vocab_size` handed to the vocab-parallel constructor in the `else:` branch at `:292-293`.\n\nIts `forward` (`ple_mmap.py:545-573`) contains **no collective, no mask, no rank arithmetic**:\n```\n$ grep -n 'rank\\|tp_\\|world_size\\|parallel\\|all_reduce\\|distributed' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n(no output; exit 1)\n```\n[M] — zero hits across the whole 1010-line file.\n\n`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n```python\nfor idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n    self.mm[idx] = np.memmap(path, dtype=np.uint8, mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))\n    self.rows_total += rows\n```\nand `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n\n**Verdict**: switching to the mmap path removes the per-token all-reduce/all-gather at the PLE lookup site entirely. The trade is that every rank independently gathers the full-width rows for every token — TP-redundant I/O and TP-redundant host→device copies, in exchange for zero NCCL/RCCL traffic at that site. (The redundancy hits page cache, shared per host, so the *disk* cost is not multiplied — that last clause is my inference from `mode=\"r\"` shared file mappings, not read from source; treat as reasoning, not evidence.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. `load_weights` under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n\nThe interception lives in `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:450-547`. Two mmap-specific branches:\n\n**(a) weight_scale kept** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:477-490` [S]:\n```python\nif (isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding)\n        and name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"):\n    ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight,\n                              cast(torch.Tensor, self.layer_multipliers).device)\n    loaded.add(name); continue\n```\n`set_weight_scale` registers a non-persistent buffer, `ple_mmap.py:587-590`. [S]\n\n**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n```python\nif isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding):\n    # Served from disk via mmap; the loader still streams\n    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n    embedding.weights_streamed = True\n    loaded.add(\"ngram_embedding.weight\")\n    continue\n```\n`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n\nThe stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n\n**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n- Header-only reads; the docstring at `:186-191` says \"never the multi-GiB tensor data\". [S/CL]\n- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n\n**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n\nNuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n\nContract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory/staging behavior relevant to UMA (line numbers at THIS head)\n\nAll in `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`:\n\n| Property | Line(s) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `np.memmap(..., mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))` | **331-333** | [S] read-only shared mapping, `dtype=np.uint8` |\n| Dedup via `np.unique(..., return_inverse=True)` | **360** | [S] `uniq, inverse = np.unique(ids, return_inverse=True)` |\n| Re-expansion to input order | **405** | [S] `gathered = out[inverse]` |\n| `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self.workers)` | **335** | [S] |\n| Chunked tasks, `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` rows each | **377-378** | [S] `for c in range(s, e, self.chunk): tasks.append((si, c, min(c + self.chunk, e)))` |\n| Fan-out over the pool | **391-395** | [S] single task runs inline; else `self.pool.map(run, tasks)` |\n| Fancy-index gather off the memmap (page faults do the I/O; GIL released) | **387** + comment **385-386** | [S/CL] `out[a:b] = mm[local[a:b]]` |\n| **Staging buffer is pageable, not pinned** | **369** | [S] `out = np.empty((uniq.size, self.row_bytes), dtype=np.uint8)` — plain NumPy heap alloc; `torch.from_numpy(rows)` at **567** wraps it |\n| Explicitly stated non-pinned | **568-571** | [CL — comment] \"`rows` … is pageable host memory, not pinned, so this H2D copy is effectively synchronous. Pinned staging (Phase-4 lever 5) is a separate, not-yet-pulled lever\" |\n| H2D copy | **572** | [S] `out = out.to(ids.device, non_blocking=True)` — no-op non-blocking per the comment above |\n| **Device→host sync of the ids, every call** | **558** | [S] `ids_np = ids.detach().to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=False).numpy().reshape(-1)` — an explicit blocking D2H per PLE layer per forward |\n| Prewarm streams via `open(..., buffering=0)` in 64 MiB blocks | **442**, **456-465** | [S] `block = 64 << 20` |\n| Prewarm bound = `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable - 8 GiB))` | **91**, **493-500** | [S] `_PREWARM_HEADROOM_BYTES = 8 << 30` |\n| `MemAvailable` read from `/proc/meminfo` | **484-490** | [S] |\n| Pool teardown idempotent | **468-481** | [S] |\n| p99 latency logging, at most once per 60 s | **92**, **411-429** | [S] `_LOG_INTERVAL_S = 60.0` |\n\n**All four campaign claims about staging are confirmed at this head**, with no line drift beyond what is tabulated above.\n\n### CUDA-specific calls that would break under ROCm/HIP — the grep\n```\n$ grep -n 'torch\\.cuda\\|pin_memory\\|cudaHost\\|hip\\|\\.cuda()' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n604:        of splitting_ops membership.\n```\n[M] — the single hit is a false positive: `hip` inside the word \"mem­ber**ship**\" on a comment line. There are **no** `torch.cuda.*` calls, no `pin_memory`, no `cudaHostRegister`, no explicit `torch.cuda.Stream`/`current_stream`, and no `.cuda()` in the file.\n\nThe only platform touchpoints in `ple_mmap.py`, and their ROCm status:\n\n1. `from vllm.platforms import current_platform` (`:67`) and `if current_platform.dispatch_key != \"CPU\": vllm_lib.impl(OP_NAME, ..., dispatch_key=\"CPU\")` (`:993-994`). [S]\n   **Safe on ROCm**: `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key: str = \"CUDA\"` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:502` [S] (same value as `vllm/platforms/cuda.py:212` [S]). PyTorch/HIP reuses the CUDA dispatch key, so `direct_register_custom_op` at `:981-986` registers correctly and the CPU-key double-registration guard behaves identically.\n2. `check_cudagraph_safety` (`:597-635`) reads only `CompilationConfig` fields. `has_full_cudagraphs()` is defined in `vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84` [S] — platform-agnostic. ROCm already drives `cudagraph_mode` through the same enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`, incl. `compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE`) and returns `\"vllm.compilation.cuda_graph.CUDAGraphWrapper\"` at `rocm.py:1002`. [S]\n3. `_mem_available_bytes` reads `/proc/meminfo` (`:484-490`) — Linux-generic, not vendor-specific. [S]\n4. **The one real ROCm risk: hardcoded `torch.float8_e4m3fn`.** `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` at `:78-84`, and `MmapNgramEmbedding.__init__` defaults `self.torch_dtype = torch.float8_e4m3fn` at `:535`. [S] On gfx94x (MI300X/MI325X) `RocmPlatform.fp8_dtype()` returns `torch.float8_e4m3fnuz` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:975-984` [S]. `is_fp8()` accepts both (`vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py:42-45` [S]), so the dequant *gate* still fires; but the tensor handed downstream is `e4m3fn`, not the platform's fnuz. Whether a ROCm PyTorch build on gfx942 supports `float8_e4m3fn` storage + the `.to(bf16)` cast used by the dequant is **UNDETERMINED** from source — settling it needs `torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\").to(torch.bfloat16)` executed on the target ROCm build. Note the bytes on disk are OCP e4m3 either way, so re-labelling them fnuz would be numerically *wrong*; if fnuz-only kernels are required downstream, that is a conversion problem, not a relabel.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The two PRs: superset or drift?\n\n**Neither is a strict superset.** `#54129` is the mmap branch built on a **newer upstream-main merge**; `#53896` has a **later-timestamped head commit** (`2026-08-28 16:54 +0800` = 08:54 UTC vs `11:52 +0700` = 04:52 UTC) carrying CI work that `#54129` does not have. [M — `git log -1 --format='%ci'` on both]\n\n`diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm` → **20 differing paths** [M]:\n```\nvllm/config/compilation.py, vllm/envs.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/{fp8,mxfp4}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/quark/schemes/quark_ocp_mx.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/{config_utils,flashinfer_utils}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/models/{qwen2_moe,qwen3_next}.py,\nvllm/models/kimi_k3/nvidia/kda_metadata.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nOnly in vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia: ple_mmap.py,\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py\n```\n\nModel-tree drift, `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp` [M]: 6 differing files + `ple_mmap.py` only in 54129. Per-file:\n\n| File | 54129 vs 53896 | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `amd/ple_layer.py` | +1 line | **Only** `# TODO: need double-check` inserted above the `NULL_BLOCK_ID` remap (53896 line 519 region → 54129 `amd/ple_layer.py:522`). [S] Zero functional change; zero mmap wiring. |\n| `amd/model.py` | −8/+0 mmap-unrelated | Drops `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` import, the `self.is_fused_shared_expert_enabled` computation, and the `enabled=` kwarg to `maybe_fuse_shared_experts`. Upstream-main API drift. [S] |\n| `amd/mtp.py` | same drift | identical `is_fused_shared_expert` removals. [S] |\n| `nvidia/mtp.py` | same drift | identical. [S] |\n| `nvidia/model.py` | +14/−12 [M] | 12 of those are upstream drift (as above); the mmap wiring is `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config` (`:12`), `from . import ple_mmap` (`:77`), and two 5-line `build_tables` blocks at `:812-817` and `:1011-1016`. [S] |\n| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | +89/−62 [M] | The whole mmap wiring, plus **a removal that changes the env-off path** — see below. |\n\n### Newer: `#54129`, by base. Later: `#53896`, by head timestamp.\n`#54129`'s head is literally a merge of `upstream-main` into `ple-mmap-upstream`, and its non-PLE churn (fused-MoE oracle, quark OCP MX, flashinfer utils, kimi_k3) is that merge. `#53896` never had `ple_mmap.py` (`grep -c PLE_MMAP vllm-pr53896/vllm/envs.py` → `0`; `find vllm-pr53896 -name 'ple_mmap*' -o -name 'test_ple_mmap*' | wc -l` → `0`) [M]. So the mmap feature is strictly additive on top of #53896's model code, but the two branches have **already diverged on shared non-mmap files** and a merge will conflict on `amd/{model,mtp}.py`, `nvidia/{model,mtp}.py`, `vllm/config/compilation.py`, and `vllm/envs.py`.\n\n### Surprise: #54129 changed the **env-off** nvidia path\n`#53896` routed the ID hashing through its own split-out custom op:\n```python\n# 53896 nvidia/ple_layer.py (removed by 54129)\n# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n# which dispatch only on the padded token count.\ntorch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, ngram_ids, self.layer_name)\n```\n`#54129` **deletes** `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids`, its fake impl, and its `direct_register_custom_op` registration (−35 lines), renames `compute_ngram_ids` → `_hash_ngram_ids`, and in the env-off branch calls it **inline, traced**: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448` `ngram_ids = self._hash_ngram_ids(...); return self.ngram_embedding(ngram_ids).flatten(-2)`. [S] The op name also disappears from `_attention_ops`: `#53896` `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` had `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids\"`; `#54129` `:771` has `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` in its place. [M — `grep -n qwen4_exp` on both]\nIt is gone tree-wide: `grep -rn 'compute_ple_ngram_ids\\|compute_ngram_ids' vllm/ tests/` → no output. [M]\n\nAlso changed in the shared hashing body: `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs, :num_tokens]` (53896) → `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs]` (54129, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:379`). [S]\n\nThis contradicts `ple_mmap.py:32-35`'s claim [CL] that \"With `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` unset, nothing in this module is ever invoked and the stock classes are untouched.\" Nothing in *ple_mmap.py* is invoked — true — but the stock nvidia PLE forward and its cudagraph-splitting behavior **did** change. Whether that regresses PIECEWISE dispatch (the removed comment's stated concern) is **UNDETERMINED** from source; settling it needs a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and a check of graph-split boundaries, or the PR author's rationale.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The piecewise-cudagraph guard, and its AMD analogue\n\n`nvidia/ple_mmap.py:597-635`, `check_cudagraph_safety(compilation_config)` — three independent refusals, any one of which alone would miss a route into a capture [S]:\n\n1. `:614-621` — `if compilation_config.cudagraph_mode.has_full_cudagraphs(): raise RuntimeError(...)`. Message: *\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 requires piecewise-only CUDA graphs … Pass `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\"*\n2. `:622-628` — `if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — *\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.\"*\n3. `:629-635` — `if QUALIFIED_OP_NAME not in (compilation_config.splitting_ops or []): raise` — guards against an operator-supplied `-cc.splitting_ops` or an attn-fusion reset silently dropping the op.\n\nCall site: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:284`, inside `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__`, i.e. at **construction time, before the ~78 GiB backbone streams** (`ple_mmap.py:703-704` comment [CL]). Tests pin all three: `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:661`, `:680`, `:691`, `:705`, plus construction-level mirrors at `:758`, `:786`, `:814`. [S]\n\n**What is the AMD analogue supposed to be? The same function, unchanged.** [S] The guard reads nothing platform-specific: `has_full_cudagraphs()` is `self.max_cudagraph_mode() == CUDAGraphMode.FULL` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84`), `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` and `splitting_ops` are plain config. ROCm drives the identical `CUDAGraphMode` enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`) and the identical `CUDAGraphWrapper` (`rocm.py:1002`). No HIP-graph-specific analogue is needed or exists.\n\nThe third check is **already satisfied on ROCm by default**: `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` sits unconditionally in the class-level `_attention_ops` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:764-782`, entry at `:771`), and `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` copies that list wholesale when `splitting_ops is None` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:1152-1161`, `self.splitting_ops = list(self._attention_ops)`). [S] The op *name* is in the default split set on every platform; only the op *implementation* is nvidia-gated.\n\n**AMD-specific tension worth flagging**: the AMD PLE short-conv path is written for FULL cudagraphs — `amd/ple_layer.py:522-525` [S]:\n```\n# TODO: need double-check\n# FULL cudagraph padded decode rows use NULL_BLOCK_ID. Remap them to\n# slot 0 for a safe gather, then zero output and skip write-back.\nvalid_state = state_indices != NULL_BLOCK_ID\n```\n(also `:642`, `:748`). Enabling `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` on AMD would force `cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` via check #1, taking the AMD tree off the mode its short-conv code was tuned for. That is a **performance/coverage decision the port must make explicitly**, not an incidental detail.\n\nAlso note: AMD's *existing* gather op, `vllm::qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (registered at `amd/ple_layer.py:1236-1241`, defined `:1217-1232`), is **not** in `_attention_ops` — `grep -n qwen4_exp vllm/config/compilation.py` returns only lines 770/771/772, none of which is the AMD op. [M] So today's AMD gather is opaque to Inductor but still *inside* the capture region. The port must not reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Every env var the PR introduces\n\nFour, all in `vllm/envs.py`. Type stubs at `:325-328`, lambdas at `:2168-2177`. [S]\n\n| Var | Default | Parse | Effect | Pins |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Master gate. `ple_mmap.enabled()` (`ple_mmap.py:116-118`) returns it; drives the constructor swap (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`), the three guards, the `load_weights` interception, and `build_tables` (`nvidia/model.py:813`, `:1012`). | `envs.py:325`, `:2170` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS` | `32` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)` for the gather pool; clamped `max(1, int(workers))`. Page faults overlap across threads. | `envs.py:326`, `:2172`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:906`; used `:327/:335` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` | `2048` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | Rows per gather task; clamped `max(1, int(chunk))`. | `envs.py:327`, `:2174`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:907`; used `:325/:377` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Streams the table once at load to warm page cache, bounded by `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable − 8 GiB))`. | `envs.py:328`, `:2177`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:913-921`; bound `:493-500` |\n\n**Compile-hash semantics** — `vllm/envs.py:2342-2350` adds the three *tuning* knobs to the ignored-factors set, deliberately leaving `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` itself a compile factor [S]:\n```\n# PLE mmap gather-pool tuning: affects only the CPU body of a\n# split-out op, never the compiled graph. VLLM_PLE_MMAP itself stays\n# a factor since it changes which op the graph splits around.\n\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM\",\n```\nConfirmed the PR adds no other env var: `grep -n '^+.*os\\.getenv\\|^+.*envs\\.VLLM_' pr54129.diff | grep -v PLE_MMAP` returns only pre-existing `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` reads and one `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` false positive. [M]\n\n---\n\n## Exact port specification (analysis only — NOT written)\n\n### Blocker A (must be resolved first): `amd/ple_layer.py` has no FP8 dequant at all\nThe mmap path is **FP8-only by construction**: `_FP8_DTYPES` admits only `F8_E4M3` (`ple_mmap.py:78-84`), and `_validate_layer_shards` raises for anything else (`ple_mmap.py:687-693`). Its output is a raw `float8_e4m3fn` tensor (`ple_mmap.py:567-573`), which the nvidia layer dequantizes at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:1194`:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nembeddings = self._dequantize_embeddings(embeddings, hidden_states.dtype)   # :1194\n```\n`_dequantize_embeddings` is `nvidia/ple_layer.py:634-648`; `_get_embedding_weight_scale` is `:632-634`.\n\n**The AMD file has none of this** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n '_dequantize_embeddings\\|is_fp8\\|weight_scale\\|quant_config\\|params_dtype' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py\n317:            dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype\n407:        quant_config = vllm_config.quant_config\n438:            quant_config=quant_config,     # key_proj only\n445:            quant_config=quant_config,     # value_proj only\n```\nand its forward goes straight into the projection with no dequant: `amd/ple_layer.py:1018-1020` [S]:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nkey, _ = self.key_proj(embeddings)\nvalue, _ = self.value_proj(embeddings)\n```\nThis gap **pre-dates #54129**: `grep -c '_dequantize_embeddings\\|Fp8EmbeddingMethod' vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` → `0`, while `#53896`'s nvidia file already had `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` at `:140` and `_dequantize_embeddings` at `:581`. [M] AMD's `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding` is constructed with no `quant_config`/`params_dtype` at all (`amd/ple_layer.py:425-433`, 7 positional args vs nvidia's 7 + 2 kwargs at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:580-589`), and its `__init__` signature (`amd/ple_layer.py:127-136`) lacks both parameters.\n\nTherefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\n\n### The mmap wiring, line by line (what to mirror)\n\n**`nvidia/ple_layer.py` — the wiring is exactly five sites:**\n\n| # | Lines | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | `:7` | `from typing import cast` |\n| W2 | `:52` | `from . import ple_mmap` |\n| W3 | **`:281-291`** | the constructor branch — annotation `self.ngram_embedding: VocabParallelEmbedding \\| ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding`; `if ple_mmap.enabled():`; `vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()`; `ple_mmap.check_cudagraph_safety(vllm_config.compilation_config)`; `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(vllm_config.model_config, layer_name, self.head_dim)`; `self.ngram_embedding = ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)`; `else:` + reindent of `:292-301` |\n| W4 | **`:428-446`** | the forward branch — `isinstance(...)` gate, symbolic `num_tokens = input_ids.reshape(-1).shape[0]`, `output = input_ids.new_empty((num_tokens, self.embedding_dim), dtype=self.ngram_embedding.torch_dtype)`, `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, output, self.layer_name)`, `return output` (10 of the 19 lines are the load-bearing ConstraintViolationError comment) |\n| W5a | **`:477-490`** | `load_weights` weight_scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(...)` |\n| W5b | **`:517-527`** (+ `:533`, `:535`, `:538-539`) | `load_weights` shard-drop → `embedding.weights_streamed = True; loaded.add(...); continue`, plus `stock_embedding = cast(VocabParallelEmbedding, embedding)` and the three renamed reads; also the hoist of `embedding = self.ngram_embedding` to `:461` |\n\nThe campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\n\n**`nvidia/model.py` — three sites:**\n- `:12` `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`\n- `:77` `from . import ple_mmap`\n- `:812-817` and `:1011-1016` — after `loader.load_weights(...)`: `if ple_mmap.enabled(): ple_mmap.build_tables(self.model_config, get_current_vllm_config().compilation_config)`; `return loaded`\n\nMeasured: `diff -u vllm-pr53896/.../nvidia/model.py vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia/model.py` = **+14/−12**, of which 12 removed + 2 added are upstream `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` drift; the mmap portion is **+12 net**. [M] `amd/model.py` differs from `nvidia/model.py` by *exactly and only these four hunks* — verified: `diff -u amd/model.py nvidia/model.py` produces 4 hunks, all mmap. [M]\n\n### Imports / env checks / guards the port must mirror\n1. `from . import ple_mmap` — but `ple_mmap.py` lives in `nvidia/`. Two options: **(a)** `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` in both `amd/ple_layer.py` and `amd/model.py` — safe, because `nvidia/__init__.py` is an SPDX-only stub (3 lines, no re-exports) [S], so this pulls in *only* `ple_mmap.py` and none of the CUDA-heavy nvidia modules; **(b)** relocate to `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py` alongside `common/ple.py` and update both trees + all `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` imports (the test file imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap` at `:27` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.model` at `:26`). (b) is the cleaner upstream shape; (a) is the smaller diff.\n2. `get_current_vllm_config` — **already imported** in `amd/ple_layer.py:12` (`from vllm.config import CacheConfig, ModelConfig, VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`) [S]. Must be *added* to `amd/model.py:12` (currently `from vllm.config import VllmConfig`) [S].\n3. `from typing import cast` — must be added to `amd/ple_layer.py` (absent; nvidia has it at `:7`) [S].\n4. Env checks: exactly one, `ple_mmap.enabled()` — three call sites, mirroring `nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`, `nvidia/model.py:813`, `nvidia/model.py:1012`. The three tuning vars are read inside `ple_mmap.py` (`:906`, `:907`, `:913`) and need no per-tree wiring.\n5. Guards: `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for`, both at construction (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:284-287`), unchanged — no AMD variant.\n6. **Op registration**: none needed. `direct_register_custom_op` fires at `ple_mmap.py:981-986` on first import of the module, under `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == \"CUDA\"` (`rocm.py:502`). The CPU double-registration guard at `:993-994` also behaves correctly on ROCm.\n7. **`splitting_ops`**: already correct — `vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` is in `_attention_ops` unconditionally (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`), copied into `splitting_ops` by `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` (`:1152-1161`).\n8. The AMD-only op `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:1217-1241`) stays for the env-off path; it must **not** be reused for the mmap path (not in `_attention_ops`, so not a split boundary, so it would run inside the capture).\n\n### Size, honestly measured\n- `amd/model.py`: **+12** lines.\n- `amd/ple_layer.py`: W1–W5 ≈ **+58 lines** including the comments the nvidia file carries (≈ **+35** code-only), plus the `else:` reindent of `:292-301`.\n- Plus Blocker A: **+10** (minimal dequant) to **+80** (full FP8 stack parity).\n- Plus, if option (b), a file move and a test-import sweep across 2048 lines of `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which is nvidia-only today: `grep -cni 'amd\\|rocm' tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` → `0`. [M]\n\n**Realistic total: 80–150 lines, not 15.**\n\n---\n\n## Open / UNDETERMINED\n\n1. **Does `float8_e4m3fn` storage + `.to(bf16)` work on the target gfx arch?** Looked at: `ple_mmap.py:78-84`, `:535`, `:567`; `rocm.py:975-984`; `fp8_utils.py:42-45`. Settled by running the cast on the target ROCm build.\n2. **Did removing `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids` regress env-off PIECEWISE dispatch on nvidia?** Looked at: `#53896` `nvidia/ple_layer.py` op + its comment, `#54129` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448`, `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` (53896) vs `:771` (54129), and the tree-wide grep showing no remaining reference. Settled by a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` inspecting graph-split boundaries, or by the author.\n3. **Is the `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522` a known AMD FULL-cudagraph correctness doubt?** It is the sole delta between the two branches' AMD PLE files. Settled by the PR thread.\n4. **Whether AMD is expected to run FULL cudagraphs for this model in practice** — `amd/ple_layer.py:522/642/748` handle `NULL_BLOCK_ID` padded decode rows, implying yes; guard #1 would forbid it under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`. Settled by the AMD serving config actually in use.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991809525}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: vLLM PR #54129 (`VLLM_PLE_MMAP`, branch `ple-mmap-upstream`) — AMD/ROCm reachability\n\nInvestigator session: 2026-08-28. Analysis only; no code written or patched.\n\n**Checkouts read this session**\n- `#54129`: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-tom-mecattaf-notes-qwen-next/c4262a6d-5cde-494b-9143-685dd896a84f/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129`\n  HEAD `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream`, `2026-08-28 11:52:45 +0700`, author *Christopher Deodat* [M: `git log -1 --format='%ci %an %s'`]\n- `#53896`: `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr53896`\n  HEAD `89d0bb7 update CI`, `2026-08-28 16:54:24 +0800`, author *huanghaoyan.hhy* [M: same command]\n- Raw diff: `.../scratchpad/pr54129.diff`, 26483 lines [M: `wc -l`]\n\nGrades: **[S]** read from source · **[M]** measured (command output) · **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n---\n\n## Q0 — Headline answer\n\n**Yes.** The mmap/engram path is wired exclusively into `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/`. On a ROCm build the `amd/` tree is imported instead, `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is never imported, the custom op is never registered, and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` is silently a **no-op** — no error, no warning, the stock GPU-resident `VocabParallelEmbedding` is built as if the flag were unset.\n\n**Measured reachability** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n 'ple_mmap\\|PLE_MMAP\\|mmap\\|Mmap\\|MMAP' \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output — zero hits in all four files)\n```\nversus 11 hits in `nvidia/ple_layer.py` and 5 in `nvidia/model.py`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'nvidia' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output)\n```\nThe `amd/` tree never reaches into `nvidia/`. [M]\n\n**Correction to the campaign record**: the port is **not ~15 lines**. See §\"Exact port specification\" — it is ~12 lines in `amd/model.py` plus ~45 code lines (~60 with the load-bearing comments) in `amd/ple_layer.py`, **and** it depends on an FP8 dequant capability that `amd/ple_layer.py` does not currently have at all (§Blocker A). That last item is the real finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Platform dispatch — how a ROCm build lands in `amd/`\n\nSelection is a lazy module `__getattr__` on the model package, not a registry-level split.\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/__init__.py:22-49` [S]:\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Any:\n    if name in {\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\", \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\", \"Qwen4ExpMTP\"}:\n        from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n        if current_platform.is_xpu() or current_platform.is_tpu():\n            raise NotImplementedError(\"Qwen4Exp currently supports CUDA and ROCm only\")\n        if current_platform.is_rocm():\n            from .amd.model import (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM, Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration)\n            from .amd.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n        else:\n            from .nvidia.model import (...)\n            from .nvidia.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n```\n\nChain of evidence:\n- Registry points at the package, not a platform subtree: `vllm/model_executor/models/registry.py:114-117` `\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\": (\"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\", \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\")`; `:580-583` for `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration`; `:669` for `Qwen4ExpMTP`. [S]\n- `is_rocm()` is `self._enum == PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/interface.py:192-193`. [S]\n- `RocmPlatform._enum = PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:498-499`. [S]\n- Platform selection: `vllm/platforms/__init__.py:110-128` — `rocm_platform_plugin()` returns `\"vllm.platforms.rocm.RocmPlatform\"` iff `amdsmi` imports and `amdsmi_get_processor_handles()` is non-empty. [S]\n\n**Is there any chance a ROCm build imports the nvidia tree?** No, on the model path. [S]\n- The `else:` branch at `__init__.py:38-43` is the only importer of `nvidia/model.py` / `nvidia/mtp.py`, and it is unreachable when `is_rocm()` is true.\n- `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is imported from exactly two places, both inside the nvidia tree: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:52` and `nvidia/model.py:77`. [S/M]\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/__init__.py` is a 3-line SPDX-only stub (no re-exports) [S] — so a hypothetical `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` from `amd/` would import *only* `ple_mmap.py`, dragging in no CUDA-specific nvidia module. That is what makes option (a) in §Port viable.\n\nConsequence: the module-scope `direct_register_custom_op(...)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:981-986` never runs on ROCm, so `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` does not exist there — even though the op *name* is unconditionally present in the default splitting-ops list (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`). [S]\n\n---\n\n## 2. TP semantics of the mmap path\n\n### Vocab-parallel path: a per-lookup all-reduce\n`vllm/model_executor/layers/vocab_parallel_embedding.py:490-518` [S]:\n```python\ndef forward(self, input_):\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        masked_input, input_mask = get_masked_input_and_mask(...)   # :493-500\n    ...\n    output_parallel = self.quant_method.embedding(self, masked_input.long())   # :504\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        if output_parallel.dtype in (torch.float8_e4m3fn, torch.float8_e5m2):\n            comm_output = output_parallel.view(torch.int8)                     # :512\n            comm_output.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :513\n            output = tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(comm_output)             # :514\n            return output.view(output_parallel.dtype)\n        output_parallel.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :516\n        return tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(output_parallel)               # :517\n    return output_parallel\n```\nThe FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n\nSo: **yes, one `tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce` per PLE lookup, per PLE layer, per forward** whenever `tp_size > 1`.\n\n### Mmap path: plain `nn.Module`, whole table per rank, no collective\n`MmapNgramEmbedding` is declared `class MmapNgramEmbedding(nn.Module)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:506` [S] and constructed with the **full padded vocab**: `ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:288-290` [S] — the identical `padded_vocab_size` handed to the vocab-parallel constructor in the `else:` branch at `:292-293`.\n\nIts `forward` (`ple_mmap.py:545-573`) contains **no collective, no mask, no rank arithmetic**:\n```\n$ grep -n 'rank\\|tp_\\|world_size\\|parallel\\|all_reduce\\|distributed' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n(no output; exit 1)\n```\n[M] — zero hits across the whole 1010-line file.\n\n`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n```python\nfor idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n    self.mm[idx] = np.memmap(path, dtype=np.uint8, mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))\n    self.rows_total += rows\n```\nand `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n\n**Verdict**: switching to the mmap path removes the per-token all-reduce/all-gather at the PLE lookup site entirely. The trade is that every rank independently gathers the full-width rows for every token — TP-redundant I/O and TP-redundant host→device copies, in exchange for zero NCCL/RCCL traffic at that site. (The redundancy hits page cache, shared per host, so the *disk* cost is not multiplied — that last clause is my inference from `mode=\"r\"` shared file mappings, not read from source; treat as reasoning, not evidence.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. `load_weights` under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n\nThe interception lives in `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:450-547`. Two mmap-specific branches:\n\n**(a) weight_scale kept** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:477-490` [S]:\n```python\nif (isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding)\n        and name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"):\n    ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight,\n                              cast(torch.Tensor, self.layer_multipliers).device)\n    loaded.add(name); continue\n```\n`set_weight_scale` registers a non-persistent buffer, `ple_mmap.py:587-590`. [S]\n\n**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n```python\nif isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding):\n    # Served from disk via mmap; the loader still streams\n    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n    embedding.weights_streamed = True\n    loaded.add(\"ngram_embedding.weight\")\n    continue\n```\n`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n\nThe stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n\n**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n- Header-only reads; the docstring at `:186-191` says \"never the multi-GiB tensor data\". [S/CL]\n- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n\n**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n\nNuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n\nContract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory/staging behavior relevant to UMA (line numbers at THIS head)\n\nAll in `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`:\n\n| Property | Line(s) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `np.memmap(..., mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))` | **331-333** | [S] read-only shared mapping, `dtype=np.uint8` |\n| Dedup via `np.unique(..., return_inverse=True)` | **360** | [S] `uniq, inverse = np.unique(ids, return_inverse=True)` |\n| Re-expansion to input order | **405** | [S] `gathered = out[inverse]` |\n| `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self.workers)` | **335** | [S] |\n| Chunked tasks, `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` rows each | **377-378** | [S] `for c in range(s, e, self.chunk): tasks.append((si, c, min(c + self.chunk, e)))` |\n| Fan-out over the pool | **391-395** | [S] single task runs inline; else `self.pool.map(run, tasks)` |\n| Fancy-index gather off the memmap (page faults do the I/O; GIL released) | **387** + comment **385-386** | [S/CL] `out[a:b] = mm[local[a:b]]` |\n| **Staging buffer is pageable, not pinned** | **369** | [S] `out = np.empty((uniq.size, self.row_bytes), dtype=np.uint8)` — plain NumPy heap alloc; `torch.from_numpy(rows)` at **567** wraps it |\n| Explicitly stated non-pinned | **568-571** | [CL — comment] \"`rows` … is pageable host memory, not pinned, so this H2D copy is effectively synchronous. Pinned staging (Phase-4 lever 5) is a separate, not-yet-pulled lever\" |\n| H2D copy | **572** | [S] `out = out.to(ids.device, non_blocking=True)` — no-op non-blocking per the comment above |\n| **Device→host sync of the ids, every call** | **558** | [S] `ids_np = ids.detach().to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=False).numpy().reshape(-1)` — an explicit blocking D2H per PLE layer per forward |\n| Prewarm streams via `open(..., buffering=0)` in 64 MiB blocks | **442**, **456-465** | [S] `block = 64 << 20` |\n| Prewarm bound = `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable - 8 GiB))` | **91**, **493-500** | [S] `_PREWARM_HEADROOM_BYTES = 8 << 30` |\n| `MemAvailable` read from `/proc/meminfo` | **484-490** | [S] |\n| Pool teardown idempotent | **468-481** | [S] |\n| p99 latency logging, at most once per 60 s | **92**, **411-429** | [S] `_LOG_INTERVAL_S = 60.0` |\n\n**All four campaign claims about staging are confirmed at this head**, with no line drift beyond what is tabulated above.\n\n### CUDA-specific calls that would break under ROCm/HIP — the grep\n```\n$ grep -n 'torch\\.cuda\\|pin_memory\\|cudaHost\\|hip\\|\\.cuda()' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n604:        of splitting_ops membership.\n```\n[M] — the single hit is a false positive: `hip` inside the word \"mem­ber**ship**\" on a comment line. There are **no** `torch.cuda.*` calls, no `pin_memory`, no `cudaHostRegister`, no explicit `torch.cuda.Stream`/`current_stream`, and no `.cuda()` in the file.\n\nThe only platform touchpoints in `ple_mmap.py`, and their ROCm status:\n\n1. `from vllm.platforms import current_platform` (`:67`) and `if current_platform.dispatch_key != \"CPU\": vllm_lib.impl(OP_NAME, ..., dispatch_key=\"CPU\")` (`:993-994`). [S]\n   **Safe on ROCm**: `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key: str = \"CUDA\"` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:502` [S] (same value as `vllm/platforms/cuda.py:212` [S]). PyTorch/HIP reuses the CUDA dispatch key, so `direct_register_custom_op` at `:981-986` registers correctly and the CPU-key double-registration guard behaves identically.\n2. `check_cudagraph_safety` (`:597-635`) reads only `CompilationConfig` fields. `has_full_cudagraphs()` is defined in `vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84` [S] — platform-agnostic. ROCm already drives `cudagraph_mode` through the same enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`, incl. `compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE`) and returns `\"vllm.compilation.cuda_graph.CUDAGraphWrapper\"` at `rocm.py:1002`. [S]\n3. `_mem_available_bytes` reads `/proc/meminfo` (`:484-490`) — Linux-generic, not vendor-specific. [S]\n4. **The one real ROCm risk: hardcoded `torch.float8_e4m3fn`.** `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` at `:78-84`, and `MmapNgramEmbedding.__init__` defaults `self.torch_dtype = torch.float8_e4m3fn` at `:535`. [S] On gfx94x (MI300X/MI325X) `RocmPlatform.fp8_dtype()` returns `torch.float8_e4m3fnuz` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:975-984` [S]. `is_fp8()` accepts both (`vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py:42-45` [S]), so the dequant *gate* still fires; but the tensor handed downstream is `e4m3fn`, not the platform's fnuz. Whether a ROCm PyTorch build on gfx942 supports `float8_e4m3fn` storage + the `.to(bf16)` cast used by the dequant is **UNDETERMINED** from source — settling it needs `torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\").to(torch.bfloat16)` executed on the target ROCm build. Note the bytes on disk are OCP e4m3 either way, so re-labelling them fnuz would be numerically *wrong*; if fnuz-only kernels are required downstream, that is a conversion problem, not a relabel.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The two PRs: superset or drift?\n\n**Neither is a strict superset.** `#54129` is the mmap branch built on a **newer upstream-main merge**; `#53896` has a **later-timestamped head commit** (`2026-08-28 16:54 +0800` = 08:54 UTC vs `11:52 +0700` = 04:52 UTC) carrying CI work that `#54129` does not have. [M — `git log -1 --format='%ci'` on both]\n\n`diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm` → **20 differing paths** [M]:\n```\nvllm/config/compilation.py, vllm/envs.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/{fp8,mxfp4}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/quark/schemes/quark_ocp_mx.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/{config_utils,flashinfer_utils}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/models/{qwen2_moe,qwen3_next}.py,\nvllm/models/kimi_k3/nvidia/kda_metadata.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nOnly in vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia: ple_mmap.py,\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py\n```\n\nModel-tree drift, `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp` [M]: 6 differing files + `ple_mmap.py` only in 54129. Per-file:\n\n| File | 54129 vs 53896 | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `amd/ple_layer.py` | +1 line | **Only** `# TODO: need double-check` inserted above the `NULL_BLOCK_ID` remap (53896 line 519 region → 54129 `amd/ple_layer.py:522`). [S] Zero functional change; zero mmap wiring. |\n| `amd/model.py` | −8/+0 mmap-unrelated | Drops `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` import, the `self.is_fused_shared_expert_enabled` computation, and the `enabled=` kwarg to `maybe_fuse_shared_experts`. Upstream-main API drift. [S] |\n| `amd/mtp.py` | same drift | identical `is_fused_shared_expert` removals. [S] |\n| `nvidia/mtp.py` | same drift | identical. [S] |\n| `nvidia/model.py` | +14/−12 [M] | 12 of those are upstream drift (as above); the mmap wiring is `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config` (`:12`), `from . import ple_mmap` (`:77`), and two 5-line `build_tables` blocks at `:812-817` and `:1011-1016`. [S] |\n| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | +89/−62 [M] | The whole mmap wiring, plus **a removal that changes the env-off path** — see below. |\n\n### Newer: `#54129`, by base. Later: `#53896`, by head timestamp.\n`#54129`'s head is literally a merge of `upstream-main` into `ple-mmap-upstream`, and its non-PLE churn (fused-MoE oracle, quark OCP MX, flashinfer utils, kimi_k3) is that merge. `#53896` never had `ple_mmap.py` (`grep -c PLE_MMAP vllm-pr53896/vllm/envs.py` → `0`; `find vllm-pr53896 -name 'ple_mmap*' -o -name 'test_ple_mmap*' | wc -l` → `0`) [M]. So the mmap feature is strictly additive on top of #53896's model code, but the two branches have **already diverged on shared non-mmap files** and a merge will conflict on `amd/{model,mtp}.py`, `nvidia/{model,mtp}.py`, `vllm/config/compilation.py`, and `vllm/envs.py`.\n\n### Surprise: #54129 changed the **env-off** nvidia path\n`#53896` routed the ID hashing through its own split-out custom op:\n```python\n# 53896 nvidia/ple_layer.py (removed by 54129)\n# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n# which dispatch only on the padded token count.\ntorch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, ngram_ids, self.layer_name)\n```\n`#54129` **deletes** `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids`, its fake impl, and its `direct_register_custom_op` registration (−35 lines), renames `compute_ngram_ids` → `_hash_ngram_ids`, and in the env-off branch calls it **inline, traced**: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448` `ngram_ids = self._hash_ngram_ids(...); return self.ngram_embedding(ngram_ids).flatten(-2)`. [S] The op name also disappears from `_attention_ops`: `#53896` `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` had `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids\"`; `#54129` `:771` has `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` in its place. [M — `grep -n qwen4_exp` on both]\nIt is gone tree-wide: `grep -rn 'compute_ple_ngram_ids\\|compute_ngram_ids' vllm/ tests/` → no output. [M]\n\nAlso changed in the shared hashing body: `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs, :num_tokens]` (53896) → `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs]` (54129, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:379`). [S]\n\nThis contradicts `ple_mmap.py:32-35`'s claim [CL] that \"With `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` unset, nothing in this module is ever invoked and the stock classes are untouched.\" Nothing in *ple_mmap.py* is invoked — true — but the stock nvidia PLE forward and its cudagraph-splitting behavior **did** change. Whether that regresses PIECEWISE dispatch (the removed comment's stated concern) is **UNDETERMINED** from source; settling it needs a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and a check of graph-split boundaries, or the PR author's rationale.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The piecewise-cudagraph guard, and its AMD analogue\n\n`nvidia/ple_mmap.py:597-635`, `check_cudagraph_safety(compilation_config)` — three independent refusals, any one of which alone would miss a route into a capture [S]:\n\n1. `:614-621` — `if compilation_config.cudagraph_mode.has_full_cudagraphs(): raise RuntimeError(...)`. Message: *\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 requires piecewise-only CUDA graphs … Pass `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\"*\n2. `:622-628` — `if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — *\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.\"*\n3. `:629-635` — `if QUALIFIED_OP_NAME not in (compilation_config.splitting_ops or []): raise` — guards against an operator-supplied `-cc.splitting_ops` or an attn-fusion reset silently dropping the op.\n\nCall site: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:284`, inside `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__`, i.e. at **construction time, before the ~78 GiB backbone streams** (`ple_mmap.py:703-704` comment [CL]). Tests pin all three: `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:661`, `:680`, `:691`, `:705`, plus construction-level mirrors at `:758`, `:786`, `:814`. [S]\n\n**What is the AMD analogue supposed to be? The same function, unchanged.** [S] The guard reads nothing platform-specific: `has_full_cudagraphs()` is `self.max_cudagraph_mode() == CUDAGraphMode.FULL` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84`), `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` and `splitting_ops` are plain config. ROCm drives the identical `CUDAGraphMode` enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`) and the identical `CUDAGraphWrapper` (`rocm.py:1002`). No HIP-graph-specific analogue is needed or exists.\n\nThe third check is **already satisfied on ROCm by default**: `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` sits unconditionally in the class-level `_attention_ops` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:764-782`, entry at `:771`), and `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` copies that list wholesale when `splitting_ops is None` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:1152-1161`, `self.splitting_ops = list(self._attention_ops)`). [S] The op *name* is in the default split set on every platform; only the op *implementation* is nvidia-gated.\n\n**AMD-specific tension worth flagging**: the AMD PLE short-conv path is written for FULL cudagraphs — `amd/ple_layer.py:522-525` [S]:\n```\n# TODO: need double-check\n# FULL cudagraph padded decode rows use NULL_BLOCK_ID. Remap them to\n# slot 0 for a safe gather, then zero output and skip write-back.\nvalid_state = state_indices != NULL_BLOCK_ID\n```\n(also `:642`, `:748`). Enabling `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` on AMD would force `cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` via check #1, taking the AMD tree off the mode its short-conv code was tuned for. That is a **performance/coverage decision the port must make explicitly**, not an incidental detail.\n\nAlso note: AMD's *existing* gather op, `vllm::qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (registered at `amd/ple_layer.py:1236-1241`, defined `:1217-1232`), is **not** in `_attention_ops` — `grep -n qwen4_exp vllm/config/compilation.py` returns only lines 770/771/772, none of which is the AMD op. [M] So today's AMD gather is opaque to Inductor but still *inside* the capture region. The port must not reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Every env var the PR introduces\n\nFour, all in `vllm/envs.py`. Type stubs at `:325-328`, lambdas at `:2168-2177`. [S]\n\n| Var | Default | Parse | Effect | Pins |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Master gate. `ple_mmap.enabled()` (`ple_mmap.py:116-118`) returns it; drives the constructor swap (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`), the three guards, the `load_weights` interception, and `build_tables` (`nvidia/model.py:813`, `:1012`). | `envs.py:325`, `:2170` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS` | `32` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)` for the gather pool; clamped `max(1, int(workers))`. Page faults overlap across threads. | `envs.py:326`, `:2172`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:906`; used `:327/:335` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` | `2048` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | Rows per gather task; clamped `max(1, int(chunk))`. | `envs.py:327`, `:2174`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:907`; used `:325/:377` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Streams the table once at load to warm page cache, bounded by `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable − 8 GiB))`. | `envs.py:328`, `:2177`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:913-921`; bound `:493-500` |\n\n**Compile-hash semantics** — `vllm/envs.py:2342-2350` adds the three *tuning* knobs to the ignored-factors set, deliberately leaving `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` itself a compile factor [S]:\n```\n# PLE mmap gather-pool tuning: affects only the CPU body of a\n# split-out op, never the compiled graph. VLLM_PLE_MMAP itself stays\n# a factor since it changes which op the graph splits around.\n\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM\",\n```\nConfirmed the PR adds no other env var: `grep -n '^+.*os\\.getenv\\|^+.*envs\\.VLLM_' pr54129.diff | grep -v PLE_MMAP` returns only pre-existing `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` reads and one `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` false positive. [M]\n\n---\n\n## Exact port specification (analysis only — NOT written)\n\n### Blocker A (must be resolved first): `amd/ple_layer.py` has no FP8 dequant at all\nThe mmap path is **FP8-only by construction**: `_FP8_DTYPES` admits only `F8_E4M3` (`ple_mmap.py:78-84`), and `_validate_layer_shards` raises for anything else (`ple_mmap.py:687-693`). Its output is a raw `float8_e4m3fn` tensor (`ple_mmap.py:567-573`), which the nvidia layer dequantizes at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:1194`:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nembeddings = self._dequantize_embeddings(embeddings, hidden_states.dtype)   # :1194\n```\n`_dequantize_embeddings` is `nvidia/ple_layer.py:634-648`; `_get_embedding_weight_scale` is `:632-634`.\n\n**The AMD file has none of this** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n '_dequantize_embeddings\\|is_fp8\\|weight_scale\\|quant_config\\|params_dtype' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py\n317:            dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype\n407:        quant_config = vllm_config.quant_config\n438:            quant_config=quant_config,     # key_proj only\n445:            quant_config=quant_config,     # value_proj only\n```\nand its forward goes straight into the projection with no dequant: `amd/ple_layer.py:1018-1020` [S]:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nkey, _ = self.key_proj(embeddings)\nvalue, _ = self.value_proj(embeddings)\n```\nThis gap **pre-dates #54129**: `grep -c '_dequantize_embeddings\\|Fp8EmbeddingMethod' vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` → `0`, while `#53896`'s nvidia file already had `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` at `:140` and `_dequantize_embeddings` at `:581`. [M] AMD's `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding` is constructed with no `quant_config`/`params_dtype` at all (`amd/ple_layer.py:425-433`, 7 positional args vs nvidia's 7 + 2 kwargs at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:580-589`), and its `__init__` signature (`amd/ple_layer.py:127-136`) lacks both parameters.\n\nTherefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\n\n### The mmap wiring, line by line (what to mirror)\n\n**`nvidia/ple_layer.py` — the wiring is exactly five sites:**\n\n| # | Lines | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | `:7` | `from typing import cast` |\n| W2 | `:52` | `from . import ple_mmap` |\n| W3 | **`:281-291`** | the constructor branch — annotation `self.ngram_embedding: VocabParallelEmbedding \\| ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding`; `if ple_mmap.enabled():`; `vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()`; `ple_mmap.check_cudagraph_safety(vllm_config.compilation_config)`; `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(vllm_config.model_config, layer_name, self.head_dim)`; `self.ngram_embedding = ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)`; `else:` + reindent of `:292-301` |\n| W4 | **`:428-446`** | the forward branch — `isinstance(...)` gate, symbolic `num_tokens = input_ids.reshape(-1).shape[0]`, `output = input_ids.new_empty((num_tokens, self.embedding_dim), dtype=self.ngram_embedding.torch_dtype)`, `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, output, self.layer_name)`, `return output` (10 of the 19 lines are the load-bearing ConstraintViolationError comment) |\n| W5a | **`:477-490`** | `load_weights` weight_scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(...)` |\n| W5b | **`:517-527`** (+ `:533`, `:535`, `:538-539`) | `load_weights` shard-drop → `embedding.weights_streamed = True; loaded.add(...); continue`, plus `stock_embedding = cast(VocabParallelEmbedding, embedding)` and the three renamed reads; also the hoist of `embedding = self.ngram_embedding` to `:461` |\n\nThe campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\n\n**`nvidia/model.py` — three sites:**\n- `:12` `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`\n- `:77` `from . import ple_mmap`\n- `:812-817` and `:1011-1016` — after `loader.load_weights(...)`: `if ple_mmap.enabled(): ple_mmap.build_tables(self.model_config, get_current_vllm_config().compilation_config)`; `return loaded`\n\nMeasured: `diff -u vllm-pr53896/.../nvidia/model.py vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia/model.py` = **+14/−12**, of which 12 removed + 2 added are upstream `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` drift; the mmap portion is **+12 net**. [M] `amd/model.py` differs from `nvidia/model.py` by *exactly and only these four hunks* — verified: `diff -u amd/model.py nvidia/model.py` produces 4 hunks, all mmap. [M]\n\n### Imports / env checks / guards the port must mirror\n1. `from . import ple_mmap` — but `ple_mmap.py` lives in `nvidia/`. Two options: **(a)** `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` in both `amd/ple_layer.py` and `amd/model.py` — safe, because `nvidia/__init__.py` is an SPDX-only stub (3 lines, no re-exports) [S], so this pulls in *only* `ple_mmap.py` and none of the CUDA-heavy nvidia modules; **(b)** relocate to `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py` alongside `common/ple.py` and update both trees + all `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` imports (the test file imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap` at `:27` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.model` at `:26`). (b) is the cleaner upstream shape; (a) is the smaller diff.\n2. `get_current_vllm_config` — **already imported** in `amd/ple_layer.py:12` (`from vllm.config import CacheConfig, ModelConfig, VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`) [S]. Must be *added* to `amd/model.py:12` (currently `from vllm.config import VllmConfig`) [S].\n3. `from typing import cast` — must be added to `amd/ple_layer.py` (absent; nvidia has it at `:7`) [S].\n4. Env checks: exactly one, `ple_mmap.enabled()` — three call sites, mirroring `nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`, `nvidia/model.py:813`, `nvidia/model.py:1012`. The three tuning vars are read inside `ple_mmap.py` (`:906`, `:907`, `:913`) and need no per-tree wiring.\n5. Guards: `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for`, both at construction (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:284-287`), unchanged — no AMD variant.\n6. **Op registration**: none needed. `direct_register_custom_op` fires at `ple_mmap.py:981-986` on first import of the module, under `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == \"CUDA\"` (`rocm.py:502`). The CPU double-registration guard at `:993-994` also behaves correctly on ROCm.\n7. **`splitting_ops`**: already correct — `vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` is in `_attention_ops` unconditionally (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`), copied into `splitting_ops` by `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` (`:1152-1161`).\n8. The AMD-only op `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:1217-1241`) stays for the env-off path; it must **not** be reused for the mmap path (not in `_attention_ops`, so not a split boundary, so it would run inside the capture).\n\n### Size, honestly measured\n- `amd/model.py`: **+12** lines.\n- `amd/ple_layer.py`: W1–W5 ≈ **+58 lines** including the comments the nvidia file carries (≈ **+35** code-only), plus the `else:` reindent of `:292-301`.\n- Plus Blocker A: **+10** (minimal dequant) to **+80** (full FP8 stack parity).\n- Plus, if option (b), a file move and a test-import sweep across 2048 lines of `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which is nvidia-only today: `grep -cni 'amd\\|rocm' tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` → `0`. [M]\n\n**Realistic total: 80–150 lines, not 15.**\n\n---\n\n## Open / UNDETERMINED\n\n1. **Does `float8_e4m3fn` storage + `.to(bf16)` work on the target gfx arch?** Looked at: `ple_mmap.py:78-84`, `:535`, `:567`; `rocm.py:975-984`; `fp8_utils.py:42-45`. Settled by running the cast on the target ROCm build.\n2. **Did removing `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids` regress env-off PIECEWISE dispatch on nvidia?** Looked at: `#53896` `nvidia/ple_layer.py` op + its comment, `#54129` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448`, `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` (53896) vs `:771` (54129), and the tree-wide grep showing no remaining reference. Settled by a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` inspecting graph-split boundaries, or by the author.\n3. **Is the `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522` a known AMD FULL-cudagraph correctness doubt?** It is the sole delta between the two branches' AMD PLE files. Settled by the PR thread.\n4. **Whether AMD is expected to run FULL cudagraphs for this model in practice** — `amd/ple_layer.py:522/642/748` handle `NULL_BLOCK_ID` padded decode rows, implying yes; guard #1 would forbid it under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`. Settled by the AMD serving config actually in use.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991809525}}
{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me read ple-54129.md and moe-dispatch.md. Also run-smoke.sh for the style.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":875,"output":98,"cacheRead":11264,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":27,"totalTokens":12237,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991805944,"responseId":"chatcmpl-dd424ebb-d673-4bfa-995c-6cd34f91f9e2","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: vLLM PR #54129 (`VLLM_PLE_MMAP`, branch `ple-mmap-upstream`) — AMD/ROCm reachability\n\nInvestigator session: 2026-08-28. Analysis only; no code written or patched.\n\n**Checkouts read this session**\n- `#54129`: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-tom-mecattaf-notes-qwen-next/c4262a6d-5cde-494b-9143-685dd896a84f/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129`\n  HEAD `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream`, `2026-08-28 11:52:45 +0700`, author *Christopher Deodat* [M: `git log -1 --format='%ci %an %s'`]\n- `#53896`: `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr53896`\n  HEAD `89d0bb7 update CI`, `2026-08-28 16:54:24 +0800`, author *huanghaoyan.hhy* [M: same command]\n- Raw diff: `.../scratchpad/pr54129.diff`, 26483 lines [M: `wc -l`]\n\nGrades: **[S]** read from source · **[M]** measured (command output) · **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n---\n\n## Q0 — Headline answer\n\n**Yes.** The mmap/engram path is wired exclusively into `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/`. On a ROCm build the `amd/` tree is imported instead, `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is never imported, the custom op is never registered, and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` is silently a **no-op** — no error, no warning, the stock GPU-resident `VocabParallelEmbedding` is built as if the flag were unset.\n\n**Measured reachability** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n 'ple_mmap\\|PLE_MMAP\\|mmap\\|Mmap\\|MMAP' \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output — zero hits in all four files)\n```\nversus 11 hits in `nvidia/ple_layer.py` and 5 in `nvidia/model.py`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'nvidia' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output)\n```\nThe `amd/` tree never reaches into `nvidia/`. [M]\n\n**Correction to the campaign record**: the port is **not ~15 lines**. See §\"Exact port specification\" — it is ~12 lines in `amd/model.py` plus ~45 code lines (~60 with the load-bearing comments) in `amd/ple_layer.py`, **and** it depends on an FP8 dequant capability that `amd/ple_layer.py` does not currently have at all (§Blocker A). That last item is the real finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Platform dispatch — how a ROCm build lands in `amd/`\n\nSelection is a lazy module `__getattr__` on the model package, not a registry-level split.\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/__init__.py:22-49` [S]:\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Any:\n    if name in {\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\", \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\", \"Qwen4ExpMTP\"}:\n        from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n        if current_platform.is_xpu() or current_platform.is_tpu():\n            raise NotImplementedError(\"Qwen4Exp currently supports CUDA and ROCm only\")\n        if current_platform.is_rocm():\n            from .amd.model import (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM, Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration)\n            from .amd.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n        else:\n            from .nvidia.model import (...)\n            from .nvidia.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n```\n\nChain of evidence:\n- Registry points at the package, not a platform subtree: `vllm/model_executor/models/registry.py:114-117` `\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\": (\"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\", \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\")`; `:580-583` for `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration`; `:669` for `Qwen4ExpMTP`. [S]\n- `is_rocm()` is `self._enum == PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/interface.py:192-193`. [S]\n- `RocmPlatform._enum = PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:498-499`. [S]\n- Platform selection: `vllm/platforms/__init__.py:110-128` — `rocm_platform_plugin()` returns `\"vllm.platforms.rocm.RocmPlatform\"` iff `amdsmi` imports and `amdsmi_get_processor_handles()` is non-empty. [S]\n\n**Is there any chance a ROCm build imports the nvidia tree?** No, on the model path. [S]\n- The `else:` branch at `__init__.py:38-43` is the only importer of `nvidia/model.py` / `nvidia/mtp.py`, and it is unreachable when `is_rocm()` is true.\n- `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is imported from exactly two places, both inside the nvidia tree: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:52` and `nvidia/model.py:77`. [S/M]\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/__init__.py` is a 3-line SPDX-only stub (no re-exports) [S] — so a hypothetical `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` from `amd/` would import *only* `ple_mmap.py`, dragging in no CUDA-specific nvidia module. That is what makes option (a) in §Port viable.\n\nConsequence: the module-scope `direct_register_custom_op(...)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:981-986` never runs on ROCm, so `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` does not exist there — even though the op *name* is unconditionally present in the default splitting-ops list (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`). [S]\n\n---\n\n## 2. TP semantics of the mmap path\n\n### Vocab-parallel path: a per-lookup all-reduce\n`vllm/model_executor/layers/vocab_parallel_embedding.py:490-518` [S]:\n```python\ndef forward(self, input_):\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        masked_input, input_mask = get_masked_input_and_mask(...)   # :493-500\n    ...\n    output_parallel = self.quant_method.embedding(self, masked_input.long())   # :504\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        if output_parallel.dtype in (torch.float8_e4m3fn, torch.float8_e5m2):\n            comm_output = output_parallel.view(torch.int8)                     # :512\n            comm_output.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :513\n            output = tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(comm_output)             # :514\n            return output.view(output_parallel.dtype)\n        output_parallel.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :516\n        return tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(output_parallel)               # :517\n    return output_parallel\n```\nThe FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n\nSo: **yes, one `tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce` per PLE lookup, per PLE layer, per forward** whenever `tp_size > 1`.\n\n### Mmap path: plain `nn.Module`, whole table per rank, no collective\n`MmapNgramEmbedding` is declared `class MmapNgramEmbedding(nn.Module)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:506` [S] and constructed with the **full padded vocab**: `ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:288-290` [S] — the identical `padded_vocab_size` handed to the vocab-parallel constructor in the `else:` branch at `:292-293`.\n\nIts `forward` (`ple_mmap.py:545-573`) contains **no collective, no mask, no rank arithmetic**:\n```\n$ grep -n 'rank\\|tp_\\|world_size\\|parallel\\|all_reduce\\|distributed' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n(no output; exit 1)\n```\n[M] — zero hits across the whole 1010-line file.\n\n`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n```python\nfor idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n    self.mm[idx] = np.memmap(path, dtype=np.uint8, mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))\n    self.rows_total += rows\n```\nand `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n\n**Verdict**: switching to the mmap path removes the per-token all-reduce/all-gather at the PLE lookup site entirely. The trade is that every rank independently gathers the full-width rows for every token — TP-redundant I/O and TP-redundant host→device copies, in exchange for zero NCCL/RCCL traffic at that site. (The redundancy hits page cache, shared per host, so the *disk* cost is not multiplied — that last clause is my inference from `mode=\"r\"` shared file mappings, not read from source; treat as reasoning, not evidence.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. `load_weights` under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n\nThe interception lives in `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:450-547`. Two mmap-specific branches:\n\n**(a) weight_scale kept** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:477-490` [S]:\n```python\nif (isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding)\n        and name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"):\n    ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight,\n                              cast(torch.Tensor, self.layer_multipliers).device)\n    loaded.add(name); continue\n```\n`set_weight_scale` registers a non-persistent buffer, `ple_mmap.py:587-590`. [S]\n\n**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n```python\nif isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding):\n    # Served from disk via mmap; the loader still streams\n    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n    embedding.weights_streamed = True\n    loaded.add(\"ngram_embedding.weight\")\n    continue\n```\n`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n\nThe stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n\n**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n- Header-only reads; the docstring at `:186-191` says \"never the multi-GiB tensor data\". [S/CL]\n- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n\n**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n\nNuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n\nContract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory/staging behavior relevant to UMA (line numbers at THIS head)\n\nAll in `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`:\n\n| Property | Line(s) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `np.memmap(..., mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))` | **331-333** | [S] read-only shared mapping, `dtype=np.uint8` |\n| Dedup via `np.unique(..., return_inverse=True)` | **360** | [S] `uniq, inverse = np.unique(ids, return_inverse=True)` |\n| Re-expansion to input order | **405** | [S] `gathered = out[inverse]` |\n| `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self.workers)` | **335** | [S] |\n| Chunked tasks, `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` rows each | **377-378** | [S] `for c in range(s, e, self.chunk): tasks.append((si, c, min(c + self.chunk, e)))` |\n| Fan-out over the pool | **391-395** | [S] single task runs inline; else `self.pool.map(run, tasks)` |\n| Fancy-index gather off the memmap (page faults do the I/O; GIL released) | **387** + comment **385-386** | [S/CL] `out[a:b] = mm[local[a:b]]` |\n| **Staging buffer is pageable, not pinned** | **369** | [S] `out = np.empty((uniq.size, self.row_bytes), dtype=np.uint8)` — plain NumPy heap alloc; `torch.from_numpy(rows)` at **567** wraps it |\n| Explicitly stated non-pinned | **568-571** | [CL — comment] \"`rows` … is pageable host memory, not pinned, so this H2D copy is effectively synchronous. Pinned staging (Phase-4 lever 5) is a separate, not-yet-pulled lever\" |\n| H2D copy | **572** | [S] `out = out.to(ids.device, non_blocking=True)` — no-op non-blocking per the comment above |\n| **Device→host sync of the ids, every call** | **558** | [S] `ids_np = ids.detach().to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=False).numpy().reshape(-1)` — an explicit blocking D2H per PLE layer per forward |\n| Prewarm streams via `open(..., buffering=0)` in 64 MiB blocks | **442**, **456-465** | [S] `block = 64 << 20` |\n| Prewarm bound = `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable - 8 GiB))` | **91**, **493-500** | [S] `_PREWARM_HEADROOM_BYTES = 8 << 30` |\n| `MemAvailable` read from `/proc/meminfo` | **484-490** | [S] |\n| Pool teardown idempotent | **468-481** | [S] |\n| p99 latency logging, at most once per 60 s | **92**, **411-429** | [S] `_LOG_INTERVAL_S = 60.0` |\n\n**All four campaign claims about staging are confirmed at this head**, with no line drift beyond what is tabulated above.\n\n### CUDA-specific calls that would break under ROCm/HIP — the grep\n```\n$ grep -n 'torch\\.cuda\\|pin_memory\\|cudaHost\\|hip\\|\\.cuda()' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n604:        of splitting_ops membership.\n```\n[M] — the single hit is a false positive: `hip` inside the word \"mem­ber**ship**\" on a comment line. There are **no** `torch.cuda.*` calls, no `pin_memory`, no `cudaHostRegister`, no explicit `torch.cuda.Stream`/`current_stream`, and no `.cuda()` in the file.\n\nThe only platform touchpoints in `ple_mmap.py`, and their ROCm status:\n\n1. `from vllm.platforms import current_platform` (`:67`) and `if current_platform.dispatch_key != \"CPU\": vllm_lib.impl(OP_NAME, ..., dispatch_key=\"CPU\")` (`:993-994`). [S]\n   **Safe on ROCm**: `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key: str = \"CUDA\"` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:502` [S] (same value as `vllm/platforms/cuda.py:212` [S]). PyTorch/HIP reuses the CUDA dispatch key, so `direct_register_custom_op` at `:981-986` registers correctly and the CPU-key double-registration guard behaves identically.\n2. `check_cudagraph_safety` (`:597-635`) reads only `CompilationConfig` fields. `has_full_cudagraphs()` is defined in `vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84` [S] — platform-agnostic. ROCm already drives `cudagraph_mode` through the same enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`, incl. `compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE`) and returns `\"vllm.compilation.cuda_graph.CUDAGraphWrapper\"` at `rocm.py:1002`. [S]\n3. `_mem_available_bytes` reads `/proc/meminfo` (`:484-490`) — Linux-generic, not vendor-specific. [S]\n4. **The one real ROCm risk: hardcoded `torch.float8_e4m3fn`.** `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` at `:78-84`, and `MmapNgramEmbedding.__init__` defaults `self.torch_dtype = torch.float8_e4m3fn` at `:535`. [S] On gfx94x (MI300X/MI325X) `RocmPlatform.fp8_dtype()` returns `torch.float8_e4m3fnuz` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:975-984` [S]. `is_fp8()` accepts both (`vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py:42-45` [S]), so the dequant *gate* still fires; but the tensor handed downstream is `e4m3fn`, not the platform's fnuz. Whether a ROCm PyTorch build on gfx942 supports `float8_e4m3fn` storage + the `.to(bf16)` cast used by the dequant is **UNDETERMINED** from source — settling it needs `torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\").to(torch.bfloat16)` executed on the target ROCm build. Note the bytes on disk are OCP e4m3 either way, so re-labelling them fnuz would be numerically *wrong*; if fnuz-only kernels are required downstream, that is a conversion problem, not a relabel.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The two PRs: superset or drift?\n\n**Neither is a strict superset.** `#54129` is the mmap branch built on a **newer upstream-main merge**; `#53896` has a **later-timestamped head commit** (`2026-08-28 16:54 +0800` = 08:54 UTC vs `11:52 +0700` = 04:52 UTC) carrying CI work that `#54129` does not have. [M — `git log -1 --format='%ci'` on both]\n\n`diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm` → **20 differing paths** [M]:\n```\nvllm/config/compilation.py, vllm/envs.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/{fp8,mxfp4}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/quark/schemes/quark_ocp_mx.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/{config_utils,flashinfer_utils}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/models/{qwen2_moe,qwen3_next}.py,\nvllm/models/kimi_k3/nvidia/kda_metadata.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nOnly in vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia: ple_mmap.py,\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py\n```\n\nModel-tree drift, `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp` [M]: 6 differing files + `ple_mmap.py` only in 54129. Per-file:\n\n| File | 54129 vs 53896 | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `amd/ple_layer.py` | +1 line | **Only** `# TODO: need double-check` inserted above the `NULL_BLOCK_ID` remap (53896 line 519 region → 54129 `amd/ple_layer.py:522`). [S] Zero functional change; zero mmap wiring. |\n| `amd/model.py` | −8/+0 mmap-unrelated | Drops `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` import, the `self.is_fused_shared_expert_enabled` computation, and the `enabled=` kwarg to `maybe_fuse_shared_experts`. Upstream-main API drift. [S] |\n| `amd/mtp.py` | same drift | identical `is_fused_shared_expert` removals. [S] |\n| `nvidia/mtp.py` | same drift | identical. [S] |\n| `nvidia/model.py` | +14/−12 [M] | 12 of those are upstream drift (as above); the mmap wiring is `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config` (`:12`), `from . import ple_mmap` (`:77`), and two 5-line `build_tables` blocks at `:812-817` and `:1011-1016`. [S] |\n| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | +89/−62 [M] | The whole mmap wiring, plus **a removal that changes the env-off path** — see below. |\n\n### Newer: `#54129`, by base. Later: `#53896`, by head timestamp.\n`#54129`'s head is literally a merge of `upstream-main` into `ple-mmap-upstream`, and its non-PLE churn (fused-MoE oracle, quark OCP MX, flashinfer utils, kimi_k3) is that merge. `#53896` never had `ple_mmap.py` (`grep -c PLE_MMAP vllm-pr53896/vllm/envs.py` → `0`; `find vllm-pr53896 -name 'ple_mmap*' -o -name 'test_ple_mmap*' | wc -l` → `0`) [M]. So the mmap feature is strictly additive on top of #53896's model code, but the two branches have **already diverged on shared non-mmap files** and a merge will conflict on `amd/{model,mtp}.py`, `nvidia/{model,mtp}.py`, `vllm/config/compilation.py`, and `vllm/envs.py`.\n\n### Surprise: #54129 changed the **env-off** nvidia path\n`#53896` routed the ID hashing through its own split-out custom op:\n```python\n# 53896 nvidia/ple_layer.py (removed by 54129)\n# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n# which dispatch only on the padded token count.\ntorch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, ngram_ids, self.layer_name)\n```\n`#54129` **deletes** `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids`, its fake impl, and its `direct_register_custom_op` registration (−35 lines), renames `compute_ngram_ids` → `_hash_ngram_ids`, and in the env-off branch calls it **inline, traced**: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448` `ngram_ids = self._hash_ngram_ids(...); return self.ngram_embedding(ngram_ids).flatten(-2)`. [S] The op name also disappears from `_attention_ops`: `#53896` `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` had `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids\"`; `#54129` `:771` has `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` in its place. [M — `grep -n qwen4_exp` on both]\nIt is gone tree-wide: `grep -rn 'compute_ple_ngram_ids\\|compute_ngram_ids' vllm/ tests/` → no output. [M]\n\nAlso changed in the shared hashing body: `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs, :num_tokens]` (53896) → `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs]` (54129, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:379`). [S]\n\nThis contradicts `ple_mmap.py:32-35`'s claim [CL] that \"With `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` unset, nothing in this module is ever invoked and the stock classes are untouched.\" Nothing in *ple_mmap.py* is invoked — true — but the stock nvidia PLE forward and its cudagraph-splitting behavior **did** change. Whether that regresses PIECEWISE dispatch (the removed comment's stated concern) is **UNDETERMINED** from source; settling it needs a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and a check of graph-split boundaries, or the PR author's rationale.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The piecewise-cudagraph guard, and its AMD analogue\n\n`nvidia/ple_mmap.py:597-635`, `check_cudagraph_safety(compilation_config)` — three independent refusals, any one of which alone would miss a route into a capture [S]:\n\n1. `:614-621` — `if compilation_config.cudagraph_mode.has_full_cudagraphs(): raise RuntimeError(...)`. Message: *\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 requires piecewise-only CUDA graphs … Pass `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\"*\n2. `:622-628` — `if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — *\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.\"*\n3. `:629-635` — `if QUALIFIED_OP_NAME not in (compilation_config.splitting_ops or []): raise` — guards against an operator-supplied `-cc.splitting_ops` or an attn-fusion reset silently dropping the op.\n\nCall site: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:284`, inside `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__`, i.e. at **construction time, before the ~78 GiB backbone streams** (`ple_mmap.py:703-704` comment [CL]). Tests pin all three: `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:661`, `:680`, `:691`, `:705`, plus construction-level mirrors at `:758`, `:786`, `:814`. [S]\n\n**What is the AMD analogue supposed to be? The same function, unchanged.** [S] The guard reads nothing platform-specific: `has_full_cudagraphs()` is `self.max_cudagraph_mode() == CUDAGraphMode.FULL` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84`), `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` and `splitting_ops` are plain config. ROCm drives the identical `CUDAGraphMode` enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`) and the identical `CUDAGraphWrapper` (`rocm.py:1002`). No HIP-graph-specific analogue is needed or exists.\n\nThe third check is **already satisfied on ROCm by default**: `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` sits unconditionally in the class-level `_attention_ops` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:764-782`, entry at `:771`), and `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` copies that list wholesale when `splitting_ops is None` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:1152-1161`, `self.splitting_ops = list(self._attention_ops)`). [S] The op *name* is in the default split set on every platform; only the op *implementation* is nvidia-gated.\n\n**AMD-specific tension worth flagging**: the AMD PLE short-conv path is written for FULL cudagraphs — `amd/ple_layer.py:522-525` [S]:\n```\n# TODO: need double-check\n# FULL cudagraph padded decode rows use NULL_BLOCK_ID. Remap them to\n# slot 0 for a safe gather, then zero output and skip write-back.\nvalid_state = state_indices != NULL_BLOCK_ID\n```\n(also `:642`, `:748`). Enabling `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` on AMD would force `cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` via check #1, taking the AMD tree off the mode its short-conv code was tuned for. That is a **performance/coverage decision the port must make explicitly**, not an incidental detail.\n\nAlso note: AMD's *existing* gather op, `vllm::qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (registered at `amd/ple_layer.py:1236-1241`, defined `:1217-1232`), is **not** in `_attention_ops` — `grep -n qwen4_exp vllm/config/compilation.py` returns only lines 770/771/772, none of which is the AMD op. [M] So today's AMD gather is opaque to Inductor but still *inside* the capture region. The port must not reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Every env var the PR introduces\n\nFour, all in `vllm/envs.py`. Type stubs at `:325-328`, lambdas at `:2168-2177`. [S]\n\n| Var | Default | Parse | Effect | Pins |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Master gate. `ple_mmap.enabled()` (`ple_mmap.py:116-118`) returns it; drives the constructor swap (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`), the three guards, the `load_weights` interception, and `build_tables` (`nvidia/model.py:813`, `:1012`). | `envs.py:325`, `:2170` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS` | `32` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)` for the gather pool; clamped `max(1, int(workers))`. Page faults overlap across threads. | `envs.py:326`, `:2172`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:906`; used `:327/:335` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` | `2048` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | Rows per gather task; clamped `max(1, int(chunk))`. | `envs.py:327`, `:2174`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:907`; used `:325/:377` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Streams the table once at load to warm page cache, bounded by `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable − 8 GiB))`. | `envs.py:328`, `:2177`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:913-921`; bound `:493-500` |\n\n**Compile-hash semantics** — `vllm/envs.py:2342-2350` adds the three *tuning* knobs to the ignored-factors set, deliberately leaving `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` itself a compile factor [S]:\n```\n# PLE mmap gather-pool tuning: affects only the CPU body of a\n# split-out op, never the compiled graph. VLLM_PLE_MMAP itself stays\n# a factor since it changes which op the graph splits around.\n\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM\",\n```\nConfirmed the PR adds no other env var: `grep -n '^+.*os\\.getenv\\|^+.*envs\\.VLLM_' pr54129.diff | grep -v PLE_MMAP` returns only pre-existing `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` reads and one `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` false positive. [M]\n\n---\n\n## Exact port specification (analysis only — NOT written)\n\n### Blocker A (must be resolved first): `amd/ple_layer.py` has no FP8 dequant at all\nThe mmap path is **FP8-only by construction**: `_FP8_DTYPES` admits only `F8_E4M3` (`ple_mmap.py:78-84`), and `_validate_layer_shards` raises for anything else (`ple_mmap.py:687-693`). Its output is a raw `float8_e4m3fn` tensor (`ple_mmap.py:567-573`), which the nvidia layer dequantizes at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:1194`:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nembeddings = self._dequantize_embeddings(embeddings, hidden_states.dtype)   # :1194\n```\n`_dequantize_embeddings` is `nvidia/ple_layer.py:634-648`; `_get_embedding_weight_scale` is `:632-634`.\n\n**The AMD file has none of this** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n '_dequantize_embeddings\\|is_fp8\\|weight_scale\\|quant_config\\|params_dtype' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py\n317:            dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype\n407:        quant_config = vllm_config.quant_config\n438:            quant_config=quant_config,     # key_proj only\n445:            quant_config=quant_config,     # value_proj only\n```\nand its forward goes straight into the projection with no dequant: `amd/ple_layer.py:1018-1020` [S]:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nkey, _ = self.key_proj(embeddings)\nvalue, _ = self.value_proj(embeddings)\n```\nThis gap **pre-dates #54129**: `grep -c '_dequantize_embeddings\\|Fp8EmbeddingMethod' vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` → `0`, while `#53896`'s nvidia file already had `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` at `:140` and `_dequantize_embeddings` at `:581`. [M] AMD's `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding` is constructed with no `quant_config`/`params_dtype` at all (`amd/ple_layer.py:425-433`, 7 positional args vs nvidia's 7 + 2 kwargs at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:580-589`), and its `__init__` signature (`amd/ple_layer.py:127-136`) lacks both parameters.\n\nTherefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\n\n### The mmap wiring, line by line (what to mirror)\n\n**`nvidia/ple_layer.py` — the wiring is exactly five sites:**\n\n| # | Lines | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | `:7` | `from typing import cast` |\n| W2 | `:52` | `from . import ple_mmap` |\n| W3 | **`:281-291`** | the constructor branch — annotation `self.ngram_embedding: VocabParallelEmbedding \\| ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding`; `if ple_mmap.enabled():`; `vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()`; `ple_mmap.check_cudagraph_safety(vllm_config.compilation_config)`; `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(vllm_config.model_config, layer_name, self.head_dim)`; `self.ngram_embedding = ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)`; `else:` + reindent of `:292-301` |\n| W4 | **`:428-446`** | the forward branch — `isinstance(...)` gate, symbolic `num_tokens = input_ids.reshape(-1).shape[0]`, `output = input_ids.new_empty((num_tokens, self.embedding_dim), dtype=self.ngram_embedding.torch_dtype)`, `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, output, self.layer_name)`, `return output` (10 of the 19 lines are the load-bearing ConstraintViolationError comment) |\n| W5a | **`:477-490`** | `load_weights` weight_scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(...)` |\n| W5b | **`:517-527`** (+ `:533`, `:535`, `:538-539`) | `load_weights` shard-drop → `embedding.weights_streamed = True; loaded.add(...); continue`, plus `stock_embedding = cast(VocabParallelEmbedding, embedding)` and the three renamed reads; also the hoist of `embedding = self.ngram_embedding` to `:461` |\n\nThe campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\n\n**`nvidia/model.py` — three sites:**\n- `:12` `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`\n- `:77` `from . import ple_mmap`\n- `:812-817` and `:1011-1016` — after `loader.load_weights(...)`: `if ple_mmap.enabled(): ple_mmap.build_tables(self.model_config, get_current_vllm_config().compilation_config)`; `return loaded`\n\nMeasured: `diff -u vllm-pr53896/.../nvidia/model.py vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia/model.py` = **+14/−12**, of which 12 removed + 2 added are upstream `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` drift; the mmap portion is **+12 net**. [M] `amd/model.py` differs from `nvidia/model.py` by *exactly and only these four hunks* — verified: `diff -u amd/model.py nvidia/model.py` produces 4 hunks, all mmap. [M]\n\n### Imports / env checks / guards the port must mirror\n1. `from . import ple_mmap` — but `ple_mmap.py` lives in `nvidia/`. Two options: **(a)** `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` in both `amd/ple_layer.py` and `amd/model.py` — safe, because `nvidia/__init__.py` is an SPDX-only stub (3 lines, no re-exports) [S], so this pulls in *only* `ple_mmap.py` and none of the CUDA-heavy nvidia modules; **(b)** relocate to `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py` alongside `common/ple.py` and update both trees + all `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` imports (the test file imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap` at `:27` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.model` at `:26`). (b) is the cleaner upstream shape; (a) is the smaller diff.\n2. `get_current_vllm_config` — **already imported** in `amd/ple_layer.py:12` (`from vllm.config import CacheConfig, ModelConfig, VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`) [S]. Must be *added* to `amd/model.py:12` (currently `from vllm.config import VllmConfig`) [S].\n3. `from typing import cast` — must be added to `amd/ple_layer.py` (absent; nvidia has it at `:7`) [S].\n4. Env checks: exactly one, `ple_mmap.enabled()` — three call sites, mirroring `nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`, `nvidia/model.py:813`, `nvidia/model.py:1012`. The three tuning vars are read inside `ple_mmap.py` (`:906`, `:907`, `:913`) and need no per-tree wiring.\n5. Guards: `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for`, both at construction (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:284-287`), unchanged — no AMD variant.\n6. **Op registration**: none needed. `direct_register_custom_op` fires at `ple_mmap.py:981-986` on first import of the module, under `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == \"CUDA\"` (`rocm.py:502`). The CPU double-registration guard at `:993-994` also behaves correctly on ROCm.\n7. **`splitting_ops`**: already correct — `vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` is in `_attention_ops` unconditionally (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`), copied into `splitting_ops` by `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` (`:1152-1161`).\n8. The AMD-only op `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:1217-1241`) stays for the env-off path; it must **not** be reused for the mmap path (not in `_attention_ops`, so not a split boundary, so it would run inside the capture).\n\n### Size, honestly measured\n- `amd/model.py`: **+12** lines.\n- `amd/pl