{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, I'll read moe-dispatch.md, and then run-smoke.sh to check the style. I'll also check other scripts for conventions (host tooling, receipt writing). The deliverables for the task are:\n- scripts/make-proxy.sh — run it inside the container, build a synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy: same architecture identifier (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM), a few layers, 8 experts with block-FP8 weight_block_size [128,128], and a small lookup table using the shard tensor naming that the mmap path expects (`layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{j}.weight`), reusing the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout when possible.\n- scripts/run-proxy.sh — serve single-node with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution, one greedy completion, assert finite logits via the API, clean shutdown, write out results/receipts/proxy.json.\n\nNote that the task says \"eager execution,\" but the guard rejects eager (guard 2: enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture; VLLM_COMPILE mode is required). Hmm. Wait — the task goal says \"with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" But spec P10 says \"The table path's own guard requires piecewise capture with the mmap op as a split boundary and rejects bare eager — the first boot runs that accepted mode.\" This is a contradiction. The brief says \"before modifying code, read the cited spec section and style reference.\" Spec claim 4.1: \"The proxy checkpoint is served single-node with the mmap path enabled → the proxy receipt records finite output and clean shutdown.\" P10 takes precedence: the first boot runs in the accepted mode, i.e., piecewise (`-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`), not eager. The task goal's phrasing \"eager execution\" seems to contradict the spec, but spec P10 is the authoritative ruling and is included in readFirst. The guard `check_cudagraph_safety` explicitly rejects enforce-eager. Therefore, when running with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, we must not use --enforce-eager; we must use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\n\nHmm, but wait: the task text says \"eager execution.\" Let me re-read the task goal: \"run-proxy.sh serves it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" This might be task-authoring shorthand that gets overridden by P10. Also, the deliveredBehaviors say: \"The accepted kernel path and the ported table path run on this GPU before real weight bytes are loaded.\" Since the brief says \"before modifying code, read the cited spec section\" and the spec is authoritative, I'll follow P10 (piecewise, not eager) and note this point. Actually, to be safe, should I try eager first? No — the guard literally rejects eager at build time. The script will fail. The correct implementation uses PIECEWISE. I'll implement with PIECEWISE and add a comment explaining why (spec P10, the fork's guard).\n\nWait, actually, is this guard in the fork? The fork's admission patch and table port are separate campaign tasks (fork-engineering completed in S1). The fork (mecattaf/vllm branch flashnext) has a table port that mirrors the guard. So running with --enforce-eager will raise a RuntimeError. So run-proxy.sh must use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\n\nNext, I'll check the container setup: container/build.sh, where the fork checkout lives inside the container, the tests/models/ scaffolding (in the fork, tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py builds fixtures: `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). make-proxy.sh should reuse that fixture-building code when possible.\n\nLet me read moe-dispatch.md and run-smoke.sh, and look at the container/ directory.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_23fcd3a422564f3d845feb6e","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/scripts/run-smoke.sh"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call