{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the conventions. To check how the container is invoked, let's also look at container/build.sh and stage-weights.sh (does run-smoke.sh use podman run with a bind mount?). run-smoke.sh directly executes `podman run --rm --device ... $IMAGE python3 - <<'PY'`.\n\nLet's check container/build.sh to confirm the fork checkout path on the host side and whether /opt/vllm is bind-mounted. Also, let's check how run-smoke mounts things. run-smoke.sh only mounts results. make-proxy.sh is executed inside the container and builds the checkpoint into /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. run-proxy.sh serves from that path within the container — therefore run-proxy.sh needs to either bind-mount /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy from the host, or else run-proxy needs to build inside the same container. The task says \"make-proxy.sh is executed inside the container and builds a small synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\" and \"run-proxy.sh serves it from the container on a single node.\" So both are executed inside the container. If the container is run with `podman run --rm`, /var/tmp doesn't persist across separate containers. Options: bind-mount a host directory to /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy in both. That's the natural approach: both scripts bind-mount a host-side directory (e.g., under results, or $FN_STATE_DIR?) — but the boundary says we only touch scripts and tests. Bind-mounting a host path is fine. E.g., use `$REPO_ROOT/results/proxy-checkpoint`, or use a host path like ${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy}. Hmm, results/ is outside the conflict domain, but mounting it isn't \"touching\" it... actually, writing a checkpoint into results/ via bind mount would create a file inside results/ at runtime, but not inside git. The boundary governs the commit paths. Still, better to default the proxy dir to a host state dir. Let's design:\n\nmake-proxy.sh:\n- Runs on the host? No — \"executed inside the container.\" But how does the checkpoint persist to run-proxy.sh? It could bind-mount a host directory as /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. Let's write it so the host side orchestrates: `podman run ... -v \"$PROXY_HOST_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\" $IMAGE python3 - <<'PY'`, where PROXY_HOST_DIR defaults to \"$FN_STATE_DIR/proxy-checkpoint\" or \"$REPO_ROOT/results/proxy\" — hmm, results/ is for receipts. Let's default to `${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint}`.\n\nActually, wait — the script should be self-contained like run-smoke.sh: host-side bash orchestrates the podman run with a python heredoc. make-proxy.sh \"is executed inside the container and builds a tiny synthetic checkpoint\" — meaning the checkpoint-building logic executes inside the container. Good.\n\nReuse of fork test scaffolding: the fork checkout is at /opt/vllm inside the container. tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py has fixture builders that write safetensors with shard names `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors` and use the MmapPleTable/MmapNgramEmbedding scaffolding. We don't have the fork tree at hand (it gets cloned at image build time). We can't inspect those test files directly... or can we? Is there a fork checkout on the host? Let's check container/build.sh to see if it bind-mounts a local checkout.\n\nAlso, we should identify the architecture identifier: \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" (per the registry check in run-smoke.sh) or \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\". The smoke test checks for \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" inside archs. The actual checkpoint's config uses either ForCausalLM or ConditionalGeneration. According to evidence, the registry maps both. In the proxy's config.json, architectures should match the workload's. From the dossier, the model is registered under both names. I don't know exactly which one the actual checkpoint uses. The proxy needs \"the same architecture identifier as the workload.\" Which is it? The smoke receipt checks model_arch_registered for \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\". That suggests the workload is ConditionalGeneration. Let's grep the repo for hints (README, handoff, host config, the catalog patch).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a3f4c00ec8f54bc5bae37273","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -rn \"Qwen4Exp\" --include=\"*\" -l | grep -v evidence | head && grep -rn \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\\|Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\" README.md handoff host scripts container 2>/dev/null | head"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e16d74fd356448098c95eb2c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n '1,80p' container/build.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":7477,"output":1147,"cacheRead":41984,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":955,"totalTokens":50608,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991857228,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0dd9b483-1baa-4647-ac49-80e332adf354","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
