{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"This is substantial. Key points for the proxy work:\n\n1. In the fork checkout, there's `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, and the fixtures build safetensors with shard tensors named `layers.{layer_idx}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{shard_index:05d}.weight`. The fork is at /opt/vllm inside the container, and tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py may have reusable fixture helpers. Let me check the fixture structure in the upstream test file — I don't have the fork tree locally, but I can inspect the relevant parts of patches 0009/0010 (they touch the import paths in tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py). The test file is about 2048 lines, and the fixture writes out `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors` (from ple-54129.md's §3).\n\nRather than depending on the exact contents of the fork's tests (which I can't read), make-proxy.sh should use fork's test scaffolding if it exists, and fall back gracefully if it doesn't. Good approach: inside the container, try importing the fixture helper from the fork's test file (e.g. `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` — add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path and import the module, calling its fixture-building function). However, that file is pytest-based, and fixtures might not be directly callable.\n\nLet me look at patches 0009/0010 and check the actual test file content that's in the patch context — it might reveal the fixture function name and structure."}}
