{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The fork checkout inside the container contains `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which has fixture code that builds the shard safetensors (`model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`), and also contains the newly added `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py`. I can't verify the exact function names inside those files from here. The task says \"whenever possible, reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ within the fork checkout, rather than inventing your own generator.\"\n\nA robust approach for make-proxy.sh: write the generator as follows:\n1. Look for the fork's test file `/opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` and introspect it for a fixture-building helper (e.g., a function that writes out shard safetensors). Since I can't read the fork tree, the cleanest and most honest way to \"reuse\" is to import the fork's `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap` module and, if available, its test scaffolding module. If a callable fixture is present, use it; otherwise, use my own minimal writer that uses the same naming.\n\nActually, a better approach: the test file probably defines a helper like `write_shard_file(...)` at module scope. I can inspect it at runtime: import the module (the tests directory needs to be on sys.path), find the callable that creates the fixture checkpoint, and call it with my own geometry. But this is fragile.\n\nAn alternative, more robust design: import the module and use `getattr` against several candidate names to reuse the fork scaffolding. If none of them exist, fall back to a self-contained writer that reproduces the exact shard naming from the mmap path (`_SHARD_RE = layers.(\\d+).ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_(\\d+).weight$`). In addition, include the tensors the loader expects: `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (FP8 table → global scale), plus the rest of the model's weights (embeddings, qkv, experts, and so on).\n\nActually — what does the proxy model need? vLLM's loader will try to load the model from the checkpoint. With mmap enabled, the PLE shard tensors are discarded during load (weights_streamed), and weight_scale is retained. Everything else must be present: the complete model weights for the architecture: Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration with, say, num_hidden_layers=4, hidden_size small, num_experts=8, MoE layers, vocab around 512, etc. The config.json must be shaped like a qwen4_exp config (a transformers_utils config exists in-tree: vllm/transformers_utils/configs/qwen4_exp.py). The geometry is config-driven (ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim...).\n\nSince we're synthesizing, the config fields must include everything the model class reads. That's complex. But wait — this script can't be tested here anyway (no GPU, no container). The acceptance criteria are only `bash -n` syntax check + grep for the receipt paths. This is a spec-build campaign where execution happens later on the pair (coordinator/worker). So I should write carefully, honest, well-documented scripts that follow the established patterns and degrade with a loud failure into a fail receipt (F.12: failures are recorded and never silently skipped).\n\nKey design decisions:\n\n1. How does the script reuse the fork's test scaffolding? Inside the container, /opt/vllm is the fork checkout with tests/. The test file `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` writes fixture checkpoints. Inside make-proxy.sh's python heredoc, I'll do the following:\n   - Add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path\n   - Import `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` and look for the fixture writer. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's actually in test_ple_mmap.py. From ple-54129.md: \"In the test fixtures each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`).\" And \"A contract test `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries` runs actual boundary rows through MmapPleTable.gather.\" So the fixture writer is around lines 112–124, and it's probably a helper function, or part of a pytest fixture that writes out a safetensors file containing a shard tensor named `layers.{layer_idx}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{shard_index:05d}.weight`.\n\nReuse approach: import the module and introspect: find module-level functions and call one that constructs a fixture checkpoint given a directory and geometry. Too fragile. Better: reuse what we can name — the fork's `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap` (the actual runtime module: shard-naming regex, MmapPleTable). And for the shard files, import the test module and reuse the writer if it exists; otherwise, fall back to writing with the exact regex-conforming naming, and honestly verify that the names match `_SHARD_RE` from the actual ple_mmap module. That's honest scaffolding reuse: validate my writer against the fork's own `_SHARD_RE` and `discover_shards`.\n\nAlso, the fork's test file has a new AMD file test_ple_mmap_amd.py (352 lines) from patch 0010 — \"GPU-less pins\" — not a fixture writer.\n\nSince we can't read the fork tree, the runtime reuse attempt with graceful fallback is the right approach. Write the make-proxy generator as follows:\n1. Attempt to reuse the fork's fixture writer: import the test module, look for a callable like `write_fixture`/`build_fixture`/a pytest fixture's `.func`? Too magical.\n\nAlternative honest approach: parse the test file for safetensors-writing logic and import it if importable, and at runtime check whether the generated checkpoint passes the fork's own validation (`ple_mmap.validate_shards_for` / `discover_shards` / `_attach_table`) — that's the strongest form of reuse: the fork's own contract test validates my checkpoint. Let's do this:\n\nmake-proxy.sh (in-container python):\n- Build config.json (same architectures id as the workload: \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" — the smoke test checks this arch; the workload is the vendor FP8 release; the registry registers both ForCausalLM and ForConditionalGeneration; the smoke receipt asserts registration of ConditionalGeneration, suggesting the workload uses that).\n\nHmm wait — which one does the workload use? run-smoke.sh checks `\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" in archs` as the \"registered architectures identifier.\" Q3Next-style models: these models use Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration (like qwen3_next's conditional generation). I'll use \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration.\"\n\n- num_hidden_layers: a few (4, mixing linear and MoE layers per the model's layer pattern — Qwen4Exp/Qwen3Next use a pattern of interleaved full attention and MoE layers with linear_attention... the config needs a layer_types list or decoders pattern). The exact config schema is unknown. This is the risky part, but I can do my best: read the config class fields at runtime: `from vllm.transformers_utils.configs.qwen4_exp import ...` hmm, I don't know the module path. I can introspect at runtime: load the config class and use defaults where possible.\n\nActually, a more robust approach: construct the config minimally but completely with plausible fields, and let vLLM fail loudly if it's wrong — but the campaign runs unattended overnight, and a wrong config means the proxy fails and gets recorded. I want to maximize the chance it works.\n\nLet me search the repo for evidence of the actual config fields. There might be config excerpts in the evidence files (ds4-vllm-manifest.md or the kyuz0 dossier). Let me grep."}}
