ecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:644:  `ple_embed_dim`, `make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by` — read at\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:148:- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:153:**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:308:embeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:323:embeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:329:Therefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:344:The campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:171:The exact number of QSA layers is **UNDETERMINED** — it is `layer_types == \"full_attention\"`\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:291:`num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` (`:239-244`). **[S]**\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:295:config.num_experts_per_tok, ...)` at `:215-235` and calls it at `:256-258`. **[S]** So: **vLLM\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:312:`num_experts_per_tok = 10` routes fine.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:510:2. **`num_experts_per_tok = 10`.** Not present anywhere in the PR —\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:511:   `grep -rn \"num_experts_per_tok\"` over `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/` and\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:516:   depends on the checkpoint's `layer_types`; not available locally.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/tally-fit-analysis.md:38:- The HF tensor name `ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight` and GGUF `per_layer_token_embd.weight` are safe (no lexicon hit). `strix-halo-llamacpp` is NOT safe (llama). `EngramHalo.cpp` is safe.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991968439}}
