block scale** (numerically wrong) and doubling its bytes (23.9→47.7 GiB/rank — which breaks the 96 GiB aperture arithmetic). [`evidence/ple-54129.md`, `evidence/moe-dispatch.md` §5] | **AMD FP8-PLE support is enablement, not headroom** — contrary to Addendum B.1. The mmap port (which is FP8-native and skips the resident table entirely) is the *cheapest correct* fix, not a luxury. |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:137:### 2.3 vLLM PR #54129 (Trosfy, `ple-mmap-upstream`) — the engram-on-SSD mechanism\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:139:`ple_mmap.py` (1010 lines, nvidia-tree-only today): `np.memmap` views over the\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:153:(W1–W5b, exactly pinned in [`evidence/ple-54129.md`] §\"Exact port specification\") into\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:156:well), plus relocating `ple_mmap.py` to `common/` (the clean upstream shape). One\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:159:(`NULL_BLOCK_ID` remaps, plus a fresh `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522`)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:191:  at boot, and inject MES firmware; upstream's own example imports it, which is the\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:273:| PM QoS + tripwire | `od /dev/cpu_dma_latency` both ends; tripwire timer armed | systemctl stop (drill exists) | 200-sample RTT within budget (committed threshold 200 µs) |\n"}],"details":{}}}
