{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There's a partial config snapshot on the NAS. The proxy script is intended to run on a coordinator that has the staged workload checkpoint, but per spec: run the proxy \"before touching the real weights\" — the proxy checkpoint is synthetic, so we can't copy from the actual config.json (which might not even be staged yet… actually, staging runs alongside the container build, and in S2's ordering, proxy comes after smoke; the weights staging might already be done). Hmm — \"before loading the actual weight bytes\" means don't load the actual weights, but reading config.json to mirror the architecture is fine, and in fact it's the honest thing to do. Best design: make-proxy.sh uses the staged workload's config.json (/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8/config.json) if it exists, shrinking the geometry down to the bare minimum (reducing num_hidden_layers, vocab, experts, etc.), and if not, falls back to a baked-in config template reflecting the evidence snapshot.\n\nHowever, note: this is a synthetic checkpoint, and what matters is the \"same architecture identifier.\" Reusing the workload config is the most faithful. That said, shrinking means changing the fields — e.g. expert count 512→8, weight_block_size [128,128] must divide the expert shapes: intermediate sizes need to be multiples of 128. Also hidden 2560, head_dim 256… but for the proxy make it small while keeping the divisibility intact.\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust approach for the proxy: define our own small geometry with the exact same config schema. Risk: I don't know the full config schema (the 72 KB config.json is huge — it includes things like indexer settings, etc.). The fork has `vllm/transformers_utils/configs/qwen4_exp.py` which defines the config class with defaults. At runtime, the best approach is: load the config class from the fork, instantiate it with overrides, and dump. This gives us the full set of fields with the correct defaults. And use the actual config.json as a template if it exists.\n\nRuntime-driven design of make-proxy.sh (in-container python):\n1. Try `from vllm.transformers_utils.configs.qwen4_exp import Qwen4ExpConfig` (guessing the name; wrap in try/except and introspect the module to look for a config class). Better: after `from transformers import AutoConfig`, do `AutoConfig.from_pretrained(model_path)`? For synthesis: load the staged workload config if available (`FN_MODEL_DIR/config.json` — mount it read-only), then shrink. Otherwise build from defaults.\n\n2. Geometry: num_hidden_layers=8? \"a handful of layers.\" The real model has MoE on some layers (decoder_sparse_step?). Per the qsa evidence: `num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` — this is for qwen3_next's MoE placement. For the proxy, all layers MoE? The admission kernel path must execute: fused-MoE block-FP8 kernel. Make all layers MoE (num_experts=8, num_experts_per_tok=2, moe_intermediate_size a multiple of 128, e.g. 256) and interleave the linear_attention/full_attention pattern following the layer_types pattern (3 linear + 1 full per group).\n\nHmm, but there's also QSA's short-convolution stuff and such. The amd model includes the QSA indexer (indexer_budget). A proxy with full_attention layers triggers the QSA indexer path. That's fine — small.\n\nI'm going too deep into the unknowns here. The script should be data-driven: take the workload's config.json (staged at /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8/config.json, mounted read-only into the make-proxy container) and programmatically shrink it:\n\n- architectures unchanged\n- num_hidden_layers → e.g. 8 (respecting the full_attention_interval pattern)\n- num_experts → 8, num_experts_per_tok → 2 (keep it ≤8)\n- vocab_size → small (e.g. 2048); head_dim etc. kept as-is? hidden_size 2560 is small enough. Keeping the actual hidden/head dimensions preserves weight-name/dimension compatibility with the config class's expectations… the weights we synthesize derive from the config anyway.\n\nThe actual weights need to be generated with the correct names for this architecture. This is the hardest part: writing out a complete set of weight tensors with the correct names for a vLLM model implementation. The names must match what the model's load_weights expects (stacked qkv, etc.). A hand-written generic generator is error-prone.\n\nBetter idea — reuse the fork's test scaffolding even more deeply: the fork's tests directory probably contains a test that constructs a tiny Qwen4Exp model (e.g. tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple.py, test_config.py). But constructing the weights still requires matching names.\n\nAnother reliable approach that avoids writing out per-tensor names: use the fork's own model class to initialize the model in vLLM and save its state_dict? Inside the container, under vllm's model config, instantiate the model on meta/CPU with random weights, then save the state_dict with HF-style names via a weight map. Does vLLM's model have `hf_to_vllm_mapper`? Newer vLLM has WeightsMapper. The checkpoint tensor names are HF names; the vLLM model parameter names are different. The model file typically defines `hf_to_vllm_mapper = WeightsMapper(...)`. So: build the vLLM model class with random init (on CPU), take the reverse mapping of the mapper, and save it… that's still fragile.\n\nHonestly, since I can't test any of this, the best approach is to make the generator introspection-driven and fall back gracefully:\n\nPlan for make-proxy.sh (python in the container):\n1. Resolve the base config: read /models/config.json if mounted (read-only mount from FN_MODEL_DIR), else fall back to a built-in minimal config dict derived from evidence (architectures, model_type qwen4_exp, layer_types pattern, etc.).\n2. Shrink: layers → N (FN_PROXY_LAYERS, default 8), experts → 8, keep block size 128×128, quantization_config is fp8 with weight_block_size [128,128] (only experts? the actual checkpoint's ignore list leaves only mlp.experts.* as quantized). To ensure the admission path executes, the routed experts need to be quantized with block fp8: quantization_config with modules_to_not_convert listing everything except experts… the simplest is: quantization_config {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]} with no modules_to_not_convert → all linears get Fp8LinearMethod (does it work on gfx1151 via the 0012 admission? TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel is allowed). This exercises both the admitted linear and MoE paths. But wait — F.6/F.9… it's fine.\n\n   Actually, the real checkpoint has 943 modules_to_not_convert — everything except experts. Keeping the proxy simpler: quantize only the experts, mirroring the workload. Can I copy the actual quantization_config as-is? Its modules_to_not_convert refers to named layers (layer indices!) and those change when shrinking. So synthesize: modules_to_not_convert = all linear layer names except mlp.experts. Generating the ignore list requires enumerating per-layer names — annoying but doable: e.g. for each layer, add entries like model.layers.{i}.mlp.linear_attn... Hmm, I don't know the exact submodule names.\n\n   Simpler: quantize everything (no ignore list). All linears + experts go through Fp8 methods with block scales. Block size 128×128 requires each weight dim ≥128 and divisible… actually weight_block_size requires that the dims be divisible by the block size for the per-block scales; if not divisible vLLM pads. hidden 2560, intermediate are multiples of 128. Make all sizes multiples of 128.\n\n3. Weight generation: introspection-driven via the fork:\n   - Try: `from vllm.model_executor.models.qwen3_next import ...` no wait — the correct way: build the vLLM model? Too heavy.\n\n   Realistic fallback: write out the tensors under names vLLM's load_weights maps from HF names. For the qwen3_next family, HF checkpoint names look like `model.layers.0.self_attn.qkv_proj.weight`? No, HF stores `q_proj/k_proj/v_proj` separately and vLLM stacks them with the mapper. I really can't derive this with certainty.\n\n   The most honest thing: attempt reuse of the fork's own scaffolding at runtime: look under /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/ for fixture builders that generate a tiny model checkpoint (test_ple.py (410 lines), test_config.py (493), test_ple_mmap.py (2048) exist in the base — per the line count in qsa-53896.md's table: \"test_qsa_pre_indexer.py 297, test_config.py 493, test_ple.py 410, test_hc_ops.py 94\"). Import each module, hunt for functions/fixtures that write out safetensors checkpoints, or construct a model config + weights, and call them with the proxy directory. If nothing exists, use my own minimal writer that deterministically constructs tensors from the config with the standard qwen3-next HF naming pattern and validates the result via the fork's own load path (the ultimate validation is in run-proxy.sh serving anyway).\n\nWait — actually, there's a much more powerful reuse path I overlooked: use vLLM itself to do the HF-name generation. Hmm.\n\nOr: build the model with HF transformers? Not installed / model not supported.\n\nLet me reconsider: what does vLLM require for load? Its `load_weights` iterates over the checkpoint tensors, maps names, and copies them. If a tensor is missing, many implementations throw an exception at the end about missing weights… typically `params_dict` keys not loaded trigger an error? vLLM's qwen load_weights does not validate completeness (it only loads what's present; missing parameters remain randomly initialized — actually, on `ModelLoader.load_model`, after load_weights, missing keys are… vLLM doesn't strictly validate; some models assert). In fact, does vLLM throw on missing weights? No — vLLM is famously lenient; it only loads present tensors (that's why LoRA etc. works… no). Actually recent vLLM has `weight_loader` that throws on unmatched names, and at the end of the loader there's a check: `if not loaded: error`. Hmm, vllm's default_loader checks that each weight in the model got loaded: \"Some weights are not used in the checkpoint\"? It logs \"Weights loaded from ...\". In v1, `load_weights` returning a set of loaded params is new API. When checkpoint params are not consumed, models throw (the \"There is no module or parameter named\" error the AMD loader raises for unknown names — per moe-dispatch). Unknown checkpoint name → error. Missing model parameters → usually no error? For AutoWeightsLoader, `load_weights` throws on unmatched checkpoint names, but missing model parameters remain at random init. For the proxy (synthetic anyway), random init for some tensors is tolerable as long as logits are finite… but NaN risk with random fp8 scales. Safer to provide everything.\n\nGiven the uncertainties, writing a fully correct weight generator by hand without seeing the tree is really not possible. The correct engineering move under the instruction \"reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout as much as possible instead of inventing a generator\" is to have make-proxy.sh drive the fork's scaffolding at runtime via introspection. The campaign will run it on the real machine where the fork checkout exists. So let me design the generator like this:\n\nStage A (reuse attempt): For each candidate module under /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp (test_ple_mmap.py, test_ple.py, test_config.py, test_ple_mmap_amd.py, and the rest): import it, and hunt for (in priority order):\n  1. A module-level callable that returns/writes a full checkpoint (regex on name: `make|build|write|create|fixture|ckpt|checkpoint`), accepting a directory argument.\n  2. The shard writer specifically for PLE: a function that writes `shard_*.weight` safetensors — reuse for the table part.\nRecord which scaffolding we reused in the receipt (data[\"scaffolding_reused\"]).\n\nStage B (always): Write the checkpoint ourselves using config-driven synthesis with qwen3-next-family HF tensor naming, block-FP8 quantize the experts (fp8 weight + weight_scale_inv), provide the PLE shard tensors per `_SHARD_RE` naming + `ngram_embedding.weight_scale`, and validate:\n  - Header parse via the fork's ple_mmap module: `discover_shards(model_path)` finds the shards; `_attach_table`/`validate_shards_for` accepts the geometry (if it can run GPU-less on CPU).\n  - All safetensors are parseable.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. I might be overcomplicating. Let me look at what the actual checkpoint's HF names look like — maybe there's a list in the evidence (ds4-vllm-manifest is about DeepSeek; but the flashnext checkpoint = Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 — hmm per moe-dispatch UNDETERMINED: \"actual checkpoint tensor name list for Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 (doesn't exist on this box)\"). So even the evidence author didn't have it. But there were 38 shards on the NAS… no index.\n\nOK, another angle: vLLM's qwen3_next.py load_weights handles stacked params with `stacked_params_mapping` (qkv_proj ← q_proj/k_proj/v_proj etc.), and HF-style names are `model.layers.{i}.self_attn.q_proj.weight`… for Qwen3Next HF format: from the qwen3_next HF checkpoint, names are: `model.layers.N.self_attn.q_proj.{weight, q_norm/k_norm}`, `gate_up_proj`? HF splits it into gate_proj/up_proj. And Qwen4Exp has additional PLE/QSA tensors: `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_M.weight` (from _SHARD_RE: `layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight` — probably with `model.` prefix? The regex says `layers\\.` with no leading anchor — re.search, not fullmatch? discover_shards matches tensor names against the regex; the regex starts with `layers\\.` — search matches anywhere if no ^, so `model.layers.0.ple...` matches. Good, safe either way.)\n\nAlso `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` per loader interception name: the loader checks `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — that's the checkpoint-side name after prefix stripping inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights. The full checkpoint name is something like `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`. Hmm, inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding's load_weights, is the name relative? moe-dispatch says \"The checkpoint scale tensor is named `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (per explicit check in NVIDIA loader nvidia/ple_layer.py:478-480).\" That check is `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — probably after the model strips the `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.` prefix. Write both: a scale tensor per PLE layer with the full path `model.layers.{N}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`.\n\nAnd the main vocab embedding: `model.embed_tokens.weight`, lm_head: `lm_head.weight`, final norm `model.norm.weight`. Plus QSA indexer tensors for full-attention layers (indexer_budget 2048): qwen3_next's sparse attention has `self_attn.indexer` with wqz/… I'm getting out of my depth on exact names again.\n\nDecision: I'll write make-proxy.sh as an introspection-driven tool with a clear hierarchy:\n\n1. Primary: Fork scaffolding reuse — programmatically discover the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp/ checkpoint builders, and if one builds a full model checkpoint, drive it with proxy geometry.\n2. Fallback: My own config-driven writer that covers the tensor families I know of (embeddings, norms, attention qkv+o, MLP gate/up/down or MoE experts+gate+shared expert, PLE shards + scale). For unknown extra parameters the model requires, the vLLM loader typically doesn't fail on model-side missing keys (leaves random init) — record the risk honestly. Actually wait, does it fail? Let me think about AutoWeightsLoader: it iterates checkpoint weights and routes them; model params that didn't receive weights… in vLLM v1, after load_weights, the loader does… not validate completeness (it's the checkpoint that's authoritative). Yeah — vLLM doesn't error on missing checkpoint keys; parameters remain at their init values (that's how the \"random init\" debugging works). Since we init with a controlled seed and sane values (small uniform + proper norms), logits will be finite.\n\nAlso fp8: for block-fp8 experts, weights are `mlp.experts.{e}.{gate,up,down}_proj.weight` (fp8 e4m3) + `.weight_scale_inv` (fp32, shape [ceil(n/128), ceil(k/128)]). vLLM's Fp8 block quant checkpoint convention: `weight_scale_inv`. For the linear layers that get quantized: same pattern. And the top-level quantization_config in config.json: {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]}.\n\nTo exercise only the MoE admission (UNKNOWN-1: \"numerical sanity of fused mixture block-FP8 kernel\") — and the linear twin is also admitted via 0012 (FN_FP8_LINEAR) — quantize all linears too. Both get exercised; that's the goal of \"admitted kernel path.\"\n\nHmm wait, actually one consideration: should I mirror the workload (modules_to_not_convert, only experts quantized)? That requires the ignore list; can I generate it programmatically? Can't enumerate reliably. Quantize everything: simpler and it exercises both admissions. The workload's own quantization choice is not part of the proxy contract — \"same architecture identifier and expert format\" is the contract (vocabulary section of the spec): \"small synthetic checkpoint with same architecture identifier and expert format.\" Expert format = block-FP8 with [128,128]. Quantizing the dense layers too does not violate this.\n\nBut note: if the dense layers are block-FP8 quantized, the embedding/lm_head are also typically excluded (embeddings can't be fp8 in vLLM's Fp8Config? Fp8Config's ignored_layers usually auto-adds embed/lm_head? In Fp8Config, `modules_to_not_convert` defaults to None… if None, it quantizes every Linear, including lm_head? vLLM handles lm_head specially (unquantized if no quant). VocabParallelEmbedding for embed_tokens: quant method from Fp8Config → Fp8Config.get_quant_method for embedding returns UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod unless is_checkpoint_fp8_serialized… embeddings stay bf16. lm_head is ParallelLMHead — unquantized. OK, quantizing everything is fine: linears take Fp8LinearMethod, embeddings stay as-is.\n\nNow the runtime of run-proxy.sh:\n- Serve the proxy checkpoint single-node inside the container: `podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host -v results:/results -v $PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy -p port $IMAGE ...`.\n- Serve with `vllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy --served-model-name flashnext-proxy --port ...`.\n- With VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1. Task says \"eager execution\" but the fork's guard rejects --enforce-eager (\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\") and rejects FULL graphs; only PIECEWISE with the splitting op is allowed. Spec P10: \"The table path's own guards require piecewise capture with mmap op as splitting boundary and reject raw eager — first light runs in this sanctioned mode.\" So use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and note in the script's comments that the worklist's phrasing \"eager\" is superseded by the fork guard and spec P10. This is the correct reading: the spec section is authoritative (\"readFirst\" instruction). Also fn-env.sh says \"safe with --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs\" — hmm, that comment refers to the allocator config; but run-tp2.sh serves the actual model via fn-cluster-up.sh… let me check fn-cluster-up.sh to see what serve args it uses — that's the sanctioned-mode reference."}}
