loaded: error`. Hmm, vllm's default_loader checks that each weight in the model got loaded: \"Some weights are not used in the checkpoint\"? It logs \"Weights loaded from ...\". In v1, `load_weights` returning a set of loaded params is new API. When checkpoint params are not consumed, models throw (the \"There is no module or parameter named\" error the AMD loader raises for unknown names — per moe-dispatch). Unknown checkpoint name → error. Missing model parameters → usually no error? For AutoWeightsLoader, `load_weights` throws on unmatched checkpoint names, but missing model parameters remain at random init. For the proxy (synthetic anyway), random init for some tensors is tolerable as long as logits are finite… but NaN risk with random fp8 scales. Safer to provide everything.\n\nGiven the uncertainties, writing a fully correct weight generator by hand without seeing the tree is really not possible. The correct engineering move under the instruction \"reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout as much as possible instead of inventing a generator\" is to have make-proxy.sh drive the fork's scaffolding at runtime via introspection. The campaign will run it on the real machine where the fork checkout exists. So let me design the generator like this:\n\nStage A (reuse attempt): For each candidate module under /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp (test_ple_mmap.py, test_ple.py, test_config.py, test_ple_mmap_amd.py, and the rest): import it, and hunt for (in priority order):\n  1. A module-level callable that returns/writes a full checkpoint (regex on name: `make|build|write|create|fixture|ckpt|checkpoint`), accepting a directory argument.\n  2. The shard writer specifically for PLE: a function that writes `shard_*.weight` safetensors — reuse for the table part.\nRecord which scaffolding we reused in the receipt (data[\"scaffolding_reused\"]).\n\nStage B (always): Write the checkpoint ourselves using config-driven synthesis with qwen3-next-family HF tensor naming, block-FP8 quantize the experts (fp8 weight + weight_scale_inv), provide the PLE shard tensors per `_SHARD_RE` naming + `ngram_embedding.weight_scale`, and validate:\n  - Header parse via the fork's ple_mmap module: `discover_shards(model_path)` finds the shards; `_attach_table`/`validate_shards_for` accepts the geometry (if it can run GPU-less on CPU).\n  - All safetensors are parseable.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. I might be overcomplicating. Let me look at what the actual checkpoint's HF names look like — maybe there's a list in the evidence (ds4-vllm-manifest is about DeepSeek; but the flashnext checkpoint = Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 — hmm per moe-dispatch UNDETERMINED: \"actual checkpoint tensor name list for Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 (doesn't exist on this box)\"). So even the evidence author didn't have it. But there were 38 shards on the NAS… no index.\n\nOK, another angle: vLLM's qwen3_next.py load_weights handles stacked params with `stacked_params_mapping` (qkv_proj ← q_proj/k_proj/v_proj etc.), and HF-style names are `model.layers.{i}.self_attn.q_proj.weight`… for Qwen3Next HF format: from the qwen3_next HF checkpoint, names are: `model.layers.N.self_attn.q_proj.{weight, q_norm/k_norm}`, `gate_up_proj`? HF splits it into gate_proj/up_proj. And Qwen4Exp has additional PLE/QSA tensors: `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_M.weight` (from _SHARD_RE: `layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight` — probably with `model.` prefix? The regex says `layers\\.` with no leading anchor — re.search, not fullmatch? discover_shards matches tensor names against the regex; the regex starts with `layers\\.` — search matches anywhere if no ^, so `model.layers.0.ple...` matches. Good, safe either way.)\n\nAlso `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` per loader interception name: the loader checks `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — that's the checkpoint-side name after prefix stripping inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights. The full checkpoint name is something like `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`. Hmm, inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding's load_weights, is the name relative? moe-dispatch says \"The checkpoint scale tensor is named `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (per explicit check in NVIDIA loader nvidia/ple_layer.py:478-480).\" That check is `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — probably after the model strips the `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.` prefix. Write both: a scale tensor per PLE layer with the full path `model.layers.{N}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`.\n\nAnd the main vocab embedding: `model.embed_tokens.weight`, lm_head: `lm_head.weight`, final norm `model.norm.weight`. Plus QSA indexer tensors for full-attention layers (indexer_budget 2048): qwen3_next's sparse attention has `self_attn.indexer` with wqz/… I'm getting out of my depth on exact names again.\n\nDecision: I'll write make-proxy.sh as an introspection-driven tool with a clear hierarchy:\n\n1. Primary: Fork scaffolding reuse — programmatically discover the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp/ checkpoint builders, and if one builds a full model checkpoint, drive it with proxy geometry.\n2. Fallback: My own config-driven writer that covers the tensor families I know of (embeddings, norms, attention qkv+o, MLP gate/up/down or MoE experts+gate+shared expert, PLE shards + scale). For unknown extra parameters the model requires, the vLLM loader typically doesn't fail on model-side missing keys (leaves random init) — record the risk honestly. Actually wait, does it fail? Let me think about AutoWeightsLoader: it iterates checkpoint weights and routes them; model params that didn't receive weights… in vLLM v1, after load_weights, the loader does… not validate completeness (it's the checkpoint that's authoritative). Yeah — vLLM doesn't error on missing checkpoint keys; parameters remain at their init values (that's how the \"random init\" debugging works). Since we init with a controlled seed and sane values (small uniform + proper norms), logits will be finite.\n\nAlso fp8: for block-fp8 experts, weights are `mlp.experts.{e}.{gate,up,down}_proj.weight` (fp8 e4m3) + `.weight_scale_inv` (fp32, shape [ceil(n/128), ceil(k/128)]). vLLM's Fp8 block quant checkpoint convention: `weight_scale_inv`. For the linear layers that get quantized: same pattern. And the top-level quantization_config in config.json: {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]}.\n\nTo exercise only the MoE admission (UNKNOWN-1: \"numerical sanity of fused mixture block-FP8 kernel\") — and the linear twin is also admitted via 0012 (FN_FP8_LINEAR) — quantize all linears too. Both get exercised; that's the goal of \"admitted kernel path.\"\n\nHmm wait, actually one consideration: should I mirror the workload (modules_to_not_convert, only experts quantized)? That requires the ignore list; can I generate it programmatically? Can't enumerate reliably. Quantize everything: simpler and it exercises both admissions. The workload's own quantization choice is not part of the proxy contract — \"same architecture identifier and expert format\" is the contract (vocabulary section of the spec): \"small synthetic checkpoint with same architecture identifier and expert format.\" Expert format = block-FP8 with [128,128]. Quantizing the dense layers too does not violate this.\n\nBut note: if the dense layers are block-FP8 quantized, the embedding/lm_head are also typically excluded (embeddings can't be fp8 in vLLM's Fp8Config? Fp8Config's ignored_layers usually auto-adds embed/lm_head? In Fp8Config, `modules_to_not_convert` defaults to None… if None, it quantizes every Linear, including lm_head? vLLM handles lm_head specially (unquantized if no quant). VocabParallelEmbedding for embed_tokens: quant method from Fp8Config → Fp8Config.get_quant_method for embedding returns UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod unless is_checkpoint_fp8_serialized… embeddings stay bf16. lm_head is ParallelLMHead — unquantized. OK, quantizing everything is fine: linears take Fp8LinearMethod, embeddings stay as-is.\n\nNow the runtime of run-proxy.sh:\n- Serve the proxy checkpoint single-node inside the container: `podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host -v results:/results -v $PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy -p port $IMAGE ...`.\n- Serve with `vllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy --served-model-name flashnext-proxy --port ...`.\n- With VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1. Task says \"eager execution\" but the fork's guard rejects --enforce-eager (\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\") and rejects FULL graphs; only PIECEWISE with the splitting op is allowed. Spec P10: \"The table path's own guards require piecewise capture with mmap op as splitting boundary and reject raw eager — first light runs in this sanctioned mode.\" So use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and note in the script's comments that the worklist's phrasing \"eager\" is superseded by the fork guard and spec P10. This is the correct reading: the spec section is authoritative (\"readFirst\" instruction). Also fn-env.sh says \"safe with --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs\" — hmm, that comment refers to the allocator config; but run-tp2.sh serves the actual model via fn-cluster-up.sh… let me check fn-cluster-up.sh to see what serve args it uses — that's the sanctioned-mode reference.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a64e7108e9434cd19fc9bdfe","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -n \"serve\\|enforce\\|cudagraph\\|PIECEWISE\\|max-model\\|gpu-mem\\|port\" host/fn-cluster-up.sh | head -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3141,"output":4789,"cacheRead":61440,"cacheW