{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — fn-cluster-up.sh uses `--enforce-eager` for TP=2 serve... but when VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 (exported in fn-env.sh), the fork guard raises \"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\" at construction time. Contradiction between host-tooling (already committed) and the fork guard. Hmm. Wait, let me re-check guard #2: \"`if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — 'enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.'\"\n\nHmm, so with --enforce-eager + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, model construction throws RuntimeError. Unless the fork's admission patch changed the guard. Patch 0010 mirrors the 5 wiring sites; the W3 guard \"check_cudagraph_safety\" is mirrored unchanged (\"both cudagraph guards fire from the AMD constructor\" — test_ple_mmap_amd.py pins \"both cudagraph guards firing from the AMD constructor\"). So the guard exists on the fork: (1) FULL cudagraph rejection, (2) mode != VLLM_COMPILE rejection, (3) splitting_ops must contain the mmap op.\n\nWith --enforce-eager, compilation mode becomes DYNAMO_AS_IS / NONE → guard #2 raises. So `run-tp2.sh`'s eager serve + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 would fail at startup... unless run-tp2 isn't setting VLLM_PLE_MMAP — but fn-env.sh exports it and fn-cluster-up presumably sources fn-env. That might be a latent bug in the host-tooling task (not mine to fix; outside my boundary — fn-cluster-up.sh is under host/).\n\nMy task boundary: scripts/make-proxy.sh, scripts/run-proxy.sh, tests/. What the task goal says about run-proxy.sh: \"Serve single-node from container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" But the guard forbids eager. Tension! What to do?\n\nOptions:\na) Follow the task text literally (--enforce-eager + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1) → the serve dies at construction time due to the guard's RuntimeError → the proxy receipt fails overnight. Bad.\nb) Follow spec P10 (\"the first light runs in that permitted mode\" = piecewise capture with the mmap op as splitting boundary) → `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`, no --enforce-eager. P10 is a ratified ruling and the brief mandates reading the spec. P10 takes precedence over the task goal's loose phrasing. The proxy is literally the first light (claim 4.1 precedes 4.2). Also per P10 \"full-graph capture is morning work\" — piecewise is the permitted mode.\n\nGoing with (b), and documenting clearly in a script header comment: the worklist's \"eager execution\" phrasing is overridden by ruling P10 and the fork's own check_cudagraph_safety guard (patches 0009/0010), which explicitly rejects enforce-eager when VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1; run in the permitted piecewise mode. If the operator truly wants eager, then VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0... but claim 4.1 requires engaging the mmap path. So piecewise is the only consistent choice.\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me reconsider guard #2 more carefully. \"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\" — even with --enforce-eager, some capture happens for this model? The guard demands mode == VLLM_COMPILE. So the permitted invocation is: no --enforce-eager, `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`. The guard message #1 even says \"please pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\".\n\nAlso ROCm platform default: rocm.py:898-919 already sets compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = PIECEWISE? Per the dossier: \"ROCm already drives cudagraph_mode with the same enum (vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919, including compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE)\". Hmm — so ROCm's default is already piecewise? But that's inside some platform method (maybe check_and_update_config). If ROCm defaults to PIECEWISE then plain `vllm serve` without enforce-eager already satisfies guard #1; pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE explicitly to be loud and deterministic.\n\nNow single-node proxy serve details:\n- Proxy model path: inside container /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. How does run-proxy obtain the checkpoint? Both scripts bind-mount a host directory. Default host directory: \"$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint\"? build.sh keeps its host caches outside the repo under ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/flashnext. State directory default is $HOME/.local/state/flashnext (FN_STATE_DIR). Use FN_PROXY_DIR default \"${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint}\" — hmm, but run-smoke.sh has zero host state other than results/. The checkpoint has to persist between the make-proxy and run-proxy invocations (separate podman runs). Use FN_PROXY_DIR with the default above. Or put it under results/? results/ is the receipts/measurement tree committed to the repo — no, keep it outside the repo as build.sh does. Going with the state-directory default.\n\nHmm wait, actually — make-proxy.sh is \"run inside the container\". The entire script is a host wrapper (like run-smoke.sh which does podman run). The bash-n acceptance test only checks syntax. No problem.\n\n- Serve command inside run-proxy.sh: `podman run -d` a named container that serves, then the host polls the API port (-p 127.0.0.1:PORT:PORT), issues one greedy completion, asserts finiteness of logits (request with logprobs; assert top logprob is finite / text is non-empty; if logprobs are available, compute finiteness from top5 logprob values — vLLM's /v1/completions returns logprobs as a dict of token→logprob floats. If the model emits NaN logits, logprob is NaN/None. Assert all reported logprobs are finite, plus finish_reason), then `podman stop` (clean shutdown: SIGTERM → vllm shuts down gracefully; assert container exits 0 within timeout; capture serve-log tail into the receipt), and write results/receipts/proxy.json: {\"step\": \"proxy\", \"status\", \"ts\", \"data\": {...}} with fields like mmap_engaged, cudagraph_mode, completion tokens, finite_logits bool, clean_shutdown bool, proxy path, fork commit if available.\n\nreceipts-verify has no bounds on the \"proxy\" step; only well-formedness + status pass. Good.\n\nAlso assert \"finite logits via the API\": use /v1/completions with logprobs=5, temperature 0, max_tokens around 8. Verify completion_tokens ≥1, all logprob values are finite (math.isfinite), and no \"error\" in the response. Could also verify /health. Also record the sampled tokens.\n\nClean shutdown: `podman stop --time 60` the container; then `podman inspect` exit code == 0? vllm serve exits 0 on SIGTERM? podman stop sends SIGTERM; vLLM's API server handles SIGTERM gracefully and exits 0. Record exit code; if 0, clean_shutdown is true (also check no residual container). Use `podman run --name flashnext-proxy-serve`. On error paths, trap cleanup.\n\nNow make-proxy.sh details. In-container python heredoc:\n\n```\nimport json, math, os, re, struct, sys, time\nimport numpy as np\n```\nnumpy is available (vllm dependency). safetensors library available (vllm dependency). torch also available.\n\nSteps:\n1. Resolve geometry:\n   - Base config: mount host FN_MODEL_DIR read-only to /models? For the proxy build we could mount it if present; but make-proxy might run before staging completes (ordering: staging in parallel with container build, then smoke, then proxy — staging probably done by then). Attempt: read /models/config.json if mounted and present; run-proxy/make-proxy mount FN_MODEL_DIR ro if present on host. If absent → fall back to a synthesized config template based on the evidence snapshot.\n\nHmm, but the boundary says the script touches only scripts/ and tests/ — mounting FN_MODEL_DIR is a runtime thing, no problem.\n\n   Simpler and less coupling: make-proxy.sh takes FN_PROXY_BASE_CONFIG (default $FN_MODEL_DIR/config.json on host); if present, mount and shrink; otherwise built-in template.\n\n2. Shrink rules (in python):\n   - cfg = json.load of base, else template.\n   - Force: architectures unchanged (assert one of the Qwen4Exp ids is present); model_type is qwen4_exp.\n   - num_hidden_layers = FN_PROXY_LAYERS default 8. If 'layer_types' present, rebuild it as the workload's periodic pattern: pattern from full_attention_interval (3 linear + 1 full). layer_types = [\"linear_attention\",\"linear_attention\",\"linear_attention\",\"full_attention\"] * (L/4).\n   - num_experts = 8, num_experts_per_tok = 2; keep moe_intermediate_size small, multiple of 128 (e.g. 256). hidden_size: keep workload's (2560) — small enough (bf16 dense for 8 layers is fine). Actually keeping 2560 gives realism for head_dim 256 QSA etc. Keep hidden_size, head_dim, num_attention_heads/num_key_value_heads from base if present (must be compatible: head_dim 256 with hidden 2560, qwen3_next style (qk norm)).\n   - vocab_size: shrink to 2048? But the PLE ngram geometry is config-driven: ngram_vocab_size_base etc. — if those fields exist, shrink them proportionally; leave absent fields to defaults. padded_vocab_size must be divisible by make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by… risky. If base config present, copy all PLE-related fields verbatim but shrink ngram_vocab_size_base dramatically (e.g. 512 rows? table must have ≥1 row per shard; with split_ngram_parts default 512, a tiny org_vocab gives shard_size = ceil(org/512) = 1 row/shard → 512 shard files with 1 row. Hmm. split_ngram_parts might be a config field; if present set to 2. Then 2 shards.\n   \n   discover_shards just globs present shards; _attach_table requires all expected shards (derived from org_vocab/shard_size). So the checkpoint must contain exactly the expected number of shards: n_shards = ceil(org_vocab_size / shard_size), where shard_size = ceil(org_vocab_size / split_ngram_parts). With split_ngram_parts=2, org=512: shard_size=256, 2 shards. Good: \"a small lookup table with shard tensor naming\".\n\n   - quantization_config: {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]} (no modules_to_not_convert → all Linear layers quantized; embed/lm_head exempted by vLLM).\n   - max_position_embeddings small (e.g. 4096); set torch_dtype to \"bfloat16\".\n   - Keep indexer_budget etc. from base (QSA). If absent, hope defaults.\n\n3. Weights:\n   The honest part. Generator plan:\n   - Derive param names from base config if possible? No.\n   - Write tensors in the qwen3-next HF family naming. Based on what I know about Qwen3Next/Qwen4Exp from vLLM's model code (qwen3_next.py):\n     - model.embed_tokens.weight [vocab, hidden] bf16\n     - model.layers.{i}:\n       - For linear_attention layers (qwen3_next style): self_attn.{q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,q_norm,k_norm,o_proj}? Qwen3Next's linear attention = gated deltanet: has in_proj_qkvz, in_proj_ba, conv1d, A_log, dt_bias, norm, out_proj… can't guess reliably.\n     This is the crux of the fragility.\n\n   ⇒ Make the scaffold reuse the primary path and let my writer be a documented best-effort fallback. For the fallback writer, limit to tensor families we can name with confidence and rely on vLLM's tolerance for missing checkpoint keys (parameters left at their init). But wait — random init in vLLM's init: weights are initialized with the model's init function (typically kaiming/xavier uniform) — finite. So missing tensors won't produce NaN; they remain at finite init values. That preserves \"finite logits\" — good proxy semantics: \"first light\", not fidelity.\n\n   Hmm, but is it true that vLLM doesn't error on missing checkpoint keys? In v1, ModelLoader default: after load_weights, no completeness check — I'm fairly sure vLLM doesn't validate completeness (models often run with missing keys and warn? no, not even a warn). Actually there is `if loaded_weight...` — whatever, many people run vLLM with partially-initialized checkpoints (e.g., newly-added heads). OK.\n\n   Actually, better: if I generate tensors for params the model defines, the fallback writer can be completely generic via runtime introspection: inside make-proxy (in-container), I can instantiate the model config with vllm's ModelConfig and then… building the model needs GPU/memory-profiling context — too heavy for the builder? vllm has `ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls` and could build the model on CPU with `with target_platform`… this is what vLLM's weight-init utilities do. Too fragile.\n\n   Decision: fallback writer writes a conservative set of qwen3-next-family HF tensor names covering: embed_tokens, per-layer norms (input_layernorm, post_attention_layernorm), self_attn q/k/v/o (+ q_norm/k_norm), mlp for dense layers (gate/up/down), MoE layers (gate, experts.{e}.{gate,up,down}_proj fp8 + weight_scale_inv, shared_expert.*, shared_expert_gate), PLE tensors (shards + weight_scale + ple's other projections?), indexer (wq_z? skip), lm_head, final norm. Unknown tensors (linear-attention internals, QSA indexer) will be left at vLLM's init. Document honestly in the receipt (data.weights_written list, data.note).\n\n   Wait — actually the reverse is also possible: unknown checkpoint tensor names raise \"There is no module or parameter named\" (via AutoWeightsLoader on the AMD ple loader; similarly at top level: vLLM's model-level AutoWeightsLoader routes by prefix; unmatched top-level names → error?). qwen3_next's load_weights: uses stacked mapping + params_dict iteration, typically skips unmatched names (if name not in params_dict: continue — typical pattern is `if name.endswith(\".bias\") and name not in params_dict: continue`). Hmm, qwen3_next's load_weights skips expert weights, etc.… if a checkpoint name doesn't match anything, most implementations just `continue` (only AutoWeightsLoader raises). Qwen4Exp uses a custom load_weights that dispatches PLE names and routes the rest to AutoWeightsLoader(self) (per moe-dispatch: amd/ple_layer.py:391 AutoWeightsLoader(self).load_weights on regular_weights — raising on unmatched names within the PLE module). At the model level, qwen4_exp's model.load_weights — per the dossier, after loader.load_weights(...) comes the build_tables block. The underlying loader raising on an unknown name means: my fallback writer must not emit tensor names the model doesn't recognize! An extra wrong name = loud load failure.\n\n   Safer strategy for the fallback writer: emit only names I'm confident exist in this architecture: the PLE shard names + weight_scale (validated against the fork's own _SHARD_RE and loader code — they're in the dossier verbatim), and… the rest? If I only emit PLE tensors, everything else remains at init. The model can still run (first light!). The mmap table path is exercised (discover_shards + attach + gather), the MoE kernel runs with initialized fp8? Wait — expert weights are created by vLLM at init as bf16 parameters; the quant method expects fp8 checkpoint weights + scale_inv; if the checkpoint doesn't provide them, Fp8MoEMethod creates parameters at init in fp8 dtype (create_weights allocates w13_weight in fp8 e4m3 + w13_weight_scale_inv fp32; if not loaded, values are whatever the allocation is — uninitialized memory? vLLM's create_weights typically does weight initialization… for fp8 tensors, `torch.empty` followed by possibly random init… uninitialized fp8 bytes can contain NaN encodings (e4m3's 0x7F is NaN!). Dangerous for the \"finite logits\" assertion.\n\n   Hmm. OK, so weights matter. Write expert weights in fp8 + scale_inv myself (naming is standard Fp8 block convention: `.weight` e4m3 + `.weight_scale_inv` fp32) — vLLM Fp8MoEMethod.create_weights names: `w13_weight` (gate_up fused) and `w2_weight`? For FusedMoE in vLLM: param names are `mlp.experts.{e}.w13_weight`, `.w13_weight_scale_inv`, `.w2_weight`, `.w2_weight_scale_inv` — with HF→vLLM mapping from `gate_proj/up_proj/down_proj` via weight-loader fusion. So the checkpoint side uses HF names: `model.layers.{i}.mlp.experts.{e}.gate_proj.weight` + `.weight_scale_inv` etc. That's the standard HF fp8-block convention (DeepSeek-V3 uses weight_scale_inv). Confident enough.\n\n   Dense linear layers: qwen3_next's linear-attention layers have non-standard modules that can't be guessed. But full_attention layers: standard q/k/v/o + q_norm/k_norm (HF: self_attn.{q_proj.weight, k_proj.weight, v_proj.weight, o_proj.weight, q_norm.weight, k_norm.weight}). Dense MLP layers: gate_proj/up_proj/down_proj. Which layers are dense? In Qwen3Next every layer has MoE (decoder_sparse_step=1)? Per qsa-53896: `num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` — with decoder_sparse_step 1, all layers are MoE. Include MoE in every layer; skip dense MLP. If the config expects dense on some layers, the leftover init covers it… no wait, no checkpoint name → leftover init is fine (no extra names emitted).\n\n   But unmatched name risk: does HF-style self_attn.q_proj exist in linear_attention layers? Qwen3Next's linear attention (gated deltanet) has no q_proj. If I emit self_attn.q_proj.weight for a linear-attention layer → unknown name → AutoWeightsLoader raises. So: emit attention tensors only for full_attention layers (known every 4th per the pattern). MoE for all layers. embed/norm/lm_head always. PLE only on layers that have PLE — which layers have PLE?? Is ple in every layer? `_SHARD_RE` is `layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding...` and per the dossier ple_layer.py implements the per-layer PLE module, and build_tables iterates model layers with ple. Probably every layer. Emit PLE shards for all layers (validate: discover_shards + validate_shards_for run in-container and tell me exactly). If a layer lacks PLE, then extra checkpoint names for it → raise. Hmm, risk. But validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim) is called per-layer at construction — the model only constructs MmapNgramEmbedding on layers that have a PLE module. If I emit shards for a layer without PLE, the loader sees an unmatched name → ValueError.\n\n   Mitigation: run self-verification after generation within make-proxy, using the fork's own modules: call `discover_shards` and additionally attempt an actual lightweight load check: `python -c` with ModelConfig + ModelRegistry to build the model on CPU meta? Can't.\n\n   Final honest answer: the fallback writer can't be guaranteed; that's precisely why the task says reuse the fork's test scaffolding whenever possible. The fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp/ probably contains a complete small-checkpoint builder for test_ple.py/test_config.py that knows the exact names. So the runtime reuse path is essential. What I'll implement:\n\n   Path A (fork scaffolding): scan /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/*.py; import each as a module (add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path); find any module-scope callable with signature taking a path/dir; especially search for safetensors-writing helpers via source inspection (look for the strings `safetensors` + `shard_`). Rather than blind calls, use a very targeted contract: look for a function like `write_checkpoint(dir, ...)` — I can't know the name.\n\n   Practical middle ground: reuse what can be reliably imported by name from the fork tree:\n   1. `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common import ple_mmap` (post-relocation patch 0009) — reuse for shard-naming validation (its `_SHARD_RE`), shard discovery (`discover_shards`), dtype maps (`_FP8_DTYPES`). This is genuine reuse of the fork's scaffolding semantics.\n   2. Try importing test modules `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` and `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd`; if a module exposes a callable that returns a fixture checkpoint (search candidates by regex `.*(fixture|ckpt|checkpoint|shard).*` in the module's globals with callable), call the best candidate under guarded try/except, and verify with discover_shards that the result matches the expected naming. If it yields tensors, use it for the PLE table portion.\n\n   Path B: self-written writer as above (qwen3-next HF family names; MoE experts fp8-block; attention only on full-attention layers; PLE shards+scale on all layers — flag in the receipt if the fork's discovery rejects any layer set).\n\n   Then always: verify with ple_mmap.discover_shards → record per-layer shard counts; validate config parses via `AutoConfig`/vllm transformers utils (`from vllm.transformers_utils.config import get_config` — that runs the fork's qwen4_exp config registration → strong schema check, GPU-free); write generation_config? not needed; tokenizer! Serving needs a tokenizer — HF requires tokenizer files; with HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 and no tokenizer in the directory → serve fails. Options: copy tokenizer from the staged workload (mount FN_MODEL_DIR ro, copy tokenizer.json/tokenizer_config.json/merges.txt/vocab… if present), or generate a minimal tokenizer? The workload directory has tokenizer files (per staging: 131 shards + tokenizer files). make-proxy: if FN_MODEL_DIR is mounted and has tokenizer files, copy them (cheap, a few MB; merges.txt 3.3 MB). Fallback: write a minimal char-level tokenizer_config.json? vLLM demands a real tokenizer. Fallback: if tokenizer absent → mark the receipt as failed? No — better fallback: construct a trivial `pretrained_tokenizer_config`… can't fabricate a byte-identical tokenizer for the qwen vocab.\n\n   Actually, cleanest is: make-proxy.sh mounts the staged workload directory read-only (if present on host) and copies the tokenizer files. If absent, emit a loud, typed failure to the receipt (blocker type F.12: \"proxy requires staged workload tokenizer; none found\") — honest. Hmm, but the task says the proxy runs before touching actual weight bytes — the tokenizer isn't weight bytes. And S2 ordering has staging before proxy. Good: tokenizer copy from staged workload, fail loudly if absent.\n\n   Also chat_template/generation_config are optional.\n\n4. Write config.json + tokenizer + shards to /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy (bind-mounted host directory). Record receipt-ish output? make-proxy doesn't write a receipt (spec: the proxy receipt comes from run-proxy). But make-proxy can print a manifest; run-proxy embeds checkpoint metadata. Keep make-proxy receipt-free but exit non-zero on failure.\n\nrun-proxy.sh steps:\n1. Locate proxy directory (host): FN_PROXY_DIR default $HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint; if missing or empty → instruct make-proxy.sh and exit 2? Or auto-build? The worklist treats them as separate steps; run-proxy should fail with a clear message if absent. Actually, be friendly: if checkpoint absent, automatically invoke make-proxy.sh? Deterministic steps are better kept separate: log \"run make-proxy.sh first\" and fail. Hmm — the overnight runner invokes steps in sequence (per S2 ordering: smoke then proxy). run-proxy failing loud on missing checkpoint is the right discipline.\n\n2. podman run: named container flashnext-proxy-serve, detached:\n```\npodman run -d --name \"$CTR\" --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n  --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host \\\n  -p \"127.0.0.1:$FN_PROXY_PORT:$FN_PROXY_PORT\" \\\n  -v \"$PROXY_DIR_HOST:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$REPO_ROOT/results:/results\" \\\n  -e VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 \\\n  -e HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 -e VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1 -e VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1 \\\n  -e PYTHONHASHSEED=0 \\\n  \"$IMAGE\" bash -lc \"exec vllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy --served-model-name flashnext-proxy --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT --tensor-parallel-size 1 -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE --max-model-len 4096 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.6 --dtype bfloat16\"\n```\nWait: F.9 — do not export env defaults the engine reads through is-set probes. VLLM_PLE_MMAP is read via bool(int(os.getenv)) — not an is_set probe (fn-env.sh exports it too). PYTHONHASHSEED is fine (fn-env does too). Don't set VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* / VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM etc. Good — mirror fn-env.sh's discipline. Also, the allocator env PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF from fn-env: include expandable_segments for consistency? For the small proxy model either works; mirror fn-env's values for consistency.\n\nEnv passing: `-e` each var explicitly (run-smoke doesn't pass env; fn-cluster uses podman exec after fn-env). Fine.\n\nNote: `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` — CLI shorthand for compilation config. In newer vLLM CLI: `-cc` / `--compilation-config`. Use `-cc '{\"cudagraph_mode\": \"PIECEWISE\"}'`? The JSON form is safest across versions. The dossier quotes the guard message: \"pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\" — the dotted form is supported. Use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` (as the fork itself suggests).\n\n3. Poll http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/v1/models until ready (timeout FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT, default 1800s — first compile is slow; the proxy is small but triton compile takes time).\n\n4. One greedy completion via /v1/completions: max_tokens 16, temperature 0, logprobs 5. Assert:\n   - HTTP 200, choices[0].text non-empty, finish_reason sane\n   - Logits finite: collect all logprob values (token_logprobs + top_logprobs dicts); assert all finite via math.isfinite. If the engine returns null first-token logprob, skip None (run-tp2 does so). \"Assert finite logits via API\" — the logprobs are the API-visible proxy for logits. If zero logprob values are available → fail (can't assert).\n5. Also verify engagement: grep the container serve log (`podman logs $CTR`) for ple_mmap lines / \"MmapNgramEmbedding\" / piecewise — record counts; if VLLM_PLE_MMAP didn't engage (no mention), mark fail (residency gate leans on engagement; claim 4.1 \"with mmap path engaged\").\n6. Clean shutdown: podman stop -t 90; get exit code via podman inspect; clean_shutdown = exit==0 (treat 137/143 as non-clean… podman stop sends TERM and waits; vllm exits 0 → container exit 0). Then podman rm.\n7. Write results/receipts/proxy.json {\"step\":\"proxy\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\":{...}}. If any step fails, fail receipt (never silent).\n8. Ensure container removal on all paths via trap (podman rm -f).\n\ntests/ also within my boundary: the repo tests suite (tests/test_repo_invariants.py etc.). Should I add tests? Conflict domain includes tests — permission, not obligation. Let me look at tests/test_repo_invariants.py to see if there are script-related assertions (e.g., every scripts/*.sh is syntax-clean or referenced). Adding small repo tests asserting the two scripts exist, are bash -n clean, and that run-proxy references the receipt path + mmap engagement, mirrors claim 6.4 style (\"the tests suite's package assertions pass: syntax-clean scripts…\"). Reinforces the ladder. Let me read the existing tests first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me look at the repo test suite and the cluster-up serve block more closely:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d32dca839a544b0295862ad1","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n '1,60p' tests/test_repo_invariants.py && echo ---- && sed -n '60,200p' tests/test_repo_invariants.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5576,"output":6674,"cacheRead":64512,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6555,"totalTokens":76762,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787992109977,"responseId":"chatcmpl-e361ae86-d893-42e6-b811-a5e15c86f59a","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
