— extra shard indices in dict just unused? Probably OK (extra tolerated, missing not). So if I write shards 0..S-1 with S = max(expected over possible splits), extras harmless. If I write split = 512 case: for any org_vocab ≤ 512, expected = org_vocab shards of 1 row each. If I always write exactly 512 shards of 1 row (rows_total 512) per PLE layer:\n- If model computes org_vocab = 512 and split 512 → expects 512 ✓.\n- If org_vocab = 64 (shrunk base) → expects 64, shards 0..63 present (I wrote 0..511) ✓ extras ignored (probably).\n- If padded_vocab > 512 → expects more than present → RuntimeError. Then validation step (discover_shards + my own attach probe) catches pre-serve and I can regenerate with more rows. Make the writer adaptive: choose NGRAM_ROWS = 512 rows, 1 row/shard, shards 0..511 — but only if that matches expected. If model's padded ngram vocab > 512, need more rows.\n\nWait, actually — simpler: don't shrink ngram fields at all. Keep the workload's ngram geometry (ngram_vocab_size_base etc. copied from the real config)! Table is fp8: real table is 51.2B parameters = 51.2 GB fp8. Too big for \"small lookup table\". Shrink mandatory.\n\nHmm OK, let me look at what amd/ple_layer.py:140-195 computes... don't have the source. Dossier says the geometry is config-driven with fields ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim, make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by. org vocab for ngram ≈ ngram_vocab_size_base (×heads?) — the \"51.2B\" table is 51.2e9 rows?? No — 51.2B parameters with row_bytes ~ head_dim 256 → 200M rows × 256 = 51.2B params. So ngram_vocab_size_base is probably around 200 million / (something). Padded via make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by.\n\nAdaptive plan: runtime loop inside the generator:\n1. Write config;\n2. Compute expected shard layout by instantiating the fork's own validation: `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim)` — needs vllm ModelConfig: `from vllm.config import ModelConfig; mc = ModelConfig(model