Hmm, wait — actually, is `org_vocab` here the text vocab?? \"ngram_embedding\" rows are indexed by hashed ngram ids — `padded_vocab_size` handed to MmapNgramEmbedding is the layer's padded ngram vocab, not text vocab. OK, as above.\n\nShrink rule to keep table small while fitting likely computations: set `ngram_vocab_size_base = 512` (×heads?) if the field exists. If padded result ≤ 512 and split default 512 → shard_size=1, shards=padded. Write 512 single-row shards per layer → covers padded ∈ [1,512]. If padded > 512 → validation fails loudly with typed blocker (\"proxy PLE geometry exceeds the one-row-shard layout; adjust FN_PROXY_NGRAM_ROWS\"). Also allow env override FN_PROXY_SPLIT to write fewer multi-row shards if config field split exists (then set cfg[\"split_ngram_parts\"] = 2 and write 2 shards). Actually if the config field exists under any name containing both \"ngram\" and \"split\", set to 2 and write 2 shards of padded/2 rows. Implement key detection:\n\n```python\nsplit_key = next((k for k in cfg if \"split\" in k and \"ngram\" in k), None)\nif split_key: cfg[split_key] = 2\n```\n\nrow_bytes: heads × dim fp8 bytes → tensor shape (rows, heads*dim) dtype F8_E4M3. If heads unknown → use head_dim 256 as row bytes. Validate with fork's `_validate_layer_shards` at runtime if possible (it checks dtype and row_bytes against head_dim... per dossier validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim)). row_bytes must match what the layer expects: embedding_dim. Layer: `MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` and `self.embedding_dim`... the AMD W4 branch allocates output `(num_tokens, self.embedding_dim)` with dtype of table → dequant. Per-layer embedding_dim = heads_per_ngram * ple_embed_dim? Or head_dim (256)? nvidia :1194: `_dequantize_embeddings` after `self.ple_embedding(...)`. ngram_embedding constructed with `self.head_dim` (amd:196: `VocabParallelEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim...)`). So embedding dim = head_dim (256). row_bytes = 256 fp8 bytes. ✓ Use head_dim from config (default 256).\n\nweight_scale: per-layer scalar fp32 = 1.0. Shape (1,) or scalar — set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight, device) — buffer; any shape broadcastable; use [1].\n\nCheckpoint tensor naming for the scale: full name `model.layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`.\n\nNow fp8 expert weight generation: for expert e: gate_proj/up_proj shape (M, H) e4m3; scale_inv (ceil(M/128), ceil(H/128)) fp32 = 1.0 (value 1.0 → dequant = fp8 value; keep fp8 values small uniform like 0.004 so activations stay small → logits finite). Initialize fp8 from uniform(-a,a) quantized: simplest: zeros? All-zero experts → MoE output zero → residual stream carries through → logits from lm_head(embed chain) finite. Zero weights are safe for finiteness! But gate/router at zero → uniform routing, fine. Random init can blow up. Use small values: experts fp8 zeros with scale 1.0; dense fp8 zeros; attention zeros? All-zero attention → softmax(QK^T=0) uniform → output = mean(V)=0 → residual passes. layernorm of a constant vector? RMSNorm of zero → zero / sqrt(eps) → 0*weight=0. OK finite. embed_tokens: small random values or scaled one-hot-ish: zeros → norm(0) is fine. lm_head: identity-ish mapping: lm_head weight = scaled copy of embed → logits ≈ embeddings → nearly deterministic greedy tokens, finite. Simplest: lm_head = embed_tokens (tied content, valid float). Tokenizer's eos id is whatever.\n\nDeterminism: seed numpy/torch RNG fixed (42) → reproducible checkpoint.\n\nSafetensors writing: use the `safetensors.torch.save_file` API (available in the container). Dtype: torch.float8_e4m3fn for fp8 tensors (safetensors supports F8_E4M3 — yes, safetensors>=0.4 supports float8_e4m3fn... need serialization support: safetensors torch save handles float8_e4m3fn? Does save_file map to F8_E4M3 dtype string since 0.4.x? Hmm, I recall safetensors supports float8 via numpy workaround (save as uint8 view + metadata?). The real checkpoint stores F8_E4M3 (dossier: `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` parsed from header → safetensors writes F8_E4M3 natively). torch save_file with float8 tensor — supported in recent safetensors (0.5+). Pinned vLLM deps are new. Fallback: save with safe.save_file using raw bytes and dtype string \"F8_E4M3\" via `safetensors.numpy`? numpy has no float8. Use `safetensors.torch._SIZE`... implement helper: try save_file(fp8 tensor); on exception, manual header write: safetensors format is simple JSON header + raw bytes — implement `_write_safetensors(path, {name: (dtype_str, shape, bytes)})` minimal serializer. Robust against library version. Use manual serializer for fp8 tensors throughout? Simpler: one handcrafted writer for all tensors (bf16 via torch → numpy → bytes). About 30 lines, deterministic, no version risk. Write my own tiny serializer (format: 8-byte LE header size, JSON {name: {dtype, shape, data_offsets}}, padded data). Known format. Dtype strings: \"BF16\", \"F32\", \"F8_E4M3\".\n\nFile layout: single model.safetensors? discover_shards globs all *.safetensors and parses headers; multiple tensors per file fine. But 8 layers × (attention + MoE 8 experts×3×2 tensors + ple 512 shards) — many tensors; shard files: one file per shard named model-ple-{layer}-{shard:05d}.safetensors (mirrors the fork's test fixture naming). Main weights: model-00001-of-00001.safetensors. No index json needed (mmap discovery parses headers directly; vLLM's loader handles single-file without index fine).\n\nSizes: H=2560. Per-layer full attn: q (nq×256... let q_dim = NH×HD). Pick NH=16, NKV=2 → q 16×256=4096×2560 ×1B(fp8)=10.5 MB... quantize attn? Decision was quantize all linears → attn fp8 too. Per full-attn layer: q,k,v,o fp8 ≈ (4096+512+512+2560·… o: H×(NH·HD)=2560×4096) — about 10+1.3+1.3+10.5=23 MB fp8 per full layer. 2 full layers = 46 MB. Per-layer MoE: 8 experts × (2×(256×2560) + (2560×256)) fp8 = 8×3×0.655 MB = 15.7 MB + shared expert (if exists, 3×(S×H), S=256: 2 MB) → ~18 MB × 8 layers = 142 MB. Table: 8 layers × 512 rows × 256 B = 1 MB. Total ~200 MB. Fine for \"tiny\".\n\nHmm wait, H is 2560 for sure? In the template, yes; in the shrink path keep workload's hidden (2560) — fine.\n\nmax seq: 4096, context needed for the prompt in request is small.\n\nNow run-proxy.sh receipt data fields:\n```json\n{\"step\":\"proxy\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"ts\":..., \"data\":{\n  \"checkpoint\": \"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\",\n  \"checkpoint_source\": \"...from make-proxy marker file proxy-manifest.json\",\n  \"engine\": {\"image\": ..., \"fork_commit\": ...},\n  \"serve\": {\"cudagraph_mode\":\"PIECEWISE\",\"vllm_ple_mmap\":1,\"tp\":1,\"port\":...,\"boot_seconds\":...},\n  \"completion\": {\"prompt\":..., \"text\":..., \"tokens\": n, \"finish_reason\":...},\n  \"finite_logits\": true, \"logprobs_checked\": k,\n  \"mmap_engagement\": {\"log_mentions\": n, \"engaged\": true},\n  \"shutdown\": {\"clean\": true, \"exit_code\": 0}\n}}\n```\nmake-proxy writes proxy-manifest.json inside the checkpoint directory (geometry, reused scaffolding, base config source, file count, bytes) — run-proxy reads and embeds. Clean.\n\nEngagement grep: `podman logs $CTR 2>&1 | grep -ci 'ple_mmap\\|mmap'`... the fork's W3 has loud logging on ROCm mmap enablement (\"loud log (the flag takes ROCm off FULL cudagraphs...)\" per MANIFEST 0010). So log should contain ple_mmap lines. Engaged condition: VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 AND ple_mmap mentions in log ≥1. If zero → fail with tail of log captured in receipt data.\n\nClean shutdown: podman stop -t 120; exit_code=$(podman inspect -f '{{.State.ExitCode}}'); clean = exit_code == 0. podman rm. If stop times out → kill → record clean=false, exit 137.\n\nAlso add cleanup trap: on script error, stop+rm container, write fail receipt.\n\nWait — one more check: does the acceptance grep require literal string \"results/receipts/proxy.json\" in run-proxy.sh? Yes: `grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh`. Define OUT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/proxy.json\" — contains the literal. ✓. Both bash -n clean. ✓.\n\nEnv F.9 discipline in run-proxy: -e flags passed: VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 (bool(int) read, permitted; fn-env exports it), HF_HUB_OFFLINE/VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS/VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK (not engine probes), PYTHONHASHSEED, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF (not is_set probe), TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR/TRITON_CACHE_DIR pointing at /state mount (cache persistence across runs? Bind-mount host's $FN_STATE_DIR as /state for warm inductor/triton caches — nice for retry; mirrors fn-env). Don't pass NCCL variables (single-node; single GPU TP=1 — RCCL not needed). HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 like fn-env.\n\nContainer naming: flashnext-proxy-serve; `podman rm -f` pre-cleanup in case of leftover.\n\nTimeouts: FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT default 3600; poll /v1/models every 5s; also detect container death during poll (podman inspect state != running → fail early with log tail).\n\nvllm serve arguments:\n```\nvllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy \\\n  --served-model-name flashnext-proxy \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 1 \\\n  --max-model-len 4096 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.6 \\\n  --no-enable-prefix-caching? (leave default; PYTHONHASHSEED fixed anyway)\n  -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\n```\nNote on \"--enforce-eager\" absence: comment explains ruling P10 + guard.\n\nHmm — one worry: `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` CLI syntax: does the vLLM CLI accept dotted shorthand `-cc.cudagraph_mode=...`? For `--compilation-config` / `-cc`, vLLM's FlexibleArgumentParser supports dotted sub-args like `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` (yes — used widely, e.g. `-cc.mode=...`; guard message itself suggests it). ✓.\n\nAlso `--max-num-batched-tokens` default fine. `--load-format auto`.\n\nNow writing tests/test_proxy_tooling.py. Style of existing tests: unittest, ROOT-relative. Additions:\n\n```python\nclass ProxyTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_scripts_exist_and_syntax_clean(self): bash -n on both\n    def test_run_proxy_references_receipt(self): \"results/receipts/proxy.json\" in text\n    def test_mmap_engaged_and_piecewise(self): contains VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1; contains cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE; does NOT contain --enforce-eager (ruling P10: fork guard rejects eager under VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1)\n    def test_no_is_set_probe_exports(self): F.9 — VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER absent from both scripts\n    def test_make_proxy_targets_and_shape(self): /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy present; weight_block_size present with 128; \"ngram_embedding.shard_\" naming present; architectures id Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration present\n```\nWait: \"--enforce-eager\" string must not appear in run-proxy.sh — my script explains in comments that eager is rejected. Would a comment mentioning `--enforce-eager` fail my own test? Have the test assert the flag isn't passed to the serve command: simpler — assert absence of \"--enforce-eager\" entirely and write the script's comment without the literal flag string (say \"the fork's check_cudagraph_safety guard rejects plain-eager mode\"). Fine — avoid the literal in comments.\n\nNow, is adding tests wise for the acceptance? Only acceptance is bash -n + grep. Tests reinforce the ladder (gate repo-tests runs the suite). Risk: my tests fail on judge machine (e.g. string I assert absent). Keep assertions conservative and self-consistent with files I write. OK.\n\nLet me also verify specs/flashnext/trace.json — mentioned in test_repo_invariants (test_spec_artifacts_exist expects trace.json + contracts/trace.schema.json). Earlier `ls specs`? ls showed specs dir exists with flashnext. Whatever — existing tests probably pass.\n\nTime to write the scripts. Keep style consistent with run-smoke.sh (bash -euo pipefail, REPO_ROOT, IMAGE, podman run with devices, python heredoc, receipt JSON, final status exit).\n\nDraft make-proxy.sh:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/make-proxy.sh — the proxy checkpoint builder (spec claim 4.1 prep).\n# ... header comment\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=...\nIMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nPROXY_DIR=\"${FN_PROXY_DIR:-${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}/proxy-checkpoint}\"\nMODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nLAYERS=\"${FN_PROXY_LAYERS:-8}\"\nEXPERTS=\"${FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$PROXY_DIR\"\nrm -f \"$PROXY_DIR\"/*.safetensors \"$PROXY_DIR\"/config.json ... (rebuild clean)\n\nmounts=(-v \"$PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\nif [ -f \"$MODEL_DIR/config.json\" ]; then mounts+=(-v \"$MODEL_DIR:/models:ro\"); fi\n\npodman run --rm \"${mounts[@]}\" \\\n  -e FN_PROXY_LAYERS=\"$LAYERS\" -e FN_PROXY_EXPERTS=\"$EXPERTS\" \\\n  \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'\n... big python ...\nPY\necho \"make-proxy: checkpoint ready at $PROXY_DIR\"\n```\n\nPython generator — let me write it carefully.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Build the proxy checkpoint at /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy.\n\nContract (spec R4 claim 4.1, vocabulary \"proxy checkpoint\"):\nsame architecture identifier and expert format as the workload — a handful of\nlayers, 8 routed experts in block-FP8 [128,128], a small PLE/engram lookup\ntable under the exact shard naming the mmap path's discover_shards expects,\nand the workload's tokenizer so the server can tokenize without a hub round\ntrip (spec F.6).\n\nFork-scaffold reuse, in priority order:\n 1. If /models/config.json (the staged workload config) is mounted, shrink\n    it — every config field the model class reads stays exactly what the\n    fork's config class expects.\n 2. The PLE table layout is validated against the fork's own mmap module\n    (vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap: _SHARD_RE, discover_shards),\n    and the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp scaffolding is probed for a\n    shard-writer to reuse before falling back to the writer below.\n\"\"\"\nimport json, math, os, re, struct, sys, time, importlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\nOUT = Path(\"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\nMODELS = Path(\"/models\")\nLAYERS = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_LAYERS\", \"8\"))\nEXPERTS = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS\", \"8\"))\nSEED = 42\n```\n\nConfig construction:\n\n```python\ndef base_config():\n    p = MODELS / \"config.json\"\n    if p.is_file():\n        cfg = json.loads(p.read_text())\n        return cfg, \"workload:%s\" % p\n    # Typed fallback from specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §3.3\n    cfg = {...}\n    return cfg, \"template:dotfiles-observed-3.3\"\n```\n\nTemplate (only if staged config absent):\n```python\n{\n \"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"],\n \"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\",\n \"torch_dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n \"hidden_size\": 2560,\n \"head_dim\": 256,\n \"num_attention_heads\": 16,\n \"num_key_value_heads\": 2,\n \"num_hidden_layers\": 8,\n \"full_attention_interval\": 4,\n \"vocab_size\": 32768,\n \"max_position_embeddings\": 4096,\n \"rms_norm_eps\": 1e-6,\n \"tie_word_embeddings\": False,\n \"use_qk_norm\": True,\n \"num_experts\": 8,\n \"num_experts_per_tok\": 2,\n \"moe_intermediate_size\": 256,\n \"shared_expert_intermediate_size\": 256,\n \"decoder_sparse_step\": 1,\n \"ngram_vocab_size_base\": 512,\n \"split_ngram_parts\": 2,  # guess only; dropped by shrink if the field is unknown to the fork config class?\n}\n```\nHmm — unknown extra keys in config.json are generally tolerated by HF config classes (ignored). But the template is a last resort anyway (real machine has staged config — staging precedes proxy in S2 ordering; and if missing, loud failure acceptable? Task says proxy before real weights... but tokenizer also needs staged dir. If staged dir absent entirely, proxy can't serve (no tokenizer) → fail loudly with typed message. Hmm — unless I synthesize tokenizer too. Can I? vLLM needs tokenizer_config.json + tokenizer.json (fast tokenizer) — can I write a minimal byte-level BPE tokenizer.json?? Handcrafting a valid HF tokenizer.json for a tiny vocab is possible but fabricating tokenizer identity... For the proxy, a tiny deterministic tokenizer is actually legitimate: the proxy checkpoint is synthetic anyway! But writing a correct tokenizer.json (with model vocab, merges for BPE) by hand — feasible: simplest valid tokenizer: a \"WordLevel\" or byte-fallback BPE with ASCII chars + a few words. vLLM uses it via transformers AutoTokenizer; WordLevel with unk is tolerated. Risk: chat template absence → /v1/completions (not chat) fine.\n\nDecision: tokenizer strategy: (1) copy from staged workload if present (preferred — real tokenizer guarantees alignment with nothing in particular; fine since my vocab_size may differ... wait. If I copy the real tokenizer (vocab 151936) but config vocab_size=32768 → token ids ≥32768 get embedded OOB → gather runtime error. The real model pads vocab; vLLM embeds num_embeddings=padded vocab; id ≥ vocab → index error on GPU (assertion). Prompt \"The pair...\" in qwen tokenizer → likely low ids (<32k), but no guarantee, and the generated tokens are sampled over the full 32k logit range (ids <32k) fine, but decode is... decode is OK. Prefill risk: if the prompt contains an id ≥32768 → crash. Mitigation: keep vocab_size = workload's vocab_size (don't shrink)! embed_tokens is V×H bf16 = 151936×2560×2 = 778 MB — a bit large, but fine on a 128 GB box (\"tiny\" is relative; the alternative is fabricating a tokenizer to match the shrunk vocab).\n\nHmm, 778 MB embed + 778 MB lm_head ≈ 1.6 GB. Tolerable? Removes both OOB risk and tokenizer-fabrication risk. Yes: keep workload's vocab_size, copy tokenizer. In template fallback (no staged dir), vocab 4096 + my own minimal WordLevel tokenizer. That's a consistent pairing.\n\nWait — but vLLM pads vocab to multiple of 64 etc.: padded_vocab_size for embed — for PLE's MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size...) — no wait, that's the layer's padded ngram vocab, separate thing.\n\nNow — ngram/PLE geometry. With real config: shrink fields containing \"ngram\" that relate to size. Field names unknown. Detect: keys matching r\"ngram\" (any case). For each, if value is a large int (>4096) and key contains \"size\" or \"base\" or \"vocab\" → set to 512. Keys matching ngram+split → 2. Keys for heads/dim/divisor → leave. Log all adjustments to manifest. Then compute expected shard count via best-effort mirror:\n\n```python\norg = ngram_vocab_size_base (maybe × heads_per_ngram?) \n```\nI don't really know if heads multiply into row count... MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, head_dim): rows = padded_vocab_size. padded = make_divisible(org_total?), where org_total might = base × heads? 51.2B params / row 256 B = 200M rows. If heads_per_ngram=8, base=25M → 200M. Plausible! So rows ≈ base × heads, then padded to divisor.\n\nAdaptive safeguard: after writing config.json, before writing shards, resolve rows via fork introspection:\n\n```python\ndef expected_rows_and_split(hf_cfg, n_layers):\n    # Best-effort mirror of amd/ple_layer.py:140-195 (field names per\n    # specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md §5); anything the fork's\n    # own validate_shards_for later rejects is caught at validate time.\n    base = getattr(hf_cfg, \"ngram_vocab_size_base\", 512)\n    heads = getattr(hf_cfg, \"heads_per_ngram\", 1)\n    div = getattr(hf_cfg, \"make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by\", 1)\n    split = getattr(hf_cfg, \"split_ngram_parts\", 512)\n    total = base * heads\n    padded = ((total + div - 1) // div) * div if div else total\n    ...\n```\nThen write `split` shards per layer... but if \"split_ngram_parts\" isn't a config field and is hardcoded 512 in code, my getattr default of 512 kicks in: shards=512, rows=ceil(padded/512) per shard. If padded=512 → 1 row × 512 shards × 8 layers = 4096 files. OK tolerable. Then validate via ple_mmap.discover_shards + optional MmapPleTable construction (wrap in try, non-fatal warning if introspection fails, fatal if discover_shards finds nothing).\n\nActually, stronger validation: call `ple_mmap._validate_layer_shards` / `validate_shards_for`? Signature: validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim) — needs vllm ModelConfig object. Construct ModelConfig(model=str(OUT)) — ModelConfig init loads tokenizer via get_config... tokenizer present ✓, reads config ✓, mostly CPU-safe (may probe platform for dtype? just config parsing — fine). Then validate_shards_for(mc, f\"model.layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding\", head_dim)? layer_name format is uncertain. Wrap in try; treat exceptions as warnings recorded in manifest (not fatal — the real gate is run-proxy's serve). Hmm, but if geometry is wrong, run-proxy fails after ~20 min compile... make the discover_shards check fatal (it's pure and documented: returns per_layer[layer][shard] = (path, offset, rows)). Compare discovered per-layer shard count to expected `split` count → mismatch = fatal with typed message. That's solid: discover only depends on names/headers.\n\nAnd row counts: _attach_table maps checkpoint_start = shard_index*shard_size... if my rows per shard differ from expected shard_size, attach fails at serve. discover returns rows per shard — can I cross-check rows against expected shard_size computed via my mirror: if mismatch → fatal typed. Residual risk: mirror formula wrong. Accept it; manifest records the formula inputs for morning diagnosis. Hmm, alternatively make rows per shard generous: rows = shard_size_expected... same thing. Accept residual risk, loud diagnostics.\n\nPLE writer via fork test scaffolding probe (reuse before inventing):\n\n```python\ndef try_fork_scaffold(out_dir, n_layers, ...):\n    sys.path.insert(0, \"/opt/vllm/tests\")\n    for modname in (\"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap\", \"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd\", \"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple\"):\n        try: mod = importlib.import_module(modname)\n        except Exception: continue\n        for name in dir(mod):\n            fn = getattr(mod, name)\n            if callable(fn) and re.search(r\"(write|build|make|create).*(shard|ckpt|checkpoint|fixture)\", name, re.I):\n                try: fn(out_dir, ...)  # signature unknown — introspect parameters\n                ...\n```\nToo fragile to blindly call with wrong args. Compromise: probe, but only use a scaffold function if it has clear signature (single parameter named path/dir/out). Honestly, the most reliable \"reuse\" is import-based validation (discover_shards/_SHARD_RE) + matching my file naming to the fork's fixture convention (`model-ple-{layer}-{shard:05d}.safetensors`). Probe broadly but guard fully; record in manifest what was probed/used. This satisfies the \"whenever possible\" clause honestly: possibility is determined at runtime on the actual machine.\n\nThen weight writer. Minimal safetensors serializer:\n\n```python\n_DT = {\"F32\": (\"<f4\", 4), \"BF16\": (None, 2), \"F8_E4M3\": (None, 1)}\ndef save_safetensors(path, tensors):  # tensors: {name: (dtype_str, shape, bytes)}\n    header = {}\n    offset = 0\n    blobs = []\n    for name, (dtype, shape, raw) in tensors.items():\n        header[name] = {\"dtype\": dtype, \"shape\": shape, \"data_offsets\": [offset, offset+len(raw)]}\n        offset += len(raw); blobs.append(raw)\n    hjson = json.dumps(header).encode()\n    pad = (8 - len(hjson) % 8) % 8\n    with open(path, \"wb\") as f:\n        f.write(struct.pack(\"<Q\", len(hjson)+pad)); f.write(hjson + b\" \"*pad)\n        for b in blobs: f.write(b)\n```\n\nTensor builders (numpy → bytes):\n- bf16 via torch.tensor(...).to(torch.bfloat16).numpy()? bf16 numpy unsupported → torch tensor → tensor.view(torch.uint16)? bytes via tensor.contiguous().view(torch.int16).numpy().tobytes()? bf16→uint16 view via .view(torch.int16). Use torch and .numpy() on int16 view. Fine.\n- fp8: values are small; encode via torch float → clamp to e4m3 range → to(torch.float8_e4m3fn) → view as uint8 → bytes.\n\nValue policy: deterministic, numerically safe:\n- Attention q/k/v/o fp8: zeros (scale 1.0) → uniform attention → finite. Hmm all-zero K → softmax(0)=uniform → attn out = mean(V)=0. Fine.\n- Expert fp8: zeros. gate router bf16 zeros → uniform routing.\n- shared expert zeros.\n- Dense mlp (if any layer is dense): zeros.\n- embed_tokens bf16: seeded normal × 0.02.\n- lm_head bf16: copy of embed (logits = hidden·embedᵀ — finite).\n- Norms: ones.\n- PLE table fp8: zeros (scale 1.0) → PLE contribution zero.\n- weight_scale tensors: fp32 1.0.\n- q_norm/k_norm: ones.\n- Rotary? inv_freq typically computed inside the model (not a checkpoint tensor). Skip.\n- Linear-attention layers: skip all tensors (left at vLLM init — finite). Hmm — vLLM init for fp8? The linear-attn modules' Linears would be quantized by Fp8Config → create_weights allocates fp8 tensors... uninitialized fp8 memory could contain NaN bit patterns (e4m3: 0x7F/0xFF are NaN; S.1111.111 = NaN). torch.empty of fp8 — contents are whatever malloc gives → NaN risk! Ugh!! Does vLLM's create_weights initialize? Fp8LinearMethodBase.create_weights: `torch.empty(..., dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn)` — no explicit init... then `set_weight_attrs`. If the checkpoint doesn't provide it, whatever bits are there remain → NaN logits risk.\n\nSo I must provide fp8 weights for every quantized Linear in the model, including linear-attention's (in_proj_qkvz etc. — unknown names!). Can't name them → NaN risk → \"finite logits\" assertion could fail overnight.\n\nOptions:\n(a) Don't quantize linears outside experts: modules_to_not_convert as regex that excludes everything except experts... but regex correctness across vLLM versions is risky. Actually let me think about how ignored_layers is matched. In vLLM fp8.py:\n\n```python\nif self.ignored_layers and any(re.match(layer, prefix) for layer in self.ignored_layers)? \n```\nHmm, from memory:\n```python\ndef _is_layer_skipped(self, prefix): return any(re.match(layer, prefix) for layer in self.ignored_layers)\n```\nHmm, or `prefix in self.ignored_layers`? In compressed-tensors it's regex. In Fp8Config: `if not self.ignored_layers or not any(re.search(ignore, prefix)...`. Really not sure.\n\n(b) Quantize nothing except experts by removing quantization_config and instead... no — experts must be block-fp8 (the whole point: \"8 experts in block-FP8 with weight_block_size 128×128\" + exercise the permitted MoE kernel). quantization_config drives Fp8MoEMethod. With quantization_config present and no ignore list, all Linears get fp8.\n\n(c) Make modules_to_not_convert the actual list of all non-expert Linear module prefixes. If I could enumerate module names... could do this at runtime in the container! Instantiate the model class on meta device? Building the full Qwen4Exp model on CPU/meta with vllm config:\n\n```python\nfrom vllm.config import VllmConfig, ModelConfig...\nwith torch.device(\"meta\")? vllm model classes need vllm_config context (set_current_vllm_config)...\n```\nThere's precedent: vllm's own tests instantiate models on CPU with random weights (tests/models/... do exactly this: `with init_empty_weights()`/`vllm_config` ctx). If make-proxy could instantiate the model (meta or CPU, small config), then:\n   - Enumerate all nn.Linear modules → generate a perfect modules_to_not_convert list (everything except *.mlp.experts*) — or better, save the state_dict names of all parameters → know exactly every checkpoint tensor name to write!! That resolves everything: exact names, exact shapes.\n\n   Instantiation feasibility: small config (8 layers, hidden 2560) → CPU RAM trivial. Need vllm config context: `from vllm.config import VllmConfig; with set_current_vllm_config(vllm_config): model = ModelCls(vllm_config=vllm_config)`. ModelConfig construction needs platform init (current_platform) — in-container with ROCm, platform detection touches amdsmi/KFD — container without --device passed: patch 0003 adds KFD topology detection: reads /sys/class/kfd — no /dev/kfd access needed? _kfd_topology_has_amd_gpu reads sysfs files — /sys/class/kfd is host's sysfs — visible inside a podman container without --device? /sys is mounted ro by default — yes, /sys/class/kfd visible. So platform detects ROCm ✓. ModelConfig may call current_platform.inference_available? Just config parsing, fine.\n   \n   Model construction: Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration(vllm_config) — constructor reads config, builds layers (custom ops register, buffers...). Might touch `get_current_vllm_config` ✓ context set. Might allocate big tensors: hidden 2560, 8 layers — small ✓. PLE module with VLLM_PLE_MMAP unset in builder env → normal VocabParallelEmbedding path (needs distributed init? VocabParallelEmbedding at tp_size 1 is plain ✓; but may call get_tensor_model_parallel_world_size → needs init_distributed... vLLM models built under unit tests do so with vllm_config with parallel_config tp=1; get_tp_world_size reads GroupCoordinator state — is there a default singleton that works without init? (vLLM's tests build models this way without distributed init... e.g., tests/models/test_registry build under `init_vllm_registered_model`?). There's `vllm.model_executor.models.utils`... not sure without distributed init. vLLM has initialize_model_parallel that needs torch.distributed init... In unit tests (tests/models/language/generation/...), they instantiate via `create_model` helper that initializes the distributed env (a \"vllm executor\" fixture?). Hmm.\n\n   This rabbit hole is deep, and I can't verify. But if I wrap in try/except: scaffold-reuse attempt A (model introspection) → if succeeds, perfect name list; else fall back to curated name writer + accept NaN risk mitigation: mitigate (c-fallback) NaN by making the quantization ignore list best-effort regex and additionally setting... Hmm.\n\nActually — NaN risk mitigation without names: even unknown uninitialized fp8 tensors only matter if they're quantized. So the most robust global approach: only quantize the experts. Implement modules_to_not_convert with both a regex and explicit entries? Let me see how Fp8Config ignores. Can't read the fork... but the upstream vLLM fp8.py (~0.11):\n\n```python\n@classmethod\ndef from_config(cls, config):\n    weight_block_size = cls.get_from_keys_or(config, [\"weight_block_size\"], None)\n    ignored_layers = cls.get_from_keys_or(config, [\"modules_to_not_convert\"], None)\n    if not ignored_layers:\n        ignored_layers = cls.get_from_keys_or(config, [\"modules_to_not_convert\"], None)\n    return cls(...)\n```\nAnd in get_quant_method:\n```python\nif is_layer_skipped(prefix, self.ignored_layers): return UnquantizedLinearMethod...\n```\n`is_layer_skipped(prefix, ignored_layers)` is a util in quantization/utils: \n```python\ndef is_layer_skipped(prefix, ignored_layers): return prefix in set(ignored_layers)  # or regex?\n```\nIn vLLM `is_layer_skipped` is: `return any(re.match(proj_name, prefix) for proj_name in ignored_layers)`? Compressed-tensors uses regex; fp8 utils has both patterns in the codebase. Risky.\n\nDeep breath. Let me step back and prioritize failure modes:\n\nThe primary risk (fp8 NaN for missing-name tensors) applies equally to the \"quantize all\" plan: even quantizing all, linear-attn module names are unknown → missing from checkpoint → uninitialized fp8 → NaN. Unless vLLM initializes fp8 weights in create_weights... check upstream Fp8LinearMethod.create_weights:\n\n```python\nweight = torch.empty(...dtype=params_dtype(fp8))...\nset_weight_attrs(weight, {...})\n```\nNo initialization (empty). But — wait, vLLM v1 model init: many models call `_initialize_weights` in `Model._init_...`? Does vLLM's ModelLoader call model.load_weights then initialize missing? No.\n\nHmm, actually for fp8 MoE experts (Fp8MoEMethod.create_weights) → also torch.empty. So any parameter I don't provide could be garbage fp8. So I must enumerate all quantized Linears. That brings me back to model instantiation introspection as the robust core:\n\nCore generator design (in container):\n1. Write config.json (+ tokenizer + generation_config if available).\n2. Attempt to instantiate model class on CPU via fork's registry + vllm config context:\n```python\nfrom vllm.config import ModelConfig, VllmConfig, set_current_vllm_config? \n```\nHmm exact API: `from vllm.config import VllmConfig; from vllm.config.model import ModelConfig`... and `set_current_vllm_config` is in vllm.config. Build:\n```python\nmc = ModelConfig(model=str(OUT), task=\"generate\", ...)  # signature varies; use kwargs minimally: ModelConfig(model=str(OUT))\nvc = VllmConfig(model_config=mc)\nwith set_current_vllm_config(vc):\n    model = ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls(mc.architectures)[0](vllm_config=vc, prefix=\"\")? \n```\nvLLM test helpers do: `init_vllm_registered_model(vllm_config, prefix)` from tests/models/utils? There's `vllm.model_executor.models.interfaces`... vLLM has an official helper: `vllm.model_executor.model_loader.utils`? In tests: `from tests.models.utils import init_vllm_registered_model`. That's in the fork's tests tree! Reuse of fork scaffolding: `tests/models/utils.py:init_vllm_registered_model(vllm_config, prefix=...)` — builds model with empty weights? I recall it constructs under `torch.device(\"meta\")`? Hmm... if it constructs under meta, parameters have no data but name/shape exist — perfect for enumeration: `for name, module in model.named_modules(): if isinstance(module, torch.nn.Linear)` → collect prefix → and for parameters `named_parameters()` → names+shape!\n\nIf introspection succeeds:\n   - modules_to_not_convert = [name of every Linear not under mlp.experts] (exact prefix strings — matches any matching semantics, regex or equality: bare strings work under either `in` or re.match for full prefix match? re.match(bare_prefix_string, prefix) — the module prefix equals the Linear's own prefix — matches as regex prefix ✓; equality ✓).\n   - Write zero-fp8 weights + scale_inv for expert Linears; write bf16 zero/one weights for all other Linear/Embedding/norm parameters — with HF names?? Problem: parameter names from named_parameters() are vLLM names (e.g., model.layers.0.self_attn.qkv_proj.weight fused!), not HF checkpoint names. Checkpoint needs HF names that load_weights maps... The fused qkv_proj needs HF's q_proj/k_proj/v_proj separately with stacked mapping. HF→vLLM mapping: stacked_params_mapping entries give (vllm_name, hf_suffix, shard_id): can invert: for parameter vllm name X.qkv_proj, write 3 HF tensors X.q_proj/X.k_proj/X.v_proj with appropriate shapes (need per-shard shapes: q=NH×HD, k=NKV×HD...). Moe experts: vLLM w13_weight [E?, 2M, H] batched — HF per-expert gate/up separate ✓ mapping known: experts.{e}.w13_weight ← experts.{e}.{gate,up}_proj... vLLM FusedMoE weight_loader handles per-expert checkpoint names `experts.{e}.gate_proj.weight`. ✓.\n   - Non-fused linears (o_proj, down_proj, gate, norm, embed, lm_head, in_proj_*): name in checkpoint = vLLM parameter name? For models where HF name == vLLM name (common for non-stacked), yes. For linear-attn modules, vLLM's names likely mirror HF's (both from the same modeling file family). So: for any parameter not covered by stacked mapping, write under its vLLM name — if HF name differs, unknown-key error...\n\n   Argh. Names are the whole problem, and I can't resolve them with certainty blind.\n\nFinal strategic decision — embrace the unknown, structure the script as adaptive at runtime with validation loops, and keep the campaign's philosophy (loud, typed, graded). The make-proxy script:\n\nPhase 1: config + tokenizer (robust).\nPhase 2: fork-driven weight discovery:\n   - Try model instantiation via fork's tests/models/utils helper (if importable) → enumerate parameters (vLLM names + shapes) and stacked_params_mapping → derive checkpoint tensor list (HF names where mapping known; otherwise vLLM names) → write tensors accordingly. Record method \"fork-introspection\".\n   - If instantiation fails → fallback curated writer (embed/lm_head/norms/full-attn q k v o + qk norms/per-layer MoE gate+experts fp8/scale_inv/PLE shards+scale), with quantization_config.modules_to_not_convert = [] but accept risk, and mitigate NaN by: also writing quantization_config `modules_to_not_convert`: [\"re:^model\\\\.layers\\\\.\\\\d+\\\\.(self_attn|linear_attn|mlp\\\\.(gate|shared_expert))\"...] — nope. Drop it; accept residual risk, mark manifest honestly.\n\nHmm, wait, actually reconsider — one more NaN mitigation lever for fallback: set `\"modules_to_not_convert\"` with a regex matching everything except expert weights, and rely on regex semantics of either kind: vLLM's fp8 ignore check — I can test both interpretations at runtime in the container! Build Fp8Config.from_config({...,\"modules_to_not_convert\": [...patterns...]}) and call get_quant_method / ignore helper on sample prefixes (\"model.layers.3.mlp.experts.1.gate_proj\" vs \"model.layers.3.linear_attn.in_proj_qkvz\") and observe whether skipped or not — runtime probe of actual semantics! That's the empirical way — fully aligned with the campaign spirit. If probe shows patterns work → use them. If not → try literal full module prefix (would need module names — only from instantiation).\n\nOK. Enough analysis; the script is necessarily a runtime-adaptive tool. Let me structure the python generator as explicit stages with a manifest, each stage falling back loudly. Length maybe ~350 lines of python. Plus a fork-tests probe for the PLE fixture writer.\n\nAlso let me nail down a few remaining decisions:\n\n- Number of layers: default 8 (\"a few\"). full_attention_interval 4 → 2 full-attn layers, 6 linear-attn. All layers MoE (decoder_sparse_step 1 is default? If real config present, keep its sparse step; ensure ≥1 MoE layer: if config has decoder_sparse_step, set to 1).\n- Experts: 8 (task), top-k: 2.\n- weight_block_size [128,128] ✓ (task). moe_intermediate_size 256 (multiple of 128 ✓). hidden kept from base (2560 ✓ multiple of 128). Head/q dims multiples of 128? Only matrix dims need to be block-multiple: q_dim = NH×HD: 16×256 = 4096 ✓.\n- Determinism: seed everything.\n\n- generation_config.json: copy from workload if present; else omit.\n\n- Proxy manifest: OUT/proxy-manifest.json with {built_by, ts, base_config_source, geometry, files, bytes, scaffold:{...}, warnings:[...]}. run-proxy reads and embeds.\n\n- make-proxy exit codes: 0 success; non-zero loud.\n\nNow the tokenizer-fallback minimal builder (if no staged tokenizer): write tokenizer_config.json + tokenizer.json for a WordLevel ASCII tokenizer with vocab 4096: printable ASCII chars + common words... WordLevel with unk_token; vocab: chars + padding. And config vocab_size=4096 (aligned). Special tokens: eos/bos/pad ids 0..3; chars from id 4. vLLM /v1/completions needs only tokenize+detokenize — WordLevel works via transformers. Greedy output decodes to chars. Fine.\n\nHmm wait — if template is used, qwen4_exp config class might require certain fields (indexer_budget etc.) — defaults probably exist in the config class. If ModelConfig load fails on template, that's a loud typed failure ✓.\n\nServe args also need `--max-model-len 4096` ✓ matches template; for workload-shrunk config, override max_position_embeddings to 4096 and pass --max-model-len 4096.\n\nAlso add `--disable-log-requests`. Keep stdout for engagement grep (podman logs captures both).\n\nWait, one more: serving Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration via /v1/completions — the completion endpoint needs a causal LM head ✓.\n\nAlso `--port` inside container == host bind port. Use FN_PROXY_PORT default 1235. Bind \"127.0.0.1:$FN_PROXY_PORT:$FN_PROXY_PORT\". podman slirp4netns port forwarding — fine (run-smoke doesn't bind ports, but the fleet uses podman broadly; the pair service uses host networking? fn-cluster uses podman exec inside a long-running container with -p? Probably uses --network host). Let me quickly check fn-cluster-up.sh's container creation to see the network mode — if it uses --network=host, mirror that (simpler, avoids port mapping issues).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_99e52f0d9a3b498d951a961e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n '80,140p' host/fn-cluster-up.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":8301,"output":17526,"cacheRead":69632,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":17446,"totalTokens":95459,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787992284838,"responseId":"chatcmpl-6d8779c8-4c8b-48ae-8ee7-6017b9653303","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_99e52f0d9a3b498d951a961e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n    --env-file \"$1\" \\\n    \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity >/dev/null\n}\n\nlog \"container: coordinator\"\nrun_container \"$LOCAL_ENV_FILE\"\nlog \"container: worker\"\nworker \"podman rm -f '$FN_CONTAINER' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true \\\n  && podman run -d --name '$FN_CONTAINER' \\\n    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v '$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR' \\\n    --env-file '$REMOTE_TMP/env.list' \\\n    '$FN_IMAGE' sleep infinity >/dev/null\"\n\n# --- 3. ray: distributed head on the coordinator, worker join -----------------\nlog \"ray head on the coordinator\"\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head \\\n  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n  --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" \\\n  --include-dashboard=false >/dev/null\nlog \"ray join from the worker (dials the fleet identity $FN_HEAD_IP on the wire)\"\n# --include-dashboard is head-only: ray PANICs if it is passed to a worker.\nworker \"podman exec '$FN_CONTAINER' ray start \\\n  --address='$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT' \\\n  --num-cpus '$RAY_NUM_CPUS'\" >/dev/null\n\n# --- 4. hard two-GPU gate before serve ----------------------------------------\ngpus_total=\"\"\nfor _ in $(seq 1 120); do\n  gpus_total=\"$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null \\\n    | awk '$2 == \"GPU\" { split($1, a, \"/\"); print a[2]; exit }')\" || true\n  case \"$gpus_total\" in\n    2|2.0) break ;;\n  esac\n  sleep 2\ndone\ncase \"$gpus_total\" in\n  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed: ray reports $gpus_total GPU\" ;;\n  *)\n    log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (last reading: '$gpus_total'); refusing to serve\"\n    podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status >&2 || true\n    exit 1\n    ;;\nesac\n\n# --- 5. the serve: TP=2, eager -------------------------------------------------\n# Do not add comments inside the backslash-continued `vllm serve` command\n# below: a '#' there silently comments out every remaining argument, and\n# `bash -n` still reports the file as valid.\nlog \"serving $FN_MODEL_DIR as '$FN_SERVED_NAME' at TP=2 (eager)\"\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787992737710}]}
