{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, there's a bug: the final python heredoc for receipt writing uses env vars FN_STATUS/FN_DATA set inside the previous python — but those were set via os.environ within the same python process and don't propagate to bash. After the python heredoc completes, OUT has already been written by that python (that python should write the receipt itself, not rely on bash). Let me restructure: the final python stage writes the receipt itself (has all the data). Currently it just sets env vars and exits — bash then checks -f \"$OUT\" → file doesn't exist → writes fail receipt with \"receipt stage never ran\". Bug!\n\nFix: within the python heredoc, write the receipt directly (same never-overwrite semantics). Let me rework the end of the python stage to write the receipt file itself.\n\nAlso, the ERR trap calls write_receipt, which uses python heredoc to check existing ones — no problem.\n\nFurthermore `trap 'on_error $LINENO' ERR` — under set -e, when python exits 1 (status fail), the ERR trap fires and overwrites the receipt? write_receipt won't overwrite an existing parseable receipt ✓ good, but it will also exit 1 ✓ as desired (failure propagates). But wait: on the FAIL status path, python writes the fail receipt and then exits… currently exits with 0 (the heredoc's exit status is the last command). I want to exit the script non-zero when status is fail. Let python exit 1 when status != pass — then the ERR trap fires, tries to write the receipt (kept, since it exists), deletes the container (already deleted), and exit 1 ✓.\n\nBut be careful — the ERR trap also fires for other failed commands like `podman image exists` (I made those guarded via the if condition — commands inside if conditions don't trigger ERR ✓). `podman logs` etc. are mostly guarded by || true.\n\nOne more thing: the python heredoc uses `podman(\"inspect\", ...)` etc., no problem. `stop = podman(\"stop\", ...)` — if the container has already exited (crashed earlier), stop will fail but insp can still fetch the exit code. OK.\n\nAlso, within python's `data[\"serve\"][\"port\"]`: API.rsplit(\":\",1)[-1] → \"1235\" ✓.\n\nOne more detail: FN_DATA/FN_STATUS via os.environ aren't needed after restructuring.\n\nAlso, regarding the python stage, the bash variable `$OUT` has been exported as FN_OUT ✓.\n\nLet me rewrite the end of run-proxy.sh: python writes the receipt itself, and exits with 0/1 according to status. Then bash does cleanup_container and exits with python's code… but set -e + ERR trap: when python exits 1, the trap fires: on_error writes a receipt (kept), cleanup, exit 1 ✓. The lines after python (`if [ ! -f \"$OUT\" ]...`) won't execute on fail — no problem.\n\nAlso, the final python check for receipt status becomes redundant. Remove it, and have the python stage itself propagate the exit code.\n\nLet me edit run-proxy.sh accordingly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The last stage has a bug: the python sets env vars that never propagate back to bash. Let me fix it so the python stage writes the receipt itself and propagates the status:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6098015a12af4ae5bd349fe2","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/scripts/run-proxy.sh","edits":[{"oldText":"podman(\"rm\", \"-f\", CTR)\n\njson_payload = json.dumps(data)\nos.environ[\"FN_DATA\"] = json_payload\nos.environ[\"FN_STATUS\"] = status\nPY\n# The python stage wrote the receipt via the same env contract the trap uses;\n# if it died before writing, the ERR trap already recorded the fail receipt.\nif [ ! -f \"$OUT\" ]; then\n    write_receipt \"fail\" '{\"error\": \"receipt stage never ran\"}'\nfi\ncleanup_container\n\nlog \"receipt: $OUT\"\npython3 - \"$OUT\" <<'PY'\nimport json, sys\nr = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\nsys.exit(0 if r.get(\"status\") == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY","newT