{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Also: when the python stage exits with 1 (fail status), the ERR trap fires and calls on_error → write_receipt's fail (if already exists it gets preserved ✓) → cleanup_container → exit 1. Good. But note: in the fail path, the receipt written by python is preserved, because write_receipt checks the existing parseable receipt with step==\"proxy\" ✓.\n\nHowever — in the success path, python exits 0, then cleanup_container (no-op, already rm'd), log. Good.\n\nOne issue: in the success path, the python stage has already done podman rm; then `podman rm -f` in cleanup_container becomes a no-op ✓.\n\nAlso, the trap on the python heredoc failure line: `trap 'on_error $LINENO' ERR` — when python exits 1, LINENO = line of the heredoc. OK.\n\nNext, also verify that the FN_DATA environment variable usage in on_error is fine: it builds the data JSON via python -c using FN_TAIL/FN_LINE, then calls write_receipt. OK.\n\nWait, there's still a problem with write_receipt in on_error: the `$(python3 -c ...)` command substitution inside on_error — if it fails, set -e... inside the trap, ERR trap recursion is disabled by default. Acceptable.\n\nNext, check bash -n on both scripts. Also grep for the acceptance literals. After that, write tests/test_proxy_tooling.py and run the test suite."}}
