{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Wait — `test_checkpoint_contract` uses a weird assertRegex against \"8 experts\". make-proxy.sh has `FN_PROXY_EXPERTS=\"${FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\"`. The regex I wrote is convoluted. I'll just simply assert `'\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS\"'`... no wait, in bash it's: FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8. Simplification: check `\"${FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\"`... assertIn(\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS\", make) plus a default of 8: the regex r\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8\"? But it also gets passed via `-e FN_PROXY_EXPERTS=`. Let me simplify: assertIn(\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\", self.make)? The exact string is `\"${FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\"`. I'll go with assertRegex(self.make, r\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8\\}\").\n\nAlso `test_failure_is_graded_never_skipped`: does self.run contain `\"step\": \"proxy\"`? My write_receipt's python has `\"step\": \"proxy\"` — in run-proxy.sh: `json.dump({\"step\": \"proxy\", ...` — yes, it contains `\"step\": \"proxy\"` (with quotes). Does it exist in the python heredoc that writes the receipt inside the main stage too? The main python writes the receipt dict {\"step\": \"proxy\", ...} ✓. And \"fail\" is obviously present too ✓.\n\nAlso let me verify whether \"mmap_engaged\" is present in run-proxy ✓ (the data key).\n\n\"PIECEWISE\" ✓, \"--enforce-eager\" is absent ✓ (checking my own file — it doesn't appear anywhere; the header says \"plain-eager mode\"). Let me verify with grep.\n\nNow let's run the tests."}}
