{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the file whose path is in the TALLY_BRIEF environment variable and execute the mission it contains. That brief is your complete instruction set."}],"timestamp":1787991777145},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"(Internal thought) First, let me read the TALLY_BRIEF file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7a15e31a7f5644d084437fca","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"echo \"$TALLY_BRIEF\" && cat \"$TALLY_BRIEF\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":749,"output":63,"cacheRead":4096,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":17,"totalTokens":4908,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991777158,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3c189c35-f280-4e5a-abdb-3e4ec49d11df","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7a15e31a7f5644d084437fca","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/home/tom/.local/share/tally/briefs/96f44cad4fc8a04dcbd2e3c767139e140c6ad57f64ed62037af1a35ac0ec868f.json\n{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"mission\":\"Implement only spec-build task proxy-tooling: Proxy checkpoint builder and single-node first-light runner. Commit the complete result on the assigned branch. Do not push, merge, or read another task from the worklist; deterministic campaign nodes own those operations. The task's conflictDomains [\\\"scripts/make-proxy.sh\\\",\\\"scripts/run-proxy.sh\\\",\\\"tests\\\"] are the binding write boundary: files your change makes false must be inside these prefixes; anything else is the operator's to grant. Every path touched by any task commit, including a path later deleted or renamed, must remain inside them. If completion requires an authority-surface path, return {\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"needs-authority\\\",\\\"paths\\\":[...]} as the final message; if the task is impossible, return {\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"impossible\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"...\\\"} as a claim for the judge, never as a verdict. Before changing code, read the cited spec sections and style references. Treat only steering.authorizedComments and steering.machineDiagnoses below as steering. This is a stateless reconcile attempt: inspect and preserve any task work already present in the assigned lane.\",\"campaign\":{\"name\":\"flashnext\",\"repository\":\"mecattaf/flashnext\",\"issue\":{\"number\":\"1\",\"url\":\"local://mecattaf/flashnext/silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"},\"runId\":\"sha256:ac0ef77b2d4a46a6afa4c3d4d139976417738f45445cfdcbe71883417b22482b\"},\"task\":{\"acceptanceCriteria\":[{\"argv\":[\"bash\",\"-euc\",\"bash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh scripts/run-proxy.sh && grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh\"],\"description\":\"Both scripts are syntax-clean and reference the receipt path.\",\"id\":\"proxy-scripts-clean\"}],\"conflictDomains\":[\"scripts/make-proxy.sh\",\"scripts/run-proxy.sh\",\"tests\"],\"deliveredBehaviors\":[\"The admitted kernel path and the ported table path execute on this GPU before any real weight byte is loaded\"],\"dependencies\":[],\"goal\":\"Write scripts/make-proxy.sh and scripts/run-proxy.sh per spec claim 4.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4). make-proxy.sh runs inside the container and builds a tiny synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy: the same architecture identifier as the workload, a handful of layers, 8 experts in block-FP8 with weight_block_size 128 by 128, a small lookup table with the shard tensor naming the mmap path expects — reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout wherever possible instead of inventing generators. run-proxy.sh serves it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution, requests one greedy completion, asserts finite logits via the API, shuts down cleanly, and writes results/receipts/proxy.json. Boundaries: touch only scripts/make-proxy.sh, scripts/run-proxy.sh, and tests/.\",\"id\":\"proxy-tooling\",\"kind\":\"implementation\",\"readFirst\":{\"specSections\":[\"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4\",\"specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md\",\"specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md\"],\"styleReferences\":[\"scripts/run-smoke.sh\"]},\"revision\":\"sha256:e5bea94a618c21673e727d80dc72777cd31604d0c28385bf83f7b349c10d0bfd\",\"title\":\"Proxy checkpoint builder and single-node first-light runner\"},\"workspace\":{\"baseRev\":\"71f2beba0ecd7358eda99e574e078d081f55bbfa\",\"branch\":\"tally-work/flashnext-cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\",\"conflictDomains\":[\"scripts/make-proxy.sh\",\"scripts/run-proxy.sh\",\"tests\"],\"publishBranch\":\"tally/flashnext-campaign-01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/proxy-tooling-e5bea94a618c2167\",\"taskId\":\"proxy-tooling\",\"worktreePath\":\"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\"},\"steering\":{\"channel\":\"locally-authorized-snapshot\",\"authorizedComments\":[],\"attemptReceipt\":{\"lateRecheckCommentIds\":[],\"preparedCommentIds\":[],\"recheckTruncated\":false,\"rechecked\":true,\"source\":{\"kind\":\"local-jsonl\",\"path\":\"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/steering/01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/steering-v1.jsonl\",\"preparedCursor\":4,\"recheckedCursor\":4,\"registrationId\":\"01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae\"}},\"machineDiagnoses\":[],\"machineOutcomes\":[]}}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991780427},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a spec-build task for flashnext. Things to do:\n\n1. Read the worktree /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\n2. Read the spec sections: specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4, specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md, specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md, and scripts/run-smoke.sh\n3. Following the claim in spec 4.1, write scripts/make-proxy.sh and scripts/run-proxy.sh\n4. Commit to the assigned branch tally-work/flashnext-cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\n5. Don't push.\n\nLet me explore the worktree.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_56b9d422022f4715b7985f99","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && git status && git log --oneline -5 && git branch --show-current && ls"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1912,"output":247,"cacheRead":4224,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":169,"totalTokens":6383,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991780431,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3549012d-4311-459f-9c54-eaa36f52ac84","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_56b9d422022f4715b7985f99","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On branch tally-work/flashnext-cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n71f2beb bench-harness: Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix\nb474021 host-tooling: Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\ne4ac78a instruments: Engagement-proof instruments adapted into the container overlay\nc1363cb container-recipe: Container recipe: pinned wheel set + engine fork built from source\na428fee handoff: staging complete, campaign launches attended-by-daylight not overnight\ntally-work/flashnext-cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling\nbench\ncontainer\ndocs\nflake.lock\nflake.nix\nhandoff\nhost\nIMPORTS.md\nLICENSE\npatches\nREADME.md\nresults\nscripts\nsilent-factory-worklists\nspecs\ntests\nTHIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991786102},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — a `tests/` directory already exists in the repo. Let me take a look at the spec sections and existing files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_12177c14ebbd4654b33a55c6","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && ls scripts tests 2>/dev/null && sed -n '1,50p' specs/flashnext/spec.md | head -5"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2380,"output":118,"cacheRead":4224,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":27,"totalTokens":6722,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991786106,"responseId":"chatcmpl-104818b3-ab4a-4d15-90c3-3cb7d7b9c438","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_12177c14ebbd4654b33a55c6","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"scripts:\nbuild-engine.sh\nmirror-substrate.sh\nreceipts-verify.py\nrun-smoke.sh\nrun-tp2.sh\nstage-weights-both.sh\nstage-weights.sh\nverify-fork.sh\n\ntests:\ntest_bench_client.py\ntest_host_tooling.py\ntest_instruments.py\ntest_repo_invariants.py\n# flashnext — the vendor FP8 release at tensor-parallel two on the coordinator/worker pair\n\nStatus: ratified 2026-08-28\nGoverns: silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\nConsumers: the flashnext worklist readFirst anchors; the S1 boundary sitting; the S3 morning review\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991789931},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"tests/ already exists, but it's repo-level tests, not the fork's tests/models scaffolding. The task states \"when possible, reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork's checkout.\" The fork checkout probably happens inside the container at build time. Let me read spec.md section r4, the evidence file, and run-smoke.sh.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_42de7d0e001646ab965b08df","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -n \"r4\\|4.1\\|proxy\" specs/flashnext/spec.md | head -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":609,"output":165,"cacheRead":6272,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":77,"totalTokens":7046,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991789935,"responseId":"chatcmpl-01b4b4ec-57a3-4c17-b229-1fe2887ab00d","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_42de7d0e001646ab965b08df","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"18:light on a proxy checkpoint, first light on the pair, warmed-decode residency,\n49:- proxy checkpoint (NEW) — a small synthetic checkpoint with the same\n82:| P6 | cherry-picks | upstream pull requests 46012, 40963, 51511, 46110 enter the fork with Cherry-picked-from trailers; 44331, 46186, 46676 receive no task in this campaign and are recorded in IMPORTS.md for the four-bit lane |\n117:4.1 the proxy checkpoint serves on one node with the mmap path engaged → a proxy receipt records finite output and a clean shutdown. [gate: receipts-verify]\n150:UNKNOWN-1 whether the fused-mixture block-FP8 kernel is numerically sound on this GPU once admitted — drained by claims 4.1 and 4.4.\n153:UNKNOWN-4 the fork's runtime behavior on the ruling P4 wheel set — drained by claims 3.1 and 4.1.\n166:Order: weights staging beside container build, then smoke, then proxy, then\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991794545},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me carefully read the full text of spec.md, particularly the proxy checkpoint definition and Claim 4.1.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b019250b7ed74699917984f8","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/specs/flashnext/spec.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1050,"output":85,"cacheRead":6272,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":23,"totalTokens":7407,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991794549,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3b40fcc1-b5c6-4400-9b5f-c41ba8507c7f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b019250b7ed74699917984f8","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# flashnext — the vendor FP8 release at tensor-parallel two on the coordinator/worker pair\n\nStatus: ratified 2026-08-28\nGoverns: silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\nConsumers: the flashnext worklist readFirst anchors; the S1 boundary sitting; the S3 morning review\nSupersedes: none\n\n## Outcome\n\nToday the pair serves nothing of this class: the stock engine refuses the FP8\nmixture layer on this GPU generation, the platform tree cannot load the FP8\nlookup table, and no container, units, or benchmarks exist for the pair. After\nthis campaign the estate carries a fork whose two patches admit the mixture\npath and port the disk-backed table onto the platform tree, a container built\nfrom that fork at an exactly pinned wheel set, host tooling that stands the\ntwo-node service on both rails with the wire as control, staged weights on both\nnodes, and a witnessed measurement record. Overnight checkpoints prove first\nlight on a proxy checkpoint, first light on the pair, warmed-decode residency,\na fidelity baseline, and counterbalanced benchmarks, each leaving a receipt the\nladder verifies. The morning operator arrives to a serving pair with numbers,\nor to a typed blocker naming its upstream cause with a drafted issue — never to\na silent skip.\n\n## Vocabulary\n\n- pair — the hosts coordinator and worker, two identical 128 GB unified-memory\n  desktops on the same fleet substrate.\n- rails — the interfaces thunderbolt0 and thunderbolt1, the inference plane,\n  socket transport only; one rail is cabled TB5 and one TB3, both trained at\n  40 Gb/s in the measured record, re-read at first light.\n- wire — the interface enp191s0, the control plane: ssh, orchestration,\n  staging.\n- workload checkpoint (NEW) — the vendor FP8 release at upstream revision\n  970c569adaca6b35532111fd6b27351b2baefe50; node-side artifact directory\n  /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8; full naming lives in README.md and the\n  evidence, never in this file or the worklist.\n- engram table — the 51.2-billion-parameter hash-lookup embedding inside the\n  workload checkpoint, read once per token at one layer.\n- fork (NEW) — the engine fork published as github mecattaf/vllm branch\n  flashnext; base commit 8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da, the\n  table pull request's head, which already carries the model-support pull\n  request's model code.\n- admission patch (NEW) — the fork change that admits the block-FP8 mixture\n  scheme on this GPU and adds the in-kernel upcast, switch FN_FP8_MOE.\n- table port (NEW) — the fork change that wires the mmap table path and the\n  FP8 embedding stack into the platform tree the pair runs.\n- mmap path (NEW) — the disk-backed table mechanism behind VLLM_PLE_MMAP:\n  page-cache faults serve gathers, zero table bytes GPU-resident.\n- proxy checkpoint (NEW) — a small synthetic checkpoint with the same\n  architecture identifier and expert format, for single-node first light\n  before the real weights are touched.\n- engine build (NEW) — the fork made runnable per ruling P4: the container\n  lane tonight, the nix graduation lane once the patch set freezes; the build\n  receipt records which lane served.\n- container (NEW) — the image flashnext:dev built by container/build.sh from\n  the fork at stable vendor wheels, with a bind-mounted cached build\n  directory for incremental iteration.\n- pair service (NEW) — the orchestration under host/ standing both ranks:\n  container up on both nodes, distributed head and worker, then the serve\n  process at tensor-parallel two.\n- receipt (NEW) — one JSON record under results/receipts written by an\n  overnight step; the ladder gate validates every receipt against its bounds.\n- bench matrix (NEW) — the counterbalanced measurement protocol of ruling P12.\n- fidelity baseline (NEW) — reference losses and frontier logits captured at\n  first light, the yardstick for every later change.\n- morning ledger (NEW) — docs/MORNING.md, rendered overnight: receipts, open\n  items, promotion checklist, blocker entries.\n- RDMA package (NEW) — the operator-attended fast-path bring-up prepared under\n  host/rdma: pinned out-of-tree module fetch (never vendored), the single-rail\n  contract, the coordinated-reboot checklist, and the socket-versus-verbs\n  measurement protocol.\n\n## Rulings\n\n| id | decision | ruling |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | identity and timing | identity flashnext; the worklist and every gate id below are authored at this S1 sitting; the Governs line resolves before ratification |\n| P2 | workload naming | model-family names stay out of spec and worklist bytes (lint L16); the workload is named by revision and by the node-side artifact directory; full names live in README.md and evidence |\n| P3 | fork base | the table pull request's head is the base — it carries the model-support pull request's model code; the model-support branch's later head commit is CI-only and is not taken |\n| P4 | engine substrate | the container lane serves the night: stable-index torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 wheels — the fork's exact pin, published on the vendor stable channel — with the fork built from source in an image whose build directory is bind-mounted with compiler caching, so a one-line patch iteration recompiles only what changed; the nix derivation is the graduation lane, prepared tonight as wiring plus audit but never tonight's critical path — a moving patch set is not a thing to nixify, and the nix substrate charges a full recompile for every one-line change; the build receipt names the lane |\n| P5 | admission shape | the admission extends the upcast mechanism of upstream pull request 52970 to the fused-mixture oracle; refusal stays loud; FN_FP8_MOE=0 restores the stock refusal; no silent fallback is added |\n| P6 | cherry-picks | upstream pull requests 46012, 40963, 51511, 46110 enter the fork with Cherry-picked-from trailers; 44331, 46186, 46676 receive no task in this campaign and are recorded in IMPORTS.md for the four-bit lane |\n| P7 | transport | socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record, both rails named to the collective library; the RDMA package is prepared overnight but brought up only operator-attended, on one rail only, and only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked — the kernel half is entirely unpaid on this fleet, the worker's deploy path must move off the fast rail before any kernel change, the reboot order is worker first and never both at once, and adoption comes only on a counterbalanced win |\n| P8 | weights | staged library-to-node by scripts/stage-weights.sh with a per-file digest manifest; the fleet catalog row is a morning operator act prepared under handoff/; runtime hub downloads stay forbidden |\n| P9 | unattended overnight | GPU checkpoints run unattended; a wedge needing physical presence ends the campaign with the blocker typed in the morning ledger; no overnight reboot of either node |\n| P10 | first light mode | the table path's own guard demands piecewise capture with the mmap operation as a split boundary and refuses plain eager — first light runs that sanctioned mode; full-graph capture is morning work |\n| P11 | residency verdict | read after 50 warmed decode tokens on both ranks, never at load; GTT, RSS, and table page-cache residency recorded; pass bound 80 GiB per rank with zero table bytes GPU-resident |\n| P12 | bench protocol | three loads per arm, interleaved arms, medians, token fingerprints; depth series 0, 10240, 102400; speculative on and off arms; rows committed under results/ |\n| P13 | promotion | a morning human act; nothing overnight changes any fleet roster or default |\n| P14 | oracle provenance | the gate ids below are created in the governing worklist at this sitting; heavy overnight proofs bind through receipts validated by the receipts gate, and the trace joins each claim to its producing checkpoint |\n| P15 | compute routing | campaign adapter pi with the model unset — the host catalog answers; the fork engineering is estate bootstrap authored before arming and reviewed in-session |\n| P16 | blocker protocol | an unresolvable upstream defect ends as drafted issue text under handoff/upstream-issues plus a morning-ledger entry, never as a silent skip |\n\n## Claims\n\n### R1 — provenance and the public face\nWhy: every downstream judgment leans on the evidence corpus, and the repository is public from birth; a dangling citation is a fabricated fact.\n1.1 the seven sweep dossiers land under `specs/flashnext/evidence/` → the tests suite counts at least 7 (given) dossiers. [gate: repo-tests]\n1.2 `IMPORTS.md` names every external artifact with source, revision or pull-request id, license, and role → the notices file covers the same set, asserted by the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n1.3 `README.md` states the project, the topology, and the evidence trail → the tests suite's secret scan passes on the published tree. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n### R2 — the fork\nWhy: the fork is the only path this class of workload can take on this GPU; its content must be provable from the estate, not narrated.\n2.1 BELIEVE:IMPORTS.md — the fork base is the table pull request's head commit 8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da → the fork-verify gate confirms base ancestry on the published fork branch. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.2 the admission patch lands on the fork → the verify script finds the FN_FP8_MOE switch and the upcast plumbing in the fused-mixture layer tree. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.3 the table port lands on the fork → the verify script finds the mmap import, the FP8 dequant, and the relocated mmap module in the platform tree. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.4 the four ruling P6 cherry-picks land on the fork → the verify script finds each signature. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.5 every fork commit past the base is mirrored under `patches/` → the verify script counts mirrored patches equal to fork commits. [gate: fork-verify]\n\n### R3 — the container\nWhy: the pinned wheel set and the fork meet for the first time inside the image; the smoke step answers the cheap questions before any weight byte moves.\n3.1 the engine build lands through the ruling P4 container lane → a build receipt records status pass with the lane, the resolved torch and triton versions, and the wall-clock. [gate: receipts-verify]\n3.2 `scripts/run-smoke.sh` runs against the image → a smoke receipt records the GPU architecture string, a finite fp8 storage cast, the registered architecture identifier, the aperture, and the admission verdict. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R4 — engine proof\nWhy: nobody anywhere has run this workload on this GPU at tensor-parallel two; each step below converts one unknown into a receipt.\n4.1 the proxy checkpoint serves on one node with the mmap path engaged → a proxy receipt records finite output and a clean shutdown. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.2 the pair service reaches first light eager over both rails → a tp2 receipt records a greedy 300 (given)-token completion byte-identical across two (given) runs and the per-rail link speeds. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.3 residency is read per ruling P11 → a residency receipt lands inside the per-rank bound with zero engram table bytes GPU-resident. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.4 the fidelity baseline lands → reference losses and frontier logits are stored under `results/` beside their receipt. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.5 context rises to 262144 (given) → a context receipt records decode within 10 (given) percent of the short-context figure. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R5 — weights\nWhy: the pair serves the workload checkpoint from its own drives; staging is verified movement, not trust.\n5.1 `scripts/stage-weights.sh` places the workload checkpoint on both nodes → weight receipts record at least 131 (given) shards per node with byte totals matching the library source. [gate: receipts-verify]\n5.2 the fleet catalog patch is prepared under `handoff/` → the receipts gate parses it as a well-formed patch when present. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R6 — service and measurement\nWhy: a number without its protocol is an anecdote; the harness and its client are graded before any number is quoted.\n6.1 the pair-service tooling lands under `host/` → unit files carry a stop-post teardown and no boot-time install, asserted by the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n6.2 the bench client separates queue wait from prefill → a client unit test with injected queueing passes in the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n6.3 the bench matrix runs per ruling P12 → a bench receipt records three (given) loads per arm, counterbalanced, with token fingerprints, and rows committed under `results/`. [gate: receipts-verify]\n6.4 the RDMA package lands per ruling P7 → the tests suite's package assertions pass: syntax-clean scripts, pinned module fetch, no unattended install path. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n### R7 — the morning and the blocker protocol\nWhy: promotion is a human act and the campaign's failure mode is typed, never silent.\n7.1 the morning ledger renders overnight → the operator reads receipts, open items, and the promotion checklist in one file. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.2 an overnight step fails for an upstream cause → drafted issue text lands under `handoff/upstream-issues/` and the morning ledger names the blocker. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.3 the morning operator reviews receipts and ledger at a keyboard → a promotion or blocker disposition is recorded, the catalog patch applied on promotion. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.4 the operator brings the RDMA package up attended, one rail, per its checklist → a socket-versus-verbs comparison lands under `results/` before any transport adoption. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n\n## Unchanged\n\nU.1 the GTT page ceiling stays 33554432 (given) pages on both nodes → the smoke receipt records that value. [gate: receipts-verify]\nU.2 the latency hold and its tripwire stay armed on both nodes → the tp2 receipt records held sub-budget round trips on both ends. [gate: receipts-verify]\nU.3 runtime hub downloads stay forbidden → the tests suite rejects any worklist bytes carrying a hub-download flag. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n## Unknowns\n\nUNKNOWN-1 whether the fused-mixture block-FP8 kernel is numerically sound on this GPU once admitted — drained by claims 4.1 and 4.4.\nUNKNOWN-2 whether fp8 storage plus the widening cast works on the pinned torch build — drained by claim 3.2.\nUNKNOWN-3 whether the collective library crosses the pair at tensor-parallel two over socket transport — drained by claim 4.2.\nUNKNOWN-4 the fork's runtime behavior on the ruling P4 wheel set — drained by claims 3.1 and 4.1.\nUNKNOWN-5 speculative-decode acceptance on real prompts and the draft-length optimum — recorded by the bench receipt; tuning is morning work.\nUNKNOWN-6 the wall-clock of the first full container engine build on this machine — measured by the build receipt; iteration after it rides the cached build directory.\nUNKNOWN-7 whether the pinned packaging expression satisfies every override signature and literal against the fork tree — the graduation-lane question, drained by the substrate-compat audit; it blocks nothing tonight.\nDECISION-1 does the container carry the aiter library? proposed: no — the admission path is self-contained and the aiter half of the pattern donor stays out (given)\n\n## Stages\n\n### S1 — bootstrap\nThis sitting: estate skeleton, evidence corpus, fork engineering, worklist\nauthored, both repositories published. Claims R1, R2; rulings P1 to P16.\n\n### S2 — overnight\nOrder: weights staging beside container build, then smoke, then proxy, then\npair first light, then residency and fidelity, then the bench matrix; the\nservice and measurement tooling lands ahead of the checkpoints that call it.\nClaims R3, R4, R5, R6; rulings P4 to P12.\n\n### S3 — morning\nOrder: ledger review, disposition, catalog patch on promotion, then the\noptimization menu in the morning ledger. Claims R7; ruling P13.\n\n## Forbidden\n\nF.1 Never load an unsigned kernel module or bring RDMA up unattended, and never on both rails.\nF.2 Do not import the upstream tuning module into any host closure.\nF.3 Do not add keys to the worklist schema.\nF.4 Never write under specs/flashnext/ from any lane.\nF.5 Do not put model-family names in spec or worklist bytes.\nF.6 Do not download weights at runtime.\nF.7 Never decide the residency verdict from load-time readings.\nF.8 Do not commit a benchmark number from a single uncounterbalanced run.\nF.9 Do not export an environment default the engine reads through an is-set probe.\nF.10 Do not vendor GPL code into the estate.\nF.11 Do not pull any wheel outside the two sanctioned sources — the realized substrate in the store and the vendor stable index.\nF.12 Never record an overnight step as skipped without a typed blocker in the morning ledger.\nF.13 Do not reboot either node overnight.\nF.14 Do not open any upstream pull request or issue from a lane.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991797798},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Note: Claim 4.1 states \"the mmap path is active\" — however, the task description says \"eager execution.\" Also, Judgment P10 states \"The table path's own guard requires piecewise capture with mmap ops as split boundaries, and rejects raw eager — the initial launch operates in that permitted mode.\" However, the task body says \"serve it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" Hmm, this task body is the binding instruction set (\"the mission it contains... that brief is your complete instruction set\"). Hmm, actually the task goal says \"run-proxy.sh serves it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution, requests one greedy completion, asserts finite logits via the API, shuts down cleanly, and writes results/receipts/proxy.json.\"\n\nWait — but P10 says eager is rejected. Let me read the evidence files, particularly ple-54129.md, which likely describes the mmap guard and the permitted enforcement mode.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8a712a88506d4b80a7576ab8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && ls specs/flashnext/evidence/ && wc -l specs/flashnext/evidence/*.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":4357,"output":297,"cacheRead":7168,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":217,"totalTokens":11822,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991797803,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bc1286f5-8756-4a83-a13e-deb2b0b04aff","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8a712a88506d4b80a7576ab8","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"dotfiles-observed.md\nds4-vllm-manifest.md\ninnovation-ledger.md\nkyuz0-rocm10.md\nmoe-dispatch.md\nnix-hardening-addendum.md\nnix-packaging-brief.md\nnix-strix-halo.md\nple-54129.md\nqsa-53896.md\ntally-fit-analysis.md\n   685 specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md\n  1273 specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md\n   326 specs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md\n   689 specs/flashnext/evidence/kyuz0-rocm10.md\n   653 specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md\n   207 specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-hardening-addendum.md\n   316 specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-packaging-brief.md\n  1302 specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md\n   378 specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md\n   517 specs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md\n    54 specs/flashnext/evidence/tally-fit-analysis.md\n  6400 total\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991805940},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me read ple-54129.md and moe-dispatch.md. Also run-smoke.sh for the style.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":875,"output":98,"cacheRead":11264,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":27,"totalTokens":12237,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991805944,"responseId":"chatcmpl-dd424ebb-d673-4bfa-995c-6cd34f91f9e2","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3547c248648e434aac3d3606","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: vLLM PR #54129 (`VLLM_PLE_MMAP`, branch `ple-mmap-upstream`) — AMD/ROCm reachability\n\nInvestigator session: 2026-08-28. Analysis only; no code written or patched.\n\n**Checkouts read this session**\n- `#54129`: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-tom-mecattaf-notes-qwen-next/c4262a6d-5cde-494b-9143-685dd896a84f/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129`\n  HEAD `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream`, `2026-08-28 11:52:45 +0700`, author *Christopher Deodat* [M: `git log -1 --format='%ci %an %s'`]\n- `#53896`: `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr53896`\n  HEAD `89d0bb7 update CI`, `2026-08-28 16:54:24 +0800`, author *huanghaoyan.hhy* [M: same command]\n- Raw diff: `.../scratchpad/pr54129.diff`, 26483 lines [M: `wc -l`]\n\nGrades: **[S]** read from source · **[M]** measured (command output) · **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n---\n\n## Q0 — Headline answer\n\n**Yes.** The mmap/engram path is wired exclusively into `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/`. On a ROCm build the `amd/` tree is imported instead, `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is never imported, the custom op is never registered, and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` is silently a **no-op** — no error, no warning, the stock GPU-resident `VocabParallelEmbedding` is built as if the flag were unset.\n\n**Measured reachability** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n 'ple_mmap\\|PLE_MMAP\\|mmap\\|Mmap\\|MMAP' \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py \\\n    vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output — zero hits in all four files)\n```\nversus 11 hits in `nvidia/ple_layer.py` and 5 in `nvidia/model.py`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'nvidia' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ vllm/models/qwen4_exp/config.py\n(no output)\n```\nThe `amd/` tree never reaches into `nvidia/`. [M]\n\n**Correction to the campaign record**: the port is **not ~15 lines**. See §\"Exact port specification\" — it is ~12 lines in `amd/model.py` plus ~45 code lines (~60 with the load-bearing comments) in `amd/ple_layer.py`, **and** it depends on an FP8 dequant capability that `amd/ple_layer.py` does not currently have at all (§Blocker A). That last item is the real finding.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Platform dispatch — how a ROCm build lands in `amd/`\n\nSelection is a lazy module `__getattr__` on the model package, not a registry-level split.\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/__init__.py:22-49` [S]:\n```python\ndef __getattr__(name: str) -> Any:\n    if name in {\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\", \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\", \"Qwen4ExpMTP\"}:\n        from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n        if current_platform.is_xpu() or current_platform.is_tpu():\n            raise NotImplementedError(\"Qwen4Exp currently supports CUDA and ROCm only\")\n        if current_platform.is_rocm():\n            from .amd.model import (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM, Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration)\n            from .amd.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n        else:\n            from .nvidia.model import (...)\n            from .nvidia.mtp import Qwen4ExpMTP\n```\n\nChain of evidence:\n- Registry points at the package, not a platform subtree: `vllm/model_executor/models/registry.py:114-117` `\"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\": (\"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\", \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\")`; `:580-583` for `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration`; `:669` for `Qwen4ExpMTP`. [S]\n- `is_rocm()` is `self._enum == PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/interface.py:192-193`. [S]\n- `RocmPlatform._enum = PlatformEnum.ROCM`, `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:498-499`. [S]\n- Platform selection: `vllm/platforms/__init__.py:110-128` — `rocm_platform_plugin()` returns `\"vllm.platforms.rocm.RocmPlatform\"` iff `amdsmi` imports and `amdsmi_get_processor_handles()` is non-empty. [S]\n\n**Is there any chance a ROCm build imports the nvidia tree?** No, on the model path. [S]\n- The `else:` branch at `__init__.py:38-43` is the only importer of `nvidia/model.py` / `nvidia/mtp.py`, and it is unreachable when `is_rocm()` is true.\n- `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` is imported from exactly two places, both inside the nvidia tree: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:52` and `nvidia/model.py:77`. [S/M]\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/__init__.py` is a 3-line SPDX-only stub (no re-exports) [S] — so a hypothetical `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` from `amd/` would import *only* `ple_mmap.py`, dragging in no CUDA-specific nvidia module. That is what makes option (a) in §Port viable.\n\nConsequence: the module-scope `direct_register_custom_op(...)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:981-986` never runs on ROCm, so `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` does not exist there — even though the op *name* is unconditionally present in the default splitting-ops list (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`). [S]\n\n---\n\n## 2. TP semantics of the mmap path\n\n### Vocab-parallel path: a per-lookup all-reduce\n`vllm/model_executor/layers/vocab_parallel_embedding.py:490-518` [S]:\n```python\ndef forward(self, input_):\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        masked_input, input_mask = get_masked_input_and_mask(...)   # :493-500\n    ...\n    output_parallel = self.quant_method.embedding(self, masked_input.long())   # :504\n    if self.tp_size > 1:\n        if output_parallel.dtype in (torch.float8_e4m3fn, torch.float8_e5m2):\n            comm_output = output_parallel.view(torch.int8)                     # :512\n            comm_output.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :513\n            output = tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(comm_output)             # :514\n            return output.view(output_parallel.dtype)\n        output_parallel.masked_fill_(input_mask.unsqueeze(-1), 0)              # :516\n        return tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce(output_parallel)               # :517\n    return output_parallel\n```\nThe FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n\nSo: **yes, one `tensor_model_parallel_all_reduce` per PLE lookup, per PLE layer, per forward** whenever `tp_size > 1`.\n\n### Mmap path: plain `nn.Module`, whole table per rank, no collective\n`MmapNgramEmbedding` is declared `class MmapNgramEmbedding(nn.Module)` at `nvidia/ple_mmap.py:506` [S] and constructed with the **full padded vocab**: `ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:288-290` [S] — the identical `padded_vocab_size` handed to the vocab-parallel constructor in the `else:` branch at `:292-293`.\n\nIts `forward` (`ple_mmap.py:545-573`) contains **no collective, no mask, no rank arithmetic**:\n```\n$ grep -n 'rank\\|tp_\\|world_size\\|parallel\\|all_reduce\\|distributed' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n(no output; exit 1)\n```\n[M] — zero hits across the whole 1010-line file.\n\n`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n```python\nfor idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n    self.mm[idx] = np.memmap(path, dtype=np.uint8, mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))\n    self.rows_total += rows\n```\nand `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n\n**Verdict**: switching to the mmap path removes the per-token all-reduce/all-gather at the PLE lookup site entirely. The trade is that every rank independently gathers the full-width rows for every token — TP-redundant I/O and TP-redundant host→device copies, in exchange for zero NCCL/RCCL traffic at that site. (The redundancy hits page cache, shared per host, so the *disk* cost is not multiplied — that last clause is my inference from `mode=\"r\"` shared file mappings, not read from source; treat as reasoning, not evidence.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. `load_weights` under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n\nThe interception lives in `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:450-547`. Two mmap-specific branches:\n\n**(a) weight_scale kept** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:477-490` [S]:\n```python\nif (isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding)\n        and name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"):\n    ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight,\n                              cast(torch.Tensor, self.layer_multipliers).device)\n    loaded.add(name); continue\n```\n`set_weight_scale` registers a non-persistent buffer, `ple_mmap.py:587-590`. [S]\n\n**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n```python\nif isinstance(embedding, ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding):\n    # Served from disk via mmap; the loader still streams\n    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n    embedding.weights_streamed = True\n    loaded.add(\"ngram_embedding.weight\")\n    continue\n```\n`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n\nThe stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n\n**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n- Header-only reads; the docstring at `:186-191` says \"never the multi-GiB tensor data\". [S/CL]\n- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n\n**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n\nNuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n\nContract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory/staging behavior relevant to UMA (line numbers at THIS head)\n\nAll in `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`:\n\n| Property | Line(s) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `np.memmap(..., mode=\"r\", offset=offset, shape=(rows, row_bytes))` | **331-333** | [S] read-only shared mapping, `dtype=np.uint8` |\n| Dedup via `np.unique(..., return_inverse=True)` | **360** | [S] `uniq, inverse = np.unique(ids, return_inverse=True)` |\n| Re-expansion to input order | **405** | [S] `gathered = out[inverse]` |\n| `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self.workers)` | **335** | [S] |\n| Chunked tasks, `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` rows each | **377-378** | [S] `for c in range(s, e, self.chunk): tasks.append((si, c, min(c + self.chunk, e)))` |\n| Fan-out over the pool | **391-395** | [S] single task runs inline; else `self.pool.map(run, tasks)` |\n| Fancy-index gather off the memmap (page faults do the I/O; GIL released) | **387** + comment **385-386** | [S/CL] `out[a:b] = mm[local[a:b]]` |\n| **Staging buffer is pageable, not pinned** | **369** | [S] `out = np.empty((uniq.size, self.row_bytes), dtype=np.uint8)` — plain NumPy heap alloc; `torch.from_numpy(rows)` at **567** wraps it |\n| Explicitly stated non-pinned | **568-571** | [CL — comment] \"`rows` … is pageable host memory, not pinned, so this H2D copy is effectively synchronous. Pinned staging (Phase-4 lever 5) is a separate, not-yet-pulled lever\" |\n| H2D copy | **572** | [S] `out = out.to(ids.device, non_blocking=True)` — no-op non-blocking per the comment above |\n| **Device→host sync of the ids, every call** | **558** | [S] `ids_np = ids.detach().to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=False).numpy().reshape(-1)` — an explicit blocking D2H per PLE layer per forward |\n| Prewarm streams via `open(..., buffering=0)` in 64 MiB blocks | **442**, **456-465** | [S] `block = 64 << 20` |\n| Prewarm bound = `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable - 8 GiB))` | **91**, **493-500** | [S] `_PREWARM_HEADROOM_BYTES = 8 << 30` |\n| `MemAvailable` read from `/proc/meminfo` | **484-490** | [S] |\n| Pool teardown idempotent | **468-481** | [S] |\n| p99 latency logging, at most once per 60 s | **92**, **411-429** | [S] `_LOG_INTERVAL_S = 60.0` |\n\n**All four campaign claims about staging are confirmed at this head**, with no line drift beyond what is tabulated above.\n\n### CUDA-specific calls that would break under ROCm/HIP — the grep\n```\n$ grep -n 'torch\\.cuda\\|pin_memory\\|cudaHost\\|hip\\|\\.cuda()' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py\n604:        of splitting_ops membership.\n```\n[M] — the single hit is a false positive: `hip` inside the word \"mem­ber**ship**\" on a comment line. There are **no** `torch.cuda.*` calls, no `pin_memory`, no `cudaHostRegister`, no explicit `torch.cuda.Stream`/`current_stream`, and no `.cuda()` in the file.\n\nThe only platform touchpoints in `ple_mmap.py`, and their ROCm status:\n\n1. `from vllm.platforms import current_platform` (`:67`) and `if current_platform.dispatch_key != \"CPU\": vllm_lib.impl(OP_NAME, ..., dispatch_key=\"CPU\")` (`:993-994`). [S]\n   **Safe on ROCm**: `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key: str = \"CUDA\"` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:502` [S] (same value as `vllm/platforms/cuda.py:212` [S]). PyTorch/HIP reuses the CUDA dispatch key, so `direct_register_custom_op` at `:981-986` registers correctly and the CPU-key double-registration guard behaves identically.\n2. `check_cudagraph_safety` (`:597-635`) reads only `CompilationConfig` fields. `has_full_cudagraphs()` is defined in `vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84` [S] — platform-agnostic. ROCm already drives `cudagraph_mode` through the same enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`, incl. `compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE`) and returns `\"vllm.compilation.cuda_graph.CUDAGraphWrapper\"` at `rocm.py:1002`. [S]\n3. `_mem_available_bytes` reads `/proc/meminfo` (`:484-490`) — Linux-generic, not vendor-specific. [S]\n4. **The one real ROCm risk: hardcoded `torch.float8_e4m3fn`.** `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` at `:78-84`, and `MmapNgramEmbedding.__init__` defaults `self.torch_dtype = torch.float8_e4m3fn` at `:535`. [S] On gfx94x (MI300X/MI325X) `RocmPlatform.fp8_dtype()` returns `torch.float8_e4m3fnuz` — `vllm/platforms/rocm.py:975-984` [S]. `is_fp8()` accepts both (`vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py:42-45` [S]), so the dequant *gate* still fires; but the tensor handed downstream is `e4m3fn`, not the platform's fnuz. Whether a ROCm PyTorch build on gfx942 supports `float8_e4m3fn` storage + the `.to(bf16)` cast used by the dequant is **UNDETERMINED** from source — settling it needs `torch.zeros(1, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\").to(torch.bfloat16)` executed on the target ROCm build. Note the bytes on disk are OCP e4m3 either way, so re-labelling them fnuz would be numerically *wrong*; if fnuz-only kernels are required downstream, that is a conversion problem, not a relabel.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The two PRs: superset or drift?\n\n**Neither is a strict superset.** `#54129` is the mmap branch built on a **newer upstream-main merge**; `#53896` has a **later-timestamped head commit** (`2026-08-28 16:54 +0800` = 08:54 UTC vs `11:52 +0700` = 04:52 UTC) carrying CI work that `#54129` does not have. [M — `git log -1 --format='%ci'` on both]\n\n`diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm` → **20 differing paths** [M]:\n```\nvllm/config/compilation.py, vllm/envs.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/{fp8,mxfp4}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/quark/schemes/quark_ocp_mx.py,\nvllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/{config_utils,flashinfer_utils}.py,\nvllm/model_executor/models/{qwen2_moe,qwen3_next}.py,\nvllm/models/kimi_k3/nvidia/kda_metadata.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nvllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py,\nOnly in vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia: ple_mmap.py,\nvllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py\n```\n\nModel-tree drift, `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp` [M]: 6 differing files + `ple_mmap.py` only in 54129. Per-file:\n\n| File | 54129 vs 53896 | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `amd/ple_layer.py` | +1 line | **Only** `# TODO: need double-check` inserted above the `NULL_BLOCK_ID` remap (53896 line 519 region → 54129 `amd/ple_layer.py:522`). [S] Zero functional change; zero mmap wiring. |\n| `amd/model.py` | −8/+0 mmap-unrelated | Drops `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` import, the `self.is_fused_shared_expert_enabled` computation, and the `enabled=` kwarg to `maybe_fuse_shared_experts`. Upstream-main API drift. [S] |\n| `amd/mtp.py` | same drift | identical `is_fused_shared_expert` removals. [S] |\n| `nvidia/mtp.py` | same drift | identical. [S] |\n| `nvidia/model.py` | +14/−12 [M] | 12 of those are upstream drift (as above); the mmap wiring is `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config` (`:12`), `from . import ple_mmap` (`:77`), and two 5-line `build_tables` blocks at `:812-817` and `:1011-1016`. [S] |\n| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | +89/−62 [M] | The whole mmap wiring, plus **a removal that changes the env-off path** — see below. |\n\n### Newer: `#54129`, by base. Later: `#53896`, by head timestamp.\n`#54129`'s head is literally a merge of `upstream-main` into `ple-mmap-upstream`, and its non-PLE churn (fused-MoE oracle, quark OCP MX, flashinfer utils, kimi_k3) is that merge. `#53896` never had `ple_mmap.py` (`grep -c PLE_MMAP vllm-pr53896/vllm/envs.py` → `0`; `find vllm-pr53896 -name 'ple_mmap*' -o -name 'test_ple_mmap*' | wc -l` → `0`) [M]. So the mmap feature is strictly additive on top of #53896's model code, but the two branches have **already diverged on shared non-mmap files** and a merge will conflict on `amd/{model,mtp}.py`, `nvidia/{model,mtp}.py`, `vllm/config/compilation.py`, and `vllm/envs.py`.\n\n### Surprise: #54129 changed the **env-off** nvidia path\n`#53896` routed the ID hashing through its own split-out custom op:\n```python\n# 53896 nvidia/ple_layer.py (removed by 54129)\n# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n# which dispatch only on the padded token count.\ntorch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, ngram_ids, self.layer_name)\n```\n`#54129` **deletes** `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids`, its fake impl, and its `direct_register_custom_op` registration (−35 lines), renames `compute_ngram_ids` → `_hash_ngram_ids`, and in the env-off branch calls it **inline, traced**: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448` `ngram_ids = self._hash_ngram_ids(...); return self.ngram_embedding(ngram_ids).flatten(-2)`. [S] The op name also disappears from `_attention_ops`: `#53896` `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` had `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids\"`; `#54129` `:771` has `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` in its place. [M — `grep -n qwen4_exp` on both]\nIt is gone tree-wide: `grep -rn 'compute_ple_ngram_ids\\|compute_ngram_ids' vllm/ tests/` → no output. [M]\n\nAlso changed in the shared hashing body: `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs, :num_tokens]` (53896) → `packed = self.padded_buffer[:num_reqs]` (54129, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:379`). [S]\n\nThis contradicts `ple_mmap.py:32-35`'s claim [CL] that \"With `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` unset, nothing in this module is ever invoked and the stock classes are untouched.\" Nothing in *ple_mmap.py* is invoked — true — but the stock nvidia PLE forward and its cudagraph-splitting behavior **did** change. Whether that regresses PIECEWISE dispatch (the removed comment's stated concern) is **UNDETERMINED** from source; settling it needs a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and a check of graph-split boundaries, or the PR author's rationale.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The piecewise-cudagraph guard, and its AMD analogue\n\n`nvidia/ple_mmap.py:597-635`, `check_cudagraph_safety(compilation_config)` — three independent refusals, any one of which alone would miss a route into a capture [S]:\n\n1. `:614-621` — `if compilation_config.cudagraph_mode.has_full_cudagraphs(): raise RuntimeError(...)`. Message: *\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 requires piecewise-only CUDA graphs … Pass `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\"*\n2. `:622-628` — `if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — *\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.\"*\n3. `:629-635` — `if QUALIFIED_OP_NAME not in (compilation_config.splitting_ops or []): raise` — guards against an operator-supplied `-cc.splitting_ops` or an attn-fusion reset silently dropping the op.\n\nCall site: `nvidia/ple_layer.py:284`, inside `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.__init__`, i.e. at **construction time, before the ~78 GiB backbone streams** (`ple_mmap.py:703-704` comment [CL]). Tests pin all three: `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:661`, `:680`, `:691`, `:705`, plus construction-level mirrors at `:758`, `:786`, `:814`. [S]\n\n**What is the AMD analogue supposed to be? The same function, unchanged.** [S] The guard reads nothing platform-specific: `has_full_cudagraphs()` is `self.max_cudagraph_mode() == CUDAGraphMode.FULL` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:83-84`), `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` and `splitting_ops` are plain config. ROCm drives the identical `CUDAGraphMode` enum (`vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919`) and the identical `CUDAGraphWrapper` (`rocm.py:1002`). No HIP-graph-specific analogue is needed or exists.\n\nThe third check is **already satisfied on ROCm by default**: `\"vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward\"` sits unconditionally in the class-level `_attention_ops` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:764-782`, entry at `:771`), and `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` copies that list wholesale when `splitting_ops is None` (`vllm/config/compilation.py:1152-1161`, `self.splitting_ops = list(self._attention_ops)`). [S] The op *name* is in the default split set on every platform; only the op *implementation* is nvidia-gated.\n\n**AMD-specific tension worth flagging**: the AMD PLE short-conv path is written for FULL cudagraphs — `amd/ple_layer.py:522-525` [S]:\n```\n# TODO: need double-check\n# FULL cudagraph padded decode rows use NULL_BLOCK_ID. Remap them to\n# slot 0 for a safe gather, then zero output and skip write-back.\nvalid_state = state_indices != NULL_BLOCK_ID\n```\n(also `:642`, `:748`). Enabling `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` on AMD would force `cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` via check #1, taking the AMD tree off the mode its short-conv code was tuned for. That is a **performance/coverage decision the port must make explicitly**, not an incidental detail.\n\nAlso note: AMD's *existing* gather op, `vllm::qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (registered at `amd/ple_layer.py:1236-1241`, defined `:1217-1232`), is **not** in `_attention_ops` — `grep -n qwen4_exp vllm/config/compilation.py` returns only lines 770/771/772, none of which is the AMD op. [M] So today's AMD gather is opaque to Inductor but still *inside* the capture region. The port must not reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Every env var the PR introduces\n\nFour, all in `vllm/envs.py`. Type stubs at `:325-328`, lambdas at `:2168-2177`. [S]\n\n| Var | Default | Parse | Effect | Pins |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Master gate. `ple_mmap.enabled()` (`ple_mmap.py:116-118`) returns it; drives the constructor swap (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`), the three guards, the `load_weights` interception, and `build_tables` (`nvidia/model.py:813`, `:1012`). | `envs.py:325`, `:2170` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS` | `32` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)` for the gather pool; clamped `max(1, int(workers))`. Page faults overlap across threads. | `envs.py:326`, `:2172`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:906`; used `:327/:335` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK` | `2048` | `int(os.getenv(...))` | Rows per gather task; clamped `max(1, int(chunk))`. | `envs.py:327`, `:2174`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:907`; used `:325/:377` |\n| `VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM` | `False` (`\"0\"`) | `bool(int(os.getenv(...)))` | Streams the table once at load to warm page cache, bounded by `min(table_bytes, max(0, MemAvailable − 8 GiB))`. | `envs.py:328`, `:2177`; consumed `ple_mmap.py:913-921`; bound `:493-500` |\n\n**Compile-hash semantics** — `vllm/envs.py:2342-2350` adds the three *tuning* knobs to the ignored-factors set, deliberately leaving `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` itself a compile factor [S]:\n```\n# PLE mmap gather-pool tuning: affects only the CPU body of a\n# split-out op, never the compiled graph. VLLM_PLE_MMAP itself stays\n# a factor since it changes which op the graph splits around.\n\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_WORKERS\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_CHUNK\", \"VLLM_PLE_MMAP_PREWARM\",\n```\nConfirmed the PR adds no other env var: `grep -n '^+.*os\\.getenv\\|^+.*envs\\.VLLM_' pr54129.diff | grep -v PLE_MMAP` returns only pre-existing `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` reads and one `CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE` false positive. [M]\n\n---\n\n## Exact port specification (analysis only — NOT written)\n\n### Blocker A (must be resolved first): `amd/ple_layer.py` has no FP8 dequant at all\nThe mmap path is **FP8-only by construction**: `_FP8_DTYPES` admits only `F8_E4M3` (`ple_mmap.py:78-84`), and `_validate_layer_shards` raises for anything else (`ple_mmap.py:687-693`). Its output is a raw `float8_e4m3fn` tensor (`ple_mmap.py:567-573`), which the nvidia layer dequantizes at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:1194`:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nembeddings = self._dequantize_embeddings(embeddings, hidden_states.dtype)   # :1194\n```\n`_dequantize_embeddings` is `nvidia/ple_layer.py:634-648`; `_get_embedding_weight_scale` is `:632-634`.\n\n**The AMD file has none of this** [M]:\n```\n$ grep -n '_dequantize_embeddings\\|is_fp8\\|weight_scale\\|quant_config\\|params_dtype' vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py\n317:            dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype\n407:        quant_config = vllm_config.quant_config\n438:            quant_config=quant_config,     # key_proj only\n445:            quant_config=quant_config,     # value_proj only\n```\nand its forward goes straight into the projection with no dequant: `amd/ple_layer.py:1018-1020` [S]:\n```python\nembeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nkey, _ = self.key_proj(embeddings)\nvalue, _ = self.value_proj(embeddings)\n```\nThis gap **pre-dates #54129**: `grep -c '_dequantize_embeddings\\|Fp8EmbeddingMethod' vllm-pr53896/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` → `0`, while `#53896`'s nvidia file already had `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` at `:140` and `_dequantize_embeddings` at `:581`. [M] AMD's `Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding` is constructed with no `quant_config`/`params_dtype` at all (`amd/ple_layer.py:425-433`, 7 positional args vs nvidia's 7 + 2 kwargs at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:580-589`), and its `__init__` signature (`amd/ple_layer.py:127-136`) lacks both parameters.\n\nTherefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\n\n### The mmap wiring, line by line (what to mirror)\n\n**`nvidia/ple_layer.py` — the wiring is exactly five sites:**\n\n| # | Lines | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | `:7` | `from typing import cast` |\n| W2 | `:52` | `from . import ple_mmap` |\n| W3 | **`:281-291`** | the constructor branch — annotation `self.ngram_embedding: VocabParallelEmbedding \\| ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding`; `if ple_mmap.enabled():`; `vllm_config = get_current_vllm_config()`; `ple_mmap.check_cudagraph_safety(vllm_config.compilation_config)`; `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(vllm_config.model_config, layer_name, self.head_dim)`; `self.ngram_embedding = ple_mmap.MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)`; `else:` + reindent of `:292-301` |\n| W4 | **`:428-446`** | the forward branch — `isinstance(...)` gate, symbolic `num_tokens = input_ids.reshape(-1).shape[0]`, `output = input_ids.new_empty((num_tokens, self.embedding_dim), dtype=self.ngram_embedding.torch_dtype)`, `torch.ops.vllm.qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context, output, self.layer_name)`, `return output` (10 of the 19 lines are the load-bearing ConstraintViolationError comment) |\n| W5a | **`:477-490`** | `load_weights` weight_scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale(...)` |\n| W5b | **`:517-527`** (+ `:533`, `:535`, `:538-539`) | `load_weights` shard-drop → `embedding.weights_streamed = True; loaded.add(...); continue`, plus `stock_embedding = cast(VocabParallelEmbedding, embedding)` and the three renamed reads; also the hoist of `embedding = self.ngram_embedding` to `:461` |\n\nThe campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\n\n**`nvidia/model.py` — three sites:**\n- `:12` `from vllm.config import VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`\n- `:77` `from . import ple_mmap`\n- `:812-817` and `:1011-1016` — after `loader.load_weights(...)`: `if ple_mmap.enabled(): ple_mmap.build_tables(self.model_config, get_current_vllm_config().compilation_config)`; `return loaded`\n\nMeasured: `diff -u vllm-pr53896/.../nvidia/model.py vllm-pr54129/.../nvidia/model.py` = **+14/−12**, of which 12 removed + 2 added are upstream `is_model_fused_shared_expert_compatible` drift; the mmap portion is **+12 net**. [M] `amd/model.py` differs from `nvidia/model.py` by *exactly and only these four hunks* — verified: `diff -u amd/model.py nvidia/model.py` produces 4 hunks, all mmap. [M]\n\n### Imports / env checks / guards the port must mirror\n1. `from . import ple_mmap` — but `ple_mmap.py` lives in `nvidia/`. Two options: **(a)** `from ..nvidia import ple_mmap` in both `amd/ple_layer.py` and `amd/model.py` — safe, because `nvidia/__init__.py` is an SPDX-only stub (3 lines, no re-exports) [S], so this pulls in *only* `ple_mmap.py` and none of the CUDA-heavy nvidia modules; **(b)** relocate to `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py` alongside `common/ple.py` and update both trees + all `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` imports (the test file imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap` at `:27` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.model` at `:26`). (b) is the cleaner upstream shape; (a) is the smaller diff.\n2. `get_current_vllm_config` — **already imported** in `amd/ple_layer.py:12` (`from vllm.config import CacheConfig, ModelConfig, VllmConfig, get_current_vllm_config`) [S]. Must be *added* to `amd/model.py:12` (currently `from vllm.config import VllmConfig`) [S].\n3. `from typing import cast` — must be added to `amd/ple_layer.py` (absent; nvidia has it at `:7`) [S].\n4. Env checks: exactly one, `ple_mmap.enabled()` — three call sites, mirroring `nvidia/ple_layer.py:282`, `nvidia/model.py:813`, `nvidia/model.py:1012`. The three tuning vars are read inside `ple_mmap.py` (`:906`, `:907`, `:913`) and need no per-tree wiring.\n5. Guards: `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for`, both at construction (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:284-287`), unchanged — no AMD variant.\n6. **Op registration**: none needed. `direct_register_custom_op` fires at `ple_mmap.py:981-986` on first import of the module, under `RocmPlatform.dispatch_key == \"CUDA\"` (`rocm.py:502`). The CPU double-registration guard at `:993-994` also behaves correctly on ROCm.\n7. **`splitting_ops`**: already correct — `vllm::qwen4_exp_ple_mmap_forward` is in `_attention_ops` unconditionally (`vllm/config/compilation.py:771`), copied into `splitting_ops` by `set_splitting_ops_for_v1` (`:1152-1161`).\n8. The AMD-only op `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:1217-1241`) stays for the env-off path; it must **not** be reused for the mmap path (not in `_attention_ops`, so not a split boundary, so it would run inside the capture).\n\n### Size, honestly measured\n- `amd/model.py`: **+12** lines.\n- `amd/ple_layer.py`: W1–W5 ≈ **+58 lines** including the comments the nvidia file carries (≈ **+35** code-only), plus the `else:` reindent of `:292-301`.\n- Plus Blocker A: **+10** (minimal dequant) to **+80** (full FP8 stack parity).\n- Plus, if option (b), a file move and a test-import sweep across 2048 lines of `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which is nvidia-only today: `grep -cni 'amd\\|rocm' tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` → `0`. [M]\n\n**Realistic total: 80–150 lines, not 15.**\n\n---\n\n## Open / UNDETERMINED\n\n1. **Does `float8_e4m3fn` storage + `.to(bf16)` work on the target gfx arch?** Looked at: `ple_mmap.py:78-84`, `:535`, `:567`; `rocm.py:975-984`; `fp8_utils.py:42-45`. Settled by running the cast on the target ROCm build.\n2. **Did removing `qwen4_exp_compute_ple_ngram_ids` regress env-off PIECEWISE dispatch on nvidia?** Looked at: `#53896` `nvidia/ple_layer.py` op + its comment, `#54129` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:447-448`, `vllm/config/compilation.py:770` (53896) vs `:771` (54129), and the tree-wide grep showing no remaining reference. Settled by a compile run with `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` inspecting graph-split boundaries, or by the author.\n3. **Is the `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522` a known AMD FULL-cudagraph correctness doubt?** It is the sole delta between the two branches' AMD PLE files. Settled by the PR thread.\n4. **Whether AMD is expected to run FULL cudagraphs for this model in practice** — `amd/ple_layer.py:522/642/748` handle `NULL_BLOCK_ID` padded decode rows, implying yes; guard #1 would forbid it under `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`. Settled by the AMD serving config actually in use.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991809525},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, I'll read moe-dispatch.md, and then run-smoke.sh to check the style. I'll also check other scripts for conventions (host tooling, receipt writing). The deliverables for the task are:\n- scripts/make-proxy.sh — run it inside the container, build a synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy: same architecture identifier (Qwen4ExpForCausalLM), a few layers, 8 experts with block-FP8 weight_block_size [128,128], and a small lookup table using the shard tensor naming that the mmap path expects (`layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{j}.weight`), reusing the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout when possible.\n- scripts/run-proxy.sh — serve single-node with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution, one greedy completion, assert finite logits via the API, clean shutdown, write out results/receipts/proxy.json.\n\nNote that the task says \"eager execution,\" but the guard rejects eager (guard 2: enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture; VLLM_COMPILE mode is required). Hmm. Wait — the task goal says \"with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" But spec P10 says \"The table path's own guard requires piecewise capture with the mmap op as a split boundary and rejects bare eager — the first boot runs that accepted mode.\" This is a contradiction. The brief says \"before modifying code, read the cited spec section and style reference.\" Spec claim 4.1: \"The proxy checkpoint is served single-node with the mmap path enabled → the proxy receipt records finite output and clean shutdown.\" P10 takes precedence: the first boot runs in the accepted mode, i.e., piecewise (`-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`), not eager. The task goal's phrasing \"eager execution\" seems to contradict the spec, but spec P10 is the authoritative ruling and is included in readFirst. The guard `check_cudagraph_safety` explicitly rejects enforce-eager. Therefore, when running with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, we must not use --enforce-eager; we must use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\n\nHmm, but wait: the task text says \"eager execution.\" Let me re-read the task goal: \"run-proxy.sh serves it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" This might be task-authoring shorthand that gets overridden by P10. Also, the deliveredBehaviors say: \"The accepted kernel path and the ported table path run on this GPU before real weight bytes are loaded.\" Since the brief says \"before modifying code, read the cited spec section\" and the spec is authoritative, I'll follow P10 (piecewise, not eager) and note this point. Actually, to be safe, should I try eager first? No — the guard literally rejects eager at build time. The script will fail. The correct implementation uses PIECEWISE. I'll implement with PIECEWISE and add a comment explaining why (spec P10, the fork's guard).\n\nWait, actually, is this guard in the fork? The fork's admission patch and table port are separate campaign tasks (fork-engineering completed in S1). The fork (mecattaf/vllm branch flashnext) has a table port that mirrors the guard. So running with --enforce-eager will raise a RuntimeError. So run-proxy.sh must use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`.\n\nNext, I'll check the container setup: container/build.sh, where the fork checkout lives inside the container, the tests/models/ scaffolding (in the fork, tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py builds fixtures: `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). make-proxy.sh should reuse that fixture-building code when possible.\n\nLet me read moe-dispatch.md and run-smoke.sh, and look at the container/ directory.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_23fcd3a422564f3d845feb6e","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/scripts/run-smoke.sh"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7700d6254e2b42f18a408f23","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && ls container host results 2>/dev/null && ls results/receipts 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":13796,"output":1012,"cacheRead":10496,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":872,"totalTokens":25304,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991809529,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0d9230c5-1ee8-4412-afcb-95163066b20f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_23fcd3a422564f3d845feb6e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# In-container smoke: platform identity, fp8 storage+cast, model registry\n# resolution, and oracle admission — the cheap questions that must be\n# answered before any weight byte moves. Writes results/receipts/smoke.json.\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nIMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nOUT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/smoke.json\"\n\npodman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n  --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host \\\n  -v \"$REPO_ROOT/results:/results\" \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, time, traceback\nr = {\"step\": \"smoke\", \"status\": \"pass\", \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"), \"data\": {}}\ndef try_(name, fn):\n    try:\n        r[\"data\"][name] = fn()\n    except Exception as e:\n        r[\"data\"][name] = f\"FAIL: {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n        r[\"status\"] = \"fail\"\n        traceback.print_exc()\n\ndef arch():\n    from vllm.platforms import rocm\n    return {\"gcn_arch\": rocm._GCN_ARCH, \"on_cdna\": rocm.on_cdna(),\n            \"supports_fp8_stock_predicate\": rocm.on_cdna() or rocm.on_rdna4()}\ndef fp8_cast():\n    import torch\n    x = torch.zeros(4, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\")\n    y = (x.to(torch.bfloat16) + 1).sum().item()\n    return {\"e4m3fn_alloc_and_cast\": y == 4.0}\ndef registry():\n    from vllm.model_executor.models.registry import ModelRegistry\n    archs = ModelRegistry.get_supported_archs()\n    return {\"model_arch_registered\": \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" in archs}\ndef aperture():\n    return {\"ttm_pages_limit\": int(open(\n        \"/sys/module/ttm/parameters/pages_limit\").read().strip())}\ndef admission():\n    # The patched oracle must admit the block-FP8 scheme on this GPU.\n    from vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe.experts.triton_moe import TritonExperts\n    from vllm.model_executor.layers.quantization.utils.quant_utils import (\n        kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym)\n    ok, why = TritonExperts._supports_quant_scheme(\n        kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym) \\\n        if hasattr(TritonExperts, \"_supports_quant_scheme\") else (False, \"no hook\")\n    return {\"fp8_block_moe_admitted\": bool(ok), \"reason\": str(why)}\n\ntry_(\"arch\", arch)\ntry_(\"fp8_cast\", fp8_cast)\ntry_(\"registry\", registry)\ntry_(\"aperture\", aperture)\ntry_(\"admission\", admission)\njson.dump(r, open(\"/results/receipts/smoke.json\", \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(json.dumps(r, indent=1))\nPY\necho \"smoke receipt: $OUT\"\npython3 -c \"import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if json.load(open('$OUT'))['status']=='pass' else 1)\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991836060},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7700d6254e2b42f18a408f23","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"container:\nbuild.sh\nContainerfile\nrootfs\n\nhost:\nfn-cluster-down.sh\nfn-cluster-up.sh\nfn-env.sh\nfn-preflight.sh\nrdma\nsystemd\n\nresults:\nreceipts\nbuild.json\nweights-coordinator.json\nweights-worker.json\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991836061},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, let's look at the moe-dispatch.md evidence (the part needed to read first), container/build.sh, and container/Containerfile, to grasp where the fork checkout is located inside the container, as well as how the scripts are executed inside the container. I'll also check receipts-verify.py to find out the expected schema for the proxy receipt.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3dea59bfbb2c4b4cbacaf640","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2600,"output":143,"cacheRead":23552,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":74,"totalTokens":26295,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991836065,"responseId":"chatcmpl-567adcc0-c3ca-464d-92cb-78a3967b661e","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3dea59bfbb2c4b4cbacaf640","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Q1 dossier — 512-expert block-FP8 MoE dispatch on ROCm gfx1151\n\nSource-side truth for the B5 gate. Every claim below carries a file:line I opened\nthis session, or a command + literal output. Grades: **[S]** read from source,\n**[M]** measured/counted, **[CL]** claimed by docs/comments, unverified.\n\n## 0. Provenance of the checkouts\n\n| Checkout | HEAD | Subject |\n|---|---|---|\n| `…/scratchpad/vllm-pr53896` | `89d0bb71aeb2f3e15c16efc69d33c3fbe223a765` | `update CI` |\n| `…/scratchpad/vllm-pr54129` | `8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da` | `Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream` |\n\n`git rev-parse HEAD` in each tree. **[M]**\n\nThe two trees are near-identical: `diff -rq vllm-pr53896/vllm vllm-pr54129/vllm`\nreports **20 differing entries** **[M]**. The ones that matter here:\n\n- `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/oracle/fp8.py`\n- `vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py`\n- `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py`\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/{amd,nvidia}/{model,mtp,ple_layer}.py`\n- `Only in vllm-pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia: ple_mmap.py`\n\nLine numbers below are given per checkout where they diverge. Both were re-pinned\nthis session; the campaign record's numbers (fp8.py:466-468, rocm.py:870,\noracle/fp8.py:416-419) have all drifted.\n\n---\n\n## BOTTOM LINE (answers the primary question)\n\n**On stock vLLM at both PR heads, a 512-expert block-FP8 MoE on gfx1151 dispatches\nto NO kernel class at all. The load aborts loudly in `Fp8MoEMethod.__init__` with\n`NotImplementedError: No FP8 MoE backend supports the deployment configuration.`\nbefore a single expert weight byte is read.**\n\nConsequently **the feared BF16 twin of the expert weights cannot be materialized**:\nthere is no reachable code path in stock vLLM at these heads that upconverts\nblock-FP8 expert weights to bf16 — not at load, not lazily per-forward, not via a\ncache. The ~125 GiB/node blow-up that would kill TP=2 is **not** a stock risk. It is\na risk only under the DS4 patch, and even there only on the **linear** path, never\nthe experts.\n\nThe genuine, previously-unrecorded finding is elsewhere: **the PLE ngram FP8\nembedding table has no FP8 handling at all in the AMD tree** (it does in the NVIDIA\ntree), so on AMD it either dies loudly on an orphan `weight_scale` tensor or gets\nbare-cast to bf16 with the block scale silently discarded. See §5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Load path: which config claims it, which kernel is selected\n\n### 1a. Quant config class\n\n`Fp8Config` (`quantization/fp8.py`) claims it, not compressed-tensors.\n\n- `get_name()` returns `\"fp8\"` — `quantization/fp8.py:137-138` (54129) **[S]**\n- `from_config()` reads `weight_block_size` from the HF `quantization_config` at\n  `quantization/fp8.py:163` and falls back to `modules_to_not_convert` as\n  `ignored_layers` at `quantization/fp8.py:165-168` (54129) **[S]**:\n\n```\n163:        weight_block_size = cls.get_from_keys_or(config, [\"weight_block_size\"], None)\n165:        if not ignored_layers:\n166:            ignored_layers = cls.get_from_keys_or(\n167:                config, [\"modules_to_not_convert\"], None\n168:            )\n```\n\nThis is exactly the checkpoint's shape: `weight_block_size [128,128]` + a 943-entry\n`modules_to_not_convert`.\n\n- Dispatch to the MoE method: `get_quant_method()` at `quantization/fp8.py:176-215`\n  (54129). For a `RoutedExperts` layer it returns `UnquantizedFusedMoEMethod` if the\n  prefix is skipped, else `Fp8MoEMethod(self, layer)` —\n  `quantization/fp8.py:199-215` **[S]**. Since only `mlp.experts.*` survives the\n  943-entry ignore list, the routed experts get `Fp8MoEMethod`.\n\n- `block_quant` is set from the presence of `weight_block_size`:\n  `quantization/fp8.py:481-482` (54129) **[S]**.\n\n### 1b. Weight/activation quant keys\n\n`Fp8MoEMethod.__init__`, `quantization/fp8.py:488-497` (54129) **[S]**:\n\n```\n488:        if self.block_quant:\n489:            weight_key = kFp8Static128BlockSym\n490:            activation_key = kFp8Dynamic128Sym\n```\n\nIn **53896** this differs — the block shape is refined *before* the oracle and baked\ninto the key via `create_fp8_quant_key(...)` at `quantization/fp8.py:516-518`,\nusing `refine_fp8_moe_block_shape` (`oracle/fp8.py:272`) **[S]**. See §1e.\n\n### 1c. Oracle entry point\n\n`select_fp8_moe_backend()` is called at `quantization/fp8.py:500-505` (54129) /\n`:529` (53896) **[S]**. Critically this is the **first** thing after the keys are\ncomputed and **before** any refine logic in 54129 — ordering matters, see §1e.\n\n### 1d. Candidate kernel classes enumerated\n\n`_AVAILABLE_BACKENDS`, `oracle/fp8.py:80-95` (54129) / `:81` (53896) **[S]**, in\npriority order, with the class each resolves to via `backend_to_kernel_cls()`\n(`oracle/fp8.py:139-245`, 54129) **[S]**:\n\n| # | Backend | Kernel class | gfx1151 verdict | Evidence (54129) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `AITER` | `AiterExperts` | **REJECT** — `rocm_aiter_ops.is_fused_moe_enabled()`; `_AITER_ENABLED and _FMOE_ENABLED`, and `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER` defaults False | `experts/rocm_aiter_moe.py:452-453`; `_aiter_ops.py:1896-1897`; `envs.py:134` |\n| 2 | `FLASHINFER_TRTLLM` | `TrtLlmFp8ExpertsMonolithic/Modular` | **REJECT** — `p.is_cuda() and is_device_capability_family(100)` | `experts/trtllm_fp8_moe.py:140-147` |\n| 3 | `FLASHINFER_CUTLASS` | `FlashInferExperts` | **REJECT** — `p.is_cuda() and (…90 / 100 / 120)` | `experts/flashinfer_cutlass_moe.py:129-137` |\n| 4 | `DEEPGEMM` | `TritonOrDeepGemmExperts` | **REJECT** — `FallbackExperts` requires **both** members; `is_deep_gemm_supported()` needs `current_platform.support_deep_gemm()` (Hopper/Blackwell only) | `experts/fallback.py:52-58`; `utils/deep_gemm.py:105-110` |\n| 5 | `VLLM_CUTLASS` | `TritonOrCutlassExperts` | **REMOVED from list** — `allow_vllm_cutlass=False` passed by `Fp8MoEMethod` | `oracle/fp8.py:389-391`; `quantization/fp8.py:504` |\n| 6 | **`TRITON`** | **`TritonExperts`** | **REJECT** — device OK, expert count OK, but **quant scheme fails**: see §1f | `experts/triton_moe.py:148-156` |\n| 7 | `MARLIN` | `MarlinExperts` | **REJECT** — `p.is_cuda() and has_device_capability((7,5))` | `experts/marlin_moe.py:605-607` |\n| 8 | `HUMMING` | `Humming*Experts` | **REJECT** — `has_humming() and platform.is_cuda()` | `experts/fused_humming_moe.py:385-391` |\n| 9 | `BATCHED_DEEPGEMM` | `BatchedDeepGemmExperts` | **REJECT** — `is_deep_gemm_supported()` | `experts/batched_deep_gemm_moe.py:329-330` |\n| 10 | `BATCHED_VLLM_CUTLASS` | `CutlassBatchedExpertsFp8` | **REMOVED** — `allow_vllm_cutlass=False` | `oracle/fp8.py:389-391` |\n| 11 | `BATCHED_TRITON` | `BatchedTritonExperts` | **REJECT** — activation format is `BatchedExperts`, we are TP (Standard); also same fp8 scheme gate | `experts/fused_batched_moe.py:805-806`; base check `modular_kernel.py:590-591` |\n| 12 | `XPU` | `XPUExperts*` | **REJECT** — `current_platform.is_xpu()` | `experts/xpu_moe.py:84-85` |\n| 13 | `CPU` | `CPUExpertsFp8` | **REJECT** — `current_platform.is_cpu()` | `experts/cpu_moe.py:232-233` |\n| 14 | `HPC` | `HPCExperts` | **REJECT** — `p.is_cuda() and is_device_capability(90) and has_hpc()` | `hpc_moe.py:63-65` |\n\nAll 14 **[S]**.\n\nTwo classes present in `fused_moe/experts/` are **not reachable from this oracle**:\n\n- `NaiveBatchedExperts` — the only stock fp8 class with a real per-forward weight\n  dequant (`experts/fused_batched_moe.py:642`, called at `:693` and `:702`). Its\n  support hooks deliberately raise: *\"NaiveBatchedExperts is not yet used by an\n  Oracle. This method should not be called.\"* — `experts/fused_batched_moe.py:581-585`\n  **[S]**. It is never in `backend_to_kernel_cls`. **[M]** (grep of\n  `backend_to_kernel_cls` body, `oracle/fp8.py:139-245`, shows no import of it).\n- `Mxfp8EmulationTritonExperts` — see §1g.\n\n### 1e. The 512-expert and top-10 shape checks pass\n\n`TritonExperts.is_supported_config`, `experts/triton_moe.py:84-89` (54129) **[S]**:\n\n```\n85:        padded_num_experts = (moe_config.num_experts + 31) // 32 * 32\n86:        if padded_num_experts >= 1024:\n87:            return False, (\n88:                \"kernel's moe_align_block_size requires fewer than 1024 padded experts\"\n89:            )\n```\n\n512 experts → `padded_num_experts = 512` → `512 < 1024` → **passes**. **[S]**\nNothing in the selection path keys on top-k, so top-10 is not a constraint here.\nSo 512/top-10 is *not* what kills the dispatch — the fp8 capability gate is.\n\n**Divergence between the two heads (block-shape refine):**\n\n- **54129**: refine runs **after** the oracle, `quantization/fp8.py:506-546`, and on\n  success does `self.fp8_backend = Fp8MoeBackend.TRITON` +\n  `self.experts_cls = backend_to_kernel_cls(Fp8MoeBackend.TRITON)[0]`\n  (`:530-531`) — i.e. it **force-assigns TritonExperts bypassing\n  `is_supported_config`**. But it is **unreachable on gfx1151**, because\n  `select_fp8_moe_backend` at `:500` already raised. **[S]**\n- **53896**: refine runs **before** the oracle (`quantization/fp8.py:489-518`) and\n  encodes the refined shape in the `weight_key`\n  (`create_fp8_quant_key(static=True, group_shape=GroupShape(*self.moe_block_shape))`,\n  `:516-518`). The comment states this exists so *\"the oracle only selects kernels\n  that support it\"* **[CL]**. Net effect on gfx1151 is the same raise, and if a\n  refine did occur the refined key would additionally fail the exact-tuple match in\n  §1f. **[S]**\n\nWorth flagging for the record: **54129's force-assign at\n`quantization/fp8.py:530-531` is the one construct in either tree that installs a\nkernel class without consulting `is_supported_config`.** On gfx1151 it is dead code\nbehind the raise, but it is the shape of thing that would produce a silent wrong\ndispatch if the raise were ever removed or the ordering changed.\n\n### 1f. THE decisive gate\n\n`TritonExperts._supports_quant_scheme`, `experts/triton_moe.py:148-156` (54129) **[S]**:\n\n```\n148:        if current_platform.supports_fp8():\n149:            supported += [\n150:                (kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym),\n…\n156:        return (weight_key, activation_key) in supported\n```\n\nOur pair is exactly `(kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym)` (§1b), so\neverything hinges on `current_platform.supports_fp8()`.\n\n`platforms/rocm.py:971-972` (54129) and `platforms/rocm.py:972-973` (53896) —\nidentical bodies **[S]**:\n\n```\n971:    def supports_fp8(cls) -> bool:\n972:        return on_cdna() or on_rdna4()\n```\n\nThis is the campaign record's \"rocm.py:870 returns False for gfx1151\", **re-pinned to\nrocm.py:971-972 (54129) / 972-973 (53896)**. The \"some capability check\" is now\nresolved concretely:\n\n- `_ON_CDNA = any(arch in _GCN_ARCH for arch in [\"gfx9\", \"gfx1250\"])` —\n  `platforms/rocm.py:229` **[S]**. `\"gfx9\" not in \"gfx1151\"` and\n  `\"gfx1250\" not in \"gfx1151\"` → **False**.\n- `_ON_RDNA4 = any(arch in _GCN_ARCH for arch in [\"gfx1200\", \"gfx1201\"])` —\n  `platforms/rocm.py:232` **[S]** → **False**.\n- (For completeness, gfx1151 *is* recognised by the tree: `_ON_GFX1151` at\n  `platforms/rocm.py:220`, and the Strix Halo PCI id `\"0x1586\": \"AMD_Radeon_8060S\",\n  # gfx1151, Strix Halo` at `platforms/rocm.py:78` **[S]** — so this is a deliberate\n  capability exclusion, not an unrecognised device.)\n\n⇒ `supports_fp8() == False` on gfx1151 ⇒ the fp8 tuples are never appended ⇒\n`(kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym) not in supported` ⇒ TritonExperts\n**rejects**. Confirmed identical in both checkouts. **[S]**\n\nNote this is a *capability* statement (no MFMA/WMMA fp8 matrix path on RDNA3.5), not\na statement about whether Triton could emulate it — which is precisely why nothing\nsilently falls back.\n\n### 1g. The BF16-emulation backend exists but is unreachable for e4m3 block-FP8\n\n`Fp8MoeBackend.EMULATION` is defined at `oracle/fp8.py:57-60` (54129) with the\ncomment **[CL]**:\n\n```\n57:    # Dequantize-to-BF16 emulation for MXFP8 on devices without a native\n58:    # MXFP8 MoE kernel (e.g. ROCm). Weights pass through unchanged here.\n60:    EMULATION = \"EMULATION\"\n```\n\nThis is the closest thing in stock to the feared twin. It is **not reachable** for\nthis checkpoint:\n\n- It is **absent from `_AVAILABLE_BACKENDS`** (`oracle/fp8.py:80-95`) **[S]** —\n  the fp8 oracle never considers it.\n- It appears only in `oracle/mxfp8.py:30` `_SUPPORTED_BACKENDS` **[S]**, and\n  `_mxfp8_backend_to_kernel_cls` resolves it to `Mxfp8EmulationTritonExperts`\n  (`oracle/mxfp8.py:71-76`) **[S]**.\n- `oracle/mxfp8.py:92-98` calls `is_supported_config` with hard-coded\n  `kMxfp8Static` / `kMxfp8Dynamic` **[S]**. Our checkpoint is e4m3 + fp32\n  `[128,128]` block scales (`kFp8Static128BlockSym`), **not** MXFP8 (e8m0 group-32).\n  Different oracle, never entered.\n- The user-facing escape hatch is closed too: `moe_backend` accepts `\"emulation\"`\n  (`config/kernel.py:142`) **[S]**, but `map_fp8_backend`'s mapping dict\n  (`oracle/fp8.py:250-260`) has **no `\"emulation\"` key** **[S]**, so\n  `-O.moe_backend=emulation` raises\n  `moe_backend='emulation' is not supported for FP8 MoE.` (`oracle/fp8.py:262-265`)\n  **[S]**.\n\n**⇒ There is no user-reachable dequant-to-BF16 MoE path for e4m3 block-FP8 at these\nheads.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Does the landed path materialize BF16 weights? — N/A, and nowhere else either\n\nNo path lands (§3), so there is nothing to materialize. Confirming the negative\ndirectly:\n\n**Sweep [M]** — `grep -rn \"to(torch.bfloat16)|dequant|.to(orig_dtype)|to(layer.orig_dtype)\"`\nover `fused_moe/`, `quantization/fp8.py`, `quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py`,\nexcluding mxfp4/nvfp4/int4/wna16/mxfp8. Every surviving hit is unreachable here:\n\n| Hit | Why it does not apply |\n|---|---|\n| `fused_batched_moe.py:642,692-702` (`dequant`, `w1_dq`, `w2_dq`) | `NaiveBatchedExperts`, unreachable from any oracle — `:581-585` **[S]** |\n| `fused_moe/utils.py:301-306` `_fp8_quantize_dequantize` | **activation** QDQ, not weights **[S]** |\n| `prepare_finalize/nixl_ep.py:35-39,182` `dequant_fp8` | activations in the NIXL EP all2all prepare path **[S]** |\n| `ocp_mx_emulation_moe.py:9` \"Weights are dequantized on the fly during each forward\" | OCP-MX only **[CL]** |\n| `fp8_utils.py:1431` `per_tensor_dequantize` | per-tensor, not block **[S]** |\n\n**The campaign record's `fp8.py:466-468` re-pinned:** the `.to(torch.bfloat16)`\nweight upconversion lives at `quantization/fp8.py:440-441` (54129) **[S]**, inside\n`Fp8LinearMethod.apply` — and the record's two gates are both confirmed:\n\n```\n427:        if envs.VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT:\n428:            if self.block_quant:\n…\n431:                return self.fp8_linear.apply_weights(layer, x, bias)\n432:            else:\n…\n439:                # per-tensor/channel: dequant to BF16 and run GEMM\n440:                weight_fp8 = layer.weight.to(torch.bfloat16)\n441:                weight_scale = layer.weight_scale.to(torch.bfloat16)\n```\n\nSo: (a) gated behind `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` (default `False`, `envs.py:92`,\n`envs.py:622`) **[S]**; (b) explicitly in the **`else` (non-block-quant)** branch —\nblock-quant takes `apply_weights` with no dequant at `:429-431` **[S]**; and (c) it\nis the **Linear** method, not the MoE method, so it never touches routed experts.\nCampaign record **CONFIRMED and re-pinned**.\n\n**The feared class, checked directly.** `TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel` **does exist\nin stock** — `kernels/linear/scaled_mm/triton.py:159` **[S]** — but:\n\n- It is a **linear** kernel (`class TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel(Fp8BlockScaledMMLinearKernel)`),\n  not a `FusedMoEExperts`. It cannot serve the 512 routed experts.\n- Its stock body is only `is_supported` + `apply_block_scaled_mm`, which calls\n  `torch.ops.vllm.w8a8_triton_block_scaled_mm_func` — `triton.py:159-177` **[S]**.\n  **It defines no `apply_weights` override and holds no weight cache.**\n- **`grep -rn \"cached_bf16|_bf16_cache|bf16_weight|TritonFp8BlockScaledMM\"` over\n  `vllm/` in BOTH checkouts returns 6 hits each, all of them the bare class name\n  (1 definition + 5 references in `kernels/linear/__init__.py`). Zero hits for\n  `cached_bf16`, `_bf16_cache`, or `bf16_weight`.** **[M]**\n\n⇒ **The `cached_bf16_weight` hook does not exist in stock vLLM at either head.**\n\n**Where it does exist (contrast, DS4 patch only):**\n\n- `/home/tom/Downloads/ds4-vllm/container/patches/vllm-upstream.patch:1443-1454`\n  adds `_DS4_W8A8_BF16` / `_DS4_W8A8_BF16_DIRECT` env latches and (`:1457+`) an\n  `apply_weights` override onto `TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel` **[S]**.\n- `…/container/rootfs/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ds4_tl_indexer.py:339-343`\n  defines the module-level cache and the hook **[S]**:\n  `_BF16_WCACHE = {}` (`:339`), `def cached_bf16_weight(weight, weight_scale, block_size, m)` (`:343`).\n- Its only two call sites are `ds4_tl_indexer.py:383` (`w8a8_block_bf16_direct`) and\n  `:395` (`w8a8_block_fp8_bf16`) — both described as *\"gfx1151 fast path for\n  block-scaled fp8 **linear**\"* (`:392-394`) **[S]**. **Neither is a MoE path.**\n\n⇒ Even under DS4, the twin covers **linear** weights, never the 512 experts. The\n~125 GiB expert-twin scenario has no source basis in either tree.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Loud raise or silent fallback? — **LOUD**, and early\n\nThe oracle exhausts `AVAILABLE_BACKENDS` (loop at `oracle/fp8.py:394-411`, 54129 /\n`:428` 53896) logging each rejection at `debug_once` only, then:\n\n`oracle/fp8.py:413-416` (54129) / `:447-450` (53896) **[S]**:\n\n```\n413:    if current_platform.is_cuda() or current_platform.is_rocm():\n414:        raise NotImplementedError(\n415:            \"No FP8 MoE backend supports the deployment configuration.\"\n416:        )\n```\n\nROCm is explicitly inside the raising branch. The silent\n`return Fp8MoeBackend.NONE, None` at `oracle/fp8.py:418` (54129) **[S]** is reached\n**only** on non-CUDA/non-ROCm platforms (the TODO above it names OOT/TPU plugins) —\n**it is not the ROCm failure mode**. This is the good case and it directly answers\nthe campaign's worry about \"the platform's dominant failure mode\": here it does not\napply.\n\n**Timing — this is the important part for B5.** `select_fp8_moe_backend` is called\nfrom `Fp8MoEMethod.__init__` (`quantization/fp8.py:500-505`, 54129) **[S]**, which\nruns during **layer construction**, i.e. while the model is being instantiated and\n**before any expert weight tensor is read from the checkpoint**. So the failure is\nnot merely loud, it is *early*: you learn at startup, not after paging in ~121B FP8\nparams.\n\nSecondary raise site, same message family — when a backend is explicitly requested,\n`_return_or_raise` raises `ValueError(_make_log_unsupported(...))`,\n`oracle/fp8.py:311-313` **[S]**, text from `:296-306`:\n`\"FP8 MoE backend {backend} does not support the deployment configuration since {reason}.\"`\nThe `reason` will be `\"kernel does not support quantization scheme …\"` formatted at\n`modular_kernel.py:567-570` **[S]**.\n\nNothing in either path degrades silently to a bf16 emulation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Does Qwen4Exp use vLLM's standard FusedMoE? — **YES**\n\nNo custom MoE module; §1–3 apply unchanged.\n\n- `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py:44-47` imports\n  `Qwen3NextAttention, Qwen3NextMLP, Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock` from\n  `vllm.model_executor.models.qwen3_next` **[S]**.\n- `class Qwen4ExpSparseMoeBlock(Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock)` —\n  `qwen4_exp/amd/model.py:155-167` **[S]**. Its `__init__` only rejects\n  sequence-parallel MoE, calls `super().__init__`, and sets `n_shared_experts`.\n  **It overrides no expert construction and no forward.**\n- Instantiated at `qwen4_exp/amd/model.py:241-243` when `is_moe_layer` **[S]**.\n- The parent builds experts through the standard factory:\n  `self.experts = FusedMoEFactory(` at\n  `vllm/model_executor/models/qwen3_next.py:165`, imported from\n  `vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe` at `qwen3_next.py:21` **[S]**.\n- The AMD and NVIDIA trees agree here — both `model.py` files import the same\n  `Qwen3NextSparseMoeBlock`. **[M]** (`diff -rq` lists `model.py` as differing, but\n  the MoE class is the shared parent in both.)\n- EPLB plumbing (`Qwen4ExpMixtureOfExperts`, `qwen4_exp/amd/model.py:327-374`)\n  reaches into `layer.mlp.experts` **[S]**, i.e. the standard `FusedMoE` object —\n  further confirming there is no bespoke expert container.\n\n⇒ The Qwen4Exp routed experts go through `FusedMoE` → `Fp8MoEMethod` → the fp8\noracle. Redo of §1-3 not required.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The PLE / ngram FP8 embedding table (non-mmap path) — **the real finding**\n\n**On the AMD tree there is no FP8 handling for the PLE table at all.**\n\n`vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py:195-200` **[S]**:\n\n```\n195:        self.ngram_embedding = VocabParallelEmbedding(\n196:            padded_vocab_size,\n197:            self.head_dim,\n198:            padding_size=divisor,\n199:            prefix=f\"{prefix}.ngram_embedding\",\n200:        )\n```\n\n**No `quant_config=` and no `params_dtype=` argument.** In\n`VocabParallelEmbedding.__init__` that means **[S]**:\n\n```\n286:        if quant_method is None and quant_config is not None:\n287:            quant_method = quant_config.get_quant_method(self, prefix=prefix)\n288:        if quant_method is None:\n289:            quant_method = UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod()\n```\n\n(`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:286-289`; `quant_config` param declared at `:248`,\n`params_dtype` at `:245`.) ⇒ `UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod`, weight allocated at the\nmodel dtype = **bf16**. **[S]**\n\nThe loader then copies checkpoint shards through\n`copy_ple_embedding_shard_`, `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py:71-75` **[S]**:\n\n```\n71:    source = loaded_weight.narrow(0, overlap.source_start, overlap.row_count)\n72:    target = destination.narrow(0, overlap.destination_start, overlap.row_count)\n73:    with torch.no_grad():\n74:        target.copy_(source.to(device=target.device, dtype=target.dtype))\n```\n\nCalled from `qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py:383-389` with\n`embedding.weight.data` as `destination` **[S]**.\n\nTwo consequences, both bad, both **[S]**:\n\n1. **Byte doubling at load.** `target.dtype` is bf16, so an FP8 shard is upconverted\n   1 byte → 2 bytes **at load time, eagerly, in `load_weights`**. This is a genuine\n   \"materialized twin\" — the *only* one in the stock AMD path — but it applies to the\n   PLE table, **not** the 121B expert weights.\n2. **The block scale is silently dropped.** `.to(dtype=...)` is a raw numeric cast,\n   not a dequant. **`grep -rn \"scale\" over `common/ple.py` and `amd/ple_layer.py`\n   returns ZERO hits** **[M]** — there is no scale multiply anywhere in the AMD PLE\n   path. An e4m3 tensor cast to bf16 yields values in ±448 with no scale applied.\n\n**Contrast with the NVIDIA tree, which does it properly** —\n`qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_layer.py` **[S]**:\n- imports `Fp8Config` (`:26`) and `create_fp8_scale_parameter`,\n  `create_fp8_weight_parameter`, `is_fp8` (`:27-30`);\n- `\"\"\"FP8 PLE embedding with one global checkpoint scale.\"\"\"` (`:143`) **[CL]**;\n- allocates the table **in FP8** (`create_fp8_weight_parameter`, `:157`) and\n  registers a `weight_scale` parameter (`:162-170`);\n- `\"\"\"Select global-scale FP8 only for quantized PLE checkpoint shards.\"\"\"` (`:188`),\n  guarded by `if not quant_config.is_checkpoint_fp8_serialized:` (`:192`);\n- **gather-time** dequant: `if not is_fp8(embeddings): …` (`:643`) with\n  `_get_embedding_weight_scale()` (`:632-634`).\n\n⇒ **Answer to Q5: it depends which tree you run.** NVIDIA keeps the table FP8 with\ngather-time dequant (no doubling). **AMD has no FP8 support: it would dequantize —\nin fact bare-cast — to bf16 at load, doubling the bytes and discarding the scale.**\n\n**Does AMD actually reach that, or die first?** The checkpoint's scale tensor is\nnamed `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (per the NVIDIA loader's explicit check,\n`nvidia/ple_layer.py:478-480` **[S]**). Tracing that name through the **AMD** loader\n(`amd/ple_layer.py:338-388`) **[S]**: it is not a `hashstats_`/`token_lookup` skip\n(`:340-342`), not in `persistent_buffers` (`:343`), and does **not** match\n`name.startswith(\"ngram_embedding.shard_\") and name.endswith(\".weight\")` (`:352`)\n— so it falls to `regular_weights` (`:388`) and into\n`AutoWeightsLoader(self).load_weights(...)` (`:391`), which for an unmatched name\nraises `ValueError(f\"There is no module or parameter named {prefix!r} …\")` —\n`vllm/model_executor/models/utils.py:412-417` **[S]**.\n\n⇒ Most likely the AMD PLE path **also fails loudly at load** on the orphan\n`weight_scale`. I did not execute this, so: **the disposition of the AMD PLE FP8\ntable is UNDETERMINED between \"loud ValueError\" and \"silent bare-cast to bf16\"** —\nit turns on the exact tensor names in the real checkpoint, which is not present on\nthis box. What would settle it: `ls`/safetensors-index inspection of\n`Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8` for keys matching `*ngram_embedding*`. Either way it is\n**not** a silent 125 GiB expert twin.\n\n(The mmap path, `qwen4_exp/nvidia/ple_mmap.py`, exists **only in 54129** and **only\nunder `nvidia/`** — `diff -rq` **[M]**. It is fully FP8-aware:\n`_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn, …}` at `ple_mmap.py:78-80` with\n`_SCALE_TORCH_DTYPES` at `:85-87` **[S]**. Out of scope for the AMD run.)\n\n---\n\n## 6. Envs and flags that change the dispatch\n\nAll defaults read from `vllm/envs.py` this session **[S]**.\n\n| Env | Default | Line(s) | Effect on this model @ gfx1151 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER` | `False` | `envs.py:134`, `:1238-1239` | Latches `_AITER_ENABLED`. **If explicitly set**, `envs.is_set(...)` at `oracle/fp8.py:374` diverts to the AITER branch (`:375-387`) — either removing AITER or hard `_return_or_raise`ing on it. Leave unset. |\n| `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER_MOE` | **`True`** | `envs.py:138`, `:1258-1259` | Same `envs.is_set` trip-wire. Note the default is True but `is_set` tests *explicit* presence — exporting `=1` is not a no-op, it changes control flow. |\n| `VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM` | **`True`** | `envs.py:196`, `:1549` | **Footgun.** `envs.is_set(\"VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM\")` at `oracle/fp8.py:358`; if explicitly set truthy, `oracle/fp8.py:363-370` **returns `_return_or_raise(DEEPGEMM…)` immediately**, converting the graceful enumeration into a hard `ValueError` on DeepGEMM specifically. Also feeds `is_deep_gemm_supported()` (`utils/deep_gemm.py:110`). |\n| `VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM` | **`True`** | `envs.py:197`, `:1551-1552` | Same trip-wire, same branch. |\n| `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT` | `False` | `envs.py:92`, `:622` | Gates the linear bf16 dequant at `quantization/fp8.py:427-441`, and adds a kernel filter at `modular_kernel.py:592-593`. **Do not enable** — it is the one stock switch that turns on a `.to(torch.bfloat16)` weight upconversion (linear, non-block only). |\n| `-O.moe_backend` (`MoEBackend`) | `\"auto\"` | `config/kernel.py:122-142`, `:235` | Non-`auto` skips enumeration and goes straight to `_return_or_raise` (`oracle/fp8.py:330-356`). `\"emulation\"` is accepted by the Literal (`:142`) but rejected by `map_fp8_backend` (`oracle/fp8.py:250-265`). |\n| `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER_MOE_SITUV2_A8W4` | `False` | `envs.py:140`, `:1265-1266` | Not applicable (a8w4). |\n| `VLLM_USE_TRITON_AWQ` | `False` | `envs.py:122`, `:1172` | Not applicable (AWQ). |\n\nAlso relevant, read but not an env: `rocm_aiter_ops.is_rdna_aiter_enabled()`\n(`_aiter_ops.py:1867-1877`) returns `on_rdna4() and _AITER_ENABLED` **[S]** — it is\n**gfx12-only by construction** (docstring: *\"AITER on RDNA4 (gfx12) … The gfx12\nanalog of `is_enabled()`\"* **[CL]**), so it is **False on gfx1151** and provides no\nroute in.\n\n**Bottom line on envs: no combination flips the block-FP8 MoE onto a BF16 twin.**\nThe reachable outcomes are the `NotImplementedError` (auto) or a more specific\n`ValueError` (forced backend).\n\n---\n\n## 7. B5 gate: what to observe tomorrow\n\n### 7a. The gate will almost certainly not get to a memory measurement\n\nStock at these heads **cannot start this model on gfx1151**. Expect, during model\nconstruction and **before any expert weights load**:\n\n```\nNotImplementedError: No FP8 MoE backend supports the deployment configuration.\n```\n\nfrom `oracle/fp8.py:414-416` (54129) / `:448-450` (53896), raised via\n`quantization/fp8.py:500` (54129) / `:529` (53896). **[S]**\n\nTreat \"the server started at all\" as the *first* signal. If it starts, something is\npatched relative to these heads and the whole analysis must be re-run against what\nactually ran.\n\n**Recommended pre-flight (cheap, and it converts a hardware day into a source\nanswer):** run with `VLLM_LOGGING_LEVEL=DEBUG` and capture the `debug_once` lines\nfrom `oracle/fp8.py:410` — `_make_log_unsupported` prints, per backend, exactly\nwhich predicate failed (`oracle/fp8.py:296-306` **[S]**). That log *is* the\nelimination table in §1d, produced by the machine rather than by me.\n\n### 7b. If (and only if) it does start: the twin-vs-good discriminator\n\nThe distinguishing observable is **not** load-time RSS. Precisely:\n\n- **The good case** (a native block-FP8 kernel consuming FP8 in place): expert\n  bytes ≈ FP8 footprint, and that footprint is **flat from end-of-load through\n  warmed decode**. Cold and warmed agree.\n- **The twin case**: a **step increase after the first decode steps**, of order the\n  expert-weight FP8 size again (~+125 GiB/node at 121B FP8 params — which on a TP=2\n  node is fatal, hence the gate). The step appears **between cold and warmed**, not\n  at load.\n\n**Which source lines make load-time RSS lie.** In *stock* at these heads: **none** —\nthere is no lazy or cached weight materialization in the reachable fp8 MoE path\n(§2), so a stock run's post-load footprint would be honest. The lying construct is\n**DS4-patch-only**, and its docstring says so explicitly —\n`ds4_tl_indexer.py:344-352` **[S]**:\n\n> *\"None means 'do not take the bf16 path'. That happens when the cache is cold and M\n> is large: the startup memory-profiling forward runs at large M with an empty cache,\n> and building the copy there inflates the measured footprint and shrinks the KV cache\n> allocation. Decode (M <= 32) populates the cache; prefill reuses entries that\n> already exist unless DS4_W8A8_BF16_PREFILL=0.\"*\n\nSo the cache is **deliberately engineered to be empty during startup memory\nprofiling** and to populate only at decode `M <= 32` (`ds4_tl_indexer.py:353-358`\n**[S]**, keyed by `weight.data_ptr()` at `:353`). Under DS4, **load-time RSS is\nguaranteed not to show the twin.**\n\nOperationally, therefore:\n\n1. Measure **after ≥1 real decode step at small batch (M ≤ 32)**, not after load and\n   not after prefill-only. A prefill-only warmup can miss it entirely when\n   `DS4_W8A8_BF16_PREFILL=0`.\n2. Take **cold (post-load, pre-decode) and warmed (post-decode) readings and diff\n   them.** A single absolute number cannot distinguish the cases.\n3. **Read GTT/VRAM, not just process RSS.** The twin is built with\n   `rocm_unquantized_gemm_impl` operands on device (`ds4_tl_indexer.py:386-387`\n   **[S]**); a device-side allocation need not move process RSS proportionally.\n   Use `amd-smi`/`rocm-smi` VRAM+GTT alongside RSS.\n4. If DS4 is in play, `DS4_W8A8_BF16` / `DS4_W8A8_BF16_DIRECT` (latched at import,\n   `vllm-upstream.patch:1444-1452` **[S]**) are the on/off switch — and\n   `_DS4_DIRECT_HIT = [0, 0]  # [taken, fell-back]` (`:1454`) **[S]** is an existing\n   in-process counter that answers \"did the bf16 path actually run\" directly,\n   without inferring it from memory at all. Prefer that counter over any RSS\n   inference.\n\n### 7c. Scope correction for the gate\n\nEven in the worst DS4 case, the twin covers **linear** weights (§2), not the 512\nrouted experts — so the \"~125 GiB more per node\" figure, which was computed from the\n~121B FP8 *expert* params, **does not describe any code path found in either tree**.\nThe gate should be restated against linear-weight footprint, or against the PLE\ntable (§5), whichever DS4 actually enables.\n\n---\n\n## Surprises vs. the campaign record\n\n1. **The record's three anchors all hold, but all three line numbers drifted.**\n   fp8.py:466-468 → **`quantization/fp8.py:427-441`** (54129); rocm.py:870 →\n   **`platforms/rocm.py:971-972`** (54129) / **`:972-973`** (53896);\n   oracle/fp8.py:416-419 → **`oracle/fp8.py:413-416`** (54129) / **`:447-450`** (53896).\n2. **The \"some capability check\" at rocm.py is now concrete:** `on_cdna() or\n   on_rdna4()`, with gfx1151 excluded from both by string match\n   (`platforms/rocm.py:229,232`) — while gfx1151 *is* otherwise a first-class\n   recognised device in the same file (`:78`, `:220`). Deliberate exclusion, not an\n   oversight.\n3. **The feared class name is real but is a *linear* kernel** —\n   `TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel` at `kernels/linear/scaled_mm/triton.py:159`, with\n   **no `apply_weights` and no cache in stock**. The `cached_bf16_weight` hook has\n   **zero occurrences in either checkout** **[M]**; it is entirely DS4-patch-local\n   and, even there, linear-only. The expert-twin scenario is not supported by source.\n4. **A dequant-to-BF16 MoE backend does exist in stock** (`Fp8MoeBackend.EMULATION`,\n   `oracle/fp8.py:57-60`) — closer to the fear than the record anticipated — but it\n   is MXFP8-only, absent from the fp8 `_AVAILABLE_BACKENDS`, and unreachable even via\n   `-O.moe_backend=emulation`.\n5. **NEW / not in the record: the two PR heads implement TP block-scale refine in\n   different places, with different safety.** 53896 refines *before* the oracle and\n   encodes it in the QuantKey (`oracle/fp8.py:272`, `quantization/fp8.py:516-518`).\n   54129 refines *after* and **force-assigns `experts_cls` without calling\n   `is_supported_config`** (`quantization/fp8.py:530-531`). On gfx1151 it is dead\n   code behind the raise, but it is a latent silent-mis-dispatch shape and is the\n   one place either tree installs a kernel unchecked.\n6. **NEW / most actionable: the AMD PLE ngram embedding has no FP8 support\n   whatsoever**, while the NVIDIA one has a purpose-built FP8 embedding method with\n   a global scale and gather-time dequant. On AMD the table is a plain bf16\n   `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`amd/ple_layer.py:195-200`) and the loader bare-casts\n   with `.to(dtype=target.dtype)` and **no scale multiply anywhere**\n   (`common/ple.py:74`; zero `scale` hits in the AMD PLE files **[M]**). This is a\n   real correctness-and-bytes gap in the AMD tree independent of the MoE question.\n7. **`VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM` / `VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM` default to `True` but are read\n   via `envs.is_set()`** — explicitly exporting the value they already have is *not*\n   a no-op; it diverts the oracle into a hard single-backend raise\n   (`oracle/fp8.py:358-370`). Same trip-wire on the AITER pair.\n\n## Explicitly UNDETERMINED\n\n- **Whether the AMD PLE path raises on the orphan `weight_scale` or silently\n  bare-casts.** Looked at: `amd/ple_layer.py:338-391`, `common/ple.py:44-75`,\n  `models/utils.py:412-417`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:478-480`. Settled by: the actual\n  tensor-name list in `Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8` (not present on this box).\n- **Byte size of the PLE table**, hence the cost of its bf16 doubling. The ngram\n  geometry is config-driven (`ngram_vocab_size_base`, `heads_per_ngram`,\n  `ple_embed_dim`, `make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by` — read at\n  `amd/ple_layer.py:140-195`) and `grep` over\n  `vllm/transformers_utils/configs/qwen4_exp.py` returned **no** hits for those\n  field names **[M]**, so the defaults are not in-tree. Settled by the checkpoint's\n  `config.json`.\n- **Runtime confirmation of `_GCN_ARCH`.** The gfx1151 conclusion is derived from\n  the string predicates at `platforms/rocm.py:217-232` and the documented device id\n  at `:78`; I did not execute on the device. Settled by\n  `python -c \"import vllm.platforms.rocm as r; print(r._GCN_ARCH, r.on_cdna(), r.on_rdna4())\"`\n  on the box tomorrow — a one-liner worth running before anything else.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991841048},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, check container/build.sh and the Containerfile to see how the fork's checkout is placed inside the container (the path), and also check receipts-verify.py for the receipt schema validation of proxy.json.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b3a51ca902ca434fa32640be","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/container/Containerfile"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b513cb3e97fd4453b9117f5b","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling/scripts/receipts-verify.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":12920,"output":163,"cacheRead":25600,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":42,"totalTokens":38683,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991841053,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3bf4b536-3520-437d-8e01-ed6d4c567194","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b3a51ca902ca434fa32640be","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# flashnext:dev — the engine fork built from source at the exactly pinned wheel set\n# ============================================================================\n#\n# Recipe shape adapted from kyuz0/amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes,\n# `Dockerfile.ubuntu-repoamd` @ 23cb726, credited per THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.\n# That recipe is MIT licensed; the notice is retained here as required:\n#\n#   MIT License — Copyright (c) kyuz0 (amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes)\n#\n#   Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a\n#   copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"),\n#   to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation\n#   the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,\n#   and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the\n#   Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n#\n#   The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in\n#   all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n#\n#   THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\n#   IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n#   FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n#   AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n#   LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING\n#   FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER\n#   DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n#\n# What changed vs the donor recipe (per IMPORTS.md sections 1 and 2):\n#   - torch is pinned to the fork's exact pin 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 from the AMD\n#     stable multi-arch index (the index tops out there; no ROCm 10 wheels\n#     exist for gfx1151 — specs/flashnext/evidence/kyuz0-rocm10.md).\n#   - torchaudio is OMITTED; we drop the audio extra rather than downgrade\n#     torch to 2.11 (torchaudio capped kyuz0's auto-resolved set there).\n#   - the engine is the flashnext fork (mecattaf/vllm, branch flashnext),\n#     built from source for gfx1151, not a stock vLLM ref.\n#   - designed for overnight iteration: bind-mount a fork checkout over\n#     /opt/vllm and a host dir over /root/.ccache (see container/build.sh);\n#     the editable install then recompiles only what a one-line patch touched.\n#\n# Wheel provenance: the torch/triton/vision/rocm-sdk substrate comes ONLY\n# from the vendor stable index (spec F.11). Generic Python build tooling\n# (setuptools, wheel, packaging, setuptools-scm) and vLLM's upstream runtime\n# dependencies resolve from PyPI as upstream vLLM does; none of those is part\n# of the pinned substrate.\n\nFROM docker.io/ubuntu:24.04\n\nARG ROCM_WHL=https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/\n# The exactly pinned wheel set (IMPORTS.md section 1). The triton pin is the\n# wheel accompanying the torch 2.13.0 set on the vendor stable index —\n# verified by pip's own resolver: torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 depends on exactly\n# triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0 (anything else is ResolutionImpossible).\n# < 3.8 awareness (IMPORTS.md): the fork's fp8-upcast gate keys on triton < 3.8,\n# so on this wheel the admission stands but the in-kernel upcast is bypassed —\n# fp8 dots ride triton 3.8's own lowering, and the fidelity checkpoint grades it.\nARG TORCH_PIN=\"torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0\"\nARG TORCHVISION_PIN=\"torchvision==0.28.0+rocm7.14.0\"\nARG TRITON_PIN=\"triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0\"\n# The from-source engine build needs the HIP compiler + cmake packages, which\n# the runtime wheels do not pull in; devel files merge into _rocm_sdk_core.\nARG ROCM_DEVEL_PIN=\"rocm-sdk-devel==7.14.0\"\n# The generic torch/torchvision wheels carry no device kernels; gfx1151 kernel\n# images ship as separate device packages (torch loads them from torch/.kpack/).\n# Without these the first kernel launch dies with hipErrorInvalidImage.\nARG DEVICE_PINS=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1151==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151==0.28.0+rocm7.14.0 rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==7.14.0\"\n\nENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n        python3.12 python3.12-venv python3.12-dev \\\n        git ca-certificates curl \\\n        build-essential cmake ninja-build ccache \\\n        cargo rustc libnuma-dev \\\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\n# Python 3.12 venv at /opt/venv (pip arrives via ensurepip — no external fetch).\nRUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/venv\nENV VIRTUAL_ENV=/opt/venv \\\n    PATH=/opt/venv/bin:$PATH \\\n    PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0 \\\n    PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 \\\n    CCACHE_DIR=/root/.ccache \\\n    HOME=/root\n\n# Python build tooling (setuptools is served by the vendor index itself; the\n# rest is generic build-time tooling, not substrate).\nRUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple \\\n        setuptools wheel packaging \"setuptools-scm>=8.0\" setuptools-rust\n\n# ---- the pinned wheel substrate (vendor stable index only, spec F.11) ----\nRUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} \\\n        ${TORCH_PIN} ${TORCHVISION_PIN} ${TRITON_PIN} ${ROCM_DEVEL_PIN} ${DEVICE_PINS}\n\n# ---- the engine fork, built from source for gfx1151 ----\nARG VLLM_REPO=https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm\nARG VLLM_BRANCH=flashnext\nENV VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=rocm \\\n    PYTORCH_ROCM_ARCH=gfx1151\n\n# If a host fork checkout is bind-mounted over /opt/vllm (build.sh -v), keep\n# it — that is the incremental-iteration path. Otherwise clone the baseline.\nRUN if [ ! -d /opt/vllm/.git ]; then \\\n        git clone --depth 1 --branch flashnext https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm /opt/vllm; \\\n    fi \\\n    && git -C /opt/vllm rev-parse HEAD > /opt/flashnext-fork-commit \\\n    && git -C /opt/vllm rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD >> /opt/flashnext-fork-commit\n\n# Editable, no build isolation: the build links against the venv's pinned\n# torch and never resolves build-system.requires (the fork's torch==2.11.0\n# build-system pin is therefore irrelevant here — IMPORTS.md section 1).\n# Build artifacts land inside /opt/vllm, so a bind-mounted checkout keeps\n# them across rebuilds and ccache (on PATH, auto-detected by vLLM's cmake)\n# recompiles only what changed.\nRUN pip install -e /opt/vllm --no-build-isolation\n\n# ---- the fn_ instrument overlay ----\n# Lands the instrument modules importable at the top level of site-packages.\nCOPY container/rootfs/ /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/\n\n# ---- build identity ----\nRUN mkdir -p /opt/flashnext && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, time, importlib.metadata as md\ndef ver(name):\n    try:\n        return md.version(name)\n    except md.PackageNotFoundError:\n        return \"unknown\"\nlines = open(\"/opt/flashnext-fork-commit\").read().splitlines()\ninfo = {\n    \"lane\": \"container\",\n    \"image\": \"flashnext:dev\",\n    \"fork_repo\": \"https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm\",\n    \"fork_commit\": lines[0],\n    \"fork_branch\": lines[1] if len(lines) > 1 else \"flashnext\",\n    \"torch\": md.version(\"torch\"),\n    \"torchvision\": md.version(\"torchvision\"),\n    \"triton\": md.version(\"triton\"),\n    \"rocm_sdk_core\": ver(\"rocm-sdk-core\"),\n    \"amd_torch_device_gfx1151\": ver(\"amd-torch-device-gfx1151\"),\n    \"gpu_target\": \"gfx1151\",\n    \"built_at\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z\"),\n}\njson.dump(info, open(\"/opt/flashnext/build-info.json\", \"w\"), indent=1)\nPY\nRUN cat /opt/flashnext/build-info.json\n\nWORKDIR /opt/vllm\nCMD [\"bash\"]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991848015},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b513cb3e97fd4453b9117f5b","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate every overnight receipt under results/receipts/.\n\nA receipt is one JSON object: {\"step\": str, \"status\": \"pass\"|\"fail\",\n\"ts\": str, \"data\": {...}}. Missing receipts are legal (the step has not\nrun); a present receipt must be well-formed, and hard bounds are enforced\nper step. Any violation exits 2 — this is the ladder gate that makes the\novernight claims graded rather than narrated.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nRECEIPTS = ROOT / \"results\" / \"receipts\"\n\n# Per-step hard bounds: step-name prefix -> checker(data) -> error or None.\ndef _residency(d):\n    for rank, gib in (d.get(\"gtt_gib_per_rank\") or {}).items():\n        if gib > 80:\n            return f\"rank {rank} GTT {gib} GiB exceeds the 80 GiB bound\"\n    if d.get(\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\", 0) != 0:\n        return \"table has GPU-resident bytes; the mmap path is not engaged\"\n    if not d.get(\"read_after_warmed_decode\", False):\n        return \"residency was not read after a warmed decode\"\n    return None\n\ndef _tp2(d):\n    if not d.get(\"byte_identical_repeat\", False):\n        return \"greedy repeat was not byte-identical\"\n    return None\n\ndef _weights(d):\n    if d.get(\"shards\", 0) < 131:\n        return f\"only {d.get('shards')} shards staged\"\n    return None\n\ndef _bench(d):\n    if d.get(\"loads_per_arm\", 0) < 3:\n        return \"fewer than 3 loads per arm\"\n    if not d.get(\"counterbalanced\", False):\n        return \"arms were not counterbalanced\"\n    return None\n\ndef _context(d):\n    ratio = d.get(\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\")\n    if ratio is not None and ratio < 0.9:\n        return f\"full-context decode ratio {ratio} below the 0.9 bound\"\n    return None\n\ndef _smoke(d):\n    ap = (d.get(\"aperture\") or {}).get(\"ttm_pages_limit\")\n    if ap is not None and ap != 33554432:\n        return f\"aperture {ap} diverges from the 33554432-page ceiling\"\n    return None\n\nBOUNDS = {\n    \"residency\": _residency,\n    \"tp2\": _tp2,\n    \"weights\": _weights,\n    \"bench\": _bench,\n    \"context\": _context,\n    \"smoke\": _smoke,\n}\n\ndef _handoff_patch_ok() -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"When the catalog patch exists it must parse as a git patch.\"\"\"\n    import subprocess\n    patch = ROOT / \"handoff\" / \"catalog-row.patch\"\n    if not patch.is_file():\n        return None\n    proc = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"apply\", \"--stat\", str(patch)],\n                          capture_output=True, text=True)\n    if proc.returncode != 0:\n        return f\"handoff/catalog-row.patch does not parse: {proc.stderr.strip()}\"\n    return None\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    bad = 0\n    err = _handoff_patch_ok()\n    if err:\n        print(f\"receipts-verify: {err}\")\n        bad += 1\n    if not RECEIPTS.is_dir():\n        print(\"receipts-verify: no receipts directory; nothing ran\")\n        return 2 if bad else 0\n    seen = 0\n    for path in sorted(RECEIPTS.glob(\"*.json\")):\n        seen += 1\n        try:\n            r = json.loads(path.read_text())\n        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: invalid JSON: {e}\")\n            bad += 1\n            continue\n        for key in (\"step\", \"status\", \"ts\"):\n            if key not in r:\n                print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: missing '{key}'\")\n                bad += 1\n        if r.get(\"status\") == \"fail\":\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: step '{r.get('step')}' FAILED\")\n            bad += 1\n        for prefix, check in BOUNDS.items():\n            if str(r.get(\"step\", \"\")).startswith(prefix):\n                err = check(r.get(\"data\") or {})\n                if err:\n                    print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: {err}\")\n                    bad += 1\n    print(f\"receipts-verify: {seen} receipts checked, {bad} violations\")\n    return 2 if bad else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991848016},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, check the host tooling (fn-env.sh, run-tp2.sh? no wait, run-tp2.sh is already in scripts). Check run-tp2.sh (probably created by the host-tooling task) and container/build.sh to see the conventions for launching the container and how to run the scripts. Also check stage-weights.sh. Also check existing receipt formats (results/receipts/build.json, weights-*.json).\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f8b5d02c09b4449b94f6f03c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && cat scripts/run-tp2.sh"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9bda68f6dde84495926ee52e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && cat host/fn-env.sh && ls results/receipts && cat results/receipts/build.json 2>/dev/null | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":4011,"output":248,"cacheRead":38016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":91,"totalTokens":42275,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991848021,"responseId":"chatcmpl-43d29ada-1c9d-419d-9b2e-a946dc61dcdf","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f8b5d02c09b4449b94f6f03c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/run-tp2.sh — flashnext first-light runner on the pair.\n#\n# Sequence (spec claims 4.2–4.5):\n#   preflight -> pair up -> two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared\n#   (tp2.json) -> residency read after 50 warmed decode tokens (residency.json,\n#   ruling P11) -> fidelity baseline: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits\n#   under results/ (fidelity.json) -> the 262144-context probe (context.json).\n#\n# Every receipt is one JSON object {\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"} exactly as\n# scripts/receipts-verify.py expects. A step that cannot complete writes a\n# fail receipt (graded, never silently skipped — spec F.12) and the runner\n# exits non-zero.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts\"\nFIDELITY_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/fidelity\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\nexport RECEIPTS\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\n\nlog() { echo \"run-tp2: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# --- preflight, then stand the pair -------------------------------------------\nlog \"preflight\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-preflight.sh\"\n\nlog \"standing the pair service\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n\nexport FN_API=\"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT\"\n\n# --- step tp2: two greedy 300-token completions, byte-compared -----------------\nlog \"step tp2: greedy byte-compare\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nPROMPT = (\"The pair boots before the operator arrives, and the ledger \"\n          \"records what the night proved: \")\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\ndef greedy_300():\n    return post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": PROMPT,\n                                    \"max_tokens\": 300, \"temperature\": 0})\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    a, b = greedy_300(), greedy_300()\n    ta = a[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    tb = b[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = ta == tb\n    data[\"completion_bytes\"] = len(ta)\n    data[\"completion_tokens\"] = a[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\n    if ta != tb:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = False\n\n# Fold the preflight record into the tp2 receipt (U.2: held sub-budget round\n# trips on both ends ride with the tp2 claim).\ntry:\n    pre = json.load(open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"preflight.json\")))\n    data[\"preflight\"] = pre[\"data\"]\nexcept Exception as e:\n    data[\"preflight\"] = f\"unreadable: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"tp2\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"tp2.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: tp2 receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step residency: read after 50 warmed decode tokens, per ruling P11 --------\nlog \"step residency: warmed decode, then per-rank readings\"\ncat > \"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\" <<'PY'\n\"\"\"Per-rank residency probes: GTT from sysfs, process RSS, table page-cache\nresidency. Stdlib only; runs on either node (local or over ssh stdin).\"\"\"\nimport fnmatch, glob, json, os, shutil, subprocess\n\ndef first_int(paths):\n    for p in paths:\n        try:\n            return int(open(p).read().strip())\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n    return None\n\ndef gtt():\n    # GTT usage first; on the unified-memory APU the VRAM reading is the\n    # fallback (patch 0004 re-points VRAM reporting at unified memory).\n    for name, label in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\", \"gtt\"),\n                        (\"mem_info_vram_used\", \"vram\")):\n        v = first_int(sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\" + name)))\n        if v is not None:\n            return v, label\n    return None, None\n\ndef rss_bytes():\n    total = 0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit():\n            continue\n        try:\n            cmd = open(\"/proc/%s/cmdline\" % pid, \"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" not in cmd and \"ray::\" not in cmd:\n            continue\n        try:\n            status = open(\"/proc/%s/status\" % pid).read()\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        for line in status.splitlines():\n            if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                total += int(line.split()[1]) * 1024\n    return total\n\ndef table_cache():\n    patterns = [p.strip().lower() for p in\n                os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\", \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").split(\",\")\n                if p.strip()]\n    root = os.environ.get(\"FN_MODEL_DIR\", \"/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8\")\n    matched = []\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), pat) for pat in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath, f))\n    fincore = shutil.which(\"fincore\")\n    pages, ok = 0, False\n    if fincore:\n        ok = True\n        for m in matched:\n            out = subprocess.run([fincore, m], capture_output=True, text=True,\n                                 check=False).stdout\n            for line in out.splitlines():\n                parts = line.split()\n                if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].isdigit():\n                    pages += int(parts[1])\n                    break\n    return {\"files_matched\": len(matched), \"files\": matched[:8],\n            \"cached_pages\": pages if ok else None,\n            \"tool\": \"fincore\" if ok else None}\n\ngtt_bytes, gtt_src = gtt()\nprint(json.dumps({\"gtt_bytes\": gtt_bytes, \"gtt_source\": gtt_src,\n                  \"rss_bytes\": rss_bytes(), \"table\": table_cache()}))\nPY\n\nexport FN_METRICS=\"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nWORKER = os.environ[\"FN_WORKER_HOST\"]\nSERVE_LOG = os.path.join(os.environ[\"FN_STATE_DIR\"], \"serve.log\")\nMETRICS = os.environ[\"FN_METRICS\"]\nPAGE = 4096\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    # Compile pass, then a decode long enough to be called warmed (>50 tokens).\n    post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                             \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path: \",\n                             \"max_tokens\": 16, \"temperature\": 0})\n    r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                                 \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path further: \",\n                                 \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0})\n    warmed = int(r[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"])\n    data[\"warmed_decode_tokens\"] = warmed\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = warmed >= 50\n    if warmed < 50:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = False\n    data[\"error_warmup\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\nmetrics_src = open(METRICS).read()\ndef probe(node):\n    if node == \"coordinator\":\n        out = subprocess.run([sys.executable, METRICS], capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    else:\n        out = subprocess.run([\"ssh\", WORKER, \"python3\", \"-\"],\n                             input=metrics_src.encode(), capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    return json.loads(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])\n\ntry:\n    per_rank = {n: probe(n) for n in (\"coordinator\", \"worker\")}\n    data[\"gtt_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"gtt_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2)\n        for n, m in per_rank.items() if m[\"gtt_bytes\"] is not None}\n    data[\"gtt_source_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: m[\"gtt_source\"] for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"rss_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"rss_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2) for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"table_page_cache\"] = {\n        n: {**per_rank[n][\"table\"],\n            \"cached_gib\": (None if per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] is None\n                           else round(per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] * PAGE / 2**30, 2))}\n        for n in per_rank}\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error_metrics\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\n# The mmap engagement probe: zero table bytes GPU-resident only if the table\n# path actually engaged in this run's own serve log under VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1.\ntry:\n    log_text = open(SERVE_LOG, errors=\"ignore\").read().lower()\n    engaged = (\"ple_mmap\" in log_text\n               and os.environ.get(\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP\") == \"1\")\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = 0 if engaged else None\n    data[\"ple_mmap_log_lines\"] = log_text.count(\"ple_mmap\")\n    if not engaged:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = None\n    data[\"error_ple_probe\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"residency\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"residency.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: residency receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step fidelity: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits ---------------------\nlog \"step fidelity: reference losses and frontier logits\"\nexport FIDELITY_DIR\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nOUT_DIR = os.environ[\"FIDELITY_DIR\"]\nPROMPTS = [\n    \"The fleet measures before it claims, because \",\n    \"A receipt is a fact the morning operator can check: \",\n    \"Two ranks agree byte for byte when \",\n]\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\nfiles, hashes, losses = [], {}, []\nfor i, prompt in enumerate(PROMPTS):\n    name = f\"fidelity-{i}.json\"\n    path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, name)\n    try:\n        r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                                     \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0,\n                                     \"logprobs\": 5})\n        choice = r[\"choices\"][0]\n        token_logprobs = [x for x in choice[\"logprobs\"][\"token_logprobs\"]\n                          if x is not None]\n        record = {\n            \"model\": MODEL,\n            \"prompt\": prompt,\n            \"text\": choice[\"text\"],\n            \"tokens\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"],\n            # Reference loss: mean negative log-likelihood of the fixed\n            # continuation under the as-served stack.\n            \"loss_nll\": -sum(token_logprobs) / len(token_logprobs),\n            # Frontier logits: the top-5 at the final generated token — the\n            # yardstick later changes must not move without a reason.\n            \"frontier_logits\": {\n                \"token\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"][-1],\n                \"top5\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"top_logprobs\"][-1],\n            },\n        }\n        losses.append(record[\"loss_nll\"])\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    except Exception as e:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        record = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                  \"error\": f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"}\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    files.append(name)\n    hashes[name] = hashlib.sha256(open(path, \"rb\").read()).hexdigest()\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"fidelity\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": {\"files\": files, \"sha256\": hashes, \"losses_nll\": losses,\n                    \"prompts\": len(PROMPTS)}},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"fidelity.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: fidelity receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step context: the 262144-context probe -------------------------------------\nlog \"step context: decode at full context vs short context\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nTARGET = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CONTEXT_TARGET\", \"262144\"))\nDECODE = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CTX_DECODE\", \"128\"))\n# Claim 4.5's given context depth. The prompt is synthesized from a fixed\n# paragraph: tokenization is engine-side, so we verify the actual prompt\n# tokens the server reports and re-pad once if we undershot.\nPARAGRAPH = (\"The ledger records what the night proved. Every receipt is a \"\n             \"fact the morning operator can check. The pair serves from its \"\n             \"own drives; the rails carry tensors and the wire carries \"\n             \"control. Nothing is narrated that was not measured. \")\n\ndef stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens, timeout=7200):\n    body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt, \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n            \"temperature\": 0, \"stream\": True,\n            \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True}}\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + \"/v1/completions\",\n                                 data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    t_first = t_last = None\n    usage = None\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[5:].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                if choice.get(\"text\"):\n                    now = time.time()\n                    if t_first is None:\n                        t_first = now\n                    t_last = now\n    return usage, t_first, t_last\n\ndef decode_tps(prompt, max_tokens):\n    usage, t_first, t_last = stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens)\n    if not usage or t_first is None or t_last is None or t_last <= t_first:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"no usable decode window in the streamed response\")\n    tokens = usage[\"completion_tokens\"]\n    return (tokens - 1) / (t_last - t_first), usage[\"prompt_tokens\"]\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {\"target_context\": TARGET}\ntry:\n    short_tps, short_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(\n        \"Measure the short-context decode: \" + PARAGRAPH, DECODE)\n    data[\"short_prompt_tokens\"] = short_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"short_decode_tps\"] = round(short_tps, 3)\n\n    est_chars_per_token = 4.2  # conservative: undershoot tokens, never the cap\n    long_prompt = PARAGRAPH * int(((TARGET - 2 * DECODE) * est_chars_per_token)\n                                  / len(PARAGRAPH) + 1)\n    long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:  # undershot: re-pad once, exactly\n        long_prompt = long_prompt * int((TARGET - DECODE) / max(long_prompt_tokens, 1)) + PARAGRAPH\n        long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    data[\"long_prompt_tokens\"] = long_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"long_decode_tps\"] = round(long_tps, 3)\n    data[\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\"] = round(long_tps / short_tps, 4)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        data[\"error\"] = f\"server accepted only {long_prompt_tokens} prompt tokens\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"context\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"context.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: context receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\nlog \"first light complete; receipts: $RECEIPTS/{tp2,residency,fidelity,context}.json\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991857223},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9bda68f6dde84495926ee52e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: the ray head, the ray worker, `vllm\n# serve`, and the bring-up/teardown/preflight scripts. Both TP ranks MUST see\n# the same doctrine — fn-preflight.sh byte-diffs the exported env across the\n# pair before first light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below\n# is what carries FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default\n# copy prefixes; without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Discipline lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1–2).\n# Safe to source repeatedly; every default is override-able via the environment.\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: NEVER export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If a knob here\n# looks like \"just pinning the default\", it is not that — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# --- the FN_ configuration surface (propagated to the worker rank by ray) --\nexport FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"\nexport FN_MODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nexport FN_SERVED_NAME=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\nexport FN_PORT=\"${FN_PORT:-1234}\"\nexport FN_RAY_PORT=\"${FN_RAY_PORT:-6379}\"\nexport FN_MAX_CTX=\"${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}\"\nexport FN_GPU_UTIL=\"${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}\"\nexport FN_IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nexport FN_CONTAINER=\"${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}\"\n# Worker-side actions ride the ethernet wire on the STABLE fleet identity,\n# never a 10.99.0.x Thunderbolt rail — the rails carry tensors only\n# (dotfiles-observed.md §2.3: admin traffic prefers the 5GbE cable).\nexport FN_WORKER_HOST=\"${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}\"\nexport FN_HEAD_IP=\"${FN_HEAD_IP:-10.99.9.1}\"\n# The fleet latency budget from modules/lowlat-cluster.nix: 200 us sits well\n# above the measured held figure and well below the unheld one.\nexport FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=\"${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}\"\n\n# --- determinism -------------------------------------------------------------\n# vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from PYTHONHASHSEED:\n# without a fixed value identical token content produces different block\n# filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart.\n# The greedy byte-compare in run-tp2.sh leans on this too.\nexport PYTHONHASHSEED=0\n\n# --- allocator ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# GC is off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB means reclaim starts at\n# ~105 GiB. expandable_segments is the fragmentation half. Safe with\n# --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs.\nexport PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=\"${PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF:-expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85}\"\n\n# --- HSA ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) otherwise, i.e.\n# ~2-3 cores spinning during inference and CPU thermal throttling on a pair\n# whose PM QoS is held for latency. Set FN_HSA_INTERRUPT=0 to revert to spin.\nexport HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\"${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}\"\n\n# --- compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs ------------------\n# The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bring-up after a boot\n# then recompiles every kernel — ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can\n# answer. The state directory is on-disk on both nodes.\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/triton\" 2>/dev/null || true\nexport TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\"${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor}\"\nexport TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\"${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/triton}\"\n\n# --- NCCL: the transport-of-record block --------------------------------------\n# Socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record (spec\n# ruling P7); the RDMA package under host/rdma is operator-attended only, and\n# only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked.\n#\n# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`: only Thunderbolt rails\n# that actually carry a routable /30 IP qualify. Rail 0 (thunderbolt0,\n# 10.99.0.x) is guaranteed; rail 1 (thunderbolt1) is trained but\n# IP-unconfigured today (dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix parks it) and\n# MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP — a peerless link-local rail in\n# the list hangs RCCL bootstrap. We log which rails we chose rather than\n# hardcoding both.\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail addr chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    if [ ! -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ]; then\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent on this node; not listed\" >&2\n      continue\n    fi\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \\\n      | grep -Ev '^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)' | head -n1 || true)\"\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME ($addr is routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  printf '%s' \"$chosen\"\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"\nif [ -z \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ]; then\n  echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no Thunderbolt rail carries a routable IP; refusing to name a phantom transport\" >&2\n  return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1\nfi\n\n# NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY. Since the pre-arm bake an ibverbs device\n# exists on the rails of BOTH nodes (usb4_rdma0 + usb4_rdma5, by design);\n# without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA.\n# Sockets stay the transport of record until the attended morning A/B lands a\n# counterbalanced verdict (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md). Do NOT conditionalize\n# this on device detection — the devices are present by design.\nexport NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\n\n# Cold-kernel-cache bring-up is ~25 min of LLVM before the first collective;\n# the collective timeouts must survive that without a watchdog kill.\nexport TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=\"${TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-2400}\"\nexport TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=\"${TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING:-0}\"\nexport NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=\"${NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS:-2400000}\"\n\n# --- ray ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Propagate FN_* to the worker rank through ray. ESSENTIAL: without this the\n# two TP ranks diverge on every FN_ knob above.\nexport VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\n# Ray and ROCr visible-device handling fight on multi-rank boxes; opt out.\nexport RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"${RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-1}\"\n# On a 128 GB unified-memory box the serve legitimately claims most memory;\n# ray's OOM monitor must not reap the rank. Refresh 0 disables the monitor.\nexport RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=\"${RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms:-0}\"\n# `ray start` pre-starts one idle python worker per CPU (~40 MB each); on a\n# 32-thread box that is over a gigabyte vLLM never touches. Cap the pool\n# small; fn-cluster-up.sh passes this as --num-cpus on both nodes.\nexport RAY_NUM_CPUS=\"${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}\"\n\n# --- the table path ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Serve the engram table from NVMe via mmap: page-cache faults serve gathers,\n# zero table bytes GPU-resident (spec ruling P11's zero-GPU-residency bound\n# leans on this). VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 is the fork's kill switch.\nexport VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\n\n# --- estate hygiene -------------------------------------------------------------\n# Runtime hub downloads stay forbidden (spec F.6): the checkpoint is staged\n# library-to-node, and the engine must never reach for the hub at runtime.\nexport HF_HUB_OFFLINE=\"${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=\"${VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=\"${VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK:-1}\"\nbuild.json\nweights-coordinator.json\nweights-worker.json\n{\n \"step\": \"build\",\n \"status\": \"pass\",\n \"ts\": \"2026-08-29T07:07:21Z\",\n \"data\": {\n  \"lane\": \"container\",\n  \"image\": \"flashnext:dev\",\n  \"torch\": \"2.13.0+rocm7.14.0\",\n  \"triton\": \"3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0\",\n  \"fork_commit\": \"bdb6f0420e797de9266de593326368091085cc3b\",\n  \"image_digest\": \"sha256:175edc169402e327c4eed823caec30d196b7f824712e3a498333da87282f24de\",\n  \"wall_clock_s\": 839\n }\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991857224},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the conventions. To check how the container is invoked, let's also look at container/build.sh and stage-weights.sh (does run-smoke.sh use podman run with a bind mount?). run-smoke.sh directly executes `podman run --rm --device ... $IMAGE python3 - <<'PY'`.\n\nLet's check container/build.sh to confirm the fork checkout path on the host side and whether /opt/vllm is bind-mounted. Also, let's check how run-smoke mounts things. run-smoke.sh only mounts results. make-proxy.sh is executed inside the container and builds the checkpoint into /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. run-proxy.sh serves from that path within the container — therefore run-proxy.sh needs to either bind-mount /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy from the host, or else run-proxy needs to build inside the same container. The task says \"make-proxy.sh is executed inside the container and builds a small synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\" and \"run-proxy.sh serves it from the container on a single node.\" So both are executed inside the container. If the container is run with `podman run --rm`, /var/tmp doesn't persist across separate containers. Options: bind-mount a host directory to /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy in both. That's the natural approach: both scripts bind-mount a host-side directory (e.g., under results, or $FN_STATE_DIR?) — but the boundary says we only touch scripts and tests. Bind-mounting a host path is fine. E.g., use `$REPO_ROOT/results/proxy-checkpoint`, or use a host path like ${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy}. Hmm, results/ is outside the conflict domain, but mounting it isn't \"touching\" it... actually, writing a checkpoint into results/ via bind mount would create a file inside results/ at runtime, but not inside git. The boundary governs the commit paths. Still, better to default the proxy dir to a host state dir. Let's design:\n\nmake-proxy.sh:\n- Runs on the host? No — \"executed inside the container.\" But how does the checkpoint persist to run-proxy.sh? It could bind-mount a host directory as /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. Let's write it so the host side orchestrates: `podman run ... -v \"$PROXY_HOST_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\" $IMAGE python3 - <<'PY'`, where PROXY_HOST_DIR defaults to \"$FN_STATE_DIR/proxy-checkpoint\" or \"$REPO_ROOT/results/proxy\" — hmm, results/ is for receipts. Let's default to `${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint}`.\n\nActually, wait — the script should be self-contained like run-smoke.sh: host-side bash orchestrates the podman run with a python heredoc. make-proxy.sh \"is executed inside the container and builds a tiny synthetic checkpoint\" — meaning the checkpoint-building logic executes inside the container. Good.\n\nReuse of fork test scaffolding: the fork checkout is at /opt/vllm inside the container. tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py has fixture builders that write safetensors with shard names `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors` and use the MmapPleTable/MmapNgramEmbedding scaffolding. We don't have the fork tree at hand (it gets cloned at image build time). We can't inspect those test files directly... or can we? Is there a fork checkout on the host? Let's check container/build.sh to see if it bind-mounts a local checkout.\n\nAlso, we should identify the architecture identifier: \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" (per the registry check in run-smoke.sh) or \"Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\". The smoke test checks for \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" inside archs. The actual checkpoint's config uses either ForCausalLM or ConditionalGeneration. According to evidence, the registry maps both. In the proxy's config.json, architectures should match the workload's. From the dossier, the model is registered under both names. I don't know exactly which one the actual checkpoint uses. The proxy needs \"the same architecture identifier as the workload.\" Which is it? The smoke receipt checks model_arch_registered for \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\". That suggests the workload is ConditionalGeneration. Let's grep the repo for hints (README, handoff, host config, the catalog patch).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a3f4c00ec8f54bc5bae37273","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -rn \"Qwen4Exp\" --include=\"*\" -l | grep -v evidence | head && grep -rn \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\\|Qwen4ExpForCausalLM\" README.md handoff host scripts container 2>/dev/null | head"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e16d74fd356448098c95eb2c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n '1,80p' container/build.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":7477,"output":1147,"cacheRead":41984,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":955,"totalTokens":50608,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991857228,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0dd9b483-1baa-4647-ac49-80e332adf354","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a3f4c00ec8f54bc5bae37273","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"scripts/run-smoke.sh\npatches/0009-Model-Relocate-ple_mmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch\npatches/MANIFEST.md\nIMPORTS.md\nscripts/run-smoke.sh:35:    return {\"model_arch_registered\": \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" in archs}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991887456},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e16d74fd356448098c95eb2c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Container engine build — flashnext:dev (the ruling P4 container lane).\n#\n# Order per the task: packaging tests first, then the image build, then the\n# build receipt. Any error lands a receipt with status \"fail\" — the receipts\n# gate grades this step, so failure is recorded, never silent.\n#\n# Overnight-iteration hooks (see container/Containerfile header):\n#   FN_VLLM_SRC=/path/to/vllm-checkout   bind-mount the fork source over\n#                                        /opt/vllm; the editable install's\n#                                        build artifacts persist there, so a\n#                                        one-line patch rebuild recompiles\n#                                        only what changed.\n#   FN_CCACHE_DIR=/path/to/ccache        host compiler cache (default\n#                                        ~/.cache/flashnext/ccache — always\n#                                        OUTSIDE the repo, so the worktree\n#                                        stays clean), mounted at /root/.ccache.\n#   FN_PIP_CACHE_DIR=/path/to/pip        host pip cache (default\n#                                        ~/.cache/flashnext/pip), mounted at\n#                                        /root/.cache/pip.\n#   FN_IMAGE=name:tag                    image tag (default flashnext:dev).\n#\n# Enter the image for edit-and-restart iteration without a rebuild:\n#   podman run --rm -it --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n#     --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host \\\n#     -v \"$FN_VLLM_SRC:/opt/vllm\" flashnext:dev bash\n#   # inside: edit, then `pip install -e /opt/vllm --no-build-isolation`\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\ncd \"$REPO_ROOT\"\n\nIMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nOUT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/build.json\"\n# Host cache defaults live OUTSIDE the repository so nothing outside the\n# container/ and results/receipts/ write boundary is ever created or left\n# untracked in the worktree. Override with FN_CCACHE_DIR / FN_PIP_CACHE_DIR.\nHOST_CACHE_BASE=\"${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/flashnext\"\nCCACHE_DIR_HOST=\"${FN_CCACHE_DIR:-$HOST_CACHE_BASE/ccache}\"\nPIP_CACHE_HOST=\"${FN_PIP_CACHE_DIR:-$HOST_CACHE_BASE/pip}\"\n\nSTATUS=\"pass\"\nTORCH=\"\" TRITON=\"\" FORK_COMMIT=\"\" IMAGE_DIGEST=\"\"\nSTART_EPOCH=\"$(date +%s)\"\n\nwrite_receipt() {\n    local status=\"$1\"\n    local wall=$(( $(date +%s) - START_EPOCH ))\n    mkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$OUT\")\"\n    FN_STATUS=\"$status\" FN_TS=\"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" FN_WALL=\"$wall\" \\\n    FN_TORCH=\"$TORCH\" FN_TRITON=\"$TRITON\" FN_FORK=\"$FORK_COMMIT\" \\\n    FN_DIGEST=\"$IMAGE_DIGEST\" FN_IMAGE_NAME=\"$IMAGE\" python3 - \"$OUT\" <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys\nr = {\n    \"step\": \"build\",\n    \"status\": os.environ[\"FN_STATUS\"],\n    \"ts\": os.environ[\"FN_TS\"],\n    \"data\": {\n        \"lane\": \"container\",\n        \"image\": os.environ[\"FN_IMAGE_NAME\"],\n        \"torch\": os.environ[\"FN_TORCH\"] or None,\n        \"triton\": os.environ[\"FN_TRITON\"] or None,\n        \"fork_commit\": os.environ[\"FN_FORK\"] or None,\n        \"image_digest\": os.environ[\"FN_DIGEST\"] or None,\n        \"wall_clock_s\": int(os.environ[\"FN_WALL\"]),\n    },\n}\njson.dump(r, open(sys.argv[1], \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"build receipt ({os.environ['FN_STATUS']}): {sys.argv[1]}\")\nPY\n}\n\non_error() {\n    echo \"container/build.sh: FAILED — writing fail receipt\" >&2\n    STATUS=\"fail\"\n    write_receipt \"fail\" || true\n    exit 1\n}\ntrap on_error ERR\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991887457},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let's look at the patches — particularly patches/MANIFEST.md, 0009, and 0010, plus the acceptance patch (the FN_FP8_MOE one) — to understand the fork. Also, let's check whether a fork checkout exists on this host (it might not). Furthermore, I'll check the handoff/ directory and docs/MORNING.md for details on the proxy plan. And also the worklist file silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json for the proxy-tooling task definition.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_305cd866a6474c01a34f883f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && ls patches handoff docs && python3 -c \"\nimport json\nw=json.load(open('silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json'))\nfor t in w.get('tasks', w if isinstance(w,list) else []):\n    if t.get('id')=='proxy-tooling':\n        print(json.dumps(t,indent=1))\n\" 2>/dev/null | head -80"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2493,"output":256,"cacheRead":49152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":110,"totalTokens":51901,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991887461,"responseId":"chatcmpl-de86d1e3-51b3-4219-9a01-a7debf7ea537","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_305cd866a6474c01a34f883f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"docs:\nCONTINGENCY-sampler-cu-remaining-1024-launches.patch\nGATE0.md\n\nhandoff:\nOVERSEER-LAUNCH.md\nOVERSEER.md\nPREARM-REBOOT.md\nROUTING.md\nupstream-issues\n\npatches:\n0001-flashnext-pin-build-system-torch-to-2.11.0-for-the-g.patch\n0002-flashnext-add-tools-check_nix_substrate.py-packaging.patch\n0003-ROCm-detection-via-KFD-topology-when-amdsmi-fails.patch\n0004-Detect-AMD-APU-fix-VRAM-reporting-for-unified-memory.patch\n0005-Disable-skinny-GEMM-on-gfx1151.patch\n0006-Fix-Wave32-LDS-overflow-in-top-k-merge-launch.patch\n0007-fork-kill-switch-and-loud-logging-for-the-APU-memory.patch\n0008-ROCm-Enable-block-FP8-fused-MoE-on-gfx1151-via-in-ke.patch\n0009-Model-Relocate-ple_mmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch\n0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch\n0011-fork-route-AMD-APU-memory-into-MemorySnapshot-on-the.patch\n0012-ROCm-Apply-the-52970-fp8-dot-upcast-to-the-block-sca.patch\nMANIFEST.md\n{\n \"id\": \"proxy-tooling\",\n \"kind\": \"implementation\",\n \"title\": \"Proxy checkpoint builder and single-node first-light runner\",\n \"goal\": \"Write scripts/make-proxy.sh and scripts/run-proxy.sh per spec claim 4.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4). make-proxy.sh runs inside the container and builds a tiny synthetic checkpoint under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy: the same architecture identifier as the workload, a handful of layers, 8 experts in block-FP8 with weight_block_size 128 by 128, a small lookup table with the shard tensor naming the mmap path expects \\u2014 reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout wherever possible instead of inventing generators. run-proxy.sh serves it single-node from the container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution, requests one greedy completion, asserts finite logits via the API, shuts down cleanly, and writes results/receipts/proxy.json. Boundaries: touch only scripts/make-proxy.sh, scripts/run-proxy.sh, and tests/.\",\n \"deliveredBehaviors\": [\n  \"The admitted kernel path and the ported table path execute on this GPU before any real weight byte is loaded\"\n ],\n \"readFirst\": {\n  \"specSections\": [\n   \"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md\"\n  ],\n  \"styleReferences\": [\n   \"scripts/run-smoke.sh\"\n  ]\n },\n \"acceptanceCriteria\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"proxy-scripts-clean\",\n   \"description\": \"Both scripts are syntax-clean and reference the receipt path.\",\n   \"argv\": [\n    \"bash\",\n    \"-euc\",\n    \"bash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh scripts/run-proxy.sh && grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh\"\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"dependencies\": [],\n \"conflictDomains\": [\n  \"scripts/make-proxy.sh\",\n  \"scripts/run-proxy.sh\",\n  \"tests\"\n ]\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991895675},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, let me look at patch 0010 (ROCm: wire VLLM_PLE_MMAP and the FP8 PLE stack onto AMD) and 0009 (moving ple_mmap into common) to understand the exact mmap path of the fork, the guard behavior, and the naming of the fixture under tests/models/ inside the fork. This will determine what run-proxy needs (whether it's PIECEWISE or eager), and also whether there are reusable fixture helpers in the fork's test scaffolding.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_176ceaf6bc0b446b85fa7298","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && cat patches/MANIFEST.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2067,"output":175,"cacheRead":50688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":109,"totalTokens":52930,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991895679,"responseId":"chatcmpl-95e7f59c-e730-444d-bb52-1e062f95ac87","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_176ceaf6bc0b446b85fa7298","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# patches/ — mirror of `mecattaf/vllm` branch `flashnext`\n\nBase: `8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da` (head of upstream PR #54129,\n`Trosfy:ple-mmap-upstream`, which carries the full #53896 model support).\nBranch head mirrored here: `bdb6f0420e797de9266de593326368091085cc3b` — 12\ncommits, one `.patch` each, numbered in apply order. `scripts/verify-fork.sh`\nenforces `n_patches >= n_commits` against the live branch.\n\n**These patches are provenance and review artifacts, not build inputs.** The\nnix lane builds the fork *branch* (Mechanism A: `inputs.vllm-fork` follows\ninto `nix-strix-halo`'s `vllm-src`); feeding this mirror into the derivation's\n`patches` would double-apply every hunk and abort `patchPhase`. Substrate-only\nfixups that must ride outside the branch belong in a separate directory, not\nhere.\n\nDelta grammar: `+N` (no deletions), `+N/-M`, or `new (LINES)`.\n\n## Nix substrate compatibility (patches 0001–0002)\n\n| file | Δ | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pyproject.toml` | +4/-1 | `[build-system] torch == 2.13.0` → `2.11.0`. The gfx1151 substrate ships exactly one torch (TheRock 2.11.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719, cp313), and `nix-strix-halo overlays/therock-vllm.nix:269` hard-aborts on `--replace-fail '\"torch == 2.11.0\"'` if the literal is absent — Gate 0. Audited before pinning: no fork code needs torch ≥ 2.12 (`requirements/build/rocm.txt` already pins 2.11.0; `env_override.py` still carries the `<2.12` backports; all 54 `torch::stable/headeronly` names under `csrc/` resolve in 2.11.0 headers — presence, not signatures). `requirements/build/cuda.txt` deliberately stays 2.13.0 for the container lane, which builds `--no-build-isolation` and never resolves `build-system.requires`. |\n| `tools/check_nix_substrate.py` | new (210) | In-tree, seconds-fast guard for packaging invariants that live *outside* this repo: the 5 `--replace-fail` literals + `rm` target the nix overlay needs, the pre-image anchors of nixpkgs patches 0002/0003/0005, and a 22-name stripped-dependency import scan over the fork-added model surface. Without it a routine refactor breaks the nix lane with no local signal until a multi-hour HIP rebuild aborts. `--warn-only` is the kill switch; failure prints the exact missing literal or `file:line` to stderr and exits 1. Run clean against the assembled head. |\n\n## Cherry-picks from open upstream PRs (patches 0003–0006, `Cherry-picked-from:` trailers)\n\n| file | Δ | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `vllm/platforms/__init__.py` | +30 | #46110: `_kfd_topology_has_amd_gpu()` — ROCm platform selection from `/sys/class/kfd` (vendor 0x1002 + non-zero `gfx_target_version`) when amdsmi cannot enumerate, no HIP init. |\n| `vllm/platforms/rocm.py` (0003) | +36/-2 | #46110: `_query_gcn_arch_from_kfd_topology()` resolves `_GCN_ARCH` as `gfx<major><minor><stepping:x>` (hex stepping is what makes gfx90a come out right); the `torch.cuda` ultimate fallback now `warning_once`s instead of firing silently. |\n| `tests/test_rocm_kfd_topology.py` | new (60) | #46110: fixture-driven pins on the topology parser (110501→gfx1151 path, NVIDIA-vendor and missing-dir rejection). Logic validated standalone on this box. |\n| `vllm/platforms/rocm.py` (0004) | +63 | #40963: `is_integrated_gpu()` (sysfs VRAM > 4× HIP aperture ⇒ APU) and a `mem_get_info()` override serving (free, total) from amdgpu GTT counters with an 8 GiB reserve. On this tree the override is dead on the KV path (profiling moved to `torch.accelerator.get_memory_info`, #44825) — patch 0011 completes it; kept verbatim for provenance and for the one remaining caller. |\n| `vllm/model_executor/layers/utils.py` | +11/-2 | #51511: `and not on_gfx1151()` in `use_skinny` — wvSplitK/LLMM1 are pathologically slow on RDNA3.5; fall back to torch GEMM. No log by design: the impl is a per-layer custom op and a logger call is a torch.compile graph break; `VLLM_ROCM_USE_SKINNY_GEMM` remains the whole-path kill switch. |\n| `tests/model_executor/layers/test_rocm_unquantized_gemm.py` | +36 | #51511: new gfx1151-fallback test (verbatim upstream) + `on_gfx1151 → False` pinned in all six existing tests (upstream pinned its three; the three tests this tree grew since would otherwise read the *real* predicate and fail on the target hardware). |\n| `csrc/libtorch_stable/sampler.cu` | +5 | #46012: merge launch of `top_k_per_row_decode` — the exact custom op the amd/ QSA indexer calls — drops 1024→512 threads under `USE_ROCM`; cub BlockRadixSort's ranking counters scale with threads/wave-size and 1024 overflows 64 KB LDS on wave32. `2048 % 512 == 0` keeps `kNumFinalItemsPerThread` integral. Three gfx950-gated 1024-thread launches above it are untouched (runtime-gated, never launched on gfx1151); a contingency patch dropping them to 512 exists at `docs/CONTINGENCY-sampler-cu-remaining-1024-launches.patch` — apply only on an LDS *build* error, it pessimises gfx950. |\n| `tests/kernels/test_top_k_per_row.py` | +4/-1 | #46012: large-vocab decode test gate widened from CUDA-only to CUDA-or-ROCm. |\n\n## Fork-local hardening + APU memory completion (patches 0007, 0011)\n\n| file | Δ | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `vllm/envs.py` (0007) | +7 | `VLLM_ROCM_APU_UNIFIED_MEMORY` (default on = #40963 behaviour; `=0` is the one-flag revert to stock discrete accounting). The quality bar demands a kill switch per mechanism; upstream #40963 ships none. |\n| `vllm/platforms/rocm.py` (0007) | +50/-4 | Loud verdict log with both measured totals in `is_integrated_gpu()`, loud warnings on every sysfs failure path (upstream swallows them with bare `except: pass`), and `@lru_cache(maxsize=8)` — `release_device_memory_under_pressure` calls it per module during weight loading; globbing sysfs + `hipMemGetInfo` each time is not acceptable. |\n| `vllm/utils/mem_utils.py` (0011) | +22/-7 | The commit that makes #40963 real on this tree. `MemorySnapshot.measure`'s integrated-GPU branch was NVIDIA-Tegra-shaped: `free = psutil` (host RAM) against `total =` HIP aperture drives `cuda_memory = total − free` **negative** (poisoning `non_torch_memory`) and sizes `request_memory` off the ~16 GiB aperture instead of the 128 GiB GTT. Now dispatches by platform: ROCm APUs take both free and total from the GTT-backed `mem_get_info()` override; the psutil free-only override stays byte-identical for the NVIDIA UMA devices it was written for. Same kill switch as 0007. |\n\n## Block-FP8 admission on gfx1151 — the world-first pieces (patches 0008, 0012)\n\n| file | Δ | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/utils.py` | +36 | The `FN_FP8_MOE` latch (read once at import, default **on**) + `fp8_moe_gfx1151_admitted()` + cached `fp8_moe_gfx1151_dot_upcast()` (adds the `triton < 3.8.0` bound of #52970 — newer Triton lowers fp8 `tl.dot` on gfx1151 itself). `on_gfx1151` imported inside the function so `vllm.platforms.rocm` never imports on a non-ROCm box. |\n| `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/triton_moe.py` | +29/-1 | The oracle admission: `_supports_quant_scheme` gains an `elif fp8_moe_gfx1151_admitted():` appending **only** `(kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym)` — this checkpoint's routed-expert scheme — with `info_once` at actual admission. Per-tensor/per-channel stay refused (they take the `fp8_fast_accum` dot with no block scales to validate against). `FN_FP8_MOE=0` restores the stock `NotImplementedError: No FP8 MoE backend supports the deployment configuration.` byte-for-byte; `supports_fp8()` itself is untouched, blast radius is the MoE oracle only. |\n| `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/fused_moe.py` | +22 | The kernel half: `FORCE_FP8_DOT_UPCAST` `tl.constexpr` (default False) converting the loaded A/B tiles to bf16 before `tl.dot`; block scales still multiply in fp32, so it is the same block-scaled GEMM without FP8 WMMA. `invoke_fused_moe_triton_kernel` is the tree's **sole** launch of `fused_moe_kernel`, so one hunk covers gate/up, down, and the chunked non-modular path. |\n| `tests/kernels/moe/test_gfx1151_fp8_admission.py` | new (130) | GPU-free monkeypatched pins: admission, kill switch, gfx1151-only scope, per-tensor refusal, Triton version gate, kernel-signature drift guard. |\n| `vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/utils/fp8_utils.py` (0012) | +46 | The **linear** twin, without which the MoE runs and every dense block-FP8 projection does not: `TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel.is_supported` admits any CUDA-alike device, so gfx1151 reaches `_w8a8_triton_block_scaled_mm` with fp8 operands in `tl.dot`. Same #52970 mechanism, same `ROCm + on_gfx1151() + triton < 3.8.0` gate, own kill switch `FN_FP8_LINEAR` (default on). |\n| `tests/kernels/quantization/test_gfx1151_fp8_linear_upcast.py` | new (84) | Pins the linear gate: version bound, kill switch, gfx1151-only scope, signature drift guard. |\n\n## The amd/ PLE port — VLLM_PLE_MMAP + FP8 stack (patches 0009–0010)\n\n| file | Δ | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/{nvidia→common}/ple_mmap.py` | move + +7/-4 | The mmap module is platform-neutral (its one platform touchpoint, `dispatch_key`, is `\"CUDA\"` on ROCm too) but lived under `nvidia/`, so a ROCm build never imported it and `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1` was a **silent no-op** — the op never registered. Relocated beside `common/ple.py`; docstring names both importing trees. Both trees now reach the *same* module object (op registers exactly once). |\n| `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia/{ple_layer,model}.py`, `tests/.../test_ple_mmap.py`, `vllm/envs.py` | +4/-4 | Import-path follow-ups of the move, plus the `VLLM_PLE_MMAP` doc pointer in `envs.py`. No nvidia behaviour change. |\n| `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py` | +241/-13 | The five wiring sites of ple-54129.md mirrored into amd/ — W1 `cast` import; W2 module-scope `ple_mmap` import; W3 constructor swap behind `ple_mmap.enabled()` with `check_cudagraph_safety` + `validate_shards_for` and a loud log (the flag takes ROCm off FULL cudagraphs and off the AMD gather op; `VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0` default is the kill switch); W4 forward mmap branch placed *before* the AMD gather op (the placeholder has no `params_dtype`; the hashing must run inside the widened op boundary or `.numel()`-derived slicing specializes under torch.compile), with the trigram hashing factored into `_hash_ngram_ids` — byte-identical body, and the *name* is load-bearing (the op body probes it); W5a/W5b `load_weights` scale interception → `ple_mmap.set_weight_scale` and shard-drop with `weights_streamed = True`. Plus Blocker A at full nvidia parity (verified byte-identical): `Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` (fail-closed on a scale-less FP8 table), and `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing — without it an FP8 checkpoint's `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` is an orphan (`ValueError`) and the table is bare-cast to bf16 with the block scale discarded. One deliberate AMD-only addition: `_embedding_output_dtype()` allocates the gather-op output at the table's real dtype, because a bf16 buffer would make `output.copy_()` a scale-discarding FP8→BF16 cast before `_dequantize_embeddings` ever sees fp8. No fnuz relabelling anywhere: gfx1151 is OCP e4m3 (`is_fp8_fnuz()` is gfx94-only) and the checkpoint bytes are e4m3 on every platform. |\n| `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py` | +14/-3 | The two 5-line `build_tables` blocks after each `load_weights` + imports. `diff` against `nvidia/model.py` is now **empty**. |\n| `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py` | new (352) | GPU-free pins on every site above: same-module identity, `_hash_ngram_ids` name, env-on/off constructor swap, both cudagraph guards firing from the AMD constructor, scale-keep/shard-drop loading, FP8 method selection, output-buffer dtype, dequant round-trip and the fail-closed no-scale raise. |\n\n## Not yet executed anywhere\n\nNo engineer box had torch; every `.py` passed `py_compile` + repo-config ruff\n(check + format) and the C++ was reviewed, but **zero pytest ran and nothing\ncompiled for HIP**. First-hardware checklist: `_GCN_ARCH` one-liner, the\n`float8_e4m3fn → bfloat16` cast probe, `pytest` on the four new test files +\n`test_ple_mmap.py`, one tiny-shape MoE forward (LDS risk R1: default ROCm\nconfig stages 2×32 KiB bf16 B-tiles = the whole gfx1151 LDS — a tuned\n`BLOCK_SIZE_N=64` config JSON is the ready mitigation), and\n`triton.__version__` PEP-440 sanity.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991901714},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is substantial. Key points for the proxy work:\n\n1. In the fork checkout, there's `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, and the fixtures build safetensors with shard tensors named `layers.{layer_idx}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{shard_index:05d}.weight`. The fork is at /opt/vllm inside the container, and tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py may have reusable fixture helpers. Let me check the fixture structure in the upstream test file — I don't have the fork tree locally, but I can inspect the relevant parts of patches 0009/0010 (they touch the import paths in tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py). The test file is about 2048 lines, and the fixture writes out `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors` (from ple-54129.md's §3).\n\nRather than depending on the exact contents of the fork's tests (which I can't read), make-proxy.sh should use fork's test scaffolding if it exists, and fall back gracefully if it doesn't. Good approach: inside the container, try importing the fixture helper from the fork's test file (e.g. `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` — add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path and import the module, calling its fixture-building function). However, that file is pytest-based, and fixtures might not be directly callable.\n\nLet me look at patches 0009/0010 and check the actual test file content that's in the patch context — it might reveal the fixture function name and structure.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_32bb06d04e924adfb5ec62f4","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -n \"test_ple_mmap\" patches/0009*.patch | head -20 && grep -c \"\" patches/0009*.patch patches/0010*.patch"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5634,"output":478,"cacheRead":51200,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":372,"totalTokens":57312,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991901719,"responseId":"chatcmpl-301f0794-7994-4bdb-be7a-0c510573fda3","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_32bb06d04e924adfb5ec62f4","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22:  - tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py: the ple_mmap module import\n29: tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py              | 2 +-\n37:diff --git a/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py b/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py\n39:--- a/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py\n40:+++ b/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py\npatches/0009-Model-Relocate-ple_mmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch:98\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:902\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991915094},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The fork checkout inside the container contains `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py`, which has fixture code that builds the shard safetensors (`model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`), and also contains the newly added `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py`. I can't verify the exact function names inside those files from here. The task says \"whenever possible, reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ within the fork checkout, rather than inventing your own generator.\"\n\nA robust approach for make-proxy.sh: write the generator as follows:\n1. Look for the fork's test file `/opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` and introspect it for a fixture-building helper (e.g., a function that writes out shard safetensors). Since I can't read the fork tree, the cleanest and most honest way to \"reuse\" is to import the fork's `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap` module and, if available, its test scaffolding module. If a callable fixture is present, use it; otherwise, use my own minimal writer that uses the same naming.\n\nActually, a better approach: the test file probably defines a helper like `write_shard_file(...)` at module scope. I can inspect it at runtime: import the module (the tests directory needs to be on sys.path), find the callable that creates the fixture checkpoint, and call it with my own geometry. But this is fragile.\n\nAn alternative, more robust design: import the module and use `getattr` against several candidate names to reuse the fork scaffolding. If none of them exist, fall back to a self-contained writer that reproduces the exact shard naming from the mmap path (`_SHARD_RE = layers.(\\d+).ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_(\\d+).weight$`). In addition, include the tensors the loader expects: `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (FP8 table → global scale), plus the rest of the model's weights (embeddings, qkv, experts, and so on).\n\nActually — what does the proxy model need? vLLM's loader will try to load the model from the checkpoint. With mmap enabled, the PLE shard tensors are discarded during load (weights_streamed), and weight_scale is retained. Everything else must be present: the complete model weights for the architecture: Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration with, say, num_hidden_layers=4, hidden_size small, num_experts=8, MoE layers, vocab around 512, etc. The config.json must be shaped like a qwen4_exp config (a transformers_utils config exists in-tree: vllm/transformers_utils/configs/qwen4_exp.py). The geometry is config-driven (ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim...).\n\nSince we're synthesizing, the config fields must include everything the model class reads. That's complex. But wait — this script can't be tested here anyway (no GPU, no container). The acceptance criteria are only `bash -n` syntax check + grep for the receipt paths. This is a spec-build campaign where execution happens later on the pair (coordinator/worker). So I should write carefully, honest, well-documented scripts that follow the established patterns and degrade with a loud failure into a fail receipt (F.12: failures are recorded and never silently skipped).\n\nKey design decisions:\n\n1. How does the script reuse the fork's test scaffolding? Inside the container, /opt/vllm is the fork checkout with tests/. The test file `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py` writes fixture checkpoints. Inside make-proxy.sh's python heredoc, I'll do the following:\n   - Add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path\n   - Import `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` and look for the fixture writer. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's actually in test_ple_mmap.py. From ple-54129.md: \"In the test fixtures each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`).\" And \"A contract test `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries` runs actual boundary rows through MmapPleTable.gather.\" So the fixture writer is around lines 112–124, and it's probably a helper function, or part of a pytest fixture that writes out a safetensors file containing a shard tensor named `layers.{layer_idx}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{shard_index:05d}.weight`.\n\nReuse approach: import the module and introspect: find module-level functions and call one that constructs a fixture checkpoint given a directory and geometry. Too fragile. Better: reuse what we can name — the fork's `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap` (the actual runtime module: shard-naming regex, MmapPleTable). And for the shard files, import the test module and reuse the writer if it exists; otherwise, fall back to writing with the exact regex-conforming naming, and honestly verify that the names match `_SHARD_RE` from the actual ple_mmap module. That's honest scaffolding reuse: validate my writer against the fork's own `_SHARD_RE` and `discover_shards`.\n\nAlso, the fork's test file has a new AMD file test_ple_mmap_amd.py (352 lines) from patch 0010 — \"GPU-less pins\" — not a fixture writer.\n\nSince we can't read the fork tree, the runtime reuse attempt with graceful fallback is the right approach. Write the make-proxy generator as follows:\n1. Attempt to reuse the fork's fixture writer: import the test module, look for a callable like `write_fixture`/`build_fixture`/a pytest fixture's `.func`? Too magical.\n\nAlternative honest approach: parse the test file for safetensors-writing logic and import it if importable, and at runtime check whether the generated checkpoint passes the fork's own validation (`ple_mmap.validate_shards_for` / `discover_shards` / `_attach_table`) — that's the strongest form of reuse: the fork's own contract test validates my checkpoint. Let's do this:\n\nmake-proxy.sh (in-container python):\n- Build config.json (same architectures id as the workload: \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" — the smoke test checks this arch; the workload is the vendor FP8 release; the registry registers both ForCausalLM and ForConditionalGeneration; the smoke receipt asserts registration of ConditionalGeneration, suggesting the workload uses that).\n\nHmm wait — which one does the workload use? run-smoke.sh checks `\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" in archs` as the \"registered architectures identifier.\" Q3Next-style models: these models use Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration (like qwen3_next's conditional generation). I'll use \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration.\"\n\n- num_hidden_layers: a few (4, mixing linear and MoE layers per the model's layer pattern — Qwen4Exp/Qwen3Next use a pattern of interleaved full attention and MoE layers with linear_attention... the config needs a layer_types list or decoders pattern). The exact config schema is unknown. This is the risky part, but I can do my best: read the config class fields at runtime: `from vllm.transformers_utils.configs.qwen4_exp import ...` hmm, I don't know the module path. I can introspect at runtime: load the config class and use defaults where possible.\n\nActually, a more robust approach: construct the config minimally but completely with plausible fields, and let vLLM fail loudly if it's wrong — but the campaign runs unattended overnight, and a wrong config means the proxy fails and gets recorded. I want to maximize the chance it works.\n\nLet me search the repo for evidence of the actual config fields. There might be config excerpts in the evidence files (ds4-vllm-manifest.md or the kyuz0 dossier). Let me grep.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4290fa10f72344e8b25b42cd","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -rn \"ngram_vocab_size\\|ple_embed\\|heads_per_ngram\\|layer_types\\|linear_attention\\|num_experts\\|weight_block_size\" specs/flashnext/evidence/*.md docs handoff README.md 2>/dev/null | head -30"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f806f1756f334ce3a2a36401","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -rn \"config.json\\|architectures\" specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md specs/flashnext/evidence/kyuz0-rocm10.md specs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md 2>/dev/null | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2588,"output":2010,"cacheRead":54912,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1769,"totalTokens":59510,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991915099,"responseId":"chatcmpl-e029b214-c9bf-4588-9c51-e231c61f1391","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4290fa10f72344e8b25b42cd","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md:343:**[M] 38 of 131 shards present; 57 G on disk; mtimes 21:25–22:06 today; owner `tom:users` (library-fetch chowns to `tom:users`, `hosts/nas/models.nix:146` — but this dir was created BEFORE any catalog row exists, so it was NOT placed by library-fetch).** The download is in flight or partial. `config.json` reads `\"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"]`, `\"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\"`, with `layer_types` alternating `linear_attention` ×3 / `full_attention`, `full_attention_interval: 4`, `hidden_size: 2560`, `head_dim: 256`, `indexer_budget: 2048` **[S]**.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:325:6. `layer_types` full-attention count (12 expected from `full_attention_interval: 4`)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:59:- `from_config()` reads `weight_block_size` from the HF `quantization_config` at\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:64:163:        weight_block_size = cls.get_from_keys_or(config, [\"weight_block_size\"], None)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:71:This is exactly the checkpoint's shape: `weight_block_size [128,128]` + a 943-entry\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:80:- `block_quant` is set from the presence of `weight_block_size`:\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:143:85:        padded_num_experts = (moe_config.num_experts + 31) // 32 * 32\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:144:86:        if padded_num_experts >= 1024:\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:150:512 experts → `padded_num_experts = 512` → `512 < 1024` → **passes**. **[S]**\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:424:`copy_ple_embedding_shard_`, `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple.py:71-75` **[S]**:\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:643:  geometry is config-driven (`ngram_vocab_size_base`, `heads_per_ngram`,\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md:644:  `ple_embed_dim`, `make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by` — read at\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:148:- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:153:**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:308:embeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:323:embeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:329:Therefore the AMD port is **either**: (i) port the whole nvidia FP8 embedding stack too (`Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod` `nvidia/ple_layer.py:142-181`, `_get_ple_embedding_quant_method` `:184-207`, `_get_embedding_weight_scale`/`_dequantize_embeddings` `:632-648`, the `quant_config=`/`params_dtype=` plumbing at `:580-589`, and the `:1194` call) — roughly **+80 further lines**; **or** (ii) port only the mmap-specific dequant: read `weight_scale` off the placeholder and multiply, ~10 lines, accepting that env-off AMD FP8 PLE stays unsupported. Either way, **the \"~15-line port\" figure in the campaign record is wrong.**\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:344:The campaign record's citation of **`nvidia/ple_layer.py:281-288` plus a piecewise-cudagraph guard is accurate but incomplete** — it names W3 only (and `:281-288` is 8 of W3's 11 lines; the block ends at `:291` with the `else:`). W4/W5 are equally load-bearing: without W4 the AMD path still calls its own `qwen4_exp_amd_ple_ngram_embedding` op with `dtype=self.ngram_embedding.params_dtype` (`amd/ple_layer.py:315-323`), and `MmapNgramEmbedding` **has no `params_dtype` attribute** (it exposes `torch_dtype`, `ple_mmap.py:535`) — an immediate `AttributeError` on the first forward. Without W5 the loader would materialize the full table into a placeholder that has no `.weight`, raising in `copy_ple_embedding_shard_`.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:171:The exact number of QSA layers is **UNDETERMINED** — it is `layer_types == \"full_attention\"`\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:291:`num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` (`:239-244`). **[S]**\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:295:config.num_experts_per_tok, ...)` at `:215-235` and calls it at `:256-258`. **[S]** So: **vLLM\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:312:`num_experts_per_tok = 10` routes fine.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:510:2. **`num_experts_per_tok = 10`.** Not present anywhere in the PR —\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:511:   `grep -rn \"num_experts_per_tok\"` over `vllm/models/qwen4_exp/` and\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:516:   depends on the checkpoint's `layer_types`; not available locally.\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/tally-fit-analysis.md:38:- The HF tensor name `ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight` and GGUF `per_layer_token_embd.weight` are safe (no lexicon hit). `strix-halo-llamacpp` is NOT safe (llama). `EngramHalo.cpp` is safe.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991968439},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f806f1756f334ce3a2a36401","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(no output)"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991968439},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good — the actual config excerpt of the workload: `\"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"]`, `\"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\"`, `layer_types` alternates with `linear_attention`×3 / `full_attention`, `full_attention_interval: 4`, `hidden_size: 2560`, `head_dim: 256`, `indexer_budget: 2048`.\n\nCheck the full context of dotfiles-observed.md around line 343, and further check whether there's a more complete field listing of config.json somewhere. Also check qsa-53896.md (QSA = sparse attention indexer?) regarding the config fields. Additionally, grep for the \"ple\" config fields inside the evidence.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8a03c00b7464410aa48cb7a0","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n 320,360p specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_704c17143d204b53937140cb","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -n \"split_ngram_parts\\|ngram_context\\|ple\\b\\|ple_\" specs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md specs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5214,"output":362,"cacheRead":56320,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":165,"totalTokens":61896,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991968444,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c8999108-b983-46ac-b903-e5a304ba0d54","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8a03c00b7464410aa48cb7a0","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n`libraryPath` (`modules/local-models.nix:352-361`): default `\"/mnt/nas/models/weights\"`; worker overrides to `\"/mnt/library/weights\"` at `hosts/worker/default.nix:213`.\n\n**To declare the FP8 checkpoint you would add, to `lib/local-models.nix`'s `artifacts` block:**\n- a `layout = \"snapshot\"` artifact (the flat/basename collapse at `lib/model-store.nix:31-32` is wrong for a 131-shard HF tree), with `localName` if a loader must see the upstream repo basename, `hfUrl`, pinned `revision`, `primary = \"config.json\"`, and one `fileType` entry per file with exact `bytes` + `oid` + `hash`. The closest committed template is `deepseek-v4-flash-0731-bf16` at `lib/local-models.nix:771-…` (`layout = \"snapshot\"`, `localName = \"DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731\"`, `primary = \"config.json\"`).\n- Then a `deployments` row (backend `vllm`) and/or a `services.local-models.artifacts` entry in `modules/strix.nix`, at which point `wanted.json` changes → `local-models-sync` re-runs → the bytes land at `/var/lib/local-models/<artifactId>/…`.\n- Relevant assertions the row must satisfy: primary must be one of the files (`modules/local-models.nix:223-228`); paths must be safe relative paths (`:229-234`); no repeated paths (`:235-244`); snapshot layout exempts the unique-basename rule (`:245-255`).\n\n**[S] `/mnt/nas/models/weights/qwen38-flash-next-fp8/` EXISTS ON DISK AND IS NOT IN THE CATALOG.**\n```\n$ grep -rn \"flash-next\\|qwen38-flash\\|fp8\\|FP8\" --include=* .        (in dotfiles)   → NO MATCHES\n$ ls -la /mnt/nas/models/weights/qwen38-flash-next-fp8/ | head\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 tom users 2564 Aug 28 22:06 .\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users   8952 chat_template.jinja\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users  72423 config.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users    202 generation_config.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users   3235 LICENSE\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users 3353259 merges.txt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users 1040155912 model-00001-of-00131.safetensors\n…\n$ ls /mnt/nas/models/weights/qwen38-flash-next-fp8/ | grep -c safetensors   → 38\n$ du -sh /mnt/nas/models/weights/qwen38-flash-next-fp8/                     → 57G\n```\n**[M] 38 of 131 shards present; 57 G on disk; mtimes 21:25–22:06 today; owner `tom:users` (library-fetch chowns to `tom:users`, `hosts/nas/models.nix:146` — but this dir was created BEFORE any catalog row exists, so it was NOT placed by library-fetch).** The download is in flight or partial. `config.json` reads `\"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"]`, `\"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\"`, with `layer_types` alternating `linear_attention` ×3 / `full_attention`, `full_attention_interval: 4`, `hidden_size: 2560`, `head_dim: 256`, `indexer_budget: 2048` **[S]**.\n\n### 3.4 `modules/llama-swap.nix` — roster shape and the \"default model\" question **[S]**\n\n125 lines. Owns only \"the proxy package, lifecycle, state, and network boundary\" (`:9-11`); `local-models.nix` owns the roster.\n\n`services.llama-swap` settings (`:30-68`): `enable = true`, `package = pkgs.llama-swap` (pkgs/llama-swap.nix pins asset `llama-swap_240_linux_amd64.tar.gz`), `listenAddress = \"0.0.0.0\"`, `port = 9292`, `openFirewall = false`, `healthCheckTimeout = 900`, `store.path = /var/lib/llama-swap/activity.sqlite`, `captureBuffer = 0`, `performance.disabled = true`, `startPort = 10001`, `sendLoadingState = true`, **`globalTTL = 0`**, `unloadTimeout = 60`.\n\nRoster shape is injected by `modules/local-models.nix:375-381`:\n```nix\nservices.llama-swap.settings = assert catalogValid; {\n  models = localModels;\n  peers = { };\n};\n```\n`localModels = lib.mapAttrs' renderModel selectedDeployments` (`:177`), keyed by `deployment.model` (`renderModel`, `:152-175`), each value `= renderer{…} // { name; cmd; ttl; }`. Renderers live in `lib/local-model-runtime.nix` (58 lines) — five: `rocm`, `vulkan`, `ds4`, `vllm`, `mlx`.\n\n**On \"current default model\": there is NO default-model key in this module or in llama-swap's settings.** `globalTTL = 0` is the only global, and it is a residency fallback, not a model selection (`:61-66`). Selection is per-request by model ID. **UNDETERMINED as posed** — what I looked at: all 125 lines of `modules/llama-swap.nix`, the settings injection at `modules/local-models.nix:375-381`, and `grep -rn \"defaultModel\\|default model\\|LLM_MODEL\\|OPENAI_MODEL\"` over `modules/` and `home/` (only hit: `modules/printing.nix:51` `model = \"everywhere\"`, unrelated). What would settle it: naming which consumer's default you mean — e.g. `pkgs/academic-ocr/driver.sh:4` defaults its endpoint to `http://localhost:9292`, and `flake.nix:1355` asserts `monthlySources.inference.url == \"http://coordinator:9292\"`.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991977967},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_704c17143d204b53937140cb","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"specs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:12:| PR #54129 checkout (layers on top) | `.../scratchpad/vllm-pr54129` | `git rev-parse HEAD` → `8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da`, `8e4e036 Merge remote-tracking branch 'upstream-main' into ple-mmap-upstream` **[M]** |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:38:`amd/ple_layer.py`, `nvidia/model.py`, `nvidia/mtp.py`, `nvidia/ple_layer.py`, plus\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:39:`Only in …pr54129/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/nvidia: ple_mmap.py` **[M]**. Every `qsa*.py`,\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:191:| `common/ple.py` | 75 | 75 |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:200:| `nvidia/ple_layer.py` | 1226 | 1247 |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:201:| `nvidia/ple_mmap.py` | — | **1010 (new in #54129)** |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:211:| `amd/ple_layer.py` | 1101 | 1102 |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:224:`amd/ops/__init__.py`, `amd/ops/hc.py`, `amd/ops/qsa.py`, `amd/ple_layer.py`, `amd/qsa.py`\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md:355:`test_qsa_pre_indexer.py` 297, `test_config.py` 493, `test_ple.py` 410, `test_hc_ops.py` 94,\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:27:| 4 | Q0 is a \"~15-line\" `amd/ple_layer.py` mmap wiring port | The port is **80–150 lines**, because `amd/ple_layer.py` has **no FP8 handling at all** — no `weight_scale` interception, no dequant, embedding constructed without `quant_config`. On this FP8 checkpoint the stock AMD path either dies loudly on the orphan `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` tensor or bare-casts the table to bf16 **discarding the block scale** (numerically wrong) and doubling its bytes (23.9→47.7 GiB/rank — which breaks the 96 GiB aperture arithmetic). [`evidence/ple-54129.md`, `evidence/moe-dispatch.md` §5] | **AMD FP8-PLE support is enablement, not headroom** — contrary to Addendum B.1. The mmap port (which is FP8-native and skips the resident table entirely) is the *cheapest correct* fix, not a luxury. |\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:137:### 2.3 vLLM PR #54129 (Trosfy, `ple-mmap-upstream`) — the engram-on-SSD mechanism\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:139:`ple_mmap.py` (1010 lines, nvidia-tree-only today): `np.memmap` views over the\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:153:(W1–W5b, exactly pinned in [`evidence/ple-54129.md`] §\"Exact port specification\") into\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:156:well), plus relocating `ple_mmap.py` to `common/` (the clean upstream shape). One\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:159:(`NULL_BLOCK_ID` remaps, plus a fresh `# TODO: need double-check` at `amd/ple_layer.py:522`)\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:191:  at boot, and inject MES firmware; upstream's own example imports it, which is the\nspecs/flashnext/evidence/innovation-ledger.md:273:| PM QoS + tripwire | `od /dev/cpu_dma_latency` both ends; tripwire timer armed | systemctl stop (drill exists) | 200-sample RTT within budget (committed threshold 200 µs) |\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991977967},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's a partial config snapshot on the NAS. The proxy script is intended to run on a coordinator that has the staged workload checkpoint, but per spec: run the proxy \"before touching the real weights\" — the proxy checkpoint is synthetic, so we can't copy from the actual config.json (which might not even be staged yet… actually, staging runs alongside the container build, and in S2's ordering, proxy comes after smoke; the weights staging might already be done). Hmm — \"before loading the actual weight bytes\" means don't load the actual weights, but reading config.json to mirror the architecture is fine, and in fact it's the honest thing to do. Best design: make-proxy.sh uses the staged workload's config.json (/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8/config.json) if it exists, shrinking the geometry down to the bare minimum (reducing num_hidden_layers, vocab, experts, etc.), and if not, falls back to a baked-in config template reflecting the evidence snapshot.\n\nHowever, note: this is a synthetic checkpoint, and what matters is the \"same architecture identifier.\" Reusing the workload config is the most faithful. That said, shrinking means changing the fields — e.g. expert count 512→8, weight_block_size [128,128] must divide the expert shapes: intermediate sizes need to be multiples of 128. Also hidden 2560, head_dim 256… but for the proxy make it small while keeping the divisibility intact.\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust approach for the proxy: define our own small geometry with the exact same config schema. Risk: I don't know the full config schema (the 72 KB config.json is huge — it includes things like indexer settings, etc.). The fork has `vllm/transformers_utils/configs/qwen4_exp.py` which defines the config class with defaults. At runtime, the best approach is: load the config class from the fork, instantiate it with overrides, and dump. This gives us the full set of fields with the correct defaults. And use the actual config.json as a template if it exists.\n\nRuntime-driven design of make-proxy.sh (in-container python):\n1. Try `from vllm.transformers_utils.configs.qwen4_exp import Qwen4ExpConfig` (guessing the name; wrap in try/except and introspect the module to look for a config class). Better: after `from transformers import AutoConfig`, do `AutoConfig.from_pretrained(model_path)`? For synthesis: load the staged workload config if available (`FN_MODEL_DIR/config.json` — mount it read-only), then shrink. Otherwise build from defaults.\n\n2. Geometry: num_hidden_layers=8? \"a handful of layers.\" The real model has MoE on some layers (decoder_sparse_step?). Per the qsa evidence: `num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` — this is for qwen3_next's MoE placement. For the proxy, all layers MoE? The admission kernel path must execute: fused-MoE block-FP8 kernel. Make all layers MoE (num_experts=8, num_experts_per_tok=2, moe_intermediate_size a multiple of 128, e.g. 256) and interleave the linear_attention/full_attention pattern following the layer_types pattern (3 linear + 1 full per group).\n\nHmm, but there's also QSA's short-convolution stuff and such. The amd model includes the QSA indexer (indexer_budget). A proxy with full_attention layers triggers the QSA indexer path. That's fine — small.\n\nI'm going too deep into the unknowns here. The script should be data-driven: take the workload's config.json (staged at /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8/config.json, mounted read-only into the make-proxy container) and programmatically shrink it:\n\n- architectures unchanged\n- num_hidden_layers → e.g. 8 (respecting the full_attention_interval pattern)\n- num_experts → 8, num_experts_per_tok → 2 (keep it ≤8)\n- vocab_size → small (e.g. 2048); head_dim etc. kept as-is? hidden_size 2560 is small enough. Keeping the actual hidden/head dimensions preserves weight-name/dimension compatibility with the config class's expectations… the weights we synthesize derive from the config anyway.\n\nThe actual weights need to be generated with the correct names for this architecture. This is the hardest part: writing out a complete set of weight tensors with the correct names for a vLLM model implementation. The names must match what the model's load_weights expects (stacked qkv, etc.). A hand-written generic generator is error-prone.\n\nBetter idea — reuse the fork's test scaffolding even more deeply: the fork's tests directory probably contains a test that constructs a tiny Qwen4Exp model (e.g. tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple.py, test_config.py). But constructing the weights still requires matching names.\n\nAnother reliable approach that avoids writing out per-tensor names: use the fork's own model class to initialize the model in vLLM and save its state_dict? Inside the container, under vllm's model config, instantiate the model on meta/CPU with random weights, then save the state_dict with HF-style names via a weight map. Does vLLM's model have `hf_to_vllm_mapper`? Newer vLLM has WeightsMapper. The checkpoint tensor names are HF names; the vLLM model parameter names are different. The model file typically defines `hf_to_vllm_mapper = WeightsMapper(...)`. So: build the vLLM model class with random init (on CPU), take the reverse mapping of the mapper, and save it… that's still fragile.\n\nHonestly, since I can't test any of this, the best approach is to make the generator introspection-driven and fall back gracefully:\n\nPlan for make-proxy.sh (python in the container):\n1. Resolve the base config: read /models/config.json if mounted (read-only mount from FN_MODEL_DIR), else fall back to a built-in minimal config dict derived from evidence (architectures, model_type qwen4_exp, layer_types pattern, etc.).\n2. Shrink: layers → N (FN_PROXY_LAYERS, default 8), experts → 8, keep block size 128×128, quantization_config is fp8 with weight_block_size [128,128] (only experts? the actual checkpoint's ignore list leaves only mlp.experts.* as quantized). To ensure the admission path executes, the routed experts need to be quantized with block fp8: quantization_config with modules_to_not_convert listing everything except experts… the simplest is: quantization_config {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]} with no modules_to_not_convert → all linears get Fp8LinearMethod (does it work on gfx1151 via the 0012 admission? TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel is allowed). This exercises both the admitted linear and MoE paths. But wait — F.6/F.9… it's fine.\n\n   Actually, the real checkpoint has 943 modules_to_not_convert — everything except experts. Keeping the proxy simpler: quantize only the experts, mirroring the workload. Can I copy the actual quantization_config as-is? Its modules_to_not_convert refers to named layers (layer indices!) and those change when shrinking. So synthesize: modules_to_not_convert = all linear layer names except mlp.experts. Generating the ignore list requires enumerating per-layer names — annoying but doable: e.g. for each layer, add entries like model.layers.{i}.mlp.linear_attn... Hmm, I don't know the exact submodule names.\n\n   Simpler: quantize everything (no ignore list). All linears + experts go through Fp8 methods with block scales. Block size 128×128 requires each weight dim ≥128 and divisible… actually weight_block_size requires that the dims be divisible by the block size for the per-block scales; if not divisible vLLM pads. hidden 2560, intermediate are multiples of 128. Make all sizes multiples of 128.\n\n3. Weight generation: introspection-driven via the fork:\n   - Try: `from vllm.model_executor.models.qwen3_next import ...` no wait — the correct way: build the vLLM model? Too heavy.\n\n   Realistic fallback: write out the tensors under names vLLM's load_weights maps from HF names. For the qwen3_next family, HF checkpoint names look like `model.layers.0.self_attn.qkv_proj.weight`? No, HF stores `q_proj/k_proj/v_proj` separately and vLLM stacks them with the mapper. I really can't derive this with certainty.\n\n   The most honest thing: attempt reuse of the fork's own scaffolding at runtime: look under /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/ for fixture builders that generate a tiny model checkpoint (test_ple.py (410 lines), test_config.py (493), test_ple_mmap.py (2048) exist in the base — per the line count in qsa-53896.md's table: \"test_qsa_pre_indexer.py 297, test_config.py 493, test_ple.py 410, test_hc_ops.py 94\"). Import each module, hunt for functions/fixtures that write out safetensors checkpoints, or construct a model config + weights, and call them with the proxy directory. If nothing exists, use my own minimal writer that deterministically constructs tensors from the config with the standard qwen3-next HF naming pattern and validates the result via the fork's own load path (the ultimate validation is in run-proxy.sh serving anyway).\n\nWait — actually, there's a much more powerful reuse path I overlooked: use vLLM itself to do the HF-name generation. Hmm.\n\nOr: build the model with HF transformers? Not installed / model not supported.\n\nLet me reconsider: what does vLLM require for load? Its `load_weights` iterates over the checkpoint tensors, maps names, and copies them. If a tensor is missing, many implementations throw an exception at the end about missing weights… typically `params_dict` keys not loaded trigger an error? vLLM's qwen load_weights does not validate completeness (it only loads what's present; missing parameters remain randomly initialized — actually, on `ModelLoader.load_model`, after load_weights, missing keys are… vLLM doesn't strictly validate; some models assert). In fact, does vLLM throw on missing weights? No — vLLM is famously lenient; it only loads present tensors (that's why LoRA etc. works… no). Actually recent vLLM has `weight_loader` that throws on unmatched names, and at the end of the loader there's a check: `if not loaded: error`. Hmm, vllm's default_loader checks that each weight in the model got loaded: \"Some weights are not used in the checkpoint\"? It logs \"Weights loaded from ...\". In v1, `load_weights` returning a set of loaded params is new API. When checkpoint params are not consumed, models throw (the \"There is no module or parameter named\" error the AMD loader raises for unknown names — per moe-dispatch). Unknown checkpoint name → error. Missing model parameters → usually no error? For AutoWeightsLoader, `load_weights` throws on unmatched checkpoint names, but missing model parameters remain at random init. For the proxy (synthetic anyway), random init for some tensors is tolerable as long as logits are finite… but NaN risk with random fp8 scales. Safer to provide everything.\n\nGiven the uncertainties, writing a fully correct weight generator by hand without seeing the tree is really not possible. The correct engineering move under the instruction \"reuse the fork's own test scaffolding under tests/models/ inside the fork checkout as much as possible instead of inventing a generator\" is to have make-proxy.sh drive the fork's scaffolding at runtime via introspection. The campaign will run it on the real machine where the fork checkout exists. So let me design the generator like this:\n\nStage A (reuse attempt): For each candidate module under /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp (test_ple_mmap.py, test_ple.py, test_config.py, test_ple_mmap_amd.py, and the rest): import it, and hunt for (in priority order):\n  1. A module-level callable that returns/writes a full checkpoint (regex on name: `make|build|write|create|fixture|ckpt|checkpoint`), accepting a directory argument.\n  2. The shard writer specifically for PLE: a function that writes `shard_*.weight` safetensors — reuse for the table part.\nRecord which scaffolding we reused in the receipt (data[\"scaffolding_reused\"]).\n\nStage B (always): Write the checkpoint ourselves using config-driven synthesis with qwen3-next-family HF tensor naming, block-FP8 quantize the experts (fp8 weight + weight_scale_inv), provide the PLE shard tensors per `_SHARD_RE` naming + `ngram_embedding.weight_scale`, and validate:\n  - Header parse via the fork's ple_mmap module: `discover_shards(model_path)` finds the shards; `_attach_table`/`validate_shards_for` accepts the geometry (if it can run GPU-less on CPU).\n  - All safetensors are parseable.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. I might be overcomplicating. Let me look at what the actual checkpoint's HF names look like — maybe there's a list in the evidence (ds4-vllm-manifest is about DeepSeek; but the flashnext checkpoint = Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 — hmm per moe-dispatch UNDETERMINED: \"actual checkpoint tensor name list for Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 (doesn't exist on this box)\"). So even the evidence author didn't have it. But there were 38 shards on the NAS… no index.\n\nOK, another angle: vLLM's qwen3_next.py load_weights handles stacked params with `stacked_params_mapping` (qkv_proj ← q_proj/k_proj/v_proj etc.), and HF-style names are `model.layers.{i}.self_attn.q_proj.weight`… for Qwen3Next HF format: from the qwen3_next HF checkpoint, names are: `model.layers.N.self_attn.q_proj.{weight, q_norm/k_norm}`, `gate_up_proj`? HF splits it into gate_proj/up_proj. And Qwen4Exp has additional PLE/QSA tensors: `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_M.weight` (from _SHARD_RE: `layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight` — probably with `model.` prefix? The regex says `layers\\.` with no leading anchor — re.search, not fullmatch? discover_shards matches tensor names against the regex; the regex starts with `layers\\.` — search matches anywhere if no ^, so `model.layers.0.ple...` matches. Good, safe either way.)\n\nAlso `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` per loader interception name: the loader checks `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — that's the checkpoint-side name after prefix stripping inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding.load_weights. The full checkpoint name is something like `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`. Hmm, inside Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding's load_weights, is the name relative? moe-dispatch says \"The checkpoint scale tensor is named `ngram_embedding.weight_scale` (per explicit check in NVIDIA loader nvidia/ple_layer.py:478-480).\" That check is `name == \"ngram_embedding.weight_scale\"` — probably after the model strips the `model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.` prefix. Write both: a scale tensor per PLE layer with the full path `model.layers.{N}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`.\n\nAnd the main vocab embedding: `model.embed_tokens.weight`, lm_head: `lm_head.weight`, final norm `model.norm.weight`. Plus QSA indexer tensors for full-attention layers (indexer_budget 2048): qwen3_next's sparse attention has `self_attn.indexer` with wqz/… I'm getting out of my depth on exact names again.\n\nDecision: I'll write make-proxy.sh as an introspection-driven tool with a clear hierarchy:\n\n1. Primary: Fork scaffolding reuse — programmatically discover the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp/ checkpoint builders, and if one builds a full model checkpoint, drive it with proxy geometry.\n2. Fallback: My own config-driven writer that covers the tensor families I know of (embeddings, norms, attention qkv+o, MLP gate/up/down or MoE experts+gate+shared expert, PLE shards + scale). For unknown extra parameters the model requires, the vLLM loader typically doesn't fail on model-side missing keys (leaves random init) — record the risk honestly. Actually wait, does it fail? Let me think about AutoWeightsLoader: it iterates checkpoint weights and routes them; model params that didn't receive weights… in vLLM v1, after load_weights, the loader does… not validate completeness (it's the checkpoint that's authoritative). Yeah — vLLM doesn't error on missing checkpoint keys; parameters remain at their init values (that's how the \"random init\" debugging works). Since we init with a controlled seed and sane values (small uniform + proper norms), logits will be finite.\n\nAlso fp8: for block-fp8 experts, weights are `mlp.experts.{e}.{gate,up,down}_proj.weight` (fp8 e4m3) + `.weight_scale_inv` (fp32, shape [ceil(n/128), ceil(k/128)]). vLLM's Fp8 block quant checkpoint convention: `weight_scale_inv`. For the linear layers that get quantized: same pattern. And the top-level quantization_config in config.json: {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]}.\n\nTo exercise only the MoE admission (UNKNOWN-1: \"numerical sanity of fused mixture block-FP8 kernel\") — and the linear twin is also admitted via 0012 (FN_FP8_LINEAR) — quantize all linears too. Both get exercised; that's the goal of \"admitted kernel path.\"\n\nHmm wait, actually one consideration: should I mirror the workload (modules_to_not_convert, only experts quantized)? That requires the ignore list; can I generate it programmatically? Can't enumerate reliably. Quantize everything: simpler and it exercises both admissions. The workload's own quantization choice is not part of the proxy contract — \"same architecture identifier and expert format\" is the contract (vocabulary section of the spec): \"small synthetic checkpoint with same architecture identifier and expert format.\" Expert format = block-FP8 with [128,128]. Quantizing the dense layers too does not violate this.\n\nBut note: if the dense layers are block-FP8 quantized, the embedding/lm_head are also typically excluded (embeddings can't be fp8 in vLLM's Fp8Config? Fp8Config's ignored_layers usually auto-adds embed/lm_head? In Fp8Config, `modules_to_not_convert` defaults to None… if None, it quantizes every Linear, including lm_head? vLLM handles lm_head specially (unquantized if no quant). VocabParallelEmbedding for embed_tokens: quant method from Fp8Config → Fp8Config.get_quant_method for embedding returns UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod unless is_checkpoint_fp8_serialized… embeddings stay bf16. lm_head is ParallelLMHead — unquantized. OK, quantizing everything is fine: linears take Fp8LinearMethod, embeddings stay as-is.\n\nNow the runtime of run-proxy.sh:\n- Serve the proxy checkpoint single-node inside the container: `podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host -v results:/results -v $PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy -p port $IMAGE ...`.\n- Serve with `vllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy --served-model-name flashnext-proxy --port ...`.\n- With VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1. Task says \"eager execution\" but the fork's guard rejects --enforce-eager (\"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\") and rejects FULL graphs; only PIECEWISE with the splitting op is allowed. Spec P10: \"The table path's own guards require piecewise capture with mmap op as splitting boundary and reject raw eager — first light runs in this sanctioned mode.\" So use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` and note in the script's comments that the worklist's phrasing \"eager\" is superseded by the fork guard and spec P10. This is the correct reading: the spec section is authoritative (\"readFirst\" instruction). Also fn-env.sh says \"safe with --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs\" — hmm, that comment refers to the allocator config; but run-tp2.sh serves the actual model via fn-cluster-up.sh… let me check fn-cluster-up.sh to see what serve args it uses — that's the sanctioned-mode reference.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a64e7108e9434cd19fc9bdfe","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && grep -n \"serve\\|enforce\\|cudagraph\\|PIECEWISE\\|max-model\\|gpu-mem\\|port\" host/fn-cluster-up.sh | head -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3141,"output":4789,"cacheRead":61440,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4684,"totalTokens":69370,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991977972,"responseId":"chatcmpl-5a6e7c85-3a4c-44ab-9620-c6111096f612","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a64e7108e9434cd19fc9bdfe","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7:#   1. REAP stranded serve processes on BOTH nodes first, then GATE on zero\n8:#      residue. Stopping a supervising unit does not kill the `vllm serve`\n9:#      behind it; repeated restarts strand one husk each, holding the port.\n14:#   4. A hard two-GPU gate BEFORE serve — if ray never reports 2.0 GPU, we\n15:#      abort loud rather than serve at TP=1.\n16:#   5. The TP=2 eager serve, then a wait-for-reality API poll.\n29:# --- 1. reap stranded serve processes, then gate on zero residue -------------\n30:# The serve process tree is visible from the host /proc even when it runs\n33:reap_serve_node() {\n35:  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n37:    echo \"fn-cluster-up: reaping stranded serve pids: $(echo $pids | tr '\\n' ' ')\" >&2\n40:    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n44:  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n48:residue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\n50:  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the coordinator after reap\"\n54:residue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\n56:  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the worker after reap\"\n107:  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n116:# --- 4. hard two-GPU gate before serve ----------------------------------------\n127:  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed: ray reports $gpus_total GPU\" ;;\n129:    log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (last reading: '$gpus_total'); refusing to serve\"\n135:# --- 5. the serve: TP=2, eager -------------------------------------------------\n136:# Do not add comments inside the backslash-continued `vllm serve` command\n138:# `bash -n` still reports the file as valid.\n140:podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n141:  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n143:  --port $FN_PORT \\\n146:  --enforce-eager \\\n147:  --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL \\\n148:  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n149:  > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n152:# pattern): poll the models endpoint until the served name answers.\n164:  log \"FATAL: serve did not answer on :$FN_PORT inside the timeout\"\n165:  tail -n 40 \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" >&2 || true\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787992109972},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting — fn-cluster-up.sh uses `--enforce-eager` for TP=2 serve... but when VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 (exported in fn-env.sh), the fork guard raises \"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\" at construction time. Contradiction between host-tooling (already committed) and the fork guard. Hmm. Wait, let me re-check guard #2: \"`if compilation_config.mode != CompilationMode.VLLM_COMPILE: raise` — 'enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model.'\"\n\nHmm, so with --enforce-eager + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, model construction throws RuntimeError. Unless the fork's admission patch changed the guard. Patch 0010 mirrors the 5 wiring sites; the W3 guard \"check_cudagraph_safety\" is mirrored unchanged (\"both cudagraph guards fire from the AMD constructor\" — test_ple_mmap_amd.py pins \"both cudagraph guards firing from the AMD constructor\"). So the guard exists on the fork: (1) FULL cudagraph rejection, (2) mode != VLLM_COMPILE rejection, (3) splitting_ops must contain the mmap op.\n\nWith --enforce-eager, compilation mode becomes DYNAMO_AS_IS / NONE → guard #2 raises. So `run-tp2.sh`'s eager serve + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 would fail at startup... unless run-tp2 isn't setting VLLM_PLE_MMAP — but fn-env.sh exports it and fn-cluster-up presumably sources fn-env. That might be a latent bug in the host-tooling task (not mine to fix; outside my boundary — fn-cluster-up.sh is under host/).\n\nMy task boundary: scripts/make-proxy.sh, scripts/run-proxy.sh, tests/. What the task goal says about run-proxy.sh: \"Serve single-node from container with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 and eager execution.\" But the guard forbids eager. Tension! What to do?\n\nOptions:\na) Follow the task text literally (--enforce-eager + VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1) → the serve dies at construction time due to the guard's RuntimeError → the proxy receipt fails overnight. Bad.\nb) Follow spec P10 (\"the first light runs in that permitted mode\" = piecewise capture with the mmap op as splitting boundary) → `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`, no --enforce-eager. P10 is a ratified ruling and the brief mandates reading the spec. P10 takes precedence over the task goal's loose phrasing. The proxy is literally the first light (claim 4.1 precedes 4.2). Also per P10 \"full-graph capture is morning work\" — piecewise is the permitted mode.\n\nGoing with (b), and documenting clearly in a script header comment: the worklist's \"eager execution\" phrasing is overridden by ruling P10 and the fork's own check_cudagraph_safety guard (patches 0009/0010), which explicitly rejects enforce-eager when VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1; run in the permitted piecewise mode. If the operator truly wants eager, then VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0... but claim 4.1 requires engaging the mmap path. So piecewise is the only consistent choice.\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me reconsider guard #2 more carefully. \"enforce-eager does not fully suppress CUDA graph capture on this model\" — even with --enforce-eager, some capture happens for this model? The guard demands mode == VLLM_COMPILE. So the permitted invocation is: no --enforce-eager, `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE`. The guard message #1 even says \"please pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\".\n\nAlso ROCm platform default: rocm.py:898-919 already sets compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = PIECEWISE? Per the dossier: \"ROCm already drives cudagraph_mode with the same enum (vllm/platforms/rocm.py:898-919, including compilation_config.cudagraph_mode = CUDAGraphMode.PIECEWISE)\". Hmm — so ROCm's default is already piecewise? But that's inside some platform method (maybe check_and_update_config). If ROCm defaults to PIECEWISE then plain `vllm serve` without enforce-eager already satisfies guard #1; pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE explicitly to be loud and deterministic.\n\nNow single-node proxy serve details:\n- Proxy model path: inside container /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy. How does run-proxy obtain the checkpoint? Both scripts bind-mount a host directory. Default host directory: \"$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint\"? build.sh keeps its host caches outside the repo under ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/flashnext. State directory default is $HOME/.local/state/flashnext (FN_STATE_DIR). Use FN_PROXY_DIR default \"${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint}\" — hmm, but run-smoke.sh has zero host state other than results/. The checkpoint has to persist between the make-proxy and run-proxy invocations (separate podman runs). Use FN_PROXY_DIR with the default above. Or put it under results/? results/ is the receipts/measurement tree committed to the repo — no, keep it outside the repo as build.sh does. Going with the state-directory default.\n\nHmm wait, actually — make-proxy.sh is \"run inside the container\". The entire script is a host wrapper (like run-smoke.sh which does podman run). The bash-n acceptance test only checks syntax. No problem.\n\n- Serve command inside run-proxy.sh: `podman run -d` a named container that serves, then the host polls the API port (-p 127.0.0.1:PORT:PORT), issues one greedy completion, asserts finiteness of logits (request with logprobs; assert top logprob is finite / text is non-empty; if logprobs are available, compute finiteness from top5 logprob values — vLLM's /v1/completions returns logprobs as a dict of token→logprob floats. If the model emits NaN logits, logprob is NaN/None. Assert all reported logprobs are finite, plus finish_reason), then `podman stop` (clean shutdown: SIGTERM → vllm shuts down gracefully; assert container exits 0 within timeout; capture serve-log tail into the receipt), and write results/receipts/proxy.json: {\"step\": \"proxy\", \"status\", \"ts\", \"data\": {...}} with fields like mmap_engaged, cudagraph_mode, completion tokens, finite_logits bool, clean_shutdown bool, proxy path, fork commit if available.\n\nreceipts-verify has no bounds on the \"proxy\" step; only well-formedness + status pass. Good.\n\nAlso assert \"finite logits via the API\": use /v1/completions with logprobs=5, temperature 0, max_tokens around 8. Verify completion_tokens ≥1, all logprob values are finite (math.isfinite), and no \"error\" in the response. Could also verify /health. Also record the sampled tokens.\n\nClean shutdown: `podman stop --time 60` the container; then `podman inspect` exit code == 0? vllm serve exits 0 on SIGTERM? podman stop sends SIGTERM; vLLM's API server handles SIGTERM gracefully and exits 0. Record exit code; if 0, clean_shutdown is true (also check no residual container). Use `podman run --name flashnext-proxy-serve`. On error paths, trap cleanup.\n\nNow make-proxy.sh details. In-container python heredoc:\n\n```\nimport json, math, os, re, struct, sys, time\nimport numpy as np\n```\nnumpy is available (vllm dependency). safetensors library available (vllm dependency). torch also available.\n\nSteps:\n1. Resolve geometry:\n   - Base config: mount host FN_MODEL_DIR read-only to /models? For the proxy build we could mount it if present; but make-proxy might run before staging completes (ordering: staging in parallel with container build, then smoke, then proxy — staging probably done by then). Attempt: read /models/config.json if mounted and present; run-proxy/make-proxy mount FN_MODEL_DIR ro if present on host. If absent → fall back to a synthesized config template based on the evidence snapshot.\n\nHmm, but the boundary says the script touches only scripts/ and tests/ — mounting FN_MODEL_DIR is a runtime thing, no problem.\n\n   Simpler and less coupling: make-proxy.sh takes FN_PROXY_BASE_CONFIG (default $FN_MODEL_DIR/config.json on host); if present, mount and shrink; otherwise built-in template.\n\n2. Shrink rules (in python):\n   - cfg = json.load of base, else template.\n   - Force: architectures unchanged (assert one of the Qwen4Exp ids is present); model_type is qwen4_exp.\n   - num_hidden_layers = FN_PROXY_LAYERS default 8. If 'layer_types' present, rebuild it as the workload's periodic pattern: pattern from full_attention_interval (3 linear + 1 full). layer_types = [\"linear_attention\",\"linear_attention\",\"linear_attention\",\"full_attention\"] * (L/4).\n   - num_experts = 8, num_experts_per_tok = 2; keep moe_intermediate_size small, multiple of 128 (e.g. 256). hidden_size: keep workload's (2560) — small enough (bf16 dense for 8 layers is fine). Actually keeping 2560 gives realism for head_dim 256 QSA etc. Keep hidden_size, head_dim, num_attention_heads/num_key_value_heads from base if present (must be compatible: head_dim 256 with hidden 2560, qwen3_next style (qk norm)).\n   - vocab_size: shrink to 2048? But the PLE ngram geometry is config-driven: ngram_vocab_size_base etc. — if those fields exist, shrink them proportionally; leave absent fields to defaults. padded_vocab_size must be divisible by make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by… risky. If base config present, copy all PLE-related fields verbatim but shrink ngram_vocab_size_base dramatically (e.g. 512 rows? table must have ≥1 row per shard; with split_ngram_parts default 512, a tiny org_vocab gives shard_size = ceil(org/512) = 1 row/shard → 512 shard files with 1 row. Hmm. split_ngram_parts might be a config field; if present set to 2. Then 2 shards.\n   \n   discover_shards just globs present shards; _attach_table requires all expected shards (derived from org_vocab/shard_size). So the checkpoint must contain exactly the expected number of shards: n_shards = ceil(org_vocab_size / shard_size), where shard_size = ceil(org_vocab_size / split_ngram_parts). With split_ngram_parts=2, org=512: shard_size=256, 2 shards. Good: \"a small lookup table with shard tensor naming\".\n\n   - quantization_config: {\"quant_method\": \"fp8\", \"weight_block_size\": [128,128]} (no modules_to_not_convert → all Linear layers quantized; embed/lm_head exempted by vLLM).\n   - max_position_embeddings small (e.g. 4096); set torch_dtype to \"bfloat16\".\n   - Keep indexer_budget etc. from base (QSA). If absent, hope defaults.\n\n3. Weights:\n   The honest part. Generator plan:\n   - Derive param names from base config if possible? No.\n   - Write tensors in the qwen3-next HF family naming. Based on what I know about Qwen3Next/Qwen4Exp from vLLM's model code (qwen3_next.py):\n     - model.embed_tokens.weight [vocab, hidden] bf16\n     - model.layers.{i}:\n       - For linear_attention layers (qwen3_next style): self_attn.{q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,q_norm,k_norm,o_proj}? Qwen3Next's linear attention = gated deltanet: has in_proj_qkvz, in_proj_ba, conv1d, A_log, dt_bias, norm, out_proj… can't guess reliably.\n     This is the crux of the fragility.\n\n   ⇒ Make the scaffold reuse the primary path and let my writer be a documented best-effort fallback. For the fallback writer, limit to tensor families we can name with confidence and rely on vLLM's tolerance for missing checkpoint keys (parameters left at their init). But wait — random init in vLLM's init: weights are initialized with the model's init function (typically kaiming/xavier uniform) — finite. So missing tensors won't produce NaN; they remain at finite init values. That preserves \"finite logits\" — good proxy semantics: \"first light\", not fidelity.\n\n   Hmm, but is it true that vLLM doesn't error on missing checkpoint keys? In v1, ModelLoader default: after load_weights, no completeness check — I'm fairly sure vLLM doesn't validate completeness (models often run with missing keys and warn? no, not even a warn). Actually there is `if loaded_weight...` — whatever, many people run vLLM with partially-initialized checkpoints (e.g., newly-added heads). OK.\n\n   Actually, better: if I generate tensors for params the model defines, the fallback writer can be completely generic via runtime introspection: inside make-proxy (in-container), I can instantiate the model config with vllm's ModelConfig and then… building the model needs GPU/memory-profiling context — too heavy for the builder? vllm has `ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls` and could build the model on CPU with `with target_platform`… this is what vLLM's weight-init utilities do. Too fragile.\n\n   Decision: fallback writer writes a conservative set of qwen3-next-family HF tensor names covering: embed_tokens, per-layer norms (input_layernorm, post_attention_layernorm), self_attn q/k/v/o (+ q_norm/k_norm), mlp for dense layers (gate/up/down), MoE layers (gate, experts.{e}.{gate,up,down}_proj fp8 + weight_scale_inv, shared_expert.*, shared_expert_gate), PLE tensors (shards + weight_scale + ple's other projections?), indexer (wq_z? skip), lm_head, final norm. Unknown tensors (linear-attention internals, QSA indexer) will be left at vLLM's init. Document honestly in the receipt (data.weights_written list, data.note).\n\n   Wait — actually the reverse is also possible: unknown checkpoint tensor names raise \"There is no module or parameter named\" (via AutoWeightsLoader on the AMD ple loader; similarly at top level: vLLM's model-level AutoWeightsLoader routes by prefix; unmatched top-level names → error?). qwen3_next's load_weights: uses stacked mapping + params_dict iteration, typically skips unmatched names (if name not in params_dict: continue — typical pattern is `if name.endswith(\".bias\") and name not in params_dict: continue`). Hmm, qwen3_next's load_weights skips expert weights, etc.… if a checkpoint name doesn't match anything, most implementations just `continue` (only AutoWeightsLoader raises). Qwen4Exp uses a custom load_weights that dispatches PLE names and routes the rest to AutoWeightsLoader(self) (per moe-dispatch: amd/ple_layer.py:391 AutoWeightsLoader(self).load_weights on regular_weights — raising on unmatched names within the PLE module). At the model level, qwen4_exp's model.load_weights — per the dossier, after loader.load_weights(...) comes the build_tables block. The underlying loader raising on an unknown name means: my fallback writer must not emit tensor names the model doesn't recognize! An extra wrong name = loud load failure.\n\n   Safer strategy for the fallback writer: emit only names I'm confident exist in this architecture: the PLE shard names + weight_scale (validated against the fork's own _SHARD_RE and loader code — they're in the dossier verbatim), and… the rest? If I only emit PLE tensors, everything else remains at init. The model can still run (first light!). The mmap table path is exercised (discover_shards + attach + gather), the MoE kernel runs with initialized fp8? Wait — expert weights are created by vLLM at init as bf16 parameters; the quant method expects fp8 checkpoint weights + scale_inv; if the checkpoint doesn't provide them, Fp8MoEMethod creates parameters at init in fp8 dtype (create_weights allocates w13_weight in fp8 e4m3 + w13_weight_scale_inv fp32; if not loaded, values are whatever the allocation is — uninitialized memory? vLLM's create_weights typically does weight initialization… for fp8 tensors, `torch.empty` followed by possibly random init… uninitialized fp8 bytes can contain NaN encodings (e4m3's 0x7F is NaN!). Dangerous for the \"finite logits\" assertion.\n\n   Hmm. OK, so weights matter. Write expert weights in fp8 + scale_inv myself (naming is standard Fp8 block convention: `.weight` e4m3 + `.weight_scale_inv` fp32) — vLLM Fp8MoEMethod.create_weights names: `w13_weight` (gate_up fused) and `w2_weight`? For FusedMoE in vLLM: param names are `mlp.experts.{e}.w13_weight`, `.w13_weight_scale_inv`, `.w2_weight`, `.w2_weight_scale_inv` — with HF→vLLM mapping from `gate_proj/up_proj/down_proj` via weight-loader fusion. So the checkpoint side uses HF names: `model.layers.{i}.mlp.experts.{e}.gate_proj.weight` + `.weight_scale_inv` etc. That's the standard HF fp8-block convention (DeepSeek-V3 uses weight_scale_inv). Confident enough.\n\n   Dense linear layers: qwen3_next's linear-attention layers have non-standard modules that can't be guessed. But full_attention layers: standard q/k/v/o + q_norm/k_norm (HF: self_attn.{q_proj.weight, k_proj.weight, v_proj.weight, o_proj.weight, q_norm.weight, k_norm.weight}). Dense MLP layers: gate_proj/up_proj/down_proj. Which layers are dense? In Qwen3Next every layer has MoE (decoder_sparse_step=1)? Per qsa-53896: `num_experts > 0 and (layer_idx+1) % decoder_sparse_step == 0` — with decoder_sparse_step 1, all layers are MoE. Include MoE in every layer; skip dense MLP. If the config expects dense on some layers, the leftover init covers it… no wait, no checkpoint name → leftover init is fine (no extra names emitted).\n\n   But unmatched name risk: does HF-style self_attn.q_proj exist in linear_attention layers? Qwen3Next's linear attention (gated deltanet) has no q_proj. If I emit self_attn.q_proj.weight for a linear-attention layer → unknown name → AutoWeightsLoader raises. So: emit attention tensors only for full_attention layers (known every 4th per the pattern). MoE for all layers. embed/norm/lm_head always. PLE only on layers that have PLE — which layers have PLE?? Is ple in every layer? `_SHARD_RE` is `layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding...` and per the dossier ple_layer.py implements the per-layer PLE module, and build_tables iterates model layers with ple. Probably every layer. Emit PLE shards for all layers (validate: discover_shards + validate_shards_for run in-container and tell me exactly). If a layer lacks PLE, then extra checkpoint names for it → raise. Hmm, risk. But validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim) is called per-layer at construction — the model only constructs MmapNgramEmbedding on layers that have a PLE module. If I emit shards for a layer without PLE, the loader sees an unmatched name → ValueError.\n\n   Mitigation: run self-verification after generation within make-proxy, using the fork's own modules: call `discover_shards` and additionally attempt an actual lightweight load check: `python -c` with ModelConfig + ModelRegistry to build the model on CPU meta? Can't.\n\n   Final honest answer: the fallback writer can't be guaranteed; that's precisely why the task says reuse the fork's test scaffolding whenever possible. The fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp/ probably contains a complete small-checkpoint builder for test_ple.py/test_config.py that knows the exact names. So the runtime reuse path is essential. What I'll implement:\n\n   Path A (fork scaffolding): scan /opt/vllm/tests/models/qwen4_exp/*.py; import each as a module (add /opt/vllm/tests to sys.path); find any module-scope callable with signature taking a path/dir; especially search for safetensors-writing helpers via source inspection (look for the strings `safetensors` + `shard_`). Rather than blind calls, use a very targeted contract: look for a function like `write_checkpoint(dir, ...)` — I can't know the name.\n\n   Practical middle ground: reuse what can be reliably imported by name from the fork tree:\n   1. `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common import ple_mmap` (post-relocation patch 0009) — reuse for shard-naming validation (its `_SHARD_RE`), shard discovery (`discover_shards`), dtype maps (`_FP8_DTYPES`). This is genuine reuse of the fork's scaffolding semantics.\n   2. Try importing test modules `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` and `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd`; if a module exposes a callable that returns a fixture checkpoint (search candidates by regex `.*(fixture|ckpt|checkpoint|shard).*` in the module's globals with callable), call the best candidate under guarded try/except, and verify with discover_shards that the result matches the expected naming. If it yields tensors, use it for the PLE table portion.\n\n   Path B: self-written writer as above (qwen3-next HF family names; MoE experts fp8-block; attention only on full-attention layers; PLE shards+scale on all layers — flag in the receipt if the fork's discovery rejects any layer set).\n\n   Then always: verify with ple_mmap.discover_shards → record per-layer shard counts; validate config parses via `AutoConfig`/vllm transformers utils (`from vllm.transformers_utils.config import get_config` — that runs the fork's qwen4_exp config registration → strong schema check, GPU-free); write generation_config? not needed; tokenizer! Serving needs a tokenizer — HF requires tokenizer files; with HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 and no tokenizer in the directory → serve fails. Options: copy tokenizer from the staged workload (mount FN_MODEL_DIR ro, copy tokenizer.json/tokenizer_config.json/merges.txt/vocab… if present), or generate a minimal tokenizer? The workload directory has tokenizer files (per staging: 131 shards + tokenizer files). make-proxy: if FN_MODEL_DIR is mounted and has tokenizer files, copy them (cheap, a few MB; merges.txt 3.3 MB). Fallback: write a minimal char-level tokenizer_config.json? vLLM demands a real tokenizer. Fallback: if tokenizer absent → mark the receipt as failed? No — better fallback: construct a trivial `pretrained_tokenizer_config`… can't fabricate a byte-identical tokenizer for the qwen vocab.\n\n   Actually, cleanest is: make-proxy.sh mounts the staged workload directory read-only (if present on host) and copies the tokenizer files. If absent, emit a loud, typed failure to the receipt (blocker type F.12: \"proxy requires staged workload tokenizer; none found\") — honest. Hmm, but the task says the proxy runs before touching actual weight bytes — the tokenizer isn't weight bytes. And S2 ordering has staging before proxy. Good: tokenizer copy from staged workload, fail loudly if absent.\n\n   Also chat_template/generation_config are optional.\n\n4. Write config.json + tokenizer + shards to /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy (bind-mounted host directory). Record receipt-ish output? make-proxy doesn't write a receipt (spec: the proxy receipt comes from run-proxy). But make-proxy can print a manifest; run-proxy embeds checkpoint metadata. Keep make-proxy receipt-free but exit non-zero on failure.\n\nrun-proxy.sh steps:\n1. Locate proxy directory (host): FN_PROXY_DIR default $HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint; if missing or empty → instruct make-proxy.sh and exit 2? Or auto-build? The worklist treats them as separate steps; run-proxy should fail with a clear message if absent. Actually, be friendly: if checkpoint absent, automatically invoke make-proxy.sh? Deterministic steps are better kept separate: log \"run make-proxy.sh first\" and fail. Hmm — the overnight runner invokes steps in sequence (per S2 ordering: smoke then proxy). run-proxy failing loud on missing checkpoint is the right discipline.\n\n2. podman run: named container flashnext-proxy-serve, detached:\n```\npodman run -d --name \"$CTR\" --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n  --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host \\\n  -p \"127.0.0.1:$FN_PROXY_PORT:$FN_PROXY_PORT\" \\\n  -v \"$PROXY_DIR_HOST:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$REPO_ROOT/results:/results\" \\\n  -e VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 \\\n  -e HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 -e VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1 -e VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1 \\\n  -e PYTHONHASHSEED=0 \\\n  \"$IMAGE\" bash -lc \"exec vllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy --served-model-name flashnext-proxy --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT --tensor-parallel-size 1 -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE --max-model-len 4096 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.6 --dtype bfloat16\"\n```\nWait: F.9 — do not export env defaults the engine reads through is-set probes. VLLM_PLE_MMAP is read via bool(int(os.getenv)) — not an is_set probe (fn-env.sh exports it too). PYTHONHASHSEED is fine (fn-env does too). Don't set VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* / VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM etc. Good — mirror fn-env.sh's discipline. Also, the allocator env PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF from fn-env: include expandable_segments for consistency? For the small proxy model either works; mirror fn-env's values for consistency.\n\nEnv passing: `-e` each var explicitly (run-smoke doesn't pass env; fn-cluster uses podman exec after fn-env). Fine.\n\nNote: `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` — CLI shorthand for compilation config. In newer vLLM CLI: `-cc` / `--compilation-config`. Use `-cc '{\"cudagraph_mode\": \"PIECEWISE\"}'`? The JSON form is safest across versions. The dossier quotes the guard message: \"pass -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\" — the dotted form is supported. Use `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` (as the fork itself suggests).\n\n3. Poll http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/v1/models until ready (timeout FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT, default 1800s — first compile is slow; the proxy is small but triton compile takes time).\n\n4. One greedy completion via /v1/completions: max_tokens 16, temperature 0, logprobs 5. Assert:\n   - HTTP 200, choices[0].text non-empty, finish_reason sane\n   - Logits finite: collect all logprob values (token_logprobs + top_logprobs dicts); assert all finite via math.isfinite. If the engine returns null first-token logprob, skip None (run-tp2 does so). \"Assert finite logits via API\" — the logprobs are the API-visible proxy for logits. If zero logprob values are available → fail (can't assert).\n5. Also verify engagement: grep the container serve log (`podman logs $CTR`) for ple_mmap lines / \"MmapNgramEmbedding\" / piecewise — record counts; if VLLM_PLE_MMAP didn't engage (no mention), mark fail (residency gate leans on engagement; claim 4.1 \"with mmap path engaged\").\n6. Clean shutdown: podman stop -t 90; get exit code via podman inspect; clean_shutdown = exit==0 (treat 137/143 as non-clean… podman stop sends TERM and waits; vllm exits 0 → container exit 0). Then podman rm.\n7. Write results/receipts/proxy.json {\"step\":\"proxy\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\":{...}}. If any step fails, fail receipt (never silent).\n8. Ensure container removal on all paths via trap (podman rm -f).\n\ntests/ also within my boundary: the repo tests suite (tests/test_repo_invariants.py etc.). Should I add tests? Conflict domain includes tests — permission, not obligation. Let me look at tests/test_repo_invariants.py to see if there are script-related assertions (e.g., every scripts/*.sh is syntax-clean or referenced). Adding small repo tests asserting the two scripts exist, are bash -n clean, and that run-proxy references the receipt path + mmap engagement, mirrors claim 6.4 style (\"the tests suite's package assertions pass: syntax-clean scripts…\"). Reinforces the ladder. Let me read the existing tests first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me look at the repo test suite and the cluster-up serve block more closely:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d32dca839a544b0295862ad1","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && sed -n '1,60p' tests/test_repo_invariants.py && echo ---- && sed -n '60,200p' tests/test_repo_invariants.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5576,"output":6674,"cacheRead":64512,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6555,"totalTokens":76762,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787992109977,"responseId":"chatcmpl-e361ae86-d893-42e6-b811-a5e15c86f59a","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d32dca839a544b0295862ad1","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"\"\"Repo packaging invariants — the offline half of the discipline.\n\nModeled on ds4-vllm's test_patchset_packaging.py (Apache-2.0), extended:\nnew-file line counts are audited too (the upstream suite skipped them and\n3 of its 12 MANIFEST counts drifted).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\nclass RepoInvariants(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_license_is_apache2(self):\n        text = (ROOT / \"LICENSE\").read_text()\n        self.assertIn(\"Apache License\", text)\n        self.assertIn(\"Version 2.0\", text)\n\n    def test_no_gpl_text_in_tree(self):\n        needle = \"GNU GENERAL \" + \"PUBLIC LICENSE\"  # avoid self-match\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            # Evidence dossiers may QUOTE license headers they inspected.\n            if \"evidence\" in path.parts or path.name == \"test_repo_invariants.py\":\n                continue\n            if path.suffix in {\".safetensors\", \".gguf\"}:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            self.assertNotIn(needle, text, f\"GPL text found in {path}\")\n\n    def test_no_secret_material(self):\n        patterns = [re.compile(p) for p in\n                    (r\"BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY\", r\"hf_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\",\n                     r\"ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\", r\"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\")]\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            for pat in patterns:\n                self.assertIsNone(pat.search(text),\n                                  f\"secret-like material in {path}: {pat.pattern}\")\n\n    def test_spec_artifacts_exist(self):\n        self.assertTrue((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/spec.md\").is_file())\n        trace = json.loads((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/trace.json\").read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(trace[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(\n----\n        self.assertTrue(\n            (ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/contracts/trace.schema.json\").is_file())\n\n    def test_evidence_dossiers_present(self):\n        dossiers = list((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/evidence\").glob(\"*.md\"))\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(len(dossiers), 7)\n\n    def test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene(self):\n        wl_path = ROOT / \"silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"\n        wl = json.loads(wl_path.read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(wl[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(wl[\"tasks\"])\n        raw = wl_path.read_text()\n        # Runtime hub downloads are forbidden estate-wide.\n        self.assertNotIn(\"-hf \", raw)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"hf download\", raw)\n        # Model-family names never enter worklist bytes (lint L16 mirror).\n        for banned in (\"qwen\", \"llama\", \"deepseek\", \"claude\", \"opus\"):\n            self.assertNotIn(banned, raw.lower(),\n                             f\"banned token '{banned}' in worklist bytes\")\n        # Every implementation task carries executable acceptance.\n        for task in wl[\"tasks\"]:\n            if task.get(\"kind\") == \"implementation\":\n                self.assertTrue(task.get(\"acceptanceCriteria\"),\n                                f\"{task['id']} has no acceptance criteria\")\n\n    def test_scripts_are_syntax_clean(self):\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"host\").rglob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for py in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.py\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"python3\", \"-m\", \"py_compile\", str(py)],\n                           check=True)\n\n    def test_receipts_verify_runs_clean(self):\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [\"python3\", str(ROOT / \"scripts/receipts-verify.py\")],\n            capture_output=True, text=True)\n        self.assertIn(proc.returncode, (0,),\n                      f\"receipts-verify failed:\\n{proc.stdout}{proc.stderr}\")\n\n    def test_notices_cover_the_import_manifest(self):\n        notices = (ROOT / \"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\").read_text()\n        for needle in (\"vllm-project/vllm\", \"AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm\",\n                       \"amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes\", \"nix-strix-halo\"):\n            self.assertIn(needle, notices)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787992284832},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Note: `test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene` forbids things like \"qwen\" inside the worklist byte string. My scripts also need to avoid banned model family names? That lint applies only to the worklist byte string. But naming F.5: \"Don't put model family names in spec or worklist byte strings\" — only spec/worklist. Scripts may reference the architecture identifier (run-smoke.sh references Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration). Architecture identifiers are not the same as family names like \"qwen\"? \"Qwen4Exp\" contains \"Qwen\"... run-smoke.sh already has it, and the test only scans the worklist. No problem — follow the run-smoke precedent.\n\nNext, add a test file tests/test_proxy_tooling.py modeled after claim 6.4's style: syntax-clean scripts (already covered by test_scripts_are_syntax_clean), plus proxy-specific assertions:\n- run-proxy.sh references results/receipts/proxy.json\n- run-proxy.sh sets VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\n- run-proxy.sh does NOT pass --enforce-eager together with VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 (the fork's guard refuses this; ruling P10) — a good pin!\n- run-proxy.sh runs in PIECEWISE cudagraph mode\n- make-proxy.sh builds under /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy, uses weight_block_size 128×128 and 8 experts... (grep for \"weight_block_size\", shard naming `ngram_embedding.shard_`, number of experts).\n- neither script exports env vars that are the target of is-set probes (F.9): assert absence of VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER.\n\nThese mirror the \"package assertions\" style.\n\nNext — details of the config template for the fallback. Let me define the proxy geometry (fallback template when there's no staged config):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"],\n  \"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\",\n  \"torch_dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n  \"hidden_size\": 2560,\n  \"num_hidden_layers\": 8,\n  \"layer_types\": [3×linear, full]×2,\n  \"full_attention_interval\": 4,\n  \"num_attention_heads\": 32? \n```\nHmm hidden 2560, head_dim 256 → num_attention_heads = hidden/head_dim = 10 for a normal model, but qwen3_next uses head_dim independent of hidden (head_dim 256 with num_attention_heads 16, etc.). From the evidence: hidden_size 2560, head_dim 256. Qwen3Next style: q_size = num_heads*head_dim can differ from hidden. I don't know the actual heads count. Keep hidden_size 2560, head_dim 256, num_attention_heads 16 (q dim 4096), num_key_value_heads 2? Arbitrary... for the proxy, only matters for weight shapes. vLLM builds shapes from the config, and my weights must match. Since my fallback writer derives shapes from the same config I write, they'll be consistent.\n\nBut — the bigger risk is config schema completeness for the qwen4_exp config class (indexer, qsa, ple fields, mtp...). If the staged config exists, reuse it and only shrink: safest. Fallback template: include only fields I know + what the config class requires; the fork's config class has defaults (the config must have defaults since HF config.json is the sole source). Shrink the staged config:\n- num_hidden_layers → 8; rebuild layer_types from the pattern\n- num_experts → 8, num_experts_per_tok → 2\n- moe_intermediate_size → round to multiple of 128 and shrink → e.g. 256\n- intermediate_size (dense) → 512?\n- vocab_size → keep? embed_tokens vocab ~150k → embed 150k×2560×2B = 770 MB bf16. Hmm, that's big for \"tiny\". Shrink vocab to 4096. But the tokenizer I copy from the workload has the full vocab; lm_head/embed of 4096 vs tokenizer vocab 152k — vLLM pads vocab to tp/divisor; tokenizer ids > vocab → runtime error on tokenize? Greedy completion from a short ASCII prompt: qwen tokenizer encodes common text with ids less than 4096? No guarantee (byte-fallback ids for common words might be higher). Safer: vocab_size = 32768 (embed 32k×2560×2 = 160 MB, fine), and most common token ids fall under 32k. Also the ngram/PLE padded vocab is derived from org_vocab... shrink ngram_vocab_size_base if present. Hmm, org_vocab for PLE ≠ text vocab? \"The 51.2B-parameter hash-lookup embedding... reads one layer per token\" — the ngram vocab is its own thing (ngram_vocab_size_base etc.). If base config has those fields, shrink them: e.g. ngram_vocab_size_base → 1024, keep heads_per_ngram/ple_embed_dim (small). Table bytes = ngram_vocab × heads × ple_embed_dim... with fp8 row_bytes = heads_per_ngram*ple_embed_dim bytes. Keeping real dims (unknown to me) — shrink base if the field exists.\n\nHonestly, can't perfectly determine this without seeing the real config. The shrink path preserves the field names (copies the actual config and then overrides known keys) — so whatever the real config contains, it stays valid. The fallback template is a last resort, marked as such in output.\n\nAlso `tie_word_embeddings`: false; provide lm_head separately.\n\nQuantization: add quantization_config to the proxy config. Note: the real config.json likely already has a quantization_config — override with {\"quant_method\":\"fp8\",\"weight_block_size\":[128,128]} and drop modules_to_not_convert (so all linears are quantized... wait, I decided earlier to quantize all linears; but hmm — embedding-adjacent modules: VocabParallelEmbedding is unaffected. PLE's key_proj/value_proj linear layers would be fp8-quantized — the PLE FP8 stack handles fp8 embeddings, but are its projections quantized? In the real model they're in modules_to_not_convert (943 entries — the vast majority of modules are not converted; only experts are quantized!). Real model: only mlp.experts.* is quantized. Quantizing everything is a divergence; risk: fp8 path in PLE projection quant — Fp8LinearMethod on key_proj/value_proj is a normal linear; the 0012 patch permits block-fp8 linear on gfx1151. Should work. But a subtle risk remains.\n\n  Alternative that mirrors the workload exactly: generate modules_to_not_convert programmatically: everything except `mlp.experts`. Can I enumerate? Format of the modules_to_not_convert entries in the real checkpoint is unknown (names like \"model.layers.0.mlp.gate\"? \"re:.*linear_attn.*\"?). Fp8Config just does `ignore = lambda prefix: any(re.match(ignore_pattern, prefix)...`? In vLLM's Fp8Config, ignored_layers is a list of regex/strings matched against the module prefix. So I can write modules_to_not_convert as a regex covering all non-expert linears: does vLLM use `re.match(pattern, module_name)` per pattern? Let me recall: Fp8Config.from_config reads modules_to_not_convert; `self.ignored_layers` is used in get_quant_method via `if self.ignore(layer prefix)`. The check is `any(re.match(layer, ...) ...)`? In vllm/model_executor/layers/quantization/fp8.py:\n```python\ndef ignore(self, layer_name): return any(re.match(ignore_pattern, layer_name) for ignore_pattern in self.ignored_layers)\n```\nHmm, is re.match pattern→layer or layer→pattern... I think: `any(re.search(pattern, layer_name) ...)`. If patterns are regexes, one entry like `^(?!.*mlp\\.experts).*` is fragile across versions.\n\n  Decision: don't set modules_to_not_convert at all (quantize everything). This is a proxy; its role is to exercise the permitted kernel path and the mmap table path with finite outputs. Quantizing all linears exercises more of the permitted surface (both the 0008 MoE permission and the 0012 linear permission). Document this choice in the script comment.\n\nWait, another consideration: with block_quant and all lineals quantized, `Fp8LinearMethod` needs the TritonFp8BlockScaledMMKernel (permitted by 0012; FN_FP8_LINEAR defaults on). OK.\n\nBut note: qwen3_next linear-attention modules contain Conv1d and other non-Linear modules — automatically not quantized. GatedDeltaNet's A_log/dt_bias are buffers — fine.\n\nNow weight tensor generation details (fallback writer). Shape derivation from config:\n- H = hidden_size; HD = head_dim; NH = num_attention_heads; NKV = num_key_value_heads\n- V = vocab_size (proxy), L layers, E=8 experts, K=2 top-k\n- moe_intermediate_size = M (multiple of 128), shared_expert_intermediate_size = S (if config has shared_experts)\n- For full_attention layer i: self_attn.{q_proj,k_proj,v_proj}.weight: qwen3_next has separate q/k/v in HF? vLLM qwen3_next's stacked_params_mapping maps qkv_proj ← (q_proj,q,kv...). HF Qwen3Next checkpoint: `self_attn.q_proj.weight`? Or does HF store `qkv_proj`? Hmm. vLLM stacked mapping: (\"qkv_proj\", \".self_attn.q_proj\", 0) etc. means HF has separate q_proj/k_proj/v_proj. Emit separate q/k/v + o_proj + q_norm/k_norm (qwen3 has qk-norm; qwen3_next also: `q_norm`, `k_norm`).\n- Linear-attention layers: gated deltanet tensors — I can't name them reliably. Skip them (leave at init). Actually wait — if I leave the entire linear-attn at init, do those layers produce finite outputs? GatedDeltaNet with random init: conv1d random, A_log random... should be finite. OK.\n\nHmm, wait a moment. Should I include tensors for linear-attn with guessed names? No — guessing names risks ValueError on unknown names. Skipping is safe (missing keys are tolerated, unknown keys are not... mostly).\n\nIs that right about unknown keys? Top-level loader of the qwen4_exp model — per dossier, the amd ple layer's AutoWeightsLoader(self).load_weights(regular weights) raises ValueError on unmatched names *within the PLE module*. Model-level: vLLM's general DefaultModelLoader iterates checkpoint tensors; for each name it looks up params_dict[name]; if absent → typically `continue` (e.g. skipping \"lm_head\" when tied, etc.). qwen3_next.load_weights: `if name not in params_dict: continue`? I recall vLLM's load loop typically does: `if name.endswith(\".bias\") and name not in params_dict: continue` and `param = params_dict[name]` — would KeyError if absent. Hmm, both patterns exist. Qwen3Next.load_weights (vLLM):\n\n```python\nfor name, loaded_weight in weights:\n    if \"rotary_emb.inv_freq\" in name: continue\n    ...\n    for param_name, weight_name, shard_id in stacked_params_mapping: ...\n    ...\n    param = params_dict[name]\n```\nIf an unknown name comes in → KeyError → loud failure. That's the risk I flagged. So the fallback writer should only emit names guaranteed to exist. Confidence list:\n- model.embed_tokens.weight ✓ (standard)\n- model.norm.weight ✓\n- lm_head.weight ✓\n- Full-attn layers: model.layers.{i}.self_attn.{q_proj,k_proj,v_proj,o_proj}.weight, .self_attn.{q_norm,k_norm}.weight — qwen3 family naming ✓ (qwen3_next follows qwen3 naming with q_norm/k_norm).\n  Hmm — but does Qwen4Exp's full_attention layer use `self_attn` prefix? qwen4_exp/amd/model.py imports Qwen3NextAttention etc. Yes.\n- MoE every layer: model.layers.{i}.mlp.gate.weight [E, H]; experts: model.layers.{i}.mlp.experts.{e}.{gate_proj,up_proj}.weight [M, H] fp8 + weight_scale_inv [ceil(M/128), ceil(H/128)] fp32; down_proj.weight [H, M] + scale.\n  Shared experts if config has shared_expert_intermediate_size>0: mlp.shared_experts.{gate,up,down}_proj... quantize these too? These are linears — the fp8-block path handles them; or bf16? If quantization_config has no ignore list, shared experts are also quantized (they are Linear modules). Emit in fp8+scale for consistency.\n  mlp.gate.weight is not quantized (it's a router, excluded from quant by vLLM's Fp8Config? Router is a ReplicatedLinear; Fp8Config.get_quant_method for the FusedMoE router... router remains unquantized by vLLM convention (the router weight stays in params_dtype). Emit gate.weight bf16.\n- Per-layer PLE: model.layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_{j:05d}.weight (fp8, uint8 rows?) + ...ngram_embedding.weight_scale (fp32 scalar per layer).\n\nShard tensor details: dtype must be one of _FP8_DTYPES (safetensors dtype \"F8_E4M3\"), shape (rows, row_bytes) where row_bytes = heads_per_ngram * ple_embed_dim? From dossier: MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, head_dim) — hmm, embedding_dim = head_dim? row_bytes = head_dim? Table: rows × row_bytes uint8 view of fp8. The row count in the header determines `rows`. _validate_layer_shards checks dtype and row_bytes consistency. So shard weight shape: (rows, row_bytes) F8_E4M3, row_bytes = head_dim of ple embedding dim (ple_embed_dim × heads? — the layer's PLE embedding dim = self.embedding_dim). The layer passes head_dim: `MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` → row_bytes = head_dim (256 in real config). Per-layer fp8 table: rows_total = padded ngram vocab; shard_size = ceil(org_vocab/split_ngram_parts); pad so per-layer shards = split_ngram_parts count? _attach_table: for each layer, expected shard count = ceil(org_vocab_size / shard_size) = split_ngram_parts (if divisible). Set split_ngram_parts=2 (if the config field name is `split_ngram_parts`; the actual field might be `ngram_split_parts`... unknown!). Ugh.\n\nThe geometry is config-driven and read at amd/ple_layer.py:140-195 (ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim, make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by). split_ngram_parts default 512, nvidia/ple_layer.py:241 — maybe a config field or hardcoded default. If hardcoded 512, expected shard count = ceil(org_vocab/ceil(org_vocab/512)). If I set ngram org_vocab = 512 rows → shard_size = 1, 512 shard files per layer × 8 layers = 4096 files — \"a few\" is too many. Set ngram_vocab tiny, e.g. 64: shard_size = ceil(64/512)=1 → 64 shards/layer = 512 files. Still many. Hmm, but each file is tiny (~256 B fp8). Or padded_vocab... discover_shards globs *.safetensors and parses headers — fine with 4096 files, fast.\n\nBut wait: which config field controls ngram org vocab? \"ngram_vocab_size_base\" — with heads_per_ngram multiplier: total = base × heads? Not sure. If the real config is available, shrink ngram_vocab_size_base to a small number (e.g. 64) and let the model code compute; the writer needs to match that computation for the shard count... The writer can't reliably recompute.\n\n⇒ Resolution: use the fork's own module to compute the expected geometry! Inside the container, after writing config.json:\n```python\nfrom vllm.config import ModelConfig  # or vllm.transformers_utils.config.get_config\ncfg = get_config(model_path, trust_remote_code=False)\n```\nThen instantiate the PLE layer module? Too heavy. Alternative: `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common import ple_mmap` — has shard-size computation in _attach_table; but needs org_vocab and split_ngram_parts from config. The nvidia loader does `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts` — org_vocab_size is an attribute of the layer (config-derived).\n\nPractical approach: write the PLE table generically: 1 shard per layer, name it shard_00000, rows = padded_vocab (whole table in one shard)? Then _attach_table expects ceil(org/shard_size) shards; with default split 512, mismatch → RuntimeError \"missing shards\". No good.\n\nAlternative: let discover_shards itself drive verification — write N shards as computed by the model code. Can compute org_vocab and split via the actual layer class's computation — need to instantiate Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding — needs vllm_config context... heavy but maybe doable with stubs? No.\n\nOK, honest pivot: the PLE table geometry is a part where reusing the fork test scaffolding is essential — test_ple_mmap.py's fixture constructs a valid checkpoint against the real code (that's its purpose, with boundary contract tests). So make-proxy's PLE writer strategy:\n\n1. Import the fork test module; find the fixture function that builds the shard safetensors (the one at :112-124 that writes `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). Invoke/repurpose it with the proxy dir + our layer indices and its own geometry source (may take config params).\n2. If that fails: compute geometry via the fork's ple_mmap contract: org_vocab = padded ngram vocab derived from config fields via mirror of the layer's computation (implement by reading config fields with the same names as the dossier: ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim, make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by, split_ngram_parts — with defaults from the dossier: split default 512); then verify by running `ple_mmap.discover_shards(path)` + a CPU-only `MmapPleTable` attach (if MmapPleTable is CPU-safe — numpy-based, should be; `_attach_table` happens at init given model_config...). Best effort with loud failure + blocker text.\n\nGiven the campaign's reality (this runs overnight on the actual machine; the receipts ladder grades), the scripts must be robust, loud, and honest; perfection isn't guaranteeable from here. Implement a layered strategy with rich diagnostics in the receipt and a blocker protocol on failure (F.12: typed blockers; R7.2 drafts upstream issue text — that's the morning side; my runner writes a fail receipt with typed error data).\n\nOne more critical element for run-proxy: compile warmup time. vLLM v1 with piecewise cudagraph compiles — on first run with cold cache it could take ~20-30 min on this hardware. FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT default 3600s.\n\nAlso --max-model-len: proxy config's max_position_embeddings — set it to 4096 in the proxy config; pass --max-model-len 4096. KV cache is small. gpu-memory-utilization 0.5.\n\n`--dtype bfloat16` too? Config's torch_dtype bf16; omit the flag. Add `--trust-remote-code`? qwen4_exp config is registered in the fork's transformers_utils — not remote. Not needed.\n\nEnforce single GPU: `--tensor-parallel-size 1` explicitly.\n\nAnother wrinkle: podman container needs a port: use FN_PROXY_PORT default 1235 (avoid 1234, which is the pair-service port). -p binds on host.\n\nAPI for receipt's finite-logits assertion: logprobs=5 → top_logprobs list of dicts {token: logprob}. token_logprobs[0] is often None (prompt continuation). Collect all numeric values; assert count > 0 and all finite.\n\nAlso record in data: \"mmap_engaged\": greps from serve log for \"ple_mmap\" / \"MmapNgramEmbedding\" / \"piecewise\"; \"cudagraph_mode\": \"PIECEWISE\"; fork_commit if present from /opt/flashnext/build-info.json (read in-container? run-proxy is host-side; grab via podman exec/logs — simpler: include in the serve container's startup command? Just record what's available on host: image name. Fork commit: `podman run --rm $IMAGE cat /opt/flashnext/build-info.json` once — cheap, include fork_commit in receipt. Nice touch tying engine identity to the receipt (spec: build receipt names the lane; proxy receipt is graded by receipts-verify's general form).\n\nNow make-proxy.sh structure (host bash):\n- PROXY_DIR host default: \"${FN_PROXY_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint}\"\n- MODEL_DIR host: \"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\" (for tokenizer + optional base config)\n- If MODEL_DIR exists, mount: -v \"$MODEL_DIR:/models:ro\"\n- -v \"$PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\"\n- ENV: FN_PROXY_LAYERS default 8, FN_PROXY_EXPERTS default 8\n- podman run --rm (no GPU needed for generation! no --device needed) $IMAGE python3 - <<'PY'\n\nInside python:\n```python\nimport json, math, os, re, sys, time\nfrom pathlib import Path\nOUT = Path(\"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\nMODELS = Path(\"/models\")  # read-only mount if present\nlayers = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_LAYERS\", \"8\"))\nexperts = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS\", \"8\"))\n```\nSteps:\n1. Base config: if MODELS/config.json exists → load; source=\"workload\"; else → template; source=\"template\".\n2. Shrink:\n```python\ncfg[\"architectures\"]  # keep, assert it contains Qwen4Exp id\ncfg[\"num_hidden_layers\"] = layers\n# layer pattern:\ninterval = cfg.get(\"full_attention_interval\", 4)\ncfg[\"layer_types\"] = [\"linear_attention\"]*(interval-1)+[\"full_attention\"]  # times\nrepeat to num_hidden_layers\ncfg[\"num_experts\"] = experts\nif \"num_experts_per_tok\" in cfg: cfg[\"num_experts_per_tok\"] = min(cfg[\"num_experts_per_tok\"], experts)\nelse set 2\ncfg[\"moe_intermediate_size\"] = max(128, round to 128, maybe shrink)  # set 256\nif \"intermediate_size\" in cfg: cfg[\"intermediate_size\"] = 512? only if dense MLP is used... keep as-is? Shrinking intermediate_size is safe either way: set 1024.\ncfg[\"vocab_size\"] = 32768 (shrinks embed/lm_head; tokenizer ids below 32k are fine)\nif \"max_position_embeddings\" in cfg: 4096\nPLE/ngram fields: shrink if present: for k in list(cfg): if \"ngram\" in k and \"size\" in k: ... beware. Let's do: ngram_vocab_size_base → min(existing, 64)? Hmm, \"base\" might be multiplied by heads_per_ngram... total ngram vocab = base*heads? If base=64, heads unknown... table rows = padded(base*heads?) small anyway. Set it if the key exists, else leave.\nsplit parts key: unknown name; search for keys containing \"split\" and \"ngram\" → set to 2? If hardcoded in code (default 512), can't shrink; then expected shard count for small org_vocab is ceil(org / ceil(org/512)): for org≤512 → shard_size=1 → org shards. org=512 → 512 files per layer. Hmm.\n```\nUgh, PLE geometry really is fork-internal. Mitigation: compute expected geometry at runtime via fork's code. Plan: after writing config.json, ask the fork for the geometry:\n\n```python\nfrom vllm.transformers_utils.config import get_config\nhf_cfg = get_config(str(OUT), trust_remote_code=False)\n```\nThen try to import the layer module and compute org vocab the way the layer does:\namd/ple_layer.py:140-195 reads the fields. Try to reproduce: instantiate nothing; instead read the source? No.\n\nAlternative clever trick: use `MmapPleTable`/`discover_shards` verification loop: write shards iteratively — first write shard candidates generously: rows per shard = 1 row... nope.\n\nLet me think about what _attach_table actually requires: `missing = [...]` for expected shard idxs per layer from shard_size computation; RuntimeError if any missing. Extra shards present on disk but not expected? discover_shards records per_layer[layer][shard_idx] for any name that matches; _attach_table iterates expected indices and reads from the discovered dict — extra shard indices in dict just unused? Probably OK (extra tolerated, missing not). So if I write shards 0..S-1 with S = max(expected over possible splits), extras harmless. If I write split = 512 case: for any org_vocab ≤ 512, expected = org_vocab shards of 1 row each. If I always write exactly 512 shards of 1 row (rows_total 512) per PLE layer:\n- If model computes org_vocab = 512 and split 512 → expects 512 ✓.\n- If org_vocab = 64 (shrunk base) → expects 64, shards 0..63 present (I wrote 0..511) ✓ extras ignored (probably).\n- If padded_vocab > 512 → expects more than present → RuntimeError. Then validation step (discover_shards + my own attach probe) catches pre-serve and I can regenerate with more rows. Make the writer adaptive: choose NGRAM_ROWS = 512 rows, 1 row/shard, shards 0..511 — but only if that matches expected. If model's padded ngram vocab > 512, need more rows.\n\nWait, actually — simpler: don't shrink ngram fields at all. Keep the workload's ngram geometry (ngram_vocab_size_base etc. copied from the real config)! Table is fp8: real table is 51.2B parameters = 51.2 GB fp8. Too big for \"small lookup table\". Shrink mandatory.\n\nHmm OK, let me look at what amd/ple_layer.py:140-195 computes... don't have the source. Dossier says the geometry is config-driven with fields ngram_vocab_size_base, heads_per_ngram, ple_embed_dim, make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by. org vocab for ngram ≈ ngram_vocab_size_base (×heads?) — the \"51.2B\" table is 51.2e9 rows?? No — 51.2B parameters with row_bytes ~ head_dim 256 → 200M rows × 256 = 51.2B params. So ngram_vocab_size_base is probably around 200 million / (something). Padded via make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by.\n\nAdaptive plan: runtime loop inside the generator:\n1. Write config;\n2. Compute expected shard layout by instantiating the fork's own validation: `ple_mmap.validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim)` — needs vllm ModelConfig: `from vllm.config import ModelConfig; mc = ModelConfig(model=str(OUT), ...)` — ModelConfig creation is mostly CPU-safe (loads config, tokenizer...). Might work in-container! Then expected geometry can be derived from... validate_shards_for reads discover_shards and compares — raises if wrong. So: generate candidate shards, call validate_shards_for, if raises with missing info, adapt. But it doesn't tell me expected count numerically...\n\nBetter: compute directly from the layer class:\n```python\nfrom vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ple_layer import Qwen4ExpNGramEmbedding? \n```\nInstantiate the module? No.\n\nYou know what — I'm overengineering this blind. Realistic engineering stance: the generator probes the fork at runtime via introspection, picks the right computation if the names exist, and otherwise falls back to best-guess with loud validation. Write the code defensively:\n\n```python\ndef ngram_geometry(hf_cfg):\n    # Mirror amd/ple_layer.py's config read; field names per\n    # specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md §5 (config-driven geometry).\n    base = getattr(hf_cfg, \"ngram_vocab_size_base\", None)\n    heads = getattr(hf_cfg, \"heads_per_ngram\", 1)\n    dim = getattr(hf_cfg, \"ple_embed_dim\", None) or getattr(hf_cfg, \"head_dim\", 256)\n    div = getattr(hf_cfg, \"make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by\", 1)\n    split = getattr(hf_cfg, \"split_ngram_parts\", 512)\n    ...\n```\nAnd cross-check with the fork's own module when importable: after writing, `ple_mmap.discover_shards(OUT)` → per_layer dict; then attempt a `MmapPleTable` attach per layer (inspect its __init__ signature at runtime; guard with try/except) to prove the contract holds. If anything mismatches → loud failure with diagnostics (and adapt: if discover finds fewer shards than attach expects from the error message, retry with more shards once? Can parse RuntimeError \"missing\"... overkill; single retry policy: if the geometry probe fails, fall back to a full 512-shard layout (1 row per shard) that covers any org_vocab ≤ 512 at default split).\n\nHmm, wait — actually, is `org_vocab` here the text vocab?? \"ngram_embedding\" rows are indexed by hashed ngram ids — `padded_vocab_size` handed to MmapNgramEmbedding is the layer's padded ngram vocab, not text vocab. OK, as above.\n\nShrink rule to keep table small while fitting likely computations: set `ngram_vocab_size_base = 512` (×heads?) if the field exists. If padded result ≤ 512 and split default 512 → shard_size=1, shards=padded. Write 512 single-row shards per layer → covers padded ∈ [1,512]. If padded > 512 → validation fails loudly with typed blocker (\"proxy PLE geometry exceeds the one-row-shard layout; adjust FN_PROXY_NGRAM_ROWS\"). Also allow env override FN_PROXY_SPLIT to write fewer multi-row shards if config field split exists (then set cfg[\"split_ngram_parts\"] = 2 and write 2 shards). Actually if the config field exists under any name containing both \"ngram\" and \"split\", set to 2 and write 2 shards of padded/2 rows. Implement key detection:\n\n```python\nsplit_key = next((k for k in cfg if \"split\" in k and \"ngram\" in k), None)\nif split_key: cfg[split_key] = 2\n```\n\nrow_bytes: heads × dim fp8 bytes → tensor shape (rows, heads*dim) dtype F8_E4M3. If heads unknown → use head_dim 256 as row bytes. Validate with fork's `_validate_layer_shards` at runtime if possible (it checks dtype and row_bytes against head_dim... per dossier validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim)). row_bytes must match what the layer expects: embedding_dim. Layer: `MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim)` and `self.embedding_dim`... the AMD W4 branch allocates output `(num_tokens, self.embedding_dim)` with dtype of table → dequant. Per-layer embedding_dim = heads_per_ngram * ple_embed_dim? Or head_dim (256)? nvidia :1194: `_dequantize_embeddings` after `self.ple_embedding(...)`. ngram_embedding constructed with `self.head_dim` (amd:196: `VocabParallelEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim...)`). So embedding dim = head_dim (256). row_bytes = 256 fp8 bytes. ✓ Use head_dim from config (default 256).\n\nweight_scale: per-layer scalar fp32 = 1.0. Shape (1,) or scalar — set_weight_scale(embedding, loaded_weight, device) — buffer; any shape broadcastable; use [1].\n\nCheckpoint tensor naming for the scale: full name `model.layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.weight_scale`.\n\nNow fp8 expert weight generation: for expert e: gate_proj/up_proj shape (M, H) e4m3; scale_inv (ceil(M/128), ceil(H/128)) fp32 = 1.0 (value 1.0 → dequant = fp8 value; keep fp8 values small uniform like 0.004 so activations stay small → logits finite). Initialize fp8 from uniform(-a,a) quantized: simplest: zeros? All-zero experts → MoE output zero → residual stream carries through → logits from lm_head(embed chain) finite. Zero weights are safe for finiteness! But gate/router at zero → uniform routing, fine. Random init can blow up. Use small values: experts fp8 zeros with scale 1.0; dense fp8 zeros; attention zeros? All-zero attention → softmax(QK^T=0) uniform → output = mean(V)=0 → residual passes. layernorm of a constant vector? RMSNorm of zero → zero / sqrt(eps) → 0*weight=0. OK finite. embed_tokens: small random values or scaled one-hot-ish: zeros → norm(0) is fine. lm_head: identity-ish mapping: lm_head weight = scaled copy of embed → logits ≈ embeddings → nearly deterministic greedy tokens, finite. Simplest: lm_head = embed_tokens (tied content, valid float). Tokenizer's eos id is whatever.\n\nDeterminism: seed numpy/torch RNG fixed (42) → reproducible checkpoint.\n\nSafetensors writing: use the `safetensors.torch.save_file` API (available in the container). Dtype: torch.float8_e4m3fn for fp8 tensors (safetensors supports F8_E4M3 — yes, safetensors>=0.4 supports float8_e4m3fn... need serialization support: safetensors torch save handles float8_e4m3fn? Does save_file map to F8_E4M3 dtype string since 0.4.x? Hmm, I recall safetensors supports float8 via numpy workaround (save as uint8 view + metadata?). The real checkpoint stores F8_E4M3 (dossier: `_FP8_DTYPES = {\"F8_E4M3\": torch.float8_e4m3fn}` parsed from header → safetensors writes F8_E4M3 natively). torch save_file with float8 tensor — supported in recent safetensors (0.5+). Pinned vLLM deps are new. Fallback: save with safe.save_file using raw bytes and dtype string \"F8_E4M3\" via `safetensors.numpy`? numpy has no float8. Use `safetensors.torch._SIZE`... implement helper: try save_file(fp8 tensor); on exception, manual header write: safetensors format is simple JSON header + raw bytes — implement `_write_safetensors(path, {name: (dtype_str, shape, bytes)})` minimal serializer. Robust against library version. Use manual serializer for fp8 tensors throughout? Simpler: one handcrafted writer for all tensors (bf16 via torch → numpy → bytes). About 30 lines, deterministic, no version risk. Write my own tiny serializer (format: 8-byte LE header size, JSON {name: {dtype, shape, data_offsets}}, padded data). Known format. Dtype strings: \"BF16\", \"F32\", \"F8_E4M3\".\n\nFile layout: single model.safetensors? discover_shards globs all *.safetensors and parses headers; multiple tensors per file fine. But 8 layers × (attention + MoE 8 experts×3×2 tensors + ple 512 shards) — many tensors; shard files: one file per shard named model-ple-{layer}-{shard:05d}.safetensors (mirrors the fork's test fixture naming). Main weights: model-00001-of-00001.safetensors. No index json needed (mmap discovery parses headers directly; vLLM's loader handles single-file without index fine).\n\nSizes: H=2560. Per-layer full attn: q (nq×256... let q_dim = NH×HD). Pick NH=16, NKV=2 → q 16×256=4096×2560 ×1B(fp8)=10.5 MB... quantize attn? Decision was quantize all linears → attn fp8 too. Per full-attn layer: q,k,v,o fp8 ≈ (4096+512+512+2560·… o: H×(NH·HD)=2560×4096) — about 10+1.3+1.3+10.5=23 MB fp8 per full layer. 2 full layers = 46 MB. Per-layer MoE: 8 experts × (2×(256×2560) + (2560×256)) fp8 = 8×3×0.655 MB = 15.7 MB + shared expert (if exists, 3×(S×H), S=256: 2 MB) → ~18 MB × 8 layers = 142 MB. Table: 8 layers × 512 rows × 256 B = 1 MB. Total ~200 MB. Fine for \"tiny\".\n\nHmm wait, H is 2560 for sure? In the template, yes; in the shrink path keep workload's hidden (2560) — fine.\n\nmax seq: 4096, context needed for the prompt in request is small.\n\nNow run-proxy.sh receipt data fields:\n```json\n{\"step\":\"proxy\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"ts\":..., \"data\":{\n  \"checkpoint\": \"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\",\n  \"checkpoint_source\": \"...from make-proxy marker file proxy-manifest.json\",\n  \"engine\": {\"image\": ..., \"fork_commit\": ...},\n  \"serve\": {\"cudagraph_mode\":\"PIECEWISE\",\"vllm_ple_mmap\":1,\"tp\":1,\"port\":...,\"boot_seconds\":...},\n  \"completion\": {\"prompt\":..., \"text\":..., \"tokens\": n, \"finish_reason\":...},\n  \"finite_logits\": true, \"logprobs_checked\": k,\n  \"mmap_engagement\": {\"log_mentions\": n, \"engaged\": true},\n  \"shutdown\": {\"clean\": true, \"exit_code\": 0}\n}}\n```\nmake-proxy writes proxy-manifest.json inside the checkpoint directory (geometry, reused scaffolding, base config source, file count, bytes) — run-proxy reads and embeds. Clean.\n\nEngagement grep: `podman logs $CTR 2>&1 | grep -ci 'ple_mmap\\|mmap'`... the fork's W3 has loud logging on ROCm mmap enablement (\"loud log (the flag takes ROCm off FULL cudagraphs...)\" per MANIFEST 0010). So log should contain ple_mmap lines. Engaged condition: VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 AND ple_mmap mentions in log ≥1. If zero → fail with tail of log captured in receipt data.\n\nClean shutdown: podman stop -t 120; exit_code=$(podman inspect -f '{{.State.ExitCode}}'); clean = exit_code == 0. podman rm. If stop times out → kill → record clean=false, exit 137.\n\nAlso add cleanup trap: on script error, stop+rm container, write fail receipt.\n\nWait — one more check: does the acceptance grep require literal string \"results/receipts/proxy.json\" in run-proxy.sh? Yes: `grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh`. Define OUT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/proxy.json\" — contains the literal. ✓. Both bash -n clean. ✓.\n\nEnv F.9 discipline in run-proxy: -e flags passed: VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 (bool(int) read, permitted; fn-env exports it), HF_HUB_OFFLINE/VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS/VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK (not engine probes), PYTHONHASHSEED, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF (not is_set probe), TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR/TRITON_CACHE_DIR pointing at /state mount (cache persistence across runs? Bind-mount host's $FN_STATE_DIR as /state for warm inductor/triton caches — nice for retry; mirrors fn-env). Don't pass NCCL variables (single-node; single GPU TP=1 — RCCL not needed). HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 like fn-env.\n\nContainer naming: flashnext-proxy-serve; `podman rm -f` pre-cleanup in case of leftover.\n\nTimeouts: FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT default 3600; poll /v1/models every 5s; also detect container death during poll (podman inspect state != running → fail early with log tail).\n\nvllm serve arguments:\n```\nvllm serve /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy \\\n  --served-model-name flashnext-proxy \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 1 \\\n  --max-model-len 4096 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.6 \\\n  --no-enable-prefix-caching? (leave default; PYTHONHASHSEED fixed anyway)\n  -cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE\n```\nNote on \"--enforce-eager\" absence: comment explains ruling P10 + guard.\n\nHmm — one worry: `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` CLI syntax: does the vLLM CLI accept dotted shorthand `-cc.cudagraph_mode=...`? For `--compilation-config` / `-cc`, vLLM's FlexibleArgumentParser supports dotted sub-args like `-cc.cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE` (yes — used widely, e.g. `-cc.mode=...`; guard message itself suggests it). ✓.\n\nAlso `--max-num-batched-tokens` default fine. `--load-format auto`.\n\nNow writing tests/test_proxy_tooling.py. Style of existing tests: unittest, ROOT-relative. Additions:\n\n```python\nclass ProxyTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_scripts_exist_and_syntax_clean(self): bash -n on both\n    def test_run_proxy_references_receipt(self): \"results/receipts/proxy.json\" in text\n    def test_mmap_engaged_and_piecewise(self): contains VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1; contains cudagraph_mode=PIECEWISE; does NOT contain --enforce-eager (ruling P10: fork guard rejects eager under VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1)\n    def test_no_is_set_probe_exports(self): F.9 — VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER absent from both scripts\n    def test_make_proxy_targets_and_shape(self): /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy present; weight_block_size present with 128; \"ngram_embedding.shard_\" naming present; architectures id Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration present\n```\nWait: \"--enforce-eager\" string must not appear in run-proxy.sh — my script explains in comments that eager is rejected. Would a comment mentioning `--enforce-eager` fail my own test? Have the test assert the flag isn't passed to the serve command: simpler — assert absence of \"--enforce-eager\" entirely and write the script's comment without the literal flag string (say \"the fork's check_cudagraph_safety guard rejects plain-eager mode\"). Fine — avoid the literal in comments.\n\nNow, is adding tests wise for the acceptance? Only acceptance is bash -n + grep. Tests reinforce the ladder (gate repo-tests runs the suite). Risk: my tests fail on judge machine (e.g. string I assert absent). Keep assertions conservative and self-consistent with files I write. OK.\n\nLet me also verify specs/flashnext/trace.json — mentioned in test_repo_invariants (test_spec_artifacts_exist expects trace.json + contracts/trace.schema.json). Earlier `ls specs`? ls showed specs dir exists with flashnext. Whatever — existing tests probably pass.\n\nTime to write the scripts. Keep style consistent with run-smoke.sh (bash -euo pipefail, REPO_ROOT, IMAGE, podman run with devices, python heredoc, receipt JSON, final status exit).\n\nDraft make-proxy.sh:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/make-proxy.sh — the proxy checkpoint builder (spec claim 4.1 prep).\n# ... header comment\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=...\nIMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nPROXY_DIR=\"${FN_PROXY_DIR:-${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}/proxy-checkpoint}\"\nMODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nLAYERS=\"${FN_PROXY_LAYERS:-8}\"\nEXPERTS=\"${FN_PROXY_EXPERTS:-8}\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$PROXY_DIR\"\nrm -f \"$PROXY_DIR\"/*.safetensors \"$PROXY_DIR\"/config.json ... (rebuild clean)\n\nmounts=(-v \"$PROXY_DIR:/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\nif [ -f \"$MODEL_DIR/config.json\" ]; then mounts+=(-v \"$MODEL_DIR:/models:ro\"); fi\n\npodman run --rm \"${mounts[@]}\" \\\n  -e FN_PROXY_LAYERS=\"$LAYERS\" -e FN_PROXY_EXPERTS=\"$EXPERTS\" \\\n  \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'\n... big python ...\nPY\necho \"make-proxy: checkpoint ready at $PROXY_DIR\"\n```\n\nPython generator — let me write it carefully.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Build the proxy checkpoint at /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy.\n\nContract (spec R4 claim 4.1, vocabulary \"proxy checkpoint\"):\nsame architecture identifier and expert format as the workload — a handful of\nlayers, 8 routed experts in block-FP8 [128,128], a small PLE/engram lookup\ntable under the exact shard naming the mmap path's discover_shards expects,\nand the workload's tokenizer so the server can tokenize without a hub round\ntrip (spec F.6).\n\nFork-scaffold reuse, in priority order:\n 1. If /models/config.json (the staged workload config) is mounted, shrink\n    it — every config field the model class reads stays exactly what the\n    fork's config class expects.\n 2. The PLE table layout is validated against the fork's own mmap module\n    (vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap: _SHARD_RE, discover_shards),\n    and the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp scaffolding is probed for a\n    shard-writer to reuse before falling back to the writer below.\n\"\"\"\nimport json, math, os, re, struct, sys, time, importlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\nOUT = Path(\"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\nMODELS = Path(\"/models\")\nLAYERS = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_LAYERS\", \"8\"))\nEXPERTS = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_PROXY_EXPERTS\", \"8\"))\nSEED = 42\n```\n\nConfig construction:\n\n```python\ndef base_config():\n    p = MODELS / \"config.json\"\n    if p.is_file():\n        cfg = json.loads(p.read_text())\n        return cfg, \"workload:%s\" % p\n    # Typed fallback from specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §3.3\n    cfg = {...}\n    return cfg, \"template:dotfiles-observed-3.3\"\n```\n\nTemplate (only if staged config absent):\n```python\n{\n \"architectures\": [\"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\"],\n \"model_type\": \"qwen4_exp\",\n \"torch_dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n \"hidden_size\": 2560,\n \"head_dim\": 256,\n \"num_attention_heads\": 16,\n \"num_key_value_heads\": 2,\n \"num_hidden_layers\": 8,\n \"full_attention_interval\": 4,\n \"vocab_size\": 32768,\n \"max_position_embeddings\": 4096,\n \"rms_norm_eps\": 1e-6,\n \"tie_word_embeddings\": False,\n \"use_qk_norm\": True,\n \"num_experts\": 8,\n \"num_experts_per_tok\": 2,\n \"moe_intermediate_size\": 256,\n \"shared_expert_intermediate_size\": 256,\n \"decoder_sparse_step\": 1,\n \"ngram_vocab_size_base\": 512,\n \"split_ngram_parts\": 2,  # guess only; dropped by shrink if the field is unknown to the fork config class?\n}\n```\nHmm — unknown extra keys in config.json are generally tolerated by HF config classes (ignored). But the template is a last resort anyway (real machine has staged config — staging precedes proxy in S2 ordering; and if missing, loud failure acceptable? Task says proxy before real weights... but tokenizer also needs staged dir. If staged dir absent entirely, proxy can't serve (no tokenizer) → fail loudly with typed message. Hmm — unless I synthesize tokenizer too. Can I? vLLM needs tokenizer_config.json + tokenizer.json (fast tokenizer) — can I write a minimal byte-level BPE tokenizer.json?? Handcrafting a valid HF tokenizer.json for a tiny vocab is possible but fabricating tokenizer identity... For the proxy, a tiny deterministic tokenizer is actually legitimate: the proxy checkpoint is synthetic anyway! But writing a correct tokenizer.json (with model vocab, merges for BPE) by hand — feasible: simplest valid tokenizer: a \"WordLevel\" or byte-fallback BPE with ASCII chars + a few words. vLLM uses it via transformers AutoTokenizer; WordLevel with unk is tolerated. Risk: chat template absence → /v1/completions (not chat) fine.\n\nDecision: tokenizer strategy: (1) copy from staged workload if present (preferred — real tokenizer guarantees alignment with nothing in particular; fine since my vocab_size may differ... wait. If I copy the real tokenizer (vocab 151936) but config vocab_size=32768 → token ids ≥32768 get embedded OOB → gather runtime error. The real model pads vocab; vLLM embeds num_embeddings=padded vocab; id ≥ vocab → index error on GPU (assertion). Prompt \"The pair...\" in qwen tokenizer → likely low ids (<32k), but no guarantee, and the generated tokens are sampled over the full 32k logit range (ids <32k) fine, but decode is... decode is OK. Prefill risk: if the prompt contains an id ≥32768 → crash. Mitigation: keep vocab_size = workload's vocab_size (don't shrink)! embed_tokens is V×H bf16 = 151936×2560×2 = 778 MB — a bit large, but fine on a 128 GB box (\"tiny\" is relative; the alternative is fabricating a tokenizer to match the shrunk vocab).\n\nHmm, 778 MB embed + 778 MB lm_head ≈ 1.6 GB. Tolerable? Removes both OOB risk and tokenizer-fabrication risk. Yes: keep workload's vocab_size, copy tokenizer. In template fallback (no staged dir), vocab 4096 + my own minimal WordLevel tokenizer. That's a consistent pairing.\n\nWait — but vLLM pads vocab to multiple of 64 etc.: padded_vocab_size for embed — for PLE's MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size...) — no wait, that's the layer's padded ngram vocab, separate thing.\n\nNow — ngram/PLE geometry. With real config: shrink fields containing \"ngram\" that relate to size. Field names unknown. Detect: keys matching r\"ngram\" (any case). For each, if value is a large int (>4096) and key contains \"size\" or \"base\" or \"vocab\" → set to 512. Keys matching ngram+split → 2. Keys for heads/dim/divisor → leave. Log all adjustments to manifest. Then compute expected shard count via best-effort mirror:\n\n```python\norg = ngram_vocab_size_base (maybe × heads_per_ngram?) \n```\nI don't really know if heads multiply into row count... MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, head_dim): rows = padded_vocab_size. padded = make_divisible(org_total?), where org_total might = base × heads? 51.2B params / row 256 B = 200M rows. If heads_per_ngram=8, base=25M → 200M. Plausible! So rows ≈ base × heads, then padded to divisor.\n\nAdaptive safeguard: after writing config.json, before writing shards, resolve rows via fork introspection:\n\n```python\ndef expected_rows_and_split(hf_cfg, n_layers):\n    # Best-effort mirror of amd/ple_layer.py:140-195 (field names per\n    # specs/flashnext/evidence/moe-dispatch.md §5); anything the fork's\n    # own validate_shards_for later rejects is caught at validate time.\n    base = getattr(hf_cfg, \"ngram_vocab_size_base\", 512)\n    heads = getattr(hf_cfg, \"heads_per_ngram\", 1)\n    div = getattr(hf_cfg, \"make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by\", 1)\n    split = getattr(hf_cfg, \"split_ngram_parts\", 512)\n    total = base * heads\n    padded = ((total + div - 1) // div) * div if div else total\n    ...\n```\nThen write `split` shards per layer... but if \"split_ngram_parts\" isn't a config field and is hardcoded 512 in code, my getattr default of 512 kicks in: shards=512, rows=ceil(padded/512) per shard. If padded=512 → 1 row × 512 shards × 8 layers = 4096 files. OK tolerable. Then validate via ple_mmap.discover_shards + optional MmapPleTable construction (wrap in try, non-fatal warning if introspection fails, fatal if discover_shards finds nothing).\n\nActually, stronger validation: call `ple_mmap._validate_layer_shards` / `validate_shards_for`? Signature: validate_shards_for(model_config, layer_name, head_dim) — needs vllm ModelConfig object. Construct ModelConfig(model=str(OUT)) — ModelConfig init loads tokenizer via get_config... tokenizer present ✓, reads config ✓, mostly CPU-safe (may probe platform for dtype? just config parsing — fine). Then validate_shards_for(mc, f\"model.layers.{i}.ple.ple_embedding\", head_dim)? layer_name format is uncertain. Wrap in try; treat exceptions as warnings recorded in manifest (not fatal — the real gate is run-proxy's serve). Hmm, but if geometry is wrong, run-proxy fails after ~20 min compile... make the discover_shards check fatal (it's pure and documented: returns per_layer[layer][shard] = (path, offset, rows)). Compare discovered per-layer shard count to expected `split` count → mismatch = fatal with typed message. That's solid: discover only depends on names/headers.\n\nAnd row counts: _attach_table maps checkpoint_start = shard_index*shard_size... if my rows per shard differ from expected shard_size, attach fails at serve. discover returns rows per shard — can I cross-check rows against expected shard_size computed via my mirror: if mismatch → fatal typed. Residual risk: mirror formula wrong. Accept it; manifest records the formula inputs for morning diagnosis. Hmm, alternatively make rows per shard generous: rows = shard_size_expected... same thing. Accept residual risk, loud diagnostics.\n\nPLE writer via fork test scaffolding probe (reuse before inventing):\n\n```python\ndef try_fork_scaffold(out_dir, n_layers, ...):\n    sys.path.insert(0, \"/opt/vllm/tests\")\n    for modname in (\"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap\", \"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd\", \"models.qwen4_exp.test_ple\"):\n        try: mod = importlib.import_module(modname)\n        except Exception: continue\n        for name in dir(mod):\n            fn = getattr(mod, name)\n            if callable(fn) and re.search(r\"(write|build|make|create).*(shard|ckpt|checkpoint|fixture)\", name, re.I):\n                try: fn(out_dir, ...)  # signature unknown — introspect parameters\n                ...\n```\nToo fragile to blindly call with wrong args. Compromise: probe, but only use a scaffold function if it has clear signature (single parameter named path/dir/out). Honestly, the most reliable \"reuse\" is import-based validation (discover_shards/_SHARD_RE) + matching my file naming to the fork's fixture convention (`model-ple-{layer}-{shard:05d}.safetensors`). Probe broadly but guard fully; record in manifest what was probed/used. This satisfies the \"whenever possible\" clause honestly: possibility is determined at runtime on the actual machine.\n\nThen weight writer. Minimal safetensors serializer:\n\n```python\n_DT = {\"F32\": (\"<f4\", 4), \"BF16\": (None, 2), \"F8_E4M3\": (None, 1)}\ndef save_safetensors(path, tensors):  # tensors: {name: (dtype_str, shape, bytes)}\n    header = {}\n    offset = 0\n    blobs = []\n    for name, (dtype, shape, raw) in tensors.items():\n        header[name] = {\"dtype\": dtype, \"shape\": shape, \"data_offsets\": [offset, offset+len(raw)]}\n        offset += len(raw); blobs.append(raw)\n    hjson = json.dumps(header).encode()\n    pad = (8 - len(hjson) % 8) % 8\n    with open(path, \"wb\") as f:\n        f.write(struct.pack(\"<Q\", len(hjson)+pad)); f.write(hjson + b\" \"*pad)\n        for b in blobs: f.write(b)\n```\n\nTensor builders (numpy → bytes):\n- bf16 via torch.tensor(...).to(torch.bfloat16).numpy()? bf16 numpy unsupported → torch tensor → tensor.view(torch.uint16)? bytes via tensor.contiguous().view(torch.int16).numpy().tobytes()? bf16→uint16 view via .view(torch.int16). Use torch and .numpy() on int16 view. Fine.\n- fp8: values are small; encode via torch float → clamp to e4m3 range → to(torch.float8_e4m3fn) → view as uint8 → bytes.\n\nValue policy: deterministic, numerically safe:\n- Attention q/k/v/o fp8: zeros (scale 1.0) → uniform attention → finite. Hmm all-zero K → softmax(0)=uniform → attn out = mean(V)=0. Fine.\n- Expert fp8: zeros. gate router bf16 zeros → uniform routing.\n- shared expert zeros.\n- Dense mlp (if any layer is dense): zeros.\n- embed_tokens bf16: seeded normal × 0.02.\n- lm_head bf16: copy of embed (logits = hidden·embedᵀ — finite).\n- Norms: ones.\n- PLE table fp8: zeros (scale 1.0) → PLE contribution zero.\n- weight_scale tensors: fp32 1.0.\n- q_norm/k_norm: ones.\n- Rotary? inv_freq typically computed inside the model (not a checkpoint tensor). Skip.\n- Linear-attention layers: skip all tensors (left at vLLM init — finite). Hmm — vLLM init for fp8? The linear-attn modules' Linears would be quantized by Fp8Config → create_weights allocates fp8 tensors... uninitialized fp8 memory could contain NaN bit patterns (e4m3: 0x7F/0xFF are NaN; S.1111.111 = NaN). torch.empty of fp8 — contents are whatever malloc gives → NaN risk! Ugh!! Does vLLM's create_weights initialize? Fp8LinearMethodBase.create_weights: `torch.empty(..., dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn)` — no explicit init... then `set_weight_attrs`. If the checkpoint doesn't provide it, whatever bits are there remain → NaN logits risk.\n\nSo I must provide fp8 weights for every quantized Linear in the model, including linear-attention's (in_proj_qkvz etc. — unknown names!). Can't name them → NaN risk → \"finite logits\" assertion could fail overnight.\n\nOptions:\n(a) Don't quantize linears outside experts: