is wrong shape → weight_loader size mismatch error at load. Bug! Shard sizes aren't uniform for qkv. Fix: compute per-shard row counts from the config: for stacked entries, use the mapping of shard_id to dimensions if identifiable: typical shard_ids are \"q\",\"k\",\"v\" or 0,1,2 for qkv; \"gate\",\"up\"/0,1 for gate_up. Implementation: if vllm_sfx contains \"qkv\": rows for hf_sfx containing q → NH*HD; k or v → NKV*HD. Else if \"gate_up\" or 2 entries: half-half (equal in MoE/dense MLP since gate and up are both M... for shared/dense mlp, gate=up=M ✓ equal). Fallback is uniform split.\n\n   But caveat: what if qkv fusion uses packed q/kv or includes q_norm? For now let's implement:\n\n```python\ndef shard_rows(vllm_sfx, hf_sfx, n, total, cfg):\n    HD = int(cfg.get(\"head_dim\", 256)); NH=...; NKV=...\n    if \"qkv\" in vllm_sfx:\n        s = hf_sfx.lower()\n        if s.endswith(\"q_proj\") or s.endswith(\".q\"): return NH*HD\n        if s.endswith(\"k_proj\") or s.endswith(\".v_proj\"): return NKV*HD\n    return total // n\n```\nAnd verify sum == total; else fall back uniform.\n\nAlso fused MoE experts: in vLLM, experts are batched tensors w13_weight with shape (E, 2M, H) — named_parameters name is \"model.layers.X.mlp.experts.w13_weight\" (no per-expert index!). stacked_params_mapping doesn't cover experts; FusedMoE uses a specialized weight_loader that handles names like \"mlp.experts.{e}.gate_proj\" in checkpoint. In my introspection branch, name = \"...mlp.experts.w13_weight\", shape (E, 2M, H): my code: len(shape)==3 → falls into `if len(shape) >= 1 and \"scale\" not in name: tensors[name] = _bf16(zeros)` — writes a bf16 (E,2M,H) under the vLLM fused name into the checkpoint. But the loader expects per-expert HF names, and checkpoint name \"...experts.w13_weight\" is unknown → AutoWeightsLoader error! Bad.\n\n   Fix: in the introspection branch, handle fused expert tensors specially: if name matches `mlp.experts.w13_weight` → write per-expert gate_proj/up_proj in fp8; `w2_weight` → per-expert down_proj. Plus scale_inv: does FusedMoE fp8 checkpoint have per-expert scales `experts.{e}.gate_proj.weight_scale_inv`? Or is it batched `w13_weight_scale_inv` (E, ...)? HF convention: per-expert `weight_scale_inv` per projection ✓. Shape (ceil(M/128), ceil(H/128)) each.\n\n   Also `w13_weight_scale_inv` parameter exists in vLLM (fp32, shape (E, M/128*...)). My generic branch writes that too as bf16 zeros under vLLM name → the loader would see checkpoint \"...w13_weight_scale_inv\"... does FusedMoE's weight_loader handle the batched scale name directly? Fp8 MoE checkpoint: some checkpoints save the fused w13_weight_scale_inv directly (vLLM-native format). FusedMoE weight_loader does handle names containing \"w13_weight_scale_inv\" (batched). Actually, vLLM's fused_moe loading supports both per-expert and batched names? Standard HF fp8 MoE checkpoints (DeepSeek) use per-expert names, and vLLM's FusedMoE weight_loader handles `experts.{expert_id}.{gate_proj|up_proj|down_proj}` mapping to w13/w2. Plus directly batched names like \"w13_weight\" if the checkpoint is in vLLM-native format? Hmm, FusedMoE.weight_loader handles names via `expert_data`... in newer vLLM, FusedMoE.weight_loader handles checkpoint names: if \"experts\" not in name → treat as full batched tensor? I think in modern vLLM there's a branch: `if \"experts\" in name:` per-expert logic; else load as batched. If so, writing fp8 zeros (E,2M,H) under w13_weight + scale_inv (E, 2*(M/128), H/128) under w13_weight_scale_inv is the cleanest — no name guessing, shapes are exact from introspection!\n\n   Let me do this: in introspection branch, for parameters under \".mlp.experts.\": if name endswith \".weight\" → fp8 zeros (any dim) + scale_inv of shape [E?] scale_inv_shape per last 2 dims with leading batch dims preserved: for w13_weight (E, 2M, H): scale_inv shape = (E, ceil(2M/128), ceil(H/128)) ✓ generalization: scale_inv = leading dims + block-ceil of last 2 dims. For scale parameters already in params list (\"w13_weight_scale_inv\" etc.) → fp32 ones of shape; skip the synthesized ones to avoid duplicates: check the name already in params list first.\n\n   Implementation: precompute the set of parameter names `param_names`. In loop: for name under \".mlp.experts.\" with name.endswith(\".weight\") and len(shape)>=2: write fp8 + if f\"{name[:-7]}.weight_scale_inv\" not in param_names: write scale_inv with batch-aware shape. For name ending in \".weight_scale_inv\" (present in params): write fp32 ones of shape.\n\n   Wait, but is w13_weight quantized fp8 in the model? Yes, if quant config fp8 → Fp8MoEMethod.create_weights makes fp8 params ✓.\n\n   Similarly batched scale for LINEAR introspection: Fp8LinearMethod block quant creates per-linear `weight_scale_inv` param (linear, 2D → standard scale_inv_shape is fine). For non-expert linears — if the ignore list regex is active (unquantized) → parameter is bf16, no scale param in named_parameters ✓ consistent. If ignore is inactive (quantize-all) → scale_inv parameter present in the params list → handled in generic \"scale\" branch? My generic branch: `if len(shape) >= 1 and \"scale\" not in name: tensors[name] = _bf16(zeros)` — skips scale params entirely (no write) → missing scale_inv → fine? Unprovided checkpoint params stay uninitialized fp32 — scale garbage → dequant garbage → possible NaN/Inf! Must write fp32 ones for scale params. Add: `if \"scale\" in name: tensors[name] = _f32(torch.ones(shape)); continue`.\n\n   Hmm wait, but for non-expert linear under quantize-all, weight is fp8: my branch only fp8s names containing \".mlp.experts.\" — others get bf16 zeros under the name, but the model param dtype is fp8 → dtype mismatch at copy → loader error. Add: in quantize-all mode (no ignore), all 2D \".weight\" params except embed/lm_head should be fp8+scale. Determining quantize-all inside the introspection branch: same predicate: ignore_active = bool(cfg.get(\"modules_to_not_convert\")).\n\n   Let me restructure the introspection write branch:\n\n```python\nignore_active = bool(cfg.get(\"modules_to_not_convert\"))\nparam_names = {n for n, _ in params}\ndef quant_target(name):  # name ends with .weight\n    if \"embed_tokens\" in name or \"lm_head\" in name: return False\n    if \".mlp.experts.\" in name: return True\n    return not ignore_active\nfor name, shape in params:\n    if name.endswith(\"weight_scale_inv\") or name.endswith(\"weight_scale\"):\n        tensors[name] = _f32(torch.ones(shape)); continue\n    if name.endswith(\".weight\"):\n        if quant_target(name) and len(shape) >= 2:\n            tensors[name] = _fp8(torch.zeros(shape))\n            sname = name[:-len(\".weight\")] + \".weight_scale_inv\"\n            if sname not in param_names and sname not in tensors:\n                tensors[sname] = _f32(torch.ones(batch_scale_shape(shape)))\n            continue\n        if \"embed_tokens\" in name:\n            tensors[name] = _bf16(torch.randn(*shape, generator=gen)*0.02); continue\n        if \"norm\" in name and len(shape) == 1:\n            tensors[name] = _bf16(torch.ones(shape[0])); continue\n        tensors[name] = _bf16(torch.zeros(shape)); continue\n    # other params (biases, A_log, dt_bias, conv1d weights etc.)\n    if len(shape) == 1 and (\"norm\" in name or \"bias\" not in name):\n        ... keep simple: fp32 ones? conv1d weight is 3D...\n    tensors[name] = _bf16(torch.zeros(shape))\n```\nHmm dtype for non-weight params: A_log is fp32?, dt_bias is fp32. Writing bf16 zeros where model expects fp32 → dtype mismatch error at copy. Safer: write in the parameter's declared dtype! On meta device p.dtype is known. Use per-param dtype: bf16 → _bf16, fp32 → _f32, fp8 → _fp8.\n\n```python\ndef emit(name, t_torch, dtype):\n    if dtype == torch.bfloat16: return _bf16(t_torch)\n    if dtype == torch.float32: return _f32(t_torch)\n    if dtype == torch.float8_e4m3fn: return _fp8(t_torch)\n    return _f32(t_torch.to(torch.float32))\n```\nTrack dtypes: params list should include dtype: `params = [(n, tuple(p.shape), p.dtype) ...]`.\n\nValue choice: norm → ones (dtype fp32/bf16 depending); embed → small randn (dtype); others zero (dtype). Quantization targets covered above. This is robust: names exact, shapes exact, dtypes exact; values finite by construction.\n\nAnd qkv fused split handling: with batch-aware scale and dtype-driven emit, fused qkv remains: the name is vLLM-fused (\"qkv_proj.weight\"), but checkpoint expects HF names q_proj/k_proj/v_proj... Unless the fork's load_weights accepts fused names directly (stacked_params_mapping exists because HF checkpoints use split names; writing checkpoint-side fused names → unknown name error!). So yes, split back. Only use config dims for per-shard rows as noted. Implement per-shard row calc for \"qkv\" and \"gate_up\" via config; else uniform. For robustness: compute expected total from per-shard row calc; if sum != shape[0], fall back to uniform split and note.\n\nHmm — but actually, do qwen3_next-family HF checkpoints use split q/k/v? For Qwen3-Next HF: `self_attn.q_proj/k_proj/v_proj`? Qwen3 HF models split q/k/v separately and vLLM fuses via stacked_params_mapping (that mapping in qwen3_next.py: (\"qkv_proj\", \".q_proj\", \"q\")... hmm the shard ids in qwen3_next are \"q\",\"k\",\"v\"? Typical entries: `(\"qkv_proj\", \".q_proj\", \"q\"), (\"qkv_proj\", \".k_proj\", \"k\"), (\"qkv_proj\", \".v_proj\", \"v\"), (\"gate_up_proj\", \".gate_proj\", 0), (\"gate_up_proj\", \".up_proj\", 1)` or similar. Use hf_sfx suffix for dim detection: contains \"q_proj\" → NH*HD; \"k_proj\"/\"v_proj\" → NKV*HD; gate_up: rows for gate = rows for up = shape[0]//2 ✓ (equal).\n\nAlso possibility of other stacked stuff (in_proj_qkvz for linear attention?). If unknown fused suffixes appear with unequal splits — uniform fallback + note. Accept.\n\n5. Curated fallback writer: `quantize_linears` is derived from modules_to_not_convert presence ✓ consistent with ignore probe. Shared experts also written ✓. Router gate bf16 ✓.\n\n   One concern about curated fallback under quantize-all (ignore probe fails): full-attention q/k/v/o fp8 ✓ written; but other quantized linears (linear-attn's unknown ones) missing → uninitialized fp8 → NaN risk — noted in docstring ✓ residual risk. Accept.\n\n6. `write_ple_shards` uses cfg[\"num_hidden_layers\"] ✓ int after shrink ✓. Scales go to a separate file \"model-ple-scales.safetensors\" — the loader will discover fine. Shard naming matches _SHARD_RE ✓ (has model. prefix; regex isn't anchored and matches \"layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_M.weight\" ✓).\n\n   Wait — actual checkpoint shards: does the model's load_weights route names \"model.layers.N.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_M.weight\"? _SHARD_RE requires exactly `layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$` ✓ matches.\n\n   In mmap mode the shard tensors are discarded by load_weights ✓; weight_scale is intercepted ✓ (name stripped to ngram_embedding.weight_scale by layer's load_weights ✓).\n\n   If VLLM_PLE_MMAP is off... run-proxy always runs with on ✓.\n\n7. make-proxy: `find \"$PROXY_DIR\" -mindepth 1 -delete` — deletes old receipts? PROXY_DIR is separate from results ✓.\n\n8. `podman run --rm --entrypoint cat \"$IMAGE\" /opt/flashnext/build","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"error","timestamp":1787993283970,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c82e492e-78f8-4a2c-949f-fb632dd2a623","errorMessage":"The operation timed out."}],"willRetry":true}
