{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Excellent — the image exists on this host (flashnext:dev is already built!), and the script actually launched a real container with the real fork engine! The serve failed with:\n\n`vllm: error: unrecognized arguments: --disable-log-requests`\n\nSo this vLLM version doesn't accept --disable-log-requests. What's interesting is that it did manage to get through platform detection (\"Failed to import amdsmi\" warning → the fallback of patch 0003's KFD topology detection works). Also, this machine apparently has no visible GPU? The container actually ran vllm and exited with code 2 due to argument parsing — a fast failure, good.\n\nWait — but hold on, did I just launch a container on a host that might not have a GPU? The serve died at the argument parsing stage before GPU initialization. No problem.\n\nFix: remove --disable-log-requests from run-proxy.sh (or use the new name --log-requests-level? In recent vLLM it's `--log-requests-level` or the default). Simplest: remove the flag entirely.\n\nBut wait — even better, this means we can actually test the real serve locally… but this machine (the agent host) — is this a coordinator with a GPU? The container got past argument parsing… but the amdsmi import failed; KFD topology fallback. If /dev/kfd exists on this host, we might be able to fully launch the proxy! This is actually the first-light machine (campaigns run here). Let's check: ls /dev/kfd.\n\nNote: the python heredoc receipt writer worked, the container was cleaned up, and the ERR trap bug: `KeyError: 'FN_LINE'` — was on_error called with $1 unset? trap 'on_error $LINENO' — in bash, LINENO inside trap is expanded at trap definition time… it should have a value. Error: FN_LINE KeyError — meaning `python3 -c` was executed in an environment without FN_LINE… I wrote `FN_TAIL=\"$tailtxt\" FN_LINE=\"$line\" write_receipt ...` — write_receipt is a function; when you put environment variable assignments in front of a function call, the variables get set in the shell's environment for that command… in bash, `VAR=x funcname` — if funcname is a shell function, the variable assignment gets applied in the function's scope, but is it exported? Function calls: prefixed variable assignments get set in the function's local scope, but they are not exported to the child process unless they were already exported! That's the bug: FN_LINE/FN_TAIL aren't exported → python3 -c can't see them. Note however that write_receipt itself does `FN_STATUS=\"$1\" FN_DATA=\"$2\" FN_OUT=\"$OUT\" python3` — those are prefixed directly onto the python3 command → exported ✓. Fix: use export inside on_error:\n\n```\nexport FN_TAIL=\"$tailtxt\" FN_LINE=\"$line\"\nwrite_receipt \"fail\" \"$(python3 -c '...')\"\n```\nFirst the substitution — python3 -c is executed after the export ✓.\n\nAlso note: in this failure, the python stage's receipt correctly wrote \"serve container died during boot\" + log tail, and the trap preserved it ✓ — the never-overwrite design worked exactly as intended.\n\nAlso: boot_seconds 25.2 — the polling loop detected the container's death quickly ✓ (died at argument parsing; no /v1/models).\n\nFixes to run-proxy.sh:\n1. Remove --disable-log-requests.\n2. Fix the FN_LINE/FN_TAIL export inside on_error.\n\nThen retest — since this host actually has an image and (probably) a GPU, we might get a real serve attempt. First check /dev/kfd."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"Two actual findings: this host **does indeed** have the `flashnext:dev` image — the runner actually launched the real engine — and (a) `--disable-log-requests` is not a valid argument in this vLLM version, (b) my trap isn't exporting `FN_LINE`/`FN_TAIL` (function-scope variables aren't exported). Let me check whether there's a GPU here, then fix both:\n\n"}}
