{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`podman run ... python3 -` returned immediately with success, and consumed nothing?? The heredoc `<<'PY'` feeds stdin, but... under `bash -x`... wait a moment — the heredoc: in my own script, the python heredoc follows `\"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'`. python3 - reads the program from stdin. If podman run doesn't attach stdin... podman run without -i may not forward stdin! The manual test that worked used `-c \"print(...)\"`. For a program on stdin, `-i` (interactive, keep stdin open) is needed. run-smoke.sh uses the same pattern `podman run --rm ... \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'`... does podman run forward stdin by default even without -i? Normally, when stdin is a pipe/heredoc, podman/docker attach stdin (if not a TTY, then `-i` implicit? no — docker run without -i doesn't attach stdin... actually, does docker run attach stdin when it's not a TTY? hmm: docker run -i keeps STDIN open even without tty assignment; without -i, stdin is closed... no wait: `echo foo | docker run --rm image cat` works without -i. docker run attaches stdin by default (the -i flag is about keeping it open \"even when not attached\"... actually -a stdin). For podman, `podman run` attaches stdin by default if it's a pipe.\n\nBut python3 exited immediately with code 0 and empty program? Running python3 with stdin closed → exits 0 without printing anything!! So podman didn't forward the heredoc. Hmm, but run-smoke.sh probably worked (a smoke receipt with data exists? results/receipts only has build.json + weight — smoke.json doesn't exist! so maybe run-smoke has never been executed either, or it hit the same problem... receipts only have build/weights — smoke has not been executed).\n\nLet's test: `podman run --rm flashnext:dev python3 - <<<'print(\"x\")'` and compare with the `-i` variant."}}
