{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"now the actual structure is coming into view:\n\n1. **The config is nested**: text_config holds all the fields; top-level is multimodal (vision_config + language_model). Module prefixes have `model.language_model.` on them (not `model.layers`). embed = `model.language_model.embed_tokens`, lm_head is at top level.\n\n2. **`ple_layer_ids = [2]`** — PLE only exists in layer 2!! Not in every layer. My assumption of \"per-layer PLE\" was wrong for this model. The proxy should respect ple_layer_ids: shrink to fewer layers → keep ple_layer_ids within range (e.g., 8 layers → [2] is still valid ✓).\n\n3. `split_ngram_parts = 128` exists as a config field ✓; `ngram_vocab_size_base = 20000000`, heads 8 → total 160M, divisor 128. Proxy: shrink base to 512 → total 4096, split 128 → shard_size 32, 128 shards for the single PLE layer.\n\n4. `modules_to_not_convert` = literal module names, 943 entries ('lm_head', 'model.language_model.embed_tokens', ...) — i.e., membership semantics by literal prefix. My probe said membership ✓ consistent.\n\n5. quantization_config needs activation_scheme (dynamic) + weight_per_tensor/act_per_tensor fields — merge instead of replace.\n\n6. Weight names carry the `model.language_model.` prefix; the multimodal architecture builds the vision tower too — shrink vision or drop it? Can I set `language_model_only: true`? There's a config flag \"language_model_only\": False — if I set it to true the vision tower won't be built! That shrinks things dramatically. But does vLLM respect this? Probably the model class checks this. If it exists, set language_model_only=true in the proxy. Hmm, risk: unknown semantics. In fact, the log showed \"Model ... is treated as multimodal but has no registered multimodal processor; running in text-only mode\" — vLLM is already demoting to text-only. Keep the vision config but it will still be built (vision tower weights will be needed!). If I set language_model_only=true maybe I can avoid the vision tower entirely. Try: set to true in the proxy; if the fork doesn't know that key… it's part of this model's config (a field of the model's own config class) ✓ safe to set.\n\n7. mtp: mtp_num_hidden_layers=1 with an mtp sub-config — the MTP draft module will also be built (Qwen4ExpMTP?) — ConditionalGeneration registry… the MTP module is a separate registry entry (Qwen4ExpMTP), normally only loaded when speculative decoding is configured. Probably won't be built ✓.\n\n8. My curated fallback writer's names are all wrong for this architecture (model.layers.* vs model.language_model.layers.*, plus hyperconnection mixers, linear-attention internals…). The curated fallback is inadequate against the real model — but the introspection path is the correct path here; the reason it failed was solely that VllmConfig validation was missing activation_scheme. Fix the quantization merge → introspection can construct the model → exact names from the fork itself. That's the design working as intended.\n\nAlso `hc_count=4, hyper_connection_mixer` — hyperconnection modules (the GatedResidual import error during registry resolution in introspection: duplicate op registration error in the log — a WARNING-level issue inside the registry; then ModelConfig was built fine).\n\nAlso the multimodal processor warning — fine.\n\nFixes to make-proxy.sh:\n\nA. shrink(): operate on text_config if present:\n```python\ntc = cfg.get(\"text_config\", cfg)   # nested multimodal config support\n```\nAll edits apply to tc; quantization_config is at top level (in the actual config it's at top level ✓). layer_types inside text_config. num_hidden_layers inside text_config. vocab_size in text_config (248320 — keep it! paired with tokenizer ✓). max_position_embeddings in text_config → shrink to 4096.\n\nPLE fields: ngram_vocab_size_base (shrink to 512), heads_per_ngram keep, make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by keep (128), split_ngram_parts exists → set to 2 (fewer shards: total 4096, split 2 → shard_size 2048, 2 shards × 1 PLE layer ✓ tiny).\n\nple_layer_ids: after shrinking num_hidden_layers to 8, filter to ids < layers; if empty after filtering → [layers-1]? PLE needs to exist for the mmap claim! Actual ple_layer_ids=[2] → fine for 8 layers. In template mode: add \"ple_layer_ids\": [2] to the template. write_ple_shards should only write shards for layers in ple_layer_ids (default: all layers if the field is absent).\n\nAlso weight_scale only for PLE layers.\n\nB. quantization_config: merge — start from existing qc or {}; set quant_method fp8, weight_block_size [128,128], activation_scheme \"dynamic\" (keep existing if present), keep weight_per_tensor/act_per_tensor as existing or False; remove modules_to_not_convert (will re-add later depending on probe). Also \"weight_per_tensor\": False, \"act_per_tensor\": False from the actual config.\n\nC. language_model_only: set cfg[\"language_model_only\"] = True if the key exists.\n\nD. write_ple_shards(cfg, geom) → use the ple layer list: ple_layers = tc.get(\"ple_layer_ids\") or range(L). row bytes: ple_embed_dim exists (2560!) and heads 8 → ngram_row_bytes = 8*2560 = 20480 bytes/row? Wait — is that really the row width?? ple_embed_dim = 2560, heads_per_ngram 8. But the actual nvidia code constructs MmapNgramEmbedding(padded_vocab_size, self.head_dim) — \"head_dim\" there is an attribute of the PLE layer, not the attention head_dim (256). PLE head_dim = heads_per_ngram × ple_embed_dim = 20480? The table is 51.2B params: rows 160M… wait, org=base×heads=160M rows × 20480 bytes = 3.2TB. That's wrong. Table is 51.2B params = 51.2GB fp8. 51.2e9 / 20480 = 2.5M rows. Hmm: padded_vocab = base/divisor… base=20M/div 128… rows=2.5M? 20M/8=2.5M = base/heads! So rows = base / heads? I.e., total rows = ngram_vocab_size_base / heads_per_ngram × ? 2.5M rows × 20480 B = 51.2 GB ✓✓. So org_vocab = base // heads?? Weird but matches: 20000000/8 = 2,500,000 rows, row_bytes = heads×ple_embed_dim = 8×2560 = 20480 → 51.2e9 params ✓ exact match to \"51.2-billion-parameter hash-lookup embedding\".\n\nSo org_rows = ngram_vocab_size_base // heads_per_ngram, then padded to make_ngram_vocab_size_divisible_by, row_bytes = heads × ple_embed_dim. Shard mapping: shard_size = ceil(org/split), shards = ceil(org/shard_size) = split (when divisible).\n\nFix to ngram_geometry: total = base // heads (if heads>1), else base*heads?? Hmm — which one? The 51.2B arithmetic nails it: rows = base/heads = 2.5M. Check divisibility: padded 2.5M is divisible by 128 ✓. So: rows = base // heads (when heads>1 and divisible). Encode that formula with a note; when base/heads isn't integral, fall back to base*heads… choice: if base % heads == 0 then total = base // heads, else base. Document the derivation in a comment (from the spec's 51.2B number).\n\nProxy numbers: base 512, heads 8 → rows 64, div 128 → padded 128; split 2 → shard_size 64, shards 2, row_bytes 20480 → table = 128 rows × 20480 = 2.6 MB ✓ small.\n\nHmm wait, let me double-check that padding is applied after the division: 2.5M is already divisible by 128. Proxy: 64 → pad to 128 ✓ shard rows 64 each.\n\nE. Curated fallback naming: for a nested config, prefixes are model.language_model.layers.* — my curated writer writes names in the model.layers.* form, which is wrong. But on this machine introspection should succeed and the fallback should remain unused. Still, cheaply improve the curated writer: derive the layer prefix from the ignore-list entries of the real config? Overkill. Instead: make the curated writer prefix-aware: prefix = \"model.language_model\" if cfg has text_config else \"model\". embed_tokens → f\"{prefix}.embed_tokens\". Also, the real model has hyperconnection modules, etc. — the curated fallback remains best-effort, the typed risks are documented. Keep it simple: implement prefix detection.\n\nF. Probe ignore semantics: observed membership. Then in membership mode, without introspection, we can't enumerate → quantize-all risk remains as documented. But introspection will likely succeed here. Also my regex probe: to be sure of the semantics, let me actually grep the fork's fp8.py — I'll verify quickly in the container.\n\nG. VllmConfig validation also needs activation_scheme in the quantization config → merge fix (A/B) covers this.\n\nH. try_fork_introspection: after construction, enumerate parameters — the model contains the language_model subtree + possibly vision (language_model_only=true strips vision). Also MTP? params might include mtp layers — the names will tell us.\n\nAdditionally, the duplicate op registration RuntimeError I saw earlier on import of amd/ops/hc.py during registry resolution — it appeared in the ERROR log but ModelConfig continued (registry resolution retried?). Occurred inside my introspection's ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls — did the import error out? After the \"Duplicate registration\" error, logs continued with WARNING about multimodal etc — that was my own introspection's ModelConfig construction (scheduler/config logs). resolve_model_cls hit the duplicate op at import time and… the reported introspection failure was a ValidationError from VllmConfig (activation_scheme) — so resolve_model_cls didn't even get there; the ERROR block came from ModelConfig init?? No — registry resolution happens inside ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls… order: ERROR from registry.py:1072, then WARNINGs from model.py/scheduler.py (ModelConfig). Hmm, actually maybe ModelConfig init resolves architectures for mm check → triggers the import → duplicate op error logged (was it swallowed?), then VllmConfig validation failed. Duplicate registration: ops/hc.py registers the op; something imported it twice (once via a previous import path during probe_fork_scaffolds? importing models.qwen4_exp.test_ple* module → importing vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.model → registering op; then resolve_model_cls's import path `from .amd.model import ...` via the package __getattr__ — same module object shouldn't be re-executed…). Duplicate at the same line 1038 → module was executed twice → two distinct module instances (e.g., `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ops.hc` and something aliased). Did my scaffold probe import `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` from the /opt/vllm/tests rootdir?? That FAILed (in this run ModuleNotFoundError: models.qwen4_exp not found — because tests/models has no __init__.py? in-container the import failed — good, so that wasn't the cause). The double import: registry resolves \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" → vllm.models.qwen4_exp __getattr__ → `from .amd.model import` … and earlier my try_fork_introspection's own ModelConfig init had already triggered the architecture resolution for the mm check (imported once)… the second import via a different module path (`vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.model` cached in sys.modules → no re-run). Hmm, direct_register_custom_op is supposed to be idempotent via a library check… direct_register_custom_op in vllm's torch_utils handles duplicates with `if my_lib is None`… whatever — this is fork-side behavior at serve time too. But serving goes through the same import once. In my introspection, the ERROR didn't raise (it was logged via the registry error handler, then… actually the registry error handler logs and re-raises). Did resolve raise? The exception caught was ValidationError — from the VllmConfig(...) call. So the registry ERROR lines came after resolve — meaning they were from ModelConfig?? No… order in stderr: ERROR block, then WARNING multimodal (ModelConfig), then my note about introspection failure (ValidationError). VllmConfig() runs after ModelConfig — resolve_model_cls is called inside my code after VllmConfig… my code order: mc = ModelConfig(...) → logs WARNING/INFO ✓; vc = VllmConfig(model_config=mc) → ValidationError raised ✓. So where did the registry ERROR come from? Probably from the probe_fork_scaffolds import of tests.models… (import failed). Or from ModelRegistry inside ModelConfig's mm check… anyway — not fatal to my flow; the serve has its own single import path.\n\nWait, actually — that ERROR block could affect the serve too if it's triggered by the same double-import path… serving is fresh single-process import → fine.\n\nSo let's apply the fixes. Restructure shrink to be text_config-aware, merge quantization, ple layer ids, geometry fix, template ple_layer_ids + activation_scheme, prefix-aware curated writer, write PLE only for ple layers."}}
