{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Excellent! `discover_shards` is actually finding layer 2 (keys: [2]) — the shard names are correct! So then, why did my validate_ple conclude that there were zero shards? Looking at the code... inside `validate_ple`:\n\n```python\nfound = ple_mmap.discover_shards(str(OUT))\nper_layer = ({int(k): v for k, v in found.items()} if isinstance(found, dict) else found)\n...\nif not per_layer: die(\"ZERO PLE shard tensors\")\n```\n\nHmm, this shouldn't be able to end up empty... unless discover_shards returned something different at build time — oh, wait. It's `@functools.cache`! `discover_shards` is cached — during make-proxy, before writing the shard files, I had called `probe_fork_scaffolds` → imported ple_mmap but didn't call discover... no. During the build: validate_ple is the first and only call to discover_shards → the cache isn't a problem. But: the reason discover during the build returned {} was... because at that point the checkpoint directory inside the container was /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy (bind mount ✓, same files). Hmm.\n\nOh — wait. I've got it! Inside main(), the order is: weights → write_ple_shards → validate_ple. But the FATAL was emitted at validate time, yet... let me re-read the actual failure: \"make-proxy: FATAL: discover_shards found ZERO PLE shard tensors\". During that run, did write_ple_shards run normally? The manifest... check the manifest contents in that directory. Hmm, wait — actually, look at the flow of the failed run: after \"fork model introspection unavailable\" came the curated fallback... curated_weights → save_safetensors of the main file → write_ple_shards → model-ple-2-* files ✓ (the files exist now!). After that, validate_ple → zero?! But now, discover is finding them. The difference: during the run, there may have been an exception during the writing of `write_ple_shards` (e.g., an _fp8 shim issue... no, real torch). Hmm, wait — actually, during the failed run, validate_ple ran against the checkpoint at the point when the shard files did exist. discover_shards globs and parses the header — should have found them.\n\nCould it be... `ple_mmap.discover_shards` was called earlier in the same process and cached with empty results? Where? `probe_fork_scaffolds` — the fork test probe! It imports tests.models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap etc. — the import failed (ModuleNotFoundError), but if some module that got imported called discover_shards against /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy before the shards existed → the functools.cache would forever retain the empty result! Which import would call it? test_ple_mmap's import fails. Hmm. Or... validate_shards_for? Not called.\n\nWait — actually, let me check the manifest from the failed run to confirm the order, and more simply, reproduce it: run discover twice in a fresh container process: once against an empty directory, then after adding files — to prove the cache's behavior:\n\nActually, the easiest fix is: in validate_ple, bypass the cache: call `ple_mmap.discover_shards.__wrapped__(str(OUT))` if available. This deterministically eliminates any cache pollution. And in the earlier failed run — hmm, what polluted it? Ah, wait. Got it! In the failed run, the curated fallback wrote weights... then `write_ple_shards(cfg, geom)` — with ple_layer_ids... but in the failed run, had the config.json in /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy been rewritten with ple_layer_ids? Yes (shrink sets [2]). Then discover in the validation...\n\nHmm, let me look at the failed run's manifest to see what happened.\n"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"`discover_shards` finds the shards when run fresh — so during the build something poisoned it (it's `@functools.cache`d). Check the failed run's manifest, and fix with cache-bypass:\n\n"}}
