{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Big discovery! This time the test module import succeeded (models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap — tests must be in a different location, or the import path was resolved…). And my \"callable writer\" regex matched a ton of TEST functions (`test_build_tables_*`) — my regex `(write|build|make|create).*(shard|table|...)` matched `test_build_tables_...`! One of them may have been called with fn(str(OUT)) — e.g., `test_build_tables_raises_when_a_ple_layer_has_no_shards_on_disk` — a pytest test function taking one argument… could it have run with a monkeypatch fixture?? With one positional arg it should fail… but some test taking a single `tmp_path`-like arg might have run and done something. More importantly: one of these test functions likely called discover_shards against the checkpoint directory (before the shard was written) → functools.cache got poisoned with an empty result → validate saw zero!\n\nAlso check the \"invoked\" entry — look at the entire scaffold block of the manifest. Fix for both problems:\n\n1. In probe_fork_scaffolds, don't call anything starting with `test_` — those are pytest tests, not fixture writers. Restrict to names not starting with \"test\" and not starting with \"test_\". The actual fixture writers inside the test file are typically private helpers (e.g., `_write_shard_file`) or pytest fixtures (FixtureFunctionMarker objects — not plain callables with a clean signature… in fact, pytest fixture functions are plain functions decorated with @pytest.fixture; if called directly they need fixture arguments). Realistically, no safe generic invocation exists → remove blind invocation entirely; limit to import-probing + recording candidates, plus reuse ple_mmap as the naming authority. That's the honest line: blind test invocation is dangerous (just polluted the cache!).\n\nActually — better targeted reuse: the fixture helper the dossier cited (test_ple_mmap.py:112-124 writes `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`) — probably a module-level helper function without the test_ prefix. If it exists, list it; only invoke non-test callables with a single path-like argument. Keep the invocation, but strictly exclude test_* names.\n\n2. validate_ple: bypass the cache with `getattr(ple_mmap.discover_shards, \"__wrapped__\", ple_mmap.discover_shards)`.\n\n3. Introspection API: `ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls(mc.architectures, mc)` — the signature is (architectures, model_config) → returns (cls, arch). Fix the call: `cls = ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls(mc.architectures, mc)[0]`.\n\nFurthermore, from the list of test names, I learned something about the semantics of build_tables (\"build_tables attaches a table per PLE layer\", \"skips layers without an mmap placeholder\") — consistent with ple_layer_ids.\n\nLet me check the entire scaffold invocation record to see which test got invoked.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"My scaffold probe called a pytest test function (matching `build_tables`) and contaminated the cached `discover_shards`. Three fixes: exclude `test_*` callables, bypass the cache during verification, and the correct registry signature:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0d74107d80d644168fad10ad","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python3 -m json.tool ~/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint/proxy-manifest.json | grep -B2 -A4 '\"invoked\"\\|_error' | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2023,"output":759,"cacheRead":176128,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":643,"totalTokens":178910,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787994762688,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0f283d74-21db-4749-a867-a3260d69398e","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0d74107d80d644168fad10ad","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"t