{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Two new problems:\n\n1. Introspection: model architecture inspection failed — the duplicate op registration RuntimeError during `resolve_model_cls` → import of amd.model fails due to double registration of hc ops. Why double registration in a fresh process? `direct_register_custom_op` uses `my_lib = ...` in torch_utils; the duplicate message \"registered at torch_utils.py:1038 / Original registration: torch_utils.py:1038\" — same location → hc.py module got executed twice under two different module names? This happens when both `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ops.hc` and some aliased path got imported. In my process: probe_fork_scaffolds imported `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` from the /opt/vllm/tests sys.path → the test module imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia...`? The 0009 test import update: test_ple_mmap imports the `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap` + model classes… test module imports `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.model`?? Then the registry's import inside try_fork_introspection also does `from .amd.model import ...` — same module, already in sys.modules → not executed twice. Hmm.\n\nBut the failure trace from the previous run showed: registry → qwen4_exp/__init__ __getattr__ → `from .amd.model import` → hyperconnection → ops.hc → direct_register → duplicate, with the original also at 1038. So ops.hc had already been imported+executed once before within the same process (original), and then got re-executed again (duplicate) → double import under different module names. The first execution: my probe's import of test_ple_mmap! The test file (after 0009/0010) imports amd modules… For example `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd import ple_layer` … this imports ops.qsa etc. Does ops/hc.py get imported? amd/model.py imports hyperconnection → ops.hc ✓. So test import → hc ops registered. Then resolve_model_cls → `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.model import ...` — module cached → no re-execution… unless it was imported under a different name. Who would re-execute hc.py? torch_utils's `direct_register_custom_op` creates a new Library per call via `register_custom_op`… \"Duplicate registration: registered at .../torch_utils.py:1038\" twice — both registrations via direct_register_custom_op → module code of ops/hc.py executed twice.\n\nAh — surely the test module imported via the sys.path alias does `import vllm...` normally; but perhaps the second import happens because resolve_model_cls's `_ModelRegistry.inspect_model_cls` uses importlib's import_module with a different spec against the \"vllm.models.qwen4_exp\" package? Both the same.\n\nWait — maybe simpler: my probe imports both `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap` (success) and `models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd` (fails at import with duplicate error as previously seen in manifest: test_ple_mmap_amd import fails with duplicate registration). The test_ple_mmap import registered ops once (via amd.model's import), then test_ple_mmap_amd's import triggered another import path of ops.hc (probably `from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ops import hc` vs `from .ops import hc` — same name…) hmm.\n\nSimplest robust fix: don't import both test modules within the same process as the registry resolution. Or: reorder — run introspection before the scaffold test probe (fresh imports). Introspection happens after the probe. Swap: try_fork_introspection first, then probe_fork_scaffolds. Introspection also imports the amd tree (registry resolution) — no problem, single import path. Then probe test modules — if they hit duplicates, their import fails harmlessly (recorded).\n\nBut further — this double-registration problem affects SERVE too, doesn't it? In serve, vLLM imports the model once via the registry path — single path → no problem. This is only an artifact of my double-window imports within the builder. Reordering should fix introspection.\n\nHmm, wait, actually there's a subtlety: the \"failed to be inspected\" error is registry.inspect's try/except; the registry logs the full traceback (ERROR lines) and raises ValueError. The duplicate error occurred during inspection in my process — because the probe had already imported test_ple_mmap (which imports vllm's amd tree), and then inspection imported it again… same module objects are cached — re-execution shouldn't happen… unless vllm's registry inspection uses `importlib.import_module` on a package with a lazy `__getattr__` — `from .amd.model import ...` — amd.model in sys.modules? If test_ple_mmap imported `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.model` directly, it's cached. Then inspection's `from .amd.model import` would reuse it. Hmm — unless test_ple_mmap doesn't import amd.model, and the first execution happens during inspection itself, and then… the ERROR shows both the duplicate and the original as the same file line — meaning two registrations in the same process: hc.py module executed twice → two different module names in sys.modules (e.g. `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ops.hc` and `vllm.models.qwen4_exp.amd.ops.hc` — same?).\n\nWhatever the mechanism (maybe the ops package's `__init__` does `from . import hc` style aliasing), operational fix: isolate the probe to a subprocess so its import mess can't poison introspection/serve. Run the scaffold probe as a separate `python3 -c`… that complicates the heredoc structure (single podman run). Alternative: probe first in a forked subprocess:\n\n```python\ndef probe_fork_scaffolds():\n    # ... ple_mmap import stays in-process ...\n    # test module probing runs in a subprocess: pytest-style imports can\n    # double-register torch custom ops and poison this process's registry\n    # resolution (observed: qwen4_exp_grouped_gemma_rmsnorm duplicate).\n    code = \"...\"  # import and emit JSON\n    out = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], capture_output=True, ...)\n```\nThe subprocess does the test-module probe + optional non-test writer invocation against OUT (bind-mounted, same path) and outputs JSON; parent parses into MANIFEST. Clean isolation. And run introspection after the probe, but since the probe is in a subprocess → no pollution.\n\n2. `discover_shards` returns a `_LayerShards` object per layer (no len). The structure is dict[layer] -> _LayerShards (has .items()?). Let me introspect _LayerShards quickly in the container. Adapt validate_ple: iterate values; count via dict semantics — inspect attributes. Let me check quickly."}}
