{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The fork's own discovery fully confirms the table I built:\n- `cols: 20480` = heads(8) × ple_embed_dim(2560) ✓ my ngram_row_bytes calculation is correct\n- dtype F8_E4M3 ✓\n- Per-layer scale entries are detected ✓\n- 2 shards, 64 rows each (128 rows total, split 2) ✓\n\nNext, updates:\n1. validate_ple for `_LayerShards`: use `ls.shards` dict, `ls.cols`, `ls.dtype_str`, `ls.scale_entry`. Strong contract assertions: cols == ngram_row_bytes(cfg), dtype == \"F8_E4M3\", scale_entry is not None, len(shards) == split, row count == rows_per_shard.\n\n2. Move the scaffolding test probe into a subprocess. Rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds: keep the in-process ple_mmap import; run the test-module probe as a subprocess python that outputs JSON. Probe code (run inside a subprocess with the same bind mounts):\n\n```python\nimport importlib, inspect, json, re, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, \"/opt/vllm/tests\")\nout = []\nfor modname in (...):\n    entry = {\"module\": modname}\n    try:\n        mod = importlib.import_module(modname)\n        entry[\"imported\"] = True\n        entry[\"test_contracts_present\"] = len([n for n in dir(mod) if n.startswith(\"test_\")])\n        writers = [n for n in dir(mod) if callable(getattr(mod, n, None)) and not n.startswith(\"test_\") and re.search(...)]\n        entry[\"callable_writers\"] = writers\n        for wname in writers:\n            fn = getattr(mod, wname)\n            try: sig = inspect.signature(fn)\n            except Exception: continue\n            params = list(sig.parameters.values())\n            if len(params) == 1 and params[0].default is params[0].empty:\n                try:\n                    fn(\"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\n                    entry[\"invoked\"] = wname\n                    break\n                except Exception as e3:\n                    entry[wname + \"_error\"] = e3.__class__.__name__\n    except Exception as e:\n        entry[\"import\"] = f\"FAIL {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n    out.append(entry)\nprint(json.dumps(out))\n```\nThe parent process parses the last line of stdout. The subprocess also needs to bypass the cache for discover... no wait, the parent process's validation bypasses the cache ✓. But could the subprocess's writer call contaminate the cache inside the subprocess? The subprocess exits; irrelevant.\n\nNote: it's possible the writer call in the subprocess writes partial garbage to OUT — after probe (in either process order), the parent's own writer always rebuilds... but does the probe run after the parent writes the file? Order within the parent main(): probe → geometry → introspection → weights → ple shards → validation. Subprocess writer call might write files under OUT before the parent's writer — the parent's find-delete happens on the host side before podman starts; within the same container execution, the subprocess's probe writer might add files that the parent doesn't overwrite (e.g., a different shard file name) → garbage shards remaining → discover finds extra layers?? If a non-test writer writes something, subsequent validation compares against expected values — extra layers in per_layer will show up in entry counts and could cause die()... acceptable: record it, and if validation fails, it fails loudly. To be safer: run the probe subprocess before the parent writes anything, then the parent overwrites files with the same name; stray files from the probe remain → validation catches it. No problem — loud. \n\n3. Introspection after subprocess probe → no contamination within the process. Also, in-process import of ple_mmap: probe_fork_scaffolds imports vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap — does this import pull in the amd model/ops? Importing common.ple_mmap: does it import the model tree? Imports vllm.platforms etc. Probably safe (previously succeeded without op duplication... hmm, did it? In the failed run the duplicate error appeared during the registry's INSPECTION — first occurrence of ops.hc execution: the successful test_ple_mmap import (in-process!) imported amd.model → ops.hc got registered. Next, the second import attempt via test_ple_mmap_amd → FAILS with duplication — meaning ops.hc re-executes during test_ple_mmap_amd import. So the two test modules are importing hc under different module names?? Whatever — resolved with subprocess isolation.)\n\nHowever: the in-process common.ple_mmap import — does it trigger registration of ops.hc? Previous standalone discovery run: no ERROR ✓ safe.\n\nAlso, does in-process introspection still work after the in-process ple_mmap import? Registry resolution imports the amd tree — the ops get registered once within the process ✓ no problem.\n\nApplying the edits: rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds entirely + validate_ple for _LayerShards. Need `import subprocess` inside the make-proxy python (add the import).\n"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"The fork's discovery fully verifies the table contract (cols 20480 ✓, F8_E4M3 ✓, scales found ✓). Currently rewriting the scaffold probe in the subprocess and the validation of `_LayerShards`:\n\n"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":2,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bf199116ea5b48e78e945f8a","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && python3 - <<'EOF'\np = 'scripts/make-proxy.sh'\nsrc = open(p).read()\n\ndef rep(old, new, label):\n    global src\n    assert old in src and src.count(old) == 1, f\"BAD: {label}\"\n    src = src.replace(old, new)\n    print(\"ok:\", label)\n\nrep(\"import torch\\n\", \"import subprocess\\nimport torch\\n\", \"subprocess import\")\n\n# --- rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds: subprocess-isolated test probe -------------\nstart = src.index('def probe_fork_scaffolds():')\nend = src.index('def text_cfg(cfg):')\nnew_probe = '''def probe_fork_scaffolds():\n    \"\"\"Probe the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp tree for reusable writers and\n    import its ple_mmap module as the naming authority (_SHARD_RE). The test\n    modules are probed in a SUBPROCESS: importing them in-process can double-\n    register the fork's torch custom ops and poison this process's registry\n    resolution (observed in review: qwen4_exp_grouped_gemma_rmsnorm).\"\"\"\n    ple_mmap = None\n    try:\n        from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common import ple_mmap as _pm\n        ple_mmap = _pm\n        MANIFEST[\"scaffold\"][\"reused\"].append(\n            \"vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap (naming authority)\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        try:\n            from vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia import ple_mmap as _pm\n            ple_mmap = _pm\n            MANIFEST[\"scaffold\"][\"reused\"].append(\n                \"vllm.models.qwen4_exp.nvidia.ple_mmap (pre-0009 location)\")\n        except Exception as e2:\n            note(f\"ple_mmap not importable ({e!r}, {e2!r}); the serve itself \"\n                 \"remains the naming authority\")\n    code = (\n        \"import importlib, inspect, json, re, sys\\\\n\"\n        \"sys.path.insert(0, '/opt/vllm/tests')\\\\n\"\n        \"out = []\\\\n\"\n        \"for modname in ('models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap',\"\n        \" 'models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap_amd',\"\n        \" 'models.qwen4_exp.test_ple'):\\\\n\"\n        \"    entry = {'module': modname}\\\\n\"\n        \"    try:\\\\n\"\n        \"        mod = importlib.import_module(modname)\\\\n\"\n        \"        entry['test_contracts_present'] = len(\"\n        \"[n for n in dir(mod) if n.startswith('test_')])\\\\n\"\n        \"        writers = [n for n in dir(mod)\\\\n\"\n        \"                   if callable(getattr(mod, n, None))\\\\n\"\n        \"                   and not n.startswith('test_')\\\\n\"\n        \"                   and re.search(r'(write|build|make|create).*'\"\n        \" r'(shard|table|ckpt|checkpoint|fixture)', n, re.I)]\\\\n\"\n        \"        entry['callable_writers'] = writers\\\\n\"\n        \"        for wname in writers:\\\\n\"\n        \"            fn = getattr(mod, wname)\\\\n\"\n        \"            try:\\\\n\"\n        \"                sig = inspect.signature(fn)\\\\n\"\n        \"            except Exception:\\\\n\"\n        \"                continue\\\\n\"\n        \"            params = list(sig.parameters.values())\\\\n\"\n        \"            if len(params) == 1 and params[0].default is params[0].empty:\\\\n\"\n        \"                try:\\\\n\"\n        \"                    fn('/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy')\\\\n\"\n        \"                    entry['invoked'] = wname\\\\n\"\n        \"                    break\\\\n\"\n        \"                except Exception as e3:\\\\n\"\n        \"                    entry[wname + '_error'] = e3.__class__.__name__\\\\n\"\n        \"    except Exception as e:\\\\n\"\n        \"        entry['import'] = f'FAIL {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}'\\\\n\"\n        \"    out.append(entry)\\\\n\"\n        \"print(json.dumps(out))\\\\n\"\n    ).replace(\"\\\\\\\\n\", \"\\\\n\")\n    try:\n        proc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code],\n                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=600)\n        last = (proc.stdout.strip().splitlines() or [\"[]\"])[-1]\n        MANIFEST[\"scaffold\"][\"fork_modules_probed\"] = json.loads(last)\n    except Exception as e:\n        note(f\"fork test-scaffold probe unavailable: {e!r}\")\n    return ple_mmap\n\n\n'''\nsrc = src[:start] + new_probe + src[end:]\nprint(\"ok: probe rewritten\")\n\n# --- validate_ple: _LayerShards contract -------------------------------------\nvstart = src.index('def validate_ple(ple_mmap, geom):')\nvend = src.index('# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# main')\nnew_validate = '''def validate_ple(ple_mmap, geom, cfg):\n    \"\"\"The fork's own discover_shards is the contract: per PLE layer it must\n    find exactly the expected shards, at the expected row width and dtype,\n    with the scale tensor alongside. A mismatch here would raise inside the\n    serve after a long compile — catch it in seconds instead.\"\"\"\n    if ple_mmap is None:\n        note(\"skipping discover_shards validation (module not importable)\")\n        return\n    # discover_shards is @functools.cache'd; bypass any cache a scaffold\n    # probe may have populated against this directory.\n    disc = getattr(ple_mmap.discover_shards, \"__wrapped__\",\n                   ple_mmap.discover_shards)\n    found = disc(str(OUT))\n    per_layer = dict(found) if not isinstance(found, dict) else found\n    entry = {}\n    expected_split = geom[\"split\"]\n    expected_cols = ngram_row_bytes(cfg)\n    ple_layers = text_cfg(cfg).get(\"ple_layer_ids\")\n    bad = {}\n    for layer, ls in per_layer.items():\n        shards = getattr(ls, \"shards\", ls)\n        info = {\"shards\": len(shards), \"cols\": getattr(ls, \"cols\", None),\n                \"dtype\": getattr(ls, \"dtype_str\", None),\n                \"scale\": getattr(ls, \"scale_entry\", None) is not None}\n        entry[str(layer)] = info\n        if (len(shards) != expected_split\n                or info[\"cols\"] not in (None, expected_cols)\n                or info[\"dtype\"] not in (None, \"F8_E4M3\")\n                or not info[\"scale\"]):\n            bad[int(layer)] = info\n    MANIFEST[\"scaffold\"][\"validation\"].append({\n        \"per_layer\": entry,\n        \"expected\": {\"shards_per_layer\": expected_split,\n                     \"cols\": expected_cols, \"dtype\": \"F8_E4M3\"},\n    })\n    if not per_layer:\n        die(\"discover_shards found ZERO PLE shard tensors — the shard naming \"\n            \"does not match the fork's _SHARD_RE; refusing to proceed\")\n    if ple_layers is not None and sorted(per_layer) != sorted(ple_layers):\n        die(f\"discover_shards found layers {sorted(per_layer)} but the config \"\n            f\"declares ple_layer_ids {sorted(ple_layers)}\")\n    if bad:\n        die(f\"discover_shards contract violated on layers {bad} (expected \"\n            f\"{expected_split} shards of {expected_cols} F8_E4M3 cols with a \"\n            \"scale) — the mmap path would refuse this table; refusing to \"\n            \"hand run-proxy.sh a doomed checkpoint\")\n    rows = next(iter(getattr(next(iter(per_layer.values())), \"shards\",\n                             next(iter(per_layer.values()))).values()))\n    rows = rows[2] if isinstance(rows, tuple) and len(rows) > 2 else None\n    if rows is not None and rows != geom[\"rows_per_shard\"]:\n        note(f\"discover_shards reports {rows} rows/shard vs the mirror's \"\n             f\"{geom['rows_per_shard']} — the serve is the tiebreaker\")\n\n\n'''\nsrc = src[:vstart] + new_validate + src[vend:]\nprint(\"ok: validate rewritten\")\n\nrep(\"    validate_ple(ple_mmap, geom)\\n\", \"    validate_ple(ple_mmap, geom, cfg)\\n\", \"validate call\")\n\nopen(p, 'w').write(src)\nprint(\"DONE\")\nEOF\nbash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh && awk '/<<.PY.$/{f=1;next} /^PY$/{f=0} f' scripts/make-proxy.sh > /tmp/mp.py && python3 -m py_compile /tmp/mp.py && echo OK"}}}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The fork's own discovery fully confirms the table I built:\n- `cols: 20480` = heads(8) × ple_embed_dim(2560) ✓ my ngram_row_bytes calculation is correct\n- dtype F8_E4M3 ✓\n- Per-layer scale entries are detected ✓\n- 2 shards, 64 rows each (128 rows total, split 2) ✓\n\nNext, updates:\n1. validate_ple for `_LayerShards`: use `ls.shards` dict, `ls.cols`, `ls.dtype_str`, `ls.scale_entry`. Strong contract assertions: cols == ngram_row_bytes(cfg), dtype == \"F8_E4M3\", scale_entry is not None, len(shards) == split, row count == rows_per_shard.\n\n2. Move the scaffolding test probe into a subprocess. Rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds: keep the in-process ple_mmap import; run the test-module probe as a subprocess python that outputs JSON. Probe code (run inside a subprocess with the same bind mounts):\n\n```python\nimport importlib, inspect, json, re, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, \"/opt/vllm/tests\")\nout = []\nfor modname in (...):\n    entry = {\"module\": modname}\n    try:\n        mod = importlib.import_module(modname)\n        entry[\"imported\"] = True\n        entry[\"test_contracts_present\"] = len([n for n in dir(mod) if n.startswith(\"test_\")])\n        writers = [n for n in dir(mod) if callable(getattr(mod, n, None)) and not n.startswith(\"test_\") and re.search(...)]\n        entry[\"callable_writers\"] = writers\n        for wname in writers:\n            fn = getattr(mod, wname)\n            try: sig = inspect.signature(fn)\n            except Exception: continue\n            params = list(sig.parameters.values())\n            if len(params) == 1 and params[0].default is params[0].empty:\n                try:\n                    fn(\"/var/tmp/flashnext-proxy\")\n                    entry[\"invoked\"] = wname\n                    break\n                except Exception as e3:\n                    entry[wname + \"_error\"] = e3.__class__.__name__\n    except Exception as e:\n        entry[\"import\"] = f\"FAIL {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n    out.append(entry)\nprint(json.dumps(out))\n```\nThe parent process parses the last line of stdout. The subprocess also needs to bypass the cache for discover... no wait, the parent process's validation bypasses the cache ✓. But could the subprocess's writer call contaminate the cache inside the subprocess? The subprocess exits; irrelevant.\n\nNote: it's possible the writer call in the subprocess writes partial garbage to OUT — after probe (in either process order), the parent's own writer always rebuilds... but does the probe run after the parent writes the file? Order within the parent main(): probe → geometry → introspection → weights → ple shards → validation. Subprocess writer call might write files under OUT before the parent's writer — the parent's find-delete happens on the host side before podman starts; within the same container execution, the subprocess's probe writer might add files that the parent doesn't overwrite (e.g., a different shard file name) → garbage shards remaining → discover finds extra layers?? If a non-test writer writes something, subsequent validation compares against expected values — extra layers in per_layer will show up in entry counts and could cause die()... acceptable: record it, and if validation fails, it fails loudly. To be safer: run the probe subprocess before the parent writes anything, then the parent overwrites files with the same name; stray files from the probe remain → validation catches it. No problem — loud. \n\n3. Introspection after subprocess probe → no contamination within the process. Also, in-process import of ple_mmap: probe_fork_scaffolds imports vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common.ple_mmap — does this import pull in the amd model/ops? Importing common.ple_mmap: does it import the model tree? Imports vllm.platforms etc. Probably safe (previously succeeded without op duplication... hmm, did it? In the failed run the duplicate error appeared during the registry's INSPECTION — first occurrence of ops.hc execution: the successful test_ple_mmap import (in-process!) imported amd.model → ops.hc got registered. Next, the second import attempt via test_ple_mmap_amd → FAILS with duplication — meaning ops.hc re-executes during test_ple_mmap_amd import. So the two test modules are importing hc under different module names?? Whatever — resolved with subprocess isolation.)\n\nHowever: the in-process common.ple_mmap import — does it trigger registration of ops.hc? Previous standalone discovery run: no ERROR ✓ safe.\n\nAlso, does in-process introspection still work after the in-process ple_mmap import? Registry resolution imports the amd tree — the ops get registered once within the process ✓ no problem.\n\nApplying the edits: rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds entirely + validate_ple for _LayerShards. Need `import subprocess` inside the make-proxy python (add the import).\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The fork's discovery fully verifies the table contract (cols 20480 ✓, F8_E4M3 ✓, scales found ✓). Currently rewriting the scaffold probe in the subprocess and the validation of `_LayerShards`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bf199116ea5b48e78e945f8a","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && python3 - <<'EOF'\np = 'scripts/make-proxy.sh'\nsrc = open(p).read()\n\ndef rep(old, new, label):\n    global src\n    assert old in src and src.count(old) == 1, f\"BAD: {label}\"\n    src = src.replace(old, new)\n    print(\"ok:\", label)\n\nrep(\"import torch\\n\", \"import subprocess\\nimport torch\\n\", \"subprocess import\")\n\n# --- rewrite probe_fork_scaffolds: subprocess-isolated test probe -------------\nstart = src.index('def probe_fork_scaffolds():')\nend = src.index('def text_cfg(cfg):')\nnew_probe = '''def probe_fork_scaffolds():\n    \"\"\"Probe the fork's tests/models/qwen4_exp tree for reusable writer