{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Big progress — the permission line was displayed: **\"TritonExperts: permit block-FP8 fused MoE ... via in-kernel bf16 upcast on gfx1151\"** — the oracle picked the TRITON backend. But introspection still fails: \"NotImplementedError: Qwen4Exp QSA requires FlashAttention\" — the full attention layer's QSA indexer requires the FlashAttention backend at construction time (device-side check). Options:\n\n- Set `VLLM_ATTENTION_BACKEND=FLASH_ATTN` env variable? The fork's flash_attn.py reported \"Using FlashAttention version None\" — flash_attn is unavailable → version None → QSA throws an exception. Hmm — \"Using FlashAttention version None\" suggests FA wasn't detected; on ROCm, the \"FLASH_ATTN\" backend may be provided by ROCm's aotriton/ck flash — if the serving side works, it should work for introspection too. The failure might be because the container process hadn't initialized HIP yet? \"No CUDA GPUs\" was resolved by devices. FA version None might be because the flash-attn import failed during a CPU-only import phase.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: skip full model construction; the construction-time blocker is QSA's attention backend probe, which is irrelevant to the name/shape of the weights. We can enumerate parameters without constructing attention internals... monkey-patching is too hacky.\n\nA better alternative introspection: build the model while setting the attention backend env variable to something constructible. In this vllm, env `VLLM_ATTENTION_BACKEND=FLASH_ATTN` → get_attn_backend checks availability; FA's ROCm support is via triton/aiter... \"FlashAttention version None\" — in ROCm vllm, the flash_attn backend requires `flash_attn` or the ROCm equivalent; this image probably serves QSA with... hmm, the real first-light TP2 is scheduled to run with eager, and... wait, does the real serve hit the same QSA-requires-FA issue?! If serving works at night (the campaign claims it's designed to pass first light), then FA is available at serve time (GPU initialized, HIP). The reason my introspection got \"version None\" might be that flash-attn's ROCm library is lazily probed at device initialization — my builder process didn't initialize CUDA/HIP (no torch.cuda.init). Try adding torch.cuda.init_distributed... Simpler: in try_fork_introspection, before construction do `torch.cuda.init(); torch.cuda.set_device(0)`? Actually just touching `torch.cuda.device_count()` initializes HIP. If FA requires HIP init, that would resolve it. Add the following:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    torch.cuda.init()\nexcept Exception:\n    pass\n```\nAlso set env VLLM_ATTENTION_BACKEND? Don't export F.9 probes — attention backend is fine (not an is-set oracle probe), but it changes runtime behavior — introspection only, doesn't affect serving. Leave it unset and try device init first.\n\nNote: even if introspection succeeds with GPU init, construction on the meta device should avoid memory. OK.\n\nIf FA still fails, fallback: keep the curated path, but for quantize-all we also have the membership observation — the final fallback could install the ignore list... we can't enumerate. Or: accept curated + quantize-all + serve as tiebreaker. But there's a subtle point to check: the curated fallback in the nested config wrote model.language_model... ✓ and the serve loader matched? Not sure until we run it.\n\nLet me quickly test the FA question directly inside the GPU container: does `vllm serve` even work at all in the first place? That's the ultimate question — instead of endlessly polishing introspection, let's actually run run-proxy.sh end-to-end right now and see if the serve works! If the serve itself fails on QSA-FA, then the proxy needs a design-level change (e.g., a config with no full_attention layer — only linear_attention! Then no QSA/FA needed... but the workload pattern has full every 4 layers; the proxy's claim is mmap + permitted kernel; layer_types all linear_attention might be acceptable as a proxy? Architecture identifier is the same; but full-attention is part of the architecture... P10's piecewise + splitting ops concerns mmap ops, not FA. Hmm. Still, if FA works at serve time with GPU init, the real serve is fine).\n\nSteps: (1) add torch.cuda.init() to introspection; (2) run make-proxy; (3) actually run run-proxy end-to-end with a generous timeout."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"The approved MoE permissions were executed within the build. Next, introspection requires the HIP device to be initialized for the FlashAttention probe. Add that, and then proceed to actual end-to-end execution:\n\n"}}
