{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`ValueError: Attempted to load nested weight 'lm_head.weight.weight' into a single parameter 'lm_head.weight'`\n\nAutoWeightsLoader treats my checkpoint tensor `lm_head.weight` as a module prefix `lm_head` + parameter `weight`, and then finds another `.weight`... actually, the error says it tried to load `lm_head.weight.weight` — my tensor named `lm_head.weight` ... this model has a top-level module named `lm_head` and a parameter... hmm, \"nested weight lm_head.weight.weight\" means the loader interpreted the checkpoint name `lm_head.weight` and the model's `lm_head` as a module that has its own weight parameter → it expected the checkpoint key to be `lm_head.weight` for parameter `lm_head.weight`... this error occurs when the checkpoint has a name one level deeper than the parameter: the model's parameter is `lm_head.weight`; AutoWeightsLoader splits the name and walks the modules; when it reaches module `lm_head`, the remainder is `weight`, which should load parameter `weight` → that should be fine...\n\nNo wait, actually: I wrote a tensor named `lm_head.weight` — but did I really? My introspection writer writes under parameter names from named_parameters(). Parameter names inside model.named_parameters() — what does the top-level model return? Probably \"lm_head.weight\"... but the model here is ConditionalGeneration wrapping CausalLM; is the named prefix from the top \"language_model.lm_head.weight\"? The ignore sample showed \"language_model.lm_head\"! So the parameter names are like \"language_model.lm_head.weight\" etc. And I wrote those names as-is. Then the loader (AutoWeightsLoader on the top model, which has attribute `language_model`) walks it: \"language_model.lm_head.weight\" → module language_model ✓ → lm_head ✓ → parameter weight ✓ should work...\n\nBut the error says 'lm_head.weight.weight'. That means the checkpoint had a name \"lm_head.weight.weight\"?? No wait — the \"Attempted to load nested weight 'X' into a single parameter 'Y'\" message format: it tried to load nested weight 'lm_head.weight.weight' into parameter 'lm_head.weight'. So the loader has parameter 'lm_head.weight' (a model parameter named lm_head.weight — the CausalLM's named_parameters has lm_head.weight relative to CausalLM), and the checkpoint name is 'lm_head.weight.weight' — my file contains 'lm_head.weight.weight'?! Hmm — my writer: `write_linear(name...)` is for 2D weights; lm_head path: name=\"language_model.lm_head.weight\"... no wait — \"lm_head.weight.weight\" is a double-suffix. Where does that come from? My loop: for non-expert fused hits → write_linear(f\"{stem}{hf_sfx}\", ...) ✓ single. Fallback `write_linear(name, shape)` ✓. embed branch ✓. Hmm — the scale handling: `if \"scale\" in name: continue`.\n\nLet me actually check the tensor names in the generated main safetensors. I'll investigate quickly. Also, a possible mapping: does vLLM's loader apply hf_to_vllm_mapper to my already-vLLM-form names, and break them? The mapper is typically orig_to_new_substr: {\"model.language_model.\": \"language_model.model.\"}? If my checkpoint names are in vLLM format, the mapper might still rewrite some substrings... Example: mapper containing \".layers.\" ... or the checkpoint loader first strips a \"model.\" prefix...\n\nFor now, let me dump a few tensor names from the built file."}}
