{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Okay, now I understand the mapping machinery:\n\n1. CausalLM's `hf_to_vllm_mapper`: `orig_to_new_prefix={\"model.language_model.\": \"model.\"}` — the HF checkpoint name `model.language_model.layers...` → becomes `model.layers...` on the vLLM side! Furthermore, packed_modules_mapping is: `\"in_proj_ba\": [\"in_proj_b\", \"in_proj_a\"]` (HF splits the checkpoint's in_proj_b and in_proj_a), and for input_mix it's `[\"input_mix_weight_down\", \"block_inject_weight\", \"_input_mix_padding\"]`.\n\n2. `_HC_WEIGHTS_MAPPER` on the model: orig_to_new_stacked: `hyper_connection.input_mix_weight_down.weight` → (input_mix_weight_down_block_inject.weight, shard 0), `hyper_connection.block_inject_weight.weight` → shard 1.\n\n3. ConditionalGeneration's load_weights adds the substr \"mtp.\" → None (ignore), and when language_model_only also ignores the prefix \"visual.\".\n\nThat is, the correct CHECKPOINT names are on the HF side:\n- Prefix: `model.language_model.` (gets mapped to `model.`)\n- in_proj: separate `in_proj_b` + `in_proj_a` (or is it the fused in_proj_ba? The packed_modules mapping indicates HF splits it; AutoWeightsLoader + packed mapping handles the split names; if you write the fused in_proj_ba directly, the substr mapper? The earlier 'in_proj_baa' error: my name `...linear_attn.in_proj_ba.weight` — where did the mapping that creates 'baa' come from? orig_to_new_substr probably includes {\"in_proj_b\": \"in_proj_ba\"}? Where? The qwen3_next mapper (line 563) — let's not chase it; write the split HF names following packed_modules_mapping.)\n- hyperconnection: separate `input_mix_weight_down.weight` + `block_inject_weight.weight`.\n- visual.*: skip when language_model_only=True (the mapper ignores them: prefix visual. → None) — don't write the visual weights at all! Saves about 400MB and sidesteps the ignore logic.\n- mtp.*: skip.\n- lm_head: checkpoint name `lm_head.weight`? The ConditionalGeneration prefix mapping is model.language_model→model; lm_head is top-level; parameter namespace 'language_model.lm_head'? Hmm, the earlier ignore sample had 'language_model.lm_head' (vLLM module prefix). The checkpoint name for lm_head in the actual checkpoint: probably `lm_head.weight`... but the vLLM module tree: ConditionalGeneration has .language_model (CausalLM), which has .lm_head. AutoWeightsLoader walks from the top: the checkpoint name must resolve to language_model.lm_head after the mapper. Mapper's prefix mapping model.language_model. → model. — that's for the internal language model. Hmm, what checkpoint name maps to language_model.lm_head? Perhaps the actual checkpoint keeps lm_head under `lm_head.weight` and the top-level loader is... The earlier error about nested lm_head.weight.weight came from my double-suffix, so we never resolved what the correct name is. The workload's ignore list has a literal 'lm_head' entry (at the head of the 943-entry list!). That's a module prefix in the ignore semantics... For the CHECKPOINT name, AutoWeightsLoader routes by module structure: top-level module: model (Qwen4ExpModel?), language_model, visual, lm_head?? ConditionalGeneration composition: self.language_model = Qwen4ExpForCausalLM; self.visual. And CausalLM has .model and .lm_head. So the parameter path from the ConditionalGeneration top: language_model.lm_head.weight; the checkpoint name must become that after the mapper. Which HF name maps to it? If the mapper's prefix {\"model.language_model.\": \"model.\"} applies... \"model.language_model.lm_head\" → \"model.lm_head\" — not language_model.lm_head. Hmm — wait, the top-level load_weights is at ConditionalGeneration (model.py:1011) — AutoWeightsLoader(self), self=ConditionalGeneration. Its hf_to_vllm_mapper is at line 805? \"mapper = self.hf_to_vllm_mapper | ...\" — line 805 is inside a different class (the one at 607 defines a mapper with prefix model.language_model→model — that's probably Qwen4ExpForCausalLM at ~573-607). The ConditionalGeneration's own mapper is defined at ~790-810? Let's look at 805's context and the class's hf_to_vllm_mapper. Actually — simpler: instead of reverse-engineering, invert it programmatically:\n\nIn introspection, after building the model, also obtain `model.hf_to_vllm_mapper` (ConditionalGeneration level, including the load_weights merge? The merge happens inside load_weights... but the class-level mapper + known additions (mtp→None, visual→None when lm-only)).\n\nInversion algorithm: given a vLLM parameter name, apply the inverse:\n- orig_to_new_prefix {o: n}: if vllm_name starts with n → candidate orig = o + rest (one-to-one).\n- orig_to_new_substr {o: n}: replace the first occurrence of n in the name with o?? Ambiguous...\n- orig_to_new_stacked {o: (n, shard)}: vllm param n ← checkpoint tensor o (sharded). For my zero-filled weights, write the full o tensor? For stacked, the loader uses weight_loader(shard) expecting shard slices: the rows of the orig tensor correspond to that shard. Write orig tensors with the correct shard shape: for input_mix's down (shard 0)/inject (shard 1): need shard dimensions. For my zeros, simpler: can I write directly in the post-mapping name format? No — the mapper applies to every checkpoint name... If the checkpoint name doesn't match any mapping rule, it passes through unchanged. So if I write the checkpoint name already in the post-mapping vLLM form and it happens to match none of the mapping patterns, it passes through ✓. The in_proj_ba problem: some substr rule rewrites \"in_proj_b\"→\"in_proj_ba\"?? That produced 'baa' from my 'in_proj_ba'. Which mapper has substr {\"in_proj_b\": \"in_proj_ba\"}? Hmm, wait — the actual direction: packed_modules_mapping {\"in_proj_ba\": [\"in_proj_b\", \"in_proj_a\"]} is not the mapper; AutoWeightsLoader's _load_module uses packed_modules_mapping to merge checkpoint tensors in_proj_b/in_proj_a → parameter in_proj_ba. My checkpoint had in_proj_ba directly → the loader tried... and the error said it looked for 'in_proj_baa'?? Strange. Probably qwen3_next.py:563's hf_to_vllm_mapper has substr {\"in_proj_ba\": ...} — let's check line 563.\n\nTime check: this iteration loop is going deep, but each iteration is cheap now (~1 min). Let me grab the remaining mapper definitions (qwen3_next:563, qwen4_exp model.py:~790-810 and 385-395) and write the inversion precisely."}}
