{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"progress: in_proj_b/in_proj_a have been split ✓, and HC has also been split into input_mix_weight_down + block_inject_weight ✓. But the 35 \"cannot split\" warnings still remain, and in_proj_qkvz is still fused (needs to be split into in_proj_qkv + in_proj_z: sizes [2048,2048,6144,6144] — 4 partition sizes against 2 packed parts [in_proj_qkv, in_proj_z]: the mismatched lengths (4 vs 2) are the cause → fall through to the note. in_proj_qkv = 2048+2048 = 4096 (q+k), z = 6144+6144 = 12288? But 4096+12288 = 16384 ✓. In other words, the partition granularity is finer than the packed parts (q,k,v,z? no, that's not it…). The qkv part of GDN: q = 2048 (key_heads×key_dim = 16×128), k = 2048, v = 6144 (48×128), z = 6144 → so the parts are [in_proj_qkv = q+k+v?? no wait…]. packed is [in_proj_qkv, in_proj_z], sizes [2048,2048,6144,6144] → in_proj_qkv spans q+k+v = 2048+2048+6144 = 10240, and in_proj_z = 6144. Hmm, but then the sizes list has 4 entries corresponding to q,k,v,z (z is an independent part).\n\nRule: when len(sizes) > len(parts), group from the left: assign sizes greedily to parts, and the last part takes the rest? For in_proj_qkvz: parts 2, sizes 4 → qkv = 2048+2048+6144, z = 6144. How do we know qkv takes 3 and z takes 1? In general there's no way to know… but looking at it another way: these 4 sizes correspond to the q,k,v,z sub-parts, and packed's [in_proj_qkv, in_proj_z] is grouped as [q,k,v]+[z]. Heuristic: the last part takes the last size? No guarantee (input_mix: sizes [320,4,12] with parts [down, inject, padding] — 3↔3 is equal ✓ handled).\n\nAlternative: for the qkvz case, look at the stacked mapper of the parent model — earlier in the qwen3_next stacked map there were separate entries for q/k/v; but GDN's in_proj isn't included in that stacked map (only packed).\n\nHmm — but wait: does HF's checkpoint even split in_proj_qkv into qkv+z in the first place? If the real checkpoint keeps in_proj_qkvz as a single tensor (the packed mapping exists for the case where it's split, but AutoWeightsLoader also accepts the fused name directly?), then leaving in_proj_qkvz fused is correct. Evidence from my earlier failed run: the only complaint was in_proj_baa (ba was broken by substr). in_proj_qkvz as-is didn't error out — because the error stops at the first problem. Uncertain.\n\nWhy was in_proj_ba rewritten to 'baa' by substr, but in_proj_qkvz wasn't rewritten to something weird? If a substr rule {\"in_proj_b\": \"in_proj_ba\"} exists, is there also {\"in_proj_qkv\": \"in_proj_qkvz\"}? The name in_proj_qkvz contains the substring \"in_proj_qkv\" → it would be rewritten to \"in_proj_qkvzz\"?? The 'baa' error proves that in_proj_b was rewritten once. If the rule exists for qkv, then my fused qkvz → 'qkvzz' should have been the next error. The serve failed on the first one it encountered (the layer-0 error was in_proj_baa). It's possible that qkvzz exists too.\n\nLet me check the substr mapping directly — grep for orig_to_new_substr containing in_proj in the mapper of qwen3_5/qwen4_exp.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dd1804f4d8684f15b7c2c2a3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"podman run --rm --entrypoint bash flashnext:dev -c \"grep -rn 'orig_to_new_substr' -A 8 /opt/vllm/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/model.py /opt/vllm/vllm/model_executor/models/qwen3_5.py 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^\\-\\-$' | head -40\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"^WARNING\\|^INFO\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1262,"output":1092,"cacheRead":261888,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":967,"totalTokens":264242,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787997240319,"responseId":"chatcmpl-83b958db-7a30-407d-b1bc-93847478e8c2","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_dd1804f4d