{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"In these mappers, there's no in_proj substr rule — so where did 'in_proj_baa' come from? The Qwen3_5Model mapper — let me check orig_to_new_substr in qwen3_5.py, and what's contained in Qwen3_5Model.hf_to_vllm_mapper (Qwen4ExpModel.hf_to_vllm_mapper = Qwen3_5Model's | _HC). grep found nothing in qwen3_5.py (only a match in qwen4_exp). Let me check qwen3_5.py directly and how AutoWeightsLoader handles the packed submodule — actually, the 'baa' might be coming not from the mapper but from AutoWeightsLoader's packed merging logic: when looking up parameter 'in_proj_ba' via packed's reverse lookup, when checkpoint name is 'in_proj_ba', does _load_module check each fused name f\"{base}.{part}\"?? If part=\"in_proj_b\" is a prefix of \"in_proj_ba\" → the loader might be constructing 'in_proj_ba' + 'a'?? That is, if checkpoint 'in_proj_ba' exists and packed has in_proj_ba←[in_proj_b, in_proj_a], the loader tries to match checkpoint names part-by-part; my 'in_proj_ba.weight' starts with part 'in_proj_b' → the remainder is 'a.weight' → nested 'in_proj_ba'+'a' → 'in_proj_baa'! Yes — AutoWeightsLoader's packed logic treats checkpoint 'in_proj_ba...' as part 'in_proj_b' + suffix 'a'. So the fused checkpoint names collide with part-name prefix matching. This confirms: I must write the split names (in_proj_b, in_proj_a...). For in_proj_qkvz: does part 'in_proj_qkv' prefix-match checkpoint name 'in_proj_qkvz...'?? 'in_proj_qkvz'.startswith('in_proj_qkv') → True! → remainder 'z' → tries 'in_proj_qkvz' + 'z' = 'in_proj_qkvzz' → error. So qkvz must also be split: in_proj_qkv [10240] + in_proj_z [6144]. Sizes [2048,2048,6144,6144]: qkv = 2048+2048+6144 = 10240 ✓ z = 6144 ✓. Grouping rule: parts consume sizes from left, and the last part consumes the last size... in_proj_qkv consumes 3 sizes, z consumes 1. General rule: unknown; but here: if len(sizes) > len(parts): assign sizes to each part except the last via... hmm. What determines that qkv is 3? Maybe module.output_partition_sizes is paired with the module's own packed mapping... Does MergedColumnParallelLinear have its own packed subparts? Actually, this module might expose output_partition_sizes = [q,k,v,z] corresponding to internal shard ids [q_proj?, k_proj, v_proj?]. GDN qkvz: q=2048, k=2048, v=6144, z=6144.\n\nPractical rule for the proxy: if len(sizes) != len(real_parts): try grouping: last part gets last size, remaining parts get remaining sizes — but the remaining parts might still be mismatched (qkv gets [2048,2048,6144] → sum). Implementation: if len(sizes) == len(real_parts): zip; elif len(sizes) > len(real_parts): give the first (len(sizes) - len(real_parts) + 1) sizes to part[0] summed? No — qkv is the first part and takes 3 ✓ but that's just coincidence of ordering. For robustness: if len(sizes) > len(parts): give extra sizes to the largest-indexed part? For qkvz, extras = 2; if extras are appended to the last part: qkv=2048, z=2048+6144+6144=14336 ✗ wrong (total 16384 but split is wrong → loader shape mismatch error).\n\nCorrect approach: find the module's own shard semantics. MergedColumnParallelLinear with 4 partitions against packed's 2 parts — probably the module's output_partition_sizes corresponds to shard ids [\"in_proj_q\",\"in_proj_k\",\"in_proj_v\",\"in_proj_z\"]... Look for module attribute `output_sizes`... same thing. What maps 4→2? The model class's packed_modules_mapping only has the 2-part entry. Does the loader use the `packed_modules_mapping` entry for merging? AutoWeightsLoader uses the top model's packed_modules_mapping — a 2-part entry for in_proj_qkvz. Then the checkpoint's in_proj_qkv and in_proj_z are merged; the 4-element partition sizes are vllm-internal (q,k,v,z slices), and weight_loader(shard_id) for shard ids \"in_proj_qkv\"/\"in_proj_z\" maps to ranges... weight_loader handles it.\n\nSo the row count at the checkpoint level: in_proj_qkv = sum of q+k+v = 10240, in_proj_z = 6144. Derive from sizes [2048,2048,6144,6144]: which sum goes to which part? The last part (z) is last: z = 6144 (last element), qkv = sum of the rest ✓. Is that general? For input_mix (3↔3), equal case. For qkvz, sizes align as [q,k,v,z] and parts are [qkv, z]: the boundary is such that z takes the last 1. Reason: qkv is itself a 3-way merge of q/k/v. A general heuristic that works here: give the last part the last (len(sizes) - len(parts) + 1) sizes? For qkvz: last part z gets 4-2+1 = 3 sizes → 2048+6144+6144 ✗ wrong. Reverse: give the first part the extras: qkv gets first 3 → ✓. So the rule: give the extras to the first part. For in_proj_ba (2↔2) no extras. Implement: give the extras to the first part (sum sizes[:1+extra]), rest are 1:1. This matches qkvz (in_proj_qkv is q+k+v).\n\nRisk noted; all zeros anyway — what matters is shape correctness."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_51720c2d6d1340e6b3eb73b1","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/cac9adc5400c/proxy-tooling && python3 - <<'EOF'\np = 'scripts/make-proxy.sh'\nsrc = open(p).read()\n\ndef rep(old, new, label):\n    global src\n    assert src.count(old) == 1, f\"BAD: {label}\"\n    src = src.replace(old, new)\n    print(\"ok:\", label)\n\nrep('''            if len(sizes) == len(parts):\n                pairs = [(pt, sz) for pt, sz in zip(parts, sizes)\n                         if not pt.startswith(\"_\")]\n            elif len(sizes) == len(real):\n                pairs = list(zip(real, sizes))\n            else:\n                note(f\"cannot split fused param {name}: \"\n                     f\"{len(parts)} parts vs {len(sizes)} partition sizes\")\n                continue''',\n'''            if len(sizes) == len(parts):\n                pairs = [(pt, sz) for pt, sz in zip(parts, sizes)\n                         if not pt.startswith(\"_\")]\n            elif len(sizes) == len(real):\n                pairs = list(zip(real, sizes))\n            elif len(sizes) > len(real):\n                # The first part is itself a merge (in_proj_qkvz stores\n                # q+k+v under in_proj_qkv): it absorbs the extra partition\n                # sizes, the rest pair 1:1.\n                extra = len(sizes) - len(real)\n                pairs = [(real[0], sum(sizes[:extra + 1]))]\n                pairs += list(zip(real[1:], sizes[extra + 1:]))\n            else:\n                note(f\"cannot split fused param {name}: \"\n                     f\"{len(parts)} parts vs {len(sizes)} partition sizes\")\n                continue''',\n\"extra-sizes grouping\")\n\nopen(p, 'w').write(src)\nprint(\"DONE\")\nEOF\nbash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh && bash scripts/make-proxy.sh 2>&1 | tail -2 && python3 -c \"\nimport json, pathlib\nm = json.load(open(pathlib.Path.home()/'.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint/proxy-manifest.json'))\ncs = [w for w in m['warnings'] if 'cannot split' in w]\nprint('cannot-split:', len(cs), cs[:3])\nimport struct\nf = pathlib.Path.home()/'.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint/model-00001-of-00001.safetensors'\nn = struct.unpack('<Q', open(f,'rb').read(8))[0]\nh = json.loads(open(f,'rb').read()[8:8+n])\nprint({x: h[x]['shape'] for x in sorted(h) if 'layers.0.linear_attn.in_proj' in x})\nprint({x: h[x]['shape'] for x in sorted(h) if 'layers.0.mlp.shared_expert' in x})\n\"","timeout":600}}}}
