{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Chain: `get_moe_configs` → `get_config_file_name` → `get_device_name_as_file_name` → `current_platform.get_device_name(0)` → rocm's get_device_name is decorated with `@with_amdsmi_context` → calls `amdsmi_init()`, but that's undefined (amdsmi import failed) → NameError.\n\nRocmPlatform's `get_device_name` uses the amdsmi wrapper — there's no fallback path for the case when amdsmi doesn't exist. The fork's patch 0003 fixed platform DETECTION (KFD topology), but this runtime helper still assumes amdsmi. This affects any execution inside this container (including TP2 first-light) → a campaign-level blocker that should be typed/documented, but my proxy task can't patch the fork…\n\nWait — can I work around this without touching the fork? Options:\n\nA) get_moe_configs → first searches for a tuned config file… but it calls get_config_file_name before scanning the directory (needs the name to construct the path). The NameError happens in computing the name → unavoidable via configs.\n\nB) Provide amdsmi! amdsmi is a python package bundled with the ROCm install (opt/rocm/share/amd_smi). Does rocm-sdk-core in the container have amd_smi? Check `find /opt -name 'amdsmi*'` inside the container. If the python wheel for amdsmi exists but just isn't installed / isn't on the path — add a PYTHONPATH env var to the serving command! Clean, no fork change, no new wheel (part of the already-pinned rocm-sdk substrate!). amdsmi also needs the libamd_smi.so shared library — if it's included in the rocm-sdk-core package it should exist.\n\nLet me check for amdsmi inside the container.\n"}}
