b4268041","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"OK\ntemplate-run EXIT=0\nworkload-run EXIT=0\nout model.layers.0.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_00000.weight [256, 256] F8_E4M3\nout row_bytes: 256 split: 2\nout2 model.layers.0.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_00000.weight [8, 256] F8_E4M3\nout2 row_bytes: 256 split: 512\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787994096109},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both paths work (for the fake config with heads_per_ngram=8, ple_embed_dim doesn't exist → falls back to head_dim 256; if ple_embed_dim had existed, it would have been heads×dim).\n\nNext, let me also sanity-check the bash flow of run-proxy.sh — I can't run podman/vllm here, but at least let me verify the checkpoint gate path: run run-proxy.sh with a fake PROXY_DIR and no podman... podman probably doesn't exist on this host. Check: `command -v podman`. If it doesn't exist, the script will fail at `podman image exists` → the ERR trap writes a failure receipt → tests that the trap path works. Let me carefully test in a temporary REPO_ROOT-like environment... the script uses REPO_ROOT from its own path; the receipt gets written into the repo's results/receipts/proxy.json — must not contaminate the actual repo's receipts directory! Hmm: a fail receipt named proxy.json would remain in the worktree. I could delete it after testing, but committing without contamination is cleaner. Instead, copy the script into a sandbox directory structure and test it there.\n\nSandbox: /tmp/runproxytest/scripts/run-proxy.sh, and results/receipts also exist. Set FN_PROXY_DIR=/tmp/proxytest/out (config.json exists), FN_STATE_DIR unset, and podman probably doesn't exist → `podman image exists` fails → ERR trap? That's inside `if ! podman image exists` — the ERR trap doesn't fire on commands within an if condition ✓ → write_receipt fails with \"image not found\" → exit 1. Good test of the receipt writer.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_cont