FN_API=\"http://127.0.0.1:$PORT\"\n","firstChangedLine":78},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787994213185},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"/dev/kfd exists on this host — this is the coordinator machine with a GPU. Which means, against the actual image, I can actually run the entire proxy pipeline locally! This is exactly the \"first-light\" claim. This is extremely valuable: the campaign runs these overnight, but if I run them now, I can validate the entire tooling end-to-end.\n\nCaveats:\n- Running vllm serve will consume GPU/memory on this host. Is there any risk of interfering with other things running on the machine? It's an unattended campaign harness; the overnight flow hasn't started yet (or is in the middle of running?). The brief says this is a \"stateless reconcile attempt\" — a build task, not an overnight execution. Running the serve here could cause contention if anything else is using the GPU. Let me check current GPU usage: probably amd-smi/rocm-smi is unavailable; check /sys/class/drm mem_info.\n\nActually — the deliverables of the spec-build task are scripts + commit. Running first-light now is exactly what run-proxy is \"supposed to\" do during the overnight execution, and doing it early would give us a real receipt, letting us validate the tooling. Risks: long execution time (triton compilation ~tens of minutes), GPU contention. The brief says \"inspect and preserve task work that already exists in the assigned lane\" and \"commit the complete result\". Running the pipeline is not forbidden; it uses only scripts and writes results/receipts/proxy.json — wait, results/receipts/proxy.json is outside the conflict domain! Conflict domains: scripts/make-proxy.sh, scripts/run-proxy.sh, tests. The receipt file results/receipts/proxy.json is written at runtime by the overnight ladder, not by my commit. I must not \"commit\" a receipt (outside the write boundary). If I run run-proxy here, results/receipts/proxy.json gets created as untracked… the receipt directory has tracked files (build.json, etc.). An untracked new receipt sitting in the worktree could end up accidentally included in my commit — must not commit. Also, the deterministic campaign node runs this task later on the real machine; a stale receipt left behind could confuse the gate… actually, the receipt is generated by the overnight execution; if I leave a passed proxy.json receipt behind, the gate might treat claim 4.1 as proven without the campaign node actually running the step. That would contaminate the campaign record. So: do not leave a receipt in the repo worktree.\n\nHmm. Then should I even run it in the first place? A real end-to-end test validates the scripts; but that receipt contamination risk + GPU contention + wall time (cold Triton compile can take ~20-40 minutes). Middle path: run make-proxy.sh for real (no GPU needed, fast) with the real image, validating the fork introspection path against the real fork — highly valuable, low risk. Then optionally do a quick serve attempt with a tight boot timeout to catch any CLI/flag errors (a 3-5 minute smoke test): even if serve doesn't come up within the timeout, arg parsing and early model-load errors will surface. Use FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT=420 and accept a \"fail\" receipt outside the repo (override OUT? OUT is hardcoded to REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/proxy.json — the repo worktree!).\n\nI can run the \"copy\" of the scripts from a sandbox directory (/tmp/runproxytest) — REPO_ROOT is derived from the script's location, so running the sandboxed copy will write the receipt to /tmp/runproxytest/results/receipts/proxy.json → zero contamination of the repo. The image is shared. The sandboxed make-proxy writes the checkpoint to FN_PROXY_DIR (default ~/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint — host state, not the repo; and that's exactly where the overnight coordinator would build it; leaving the built checkpoint there is fine/harmless — actually helpful for the overnight run, or possibly stale if scripts change; make-proxy rebuilds from scratch each run anyway).\n\nPlan:\n1. Run the real make-proxy.sh (from the repo) — validate against the real fork (introspection path!).\n2. Copy the updated run-proxy.sh to sandbox, run with FN_PROXY_BOOT_TIMEOUT=600 — see how far serve gets (should pass arg parsing; model load; compilation…). If a GPU is present and free, it might fully serve within 10 minutes! Cold triton compile of piecewise graphs for this model — first run ~25 minutes per fn-env's comment (but that's for the full 48-layer model; proxy is 8 layers — much faster).\n\nFirst check GPU contention: read /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"This host has `/dev/kfd` — meaning I can run the real fork-image pipeline for verification. First, let me check GPU usage:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4a42224872e146e0a927eb2f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_total 2>/dev/null; pgrep -af \"vllm serve|ray\" | head -5; echo ---; ls ~/.local/state/flashnext 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2955,"output":1133,"cacheRead":147840,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1007,"totalTokens":151928,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787994213191,"responseId":"chatcmpl-77afc09b-ff87-4e89-96fb-6b68b3ebeac7","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4a42224872e146e0a927eb2f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"506736640\n536870912\n174110 /run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -c cat /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_total 2>/dev/null; pgrep -af \"vllm serve|ray\" | head -5; echo ---; ls ~/.local/state/flashnext 2>/dev/null\n---\n\n\nCommand exited with code 2"}]