esidual risks\nmake-proxy: Fp8Config appears to consume modules_to_not_convert by literal membership; skipping the ignore-list (quantize-all mode)\nmake-proxy: RESIDUAL RISK: no ignore list installed — quantize-all mode; any quantized Linear whose name the fallback writer does not know is uninitialized fp8 memory and could poison the logits\nmake-proxy: FATAL: discover_shards found ZERO PLE shard tensors — the shard naming does not match the fork's _SHARD_RE; refusing to proceed\n\nreal\t0m23.008s\nuser\t0m0.060s\nsys\t0m0.113s\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787994703446},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Progress: VllmConfig now validates (no more activation_scheme errors — ModelConfig/VllmConfig construction succeeded, config was parsed, IR ops, model runner logs). Introspection failed due to API details: `ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls()` requires `model_config` argument: `resolve_model_cls(model_config, architectures?)`. Signature in this vLLM: `ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls(model_config, architectures=None)`? Fix: pass model_config=mc: `cls = ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls(mc)[0]`? Returns a tuple (model_cls, arch). Let's verify the actual signature inside the container.\n\nAnother critical observation: \"discover_shards found ZERO PLE shard tensors\" — wait, did the curated fallback write shards with the new prefix? No, wait — the order: main() first writes the weights (curated fallback — since introspection failed, KeyError on hidden_size? No, curated is now tc-compatible ✓ executed), then write_ple_shards with prefix model.language_model... then validate → zero shards found! Why? _SHARD_RE is `layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$` — my name: `model.language_model.layers.2.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_00000.weight` — search should match (unanchored). Unless the shard write itself failed, or the fallback writer crashed before writing shards? The exit message was from validate_ple → which means shards were written... but discover found zero. Hmm — prefix! In the actual model, the PLE module prefix may not contain `.ple.` — the actual checkpoint name is... `ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding` is what _SHARD_RE expects. Wait, does the actual weight name include a `model.language_model.` prefix? The actual modules_to_not_convert entries indicate `model.language_model.layers...`. So checkpoint tensors are probably named `model.language_model.layers.2.ple...` — regex unanchored → matches.\n\nSo why zero? discover_shards globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` — flat ✓ my files are flat ✓. Let's debug directly inside the container: run discover_shards against the checkpoint and print what's found. The actual checkpoint directory is at ~/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint.\n\nAlso note: did the curated fallback write the weights? KeyError is gone. The files exist? Let's look at the directory. Also confirm prefix handling in the validate step. Let's debug inside the container.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Config validation now passes; introspection hit an API signature detail, and discovery found zero shards — let me debug both directly in the container:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_544898938a5f40729d70c024","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls ~/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint | head; echo ---; podman run -i --rm -v \"$HOME/.local/state/flashnext/proxy-checkpoint:/ckpt:ro\" flashnext:dev python3 - <<'PY'\nimport inspect\nfrom vllm.models.qwen4_exp.common import ple_mmap\nprint(\"SHARD_RE:\", ple_mmap._SHARD_RE.pattern)\nfound = ple_mmap.discover_shards(\"/ckpt\")\nprint(\"type:\", type(found))\nprint(\"keys:\", list(found.keys())[:5] if hasattr(found, \"keys\") else found)\nimport json, struct, pathlib\nf = sorted(pathlib.Path(\"/ckpt\").glob(\"model-ple-*\"))[:2]\nprint(\"ple files:\", [x.name for x in f])\nfor p in f:\n    n = struct.unpack('<Q', open(p,'rb').read(8))[0]\n    print(\"  \", p.name, list(json.loads(open(p,'rb').read()[8:8+n])))\nfrom vllm.model_executor.models.registry import ModelRegistry\nprint(\"resolve_model_cls sig:\", inspect.signature(ModelRegistry.resolve_model_cls))\nPY"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2213,"output":844,"cacheRead":173184,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":536,"totalTokens":176241,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787994703453,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0e28148c-549c-407b-8a36-f7aa1ba6c97b","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_544898938a5f40729d70c024","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"chat_template.jinja\nconfig.json\ngeneration_config.json\nmerges.txt\nmodel-00001-of-00001.safetensors\nmodel-ple-2-00000.safetensors\nmodel-ple-2-00001.safetensors\nmodel-ple-scales.safetensors\nproxy-manifest.json\ntokenizer_config.json\n---\nWARNING 08-29 09:12:07 [rocm.py:43] Failed to import from amdsmi with ModuleNotFoundError(\"No module named 'amdsmi'\")\nINFO 08-29 09:12:11 [importing.py:60] Triton is installed but 0 active driver(s) found (expected 1). Disabling Triton to prevent runtime errors.\nINFO 08-29 09:12:11 [importing.py:95] Triton not installed or not compatible; certain GPU-related functions will not be available.\nSHARD_RE: layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\ntype: <class 'dict'>\nkeys: [2]\nple files: ['model-ple-2-00000.safetensors', 'model-ple-2-00001.safetensors']\n   model-ple-2-00000.safetensors ['model.language_model.layers.2.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_00000.weight']\n   model-ple-2-00001.safetensors ['model.language_model.layers.2.ple.ple_embedding.ngram_embedding.shard_00001.weight']\nresolve_model_cls sig: (architectures: str | list[str], model_config: vllm.config.model.ModelConfig) -> tuple[type[torch.nn.modules.module.Module], str]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787994733438},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! `discover_shards` is actually finding layer 2 (keys: [2]) — the shard names are correct! So then, why did my validate_ple conclude that there were zero shards? Looking at the code... inside `validate_ple`:\n\n```python\nfound = ple_mmap.discover_shards(str(OUT))\nper_layer = ({int(k): v for k, v in found.items()} if isinstance(found, dict) else found)\n...\nif not per_layer: die(\"ZERO PLE shard tensors\")\n```\n\nHmm, this shouldn't be able to end up empty... unless discover_shards returned something different at build time — oh, wait. It's `@functools.cache`! `discover_shards` is cached — during make-proxy, before writing the shard files, I had called `probe_fork_scaffolds` → imported ple_mmap but didn't call discover... no. During the build: validate_ple is the first and only call to discover_shards → the cache isn't a problem. But: the reason discover during the build returned {} was... because at that point the checkpoint directory inside the container was /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy (bind mount ✓, same files). Hmm.\n\nOh — wait. I've got it! Inside main(), the order is: weights → write_ple_shards → validate_ple. But the FATAL was emitted at validate time, yet... let me re-read the actual failure: \"make-proxy: FATAL: discover_shards found ZERO PLE shard tensors\". During that run, did write_ple_shards run normally? The manifest... check the manifest contents in that directory. Hmm, wait — actually, look at the flow of the failed run: after \"fork model introspection unavailable\" came the curated fallback... curated_weights → save_safetensors of the main file → write_ple_shards → model-ple-2-* files ✓ (the files exist now!). After that, validate_ple → zero?! But now, discover is finding them. The difference: during the run, there may have been an exception during the writing of `write_ple_shards` (e.g., an _fp8 shim issue... no, real torch). Hmm, wait — actually, during the failed run, validate_ple ran against the checkpoint at the point when the shard files did exist. discover_shards globs and parses the header — should have found them.\n\nCould it be... `ple_mmap.discover_shards` was called earlier in the same process and cached with empty results? Where? `probe_fork_scaffolds` — the fork test probe! It imports tests.models.qwen4_exp.test_ple_mmap etc. — the import failed (ModuleNotFoundError), but if some module that got imported called discover_shards against /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy before the shards existed → the functools.cache would forever retain the empty result! Which import would call it? test_ple_mmap's import fails. Hmm. Or... validate_shards_for? Not called.\n\nWait — actually, let me check the manifest from the failed run to confirm the order, and more simply, reproduce it: run discover twice in a fresh container process: once against an empty directory, then after adding files — to prove the cache's behavior:\n\nActually, the easiest fix is: in validate_ple, bypass the cache: call `ple_mmap.discover_shards.__wrapped__(str(OUT))` if available. This deterministically eliminates any cache pollution. And in the earlier failed run — hmm, what polluted it? Ah, wait. Got it! In the failed run, the curated fallback wrote weights... then `write_ple_shards(cfg, geom)` — with ple_layer_ids... but in the failed run, had the config.json in /var/tmp/flashnext-proxy been rewritten with ple_layer_ids? Yes (shrink sets [2]). Then discover in the validation...\n\nHmm, let me look at the failed run's manifest to see what happened.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"`discover_shards` finds the shards when run fresh — so during the build something poisoned it (it's `@functools.cache`d). Check the failed run's manifest, and fix with cache-bypass:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_edd31aeba0ec422c82a02be6","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python3 -m json.tool ~/.local/state/flashnext