or/layers/fused_moe/fused_moe.py\", line 1120, in get_config_file_name\nFile \"/opt/vllm/vllm/utils/platform_utils.py\", line 72, in get_device_name_as_file_name\nFile \"/opt/vllm/vllm/platforms/rocm.py\", line 169, in wrapper\nNameError: name 'amdsmi_init' is not defined\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787998460794},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Amazing progress — **the model ran an actual forward pass** (inside torch.compile / piecewise!) — a compiled MoE forward through the permitted TritonExperts path (`triton_moe.py:314 apply`)! During the forward, it failed inside `get_moe_configs` → `get_config_file_name` → `get_device_name_as_file_name` → the wrapper at rocm.py:169 → **NameError: amdsmi_init is not defined** — a bug in the fork (or upstream rocm.py) when amdsmi doesn't exist: the device-name helper ends up calling amdsmi_init when amdsmi import fails.\n\nLet me check rocm.py:160-175 inside the container — probably a decorator that tries to use amdsmi and doesn't handle ImportError correctly. This is engine (fork) territory, not my task's lane… but the proxy's job is to surface exactly this kind of issue early (\"before any actual weight bytes are loaded\"!). Options:\n\n1. Workaround inside the runner: provide the device-name file that get_moe_configs looks for, so it doesn't need the device name? get_config_file_name builds E=…, and the filename probably contains the device name; if the lookup for the config file fails → it falls back to the default config. The NameError fires while computing the device name itself. Can't avoid it via env vars.\n\n2. Provide amdsmi inside the container? Not installed (the \"Failed to import amdsmi\" warning). Installing amdsmi at runtime would violate the pinned-image principle; but — the amdsmi module might exist somewhere in the ROCm SDK… not present in the container.\n\n3. Patch the fork — outside my write boundary (patches/ or the fork repo belongs to the fork-engineering lane). But — wait: is this maybe caused by something in my environment? rocm.py:169 wrapper — let me read the code. If it's a `with amdsmi_context()`-style helper that only references amdsmi_init inside a try when available — it's a NameError bug that ought to be fixed on the fork side. But my campaign task is the proxy tooling… The overnight ladder needs this fix for the proxy to pass. Hmm — R7.2: \"if an overnight step fails due to upstream cause → drafted issue text under handoff/upstream-issues\" — that's morning work; but this is a fork bug that also blocks TP2 first-light.\n\nWait — actually, reconsider: patch 0003 added KFD topology detection \"for when amdsmi fails\" — the fork anticipated amdsmi being absent. This NameError is in a separate helper (get_device_name…) that wasn't covered. A one-line fix in the fork… editing the fork checkout inside the image is possible (bind-mount /opt/vllm? the image has it baked in; I could bind-mount a patched copy of rocm.py into the serving container… hacky, and outside the task boundary, and worse, it hides the issue from the campaign record.\n\nAlternative workaround via the config file: get_moe_configs looks for a JSON file under VLLM_ROCM_MOE_CONFIG_DIR? If a tuned-config file exists for our shape, does it still need the device name? get_config_file_name(...) — first constructs the filename from the device name. Let me read the code path: fused_moe.py:1120 get_config_file_name → platform_utils.get_device_name_as_file_name → rocm.py's wrapper@169. Let me read rocm.py:150-180 and platform_utils:60-80 to understand the fallback semantics and whether an env var exists (e.g., does VLLM_ROCM_OVERRIDE_… bypass amdsmi?).\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The compiled MoE forward ran (admitted path!) but crashed in `get_device_name_as_file_name` — a fork/ROCm bug when amdsmi is absent. Inspect the helper:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9334ad27b2e14bf48741db1b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"podman run --rm --entrypoint bash flashnext:dev -c \"sed -n '140,185p' /opt/vllm/vllm/platforms/rocm.py; echo ====; sed -n '55,80p' /opt/vllm/vllm/utils/platform_utils.py; echo ====; sed -n '1100,1160p' /opt/vllm/vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/fused_moe.py\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"^WARNING\\|^INFO\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1916,"output":1001,"cacheRead":279552,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":804,"totalTokens":282469,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787998460803,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a33eedc0-883f-4361-9116-0539fdcb40e3","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9334ad27b2e14bf48741db1b","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"        )\n\n    if hip_val is not None and cuda_val is not None:\n        if hip_val != cuda_val:\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"Inconsistent GPU visibility env vars: \"\n                f\"HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES='{hip_val}' vs \"\n                f\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='{cuda_val}'. \"\n                f\"Please set only one, or ensure they match.\"\n            )\n    elif hip_val is not None:\n        os.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = hip_val\n    elif cuda_val is not None:\n        os.environ[\"HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = cuda_val\n\n\n# Sync at import time - catches misconfigurations from process start.\n_sync_hip_cuda_env_vars()\n\n\n# AMDSMI utils\n# Note that NVML is not affected by `{CUDA/HIP}_VISIBLE_DEVICES`,\n# all the related functions work on real physical device ids.\n# the major benefit of using AMDSMI is that it will not initialize CUDA\n\n\ndef with_amdsmi_context(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        amdsmi_init()\n        try:\n            return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n        finally:\n            amdsmi_shut_down()\n\n    return wrapper\n\n\n@with_amdsmi_context\ndef _query_gcn_arch_from_amdsmi() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Query GCN arch from amdsmi. Raises if not available.\"\"\"\n    handles = amdsmi_get_processor_handles()\n    if handles:\n        asic_info = amdsmi_get_gpu_asic_info(handles[0])\n        # Use target_graphics_version which contains the gfx name\n        # e.g., 'gfx942' for MI300X/MI325X\n====\n\n    # TODO: Add more requirements for UVA if needed.\n    return is_pin_memory_available() or current_platform.is_cpu()\n\n\n@cache\ndef num_compute_units(device_id: int = 0) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Get the number of compute units of the current device.\"\"\"\n    from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n\n    return current_platform.num_compute_units(device_id)\n\n\n@cache\ndef get_device_name_as_file_name(device_id: int = 0) -> str:\n    from vllm.platforms import current_platform\n\n    name = current_platform.get_device_name(device_id)\n    name = re.sub(r\"[\\s/]+\", \"_\", name)\n    return name\n====\n\n    grid = lambda meta: (num_tokens * (hidden_dim // meta[\"BLOCK_SIZE\"]),)\n    compute_identity_kernel[grid](\n        top_k,\n        hidden_states,\n        zero_expert_scales,\n        num_tokens,\n        output,\n        hidden_dim,\n        zero_expert_scales.stride(0),\n        BLOCK_SIZE=256,\n    )\n\n    return output\n\n\n# Adapted from: https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/2628\ndef get_config_file_name(\n    E: int, N: int, dtype: str | None, block_shape: list[int] | None = None\n) -> str:\n    device_name = get_device_name_as_file_name()\n    # Set device_name to H200 if a device from the H200 family is detected\n    if \"H200\" in device_name.split(\"_\"):\n        device_name = \"NVIDIA_H200\"\n    dtype_selector = \"\" if not dtype else f\",dtype={dtype}\"\n    block_shape_selector = (\n        \"\" if not block_shape or not all(block_shape) else f\",block_shape={block_shape}\"\n    ).replace(\" \", \"\")\n    return f\"E={E},N={N},device_name={device_name}{dtype_selector}{block_shape_selector}.json\"  # noqa: E501\n\n\n# Adapted from: https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/2628\n@functools.lru_cache\ndef get_moe_configs(\n    E: int,\n    N: int,\n    dtype: str | None,\n    block_n: int | None = None,\n    block_k: int | None = None,\n) -> dict[int, Any] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return optimized configurations for the fused MoE kernel.\n\n    The return value will be a dictionary that maps an irregular grid of\n    batch sizes to configurations of the fused_moe kernel. To evaluate the\n    kernel on a given batch size bs, the closest batch size in the grid should\n    be picked and the associated configuration chosen to invoke the kernel.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Avoid optimizing for the batch invariant case. Use default config\n    if envs.VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT:\n        return None\n\n    # First look up if an optimized configuration is available in the configs\n    # directory\n    block_shape = [block_n, block_k] if block_n and block_k else None\n    json_file_name = get_config_file_name(E, N, dtype, block_shape)\n\n    config_file_paths = []\n\n    # note that we prioritize user defined config\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787998490220},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Chain: `get_moe_configs` → `get_config_file_name` → `get_device_name_as_file_name` → `current_platform.get_device_name(0)` → rocm's get_device_name is decorated with `@with_amdsmi_context` → calls `amdsmi_init()`, but that's undefined (amdsmi import failed) → NameError.\n\nRocmPlatform's `get_device_name` uses the amdsmi wrapper — there's no fallback path for the case when amdsmi doesn't exist. The fork's patch 0003 fixed platform DETECTION (KFD topology), but this runtime helper still assumes amdsmi. This affects any execution inside this container (including TP2 first-light) → a campaign-level blocker that should be typed/documented, but my proxy task can't patch the fork…\n\nWait — can I work around this without touching the fork? Options:\n\nA) get_moe_configs → first searches for a tuned config file… but it calls get_config_file_name before scanning the directory (needs the name to construct the path). The NameError happens in computing the name → unavoidable via configs.\n\nB) Provide amdsmi! amdsmi is a python package bundled with the ROCm install (opt/rocm/share/amd_smi). Does rocm-sdk-core in the container have amd_smi? Check `find /opt -name 'amdsmi*'` inside the container. If the python wheel for amdsmi exists but just isn't installed / isn't on the path — add a PYTHONPATH env var to the serving command! Clean, no fork change, no new wheel (part of the already-pinned rocm-sdk substrate!). amdsmi also needs the libamd_smi.so shared library — if it's included in the rocm-sdk-core package it should exist.\n\nLet me check for amdsmi inside the container.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1819e204f701432596efabc3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"podman run --rm --entrypoint bash flashnext:dev -c \"python3 -c 'import amdsmi' 2>&1 | head -2; find /opt /usr -maxdepth 6 -name 'amdsmi' -o -name 'amd_smi' 2>/dev/null | head -10; ls /opt/rocm* 2>/dev/null | head\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"^WARNING\\|^INFO\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3013,"output":525,"cacheRead":280576,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":394,"totalTokens":284114,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787998490229,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3c1a73ec-541d-419d-b816-4c8006694987","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1819e204f701432596efabc3","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787998505898},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No amdsmi anywhere in the container (find turned up nothing). Therefore:\n- amdsmi cannot be imported; the with_amdsmi_context helper raises NameError at runtime.\n- This NameError should also hit the real TP2 first light → campaign-level typed blocker for the fork lane (needs a fork patch like the KFD fallback of 0003).\n\nWithin my own boundary, what can I do to get claim 4.1 proven tonight? get_moe_configs is only called during MoE forward for tuned-config lookup... the call chain was triton_moe.apply → try_get_optimal_moe_config → get_moe_configs. Can I bypass it? try_get_optimal_moe_config probably has an env var escape hatch (does VLLM_TUNED_MOE disable the lookup? or envs.VLLM_DISABLE_MOE_TUNING?). Let me check around lines 1440–1470 of fused_moe.py.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_5ce363f6287d4c108f17197c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"podman run --rm --entrypoint bash flashnext:dev -c \"sed -n '1440,1480p' /opt/vllm/vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/fused_moe.py; grep -n 'VLLM_ROCM_MOE\\|tuned_config\\|VLLM_TUNED' /opt/vllm/vllm/envs.py | head\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"^WARNING\\|^INFO\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1142,"output":337,"cacheRead":283008,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":202,"totalTokens":284487,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787998505908,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c8e86ec6-754a-48e7-81d2-807a3bd1ea3b","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_5ce363f6287d4c108f17197c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"        }\n    return config\n\n\ndef try_get_optimal_moe_config(\n    w1_shape: tuple[int, ...],\n    w2_shape: tuple[int, ...],\n    top_k: int,\n    dtype: str | None,\n    M: int,\n    block_shape: list[int] | None = None,\n) -> dict[str, int]:\n    from vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe import get_config\n\n    override_config = get_config()\n    if override_config:\n        config = override_config\n    else:\n        # First try to load optimal config from the file\n        E, _, N = w2_shape\n        if dtype == \"int4_w4a16\":\n            N = N * 2\n        block_n = block_shape[0] if block_shape else 0\n        block_k = block_shape[1] if block_shape else 0\n        configs = get_moe_configs(E, N, dtype, block_n, block_k)\n\n        if configs:\n            # If an optimal configuration map has been found, look up the\n            # optimal config\n            config = configs[min(configs.keys(), key=lambda x: abs(x - M))]\n        else:\n            # Else use the default config\n            config = get_default_config(M, E, N, w1_shape[2], top_k, dtype, block_shape)\n    return config\n\n\ndef fused_experts_op(\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\n    w1: torch.Tensor,\n    w2: torch.Tensor,\n    topk_weights: torch.Tensor,\n155:    VLLM_ROCM_MOE_PADDING: bool = True\n269:    VLLM_TUNED_CONFIG_FOLDER: str | None = None\n1357:    \"VLLM_ROCM_MOE_PADDING\": lambda: bool(int(os.getenv(\"VLLM_ROCM_MOE_PADDING\", \"1\"))),\n1890:    \"VLLM_TUNED_CONFIG_FOLDER\": lambda: os.getenv(\"VLLM_TUNED_CONFIG_FOLDER\", None),\n2312:        \"VLLM_TUNED_CONFIG_FOLDER\",\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787998516682},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`override_config = get_config()` — if an override config is set, the file lookup is skipped (the cause of the NameError). How do you set the override? `from vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe import get_config` — this is normally set via the environment variable `VLLM_MOE_CONFIG` / a config override (does `get_config` read envs.VLLM_MOE_CONFIG_FILE? or does it read \"VLLM_TUNED_CONFIG_FOLDER\"?). Let me check the implementation of get_config.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_54b1fa530452427ea965af13","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"podman run --rm --entrypoint bash flashnext:dev -c \"grep -rn 'def get_config' /opt/vllm/vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/_